// B/B Practice Test flashcards: original cards that practice what the B/B questions (Q113–171) of AAMC Practice Exams
// 1, 3, 4, 5, 6 and the Sample Test ask. One twin card per question: the same ask, the same kind of choices, new nouns and
// numbers; a passage question's card carries a short twin passage. Same shape as data/cp-test-cards.js. TODAY's B/B
// flashcard step uses only these (masteryFlashPool in js/mastery.js); they load with the C/P cards (BANKS.cpt).
// freq: cards sharing the method; tier: core (3+), common (2), rare (1). Checked by tools/test-bb-test-cards.cjs.
const BB_TEST_CARDS = [  {"id":"bbt-pe1-113a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"dominance and inheritance rules","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:113#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:113"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P1","passage":"In some liver cancers, a repair defect creates a short form of Protein R, called RΔ4. Protein R normally helps cells clear damaged proteins. Liver cancer cells making only RWT formed tumors of 900 mm3 in mice. Cells making only RΔ4 formed tumors of 200 mm3. In cells making both forms, damaged proteins built up just as in cells making RΔ4 alone.","q":"Based on the data presented in the passage, which statement best describes the RΔ4 allele?","choices":["Tumor-promoting and dominant to RWT","Tumor-promoting and recessive to RWT","Tumor-suppressing and dominant to RWT","Tumor-suppressing and recessive to RWT"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"RΔ4 tumors smaller: suppressing","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Mix acts like RΔ4: dominant","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tumor size tells promote or suppress; the mixed cells tell dominance."},"exp":"**Answer:** Tumor-suppressing and dominant to RWT\n\n**Incorrect: Tumor-promoting and dominant to RWT** RΔ4 tumors were smaller, so it does not promote tumors.\n\n**Incorrect: Tumor-promoting and recessive to RWT** RΔ4 tumors were smaller, and RΔ4 wins in mixed cells.\n\n**Correct: Tumor-suppressing and dominant to RWT** RΔ4 cells made smaller tumors. In cells with both forms, the RΔ4 effect showed, so it is dominant.\n\n**Incorrect: Tumor-suppressing and recessive to RWT** A recessive allele is hidden in mixed cells, but the RΔ4 effect showed.","how":{"ask":"Does RΔ4 help or hurt tumors, and does it win over RWT?","know":"Dominant = its effect shows when both forms are present.","steps":[{"text":"RΔ4 tumors were much smaller than RWT tumors.","hl":"Cells making only RΔ4 formed tumors of 200 mm3"},{"text":"So RΔ4 suppresses tumors. Cut both promoting choices.","strike":["Tumor-promoting and dominant to RWT","Tumor-promoting and recessive to RWT"]},{"text":"Mixed cells acted like RΔ4 alone, so RΔ4 is dominant.","hl":"In cells making both forms, damaged proteins built up just as in cells making RΔ4 alone","strike":["Tumor-suppressing and recessive to RWT"]},{"text":"Answer: Tumor-suppressing and dominant to RWT."}],"trap":"Tumor-promoting: a cancer mutant seems like it should help cancer, but the tumor sizes say no."},"teach":[{"text":"Read the two data points one at a time.","marks":[{"t":"formed tumors of 200 mm3","note":"smaller = suppressing"},{"t":"just as in cells making RΔ4 alone","note":"mix = dominant"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** If the mixed cell looks like the mutant, the mutant allele is dominant."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-114a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:114#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:114"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P1","passage":"In some liver cancers, a 15-adenine repeat in Intron 3 of the Protein R gene loses 2 to 7 bases. Longer deletions cause Exon 4 to be skipped during splicing. Skipping Exon 4 creates an early stop codon in Exon 5. Total R mRNA is normal RWT mRNA plus short RΔ4 mRNA. RΔ4 mRNA was 0% of total R mRNA at a 2-base deletion, 40% at 3 bases, 85% at 4 bases, and 100% at 6 or 7 bases.","q":"Based on the passage, in liver cancer cells, the size of the deletion in the Protein R adenine repeat is inversely correlated with:","choices":["the amount of RΔ4 protein made.","the number of mature RWT transcripts made.","the frequency of the skipping of Exon 4 during splicing.","the extent of early translation stop in Exon 5."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Protein levels were never measured","c":[0]},{"f":"Skips and stops rise with deletion","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"If RΔ4 share goes up, the RWT share must go down."},"exp":"**Answer:** the number of mature RWT transcripts made.\n\n**Incorrect: the amount of RΔ4 protein made.** The passage gives mRNA data only, and RΔ4 rises, not falls.\n\n**Correct: the number of mature RWT transcripts made.** RΔ4 mRNA climbs from 0% to 100% as deletions grow. So the RWT share drops: an inverse link.\n\n**Incorrect: the frequency of the skipping of Exon 4 during splicing.** Skipping goes up with deletion size, not down.\n\n**Incorrect: the extent of early translation stop in Exon 5.** Early stops go up with skipping, not down.","how":{"ask":"What goes DOWN as the deletion gets bigger?","know":"Inverse = one rises while the other falls.","steps":[{"text":"RΔ4 mRNA rises as deletion size rises.","hl":"RΔ4 mRNA was 0% of total R mRNA at a 2-base deletion"},{"text":"Total = RWT + RΔ4, so RWT must fall.","hl":"Total R mRNA is normal RWT mRNA plus short RΔ4 mRNA"},{"text":"Skipping and early stops rise with RΔ4; protein was never measured.","strike":["the amount of RΔ4 protein made.","the frequency of the skipping of Exon 4 during splicing.","the extent of early translation stop in Exon 5."]},{"text":"Answer: the number of mature RWT transcripts made."}],"trap":"Exon 4 skipping: it is linked to deletion size, but it rises, so the link is direct, not inverse."},"teach":[{"text":"Two parts add up to a whole.","marks":[{"t":"Total R mRNA is normal RWT mRNA plus short RΔ4 mRNA","note":"parts sum to 100%"},{"t":"100% at 6 or 7 bases","note":"RWT now 0%"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** When one share of a total rises, the other share is inversely correlated."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-115a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"dominance and inheritance rules","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:115#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:115"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P1","passage":"In some liver cancers, a repair defect deletes bases from a repeat in the Protein R gene. This deletion makes a short mRNA called RΔ4. People with this cancer have RΔ4 transcripts in tumor tissue only. Their healthy tissues make only normal RWT mRNA.","q":"A woman with a liver cancer mutation that results in the synthesis of RΔ4 and a man that does not carry this mutation in any of his tissues have a child. What is the percent chance that the child will inherit the liver cancer mutation?","choices":["0%","25%","50%","100%"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Tumor-only mutation: not in eggs","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tumor-only means somatic, and somatic mutations are not passed on."},"exp":"**Answer:** 0%\n\n**Correct: 0%** The mutation is found only in tumor tissue, so it is somatic. Eggs do not carry it, so the child cannot inherit it.\n\n**Incorrect: 25%** This treats it like a recessive germline cross, but it is not in germ cells.\n\n**Incorrect: 50%** This treats the mother as a germline carrier, but her eggs lack the mutation.\n\n**Incorrect: 100%** Nothing makes the child certain to get a tumor-only mutation.","how":{"ask":"Can a tumor-only mutation pass to a child?","know":"Only germline (egg or sperm) mutations are inherited.","steps":[{"text":"RΔ4 is found only in tumor tissue.","hl":"RΔ4 transcripts in tumor tissue only"},{"text":"So the mutation is somatic, not in her eggs.","hl":"Their healthy tissues make only normal RWT mRNA"},{"text":"No Punnett square applies. Cut every nonzero answer.","strike":["25%","50%","100%"]},{"text":"Answer: 0%."}],"trap":"50%: it looks like a carrier-parent cross, but the mother has no germline copy."},"teach":[{"text":"Check where the mutation lives.","marks":[{"t":"in tumor tissue only","note":"somatic"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A mutation found only in tumor cells is somatic, so the chance of inheriting it is 0%."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-116a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:116#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:116"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P1","passage":"Some endometrial cancers lack a working mismatch repair (MMR) system. MMR finds bases that were paired wrongly during DNA replication and replaces them. Without MMR, short repeats in the genome gain and lose bases quickly. This genetic instability is called MSI.","q":"Which of the following nucleotide pairs would the MMR system described in the passage most likely recognize?\nI. dGMP and dTMP\nII. dCMP and dAMP\nIII. dGMP and dCMP","choices":["I only","III only","I and II only","I, II, and III"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"G–C is a correct pair","c":[1,3]},{"f":"C–A is also a mismatch","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only A–T and G–C are correct; MMR flags every other pair."},"exp":"**Answer:** I and II only\n\n**Incorrect: I only** C paired with A is also a mismatch, so II is recognized too.\n\n**Incorrect: III only** G with C is a correct pair, so MMR ignores it.\n\n**Correct: I and II only** G–T and C–A are both wrong pairs. G–C is a correct pair and is left alone.\n\n**Incorrect: I, II, and III** G–C is a correct Watson-Crick pair, not a mismatch.","how":{"ask":"Which pairs are wrong pairs?","know":"Correct pairs: A–T and G–C only.","steps":[{"text":"MMR fixes bases paired wrongly.","hl":"MMR finds bases that were paired wrongly during DNA replication"},{"text":"III (G–C) is a correct pair. Cut choices with III.","strike":["III only","I, II, and III"]},{"text":"I (G–T) and II (C–A) are both mismatches. Cut I only.","strike":["I only"]},{"text":"Answer: I and II only."}],"trap":"I only: G–T is the famous mismatch, but C–A is wrong too."},"teach":[{"text":"MMR acts on wrong pairs.","marks":[{"t":"paired wrongly","note":"mismatch = target"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Any pair that is not A–T or G–C is a mismatch for MMR."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-117a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mrna splicing","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:117#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:117"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P1","passage":"A 15-adenine repeat sits in Intron 3 of the gene for Protein R. Long deletions in this repeat cause Exon 4 to be skipped during pre-mRNA splicing. Translation of the normal R mRNA ends in Exon 6.","q":"The information in the passage best supports the conclusion that Intron 3 of Protein R most likely contains which of the following?","choices":["Stop codon","Splice acceptor site","Protein R gene promoter","Partial coding sequence of Protein R"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Introns are spliced out, not coded","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Promoter sits upstream of gene","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"An intron change that makes an exon skip points to a splice site."},"exp":"**Answer:** Splice acceptor site\n\n**Incorrect: Stop codon** Introns are removed before translation, and translation ends in Exon 6.\n\n**Correct: Splice acceptor site** The end of Intron 3 holds the site that joins Exon 4. Damage it and Exon 4 gets skipped.\n\n**Incorrect: Protein R gene promoter** The promoter sits upstream of the gene, not inside an intron.\n\n**Incorrect: Partial coding sequence of Protein R** Coding sequence is in exons; introns are cut out.","how":{"ask":"What in an intron controls whether the next exon is kept?","know":"Splice sites at intron ends mark where exons join.","steps":[{"text":"A change in Intron 3 makes Exon 4 drop out.","hl":"Long deletions in this repeat cause Exon 4 to be skipped"},{"text":"Introns are spliced out, so they hold no codons.","strike":["Stop codon","Partial coding sequence of Protein R"]},{"text":"The promoter is upstream, not in Intron 3.","strike":["Protein R gene promoter"]},{"text":"Answer: Splice acceptor site."}],"trap":"Stop codon: the passage talks about stops, but those are in exons."},"teach":[{"text":"Link the intron change to splicing.","marks":[{"t":"Exon 4 to be skipped during pre-mRNA splicing","note":"splice site hit"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An intron mutation that causes exon skipping most likely hits a splice site."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-118a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:118#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:118"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P1","passage":"Protein R is a heat shock protein. It binds partly folded proteins and helps them reach their proper shape. It also lowers the amount of clumped, useless protein in the cell.","q":"According to the information in the passage, Protein R is most likely which type of protein?","choices":["Transport","Chaperone","Cytoskeletal","Hormone"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Helps folding = chaperone","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Heat shock plus folding help means chaperone."},"exp":"**Answer:** Chaperone\n\n**Incorrect: Transport** Transport proteins move molecules across membranes, not fold proteins.\n\n**Correct: Chaperone** Protein R helps other proteins fold and stops clumping. That is what chaperones do.\n\n**Incorrect: Cytoskeletal** Cytoskeletal proteins give shape and movement, not folding help.\n\n**Incorrect: Hormone** Hormones carry signals between cells, not fold proteins.","how":{"ask":"What job does Protein R do?","know":"Chaperones help proteins fold and prevent clumps.","steps":[{"text":"Protein R helps other proteins fold.","hl":"helps them reach their proper shape"},{"text":"It also cuts down on clumps.","hl":"lowers the amount of clumped, useless protein"},{"text":"No other choice folds proteins.","strike":["Transport","Cytoskeletal","Hormone"]},{"text":"Answer: Chaperone."}],"trap":"Transport: it binds other proteins, but binding to fold is not moving."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the job word.","marks":[{"t":"heat shock protein","note":"classic chaperone"},{"t":"proper shape","note":"folding help"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A protein that helps others fold is a chaperone."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-119a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:119#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:119"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P2","passage":"Gut cells absorb leucine through NT3, a transporter for neutral amino acids. NT3 moves leucine into the cell against its gradient. At the same time, it moves one sodium ion into the cell down its gradient. Loss of NT3 lowers leucine absorption and can cause skin and gut disease.","q":"Which amino acid, in addition to leucine, is most likely to be transported by NT3?","choices":["Arginine","Isoleucine","Aspartate","Lysine"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Arg and Lys are +charged","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Aspartate is −charged","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Neutral transporter: pick the side chain with no charge."},"exp":"**Answer:** Isoleucine\n\n**Incorrect: Arginine** Arginine is positively charged, not neutral.\n\n**Correct: Isoleucine** NT3 carries neutral amino acids. Isoleucine has a nonpolar, uncharged side chain, like leucine.\n\n**Incorrect: Aspartate** Aspartate is negatively charged, not neutral.\n\n**Incorrect: Lysine** Lysine is positively charged, not neutral.","how":{"ask":"Which choice is a neutral amino acid?","know":"Basic = Lys, Arg, His; acidic = Asp, Glu.","steps":[{"text":"NT3 carries neutral amino acids.","hl":"a transporter for neutral amino acids"},{"text":"Arginine and lysine are basic. Cut them.","strike":["Arginine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Aspartate is acidic. Cut it.","strike":["Aspartate"]},{"text":"Answer: Isoleucine."}],"trap":"Lysine: it is common in diet, but its + charge rules it out."},"teach":[{"text":"The transporter class sets the rule.","marks":[{"t":"neutral amino acids","note":"no charge"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A neutral amino acid transporter carries uncharged side chains, not Lys, Arg, Asp or Glu."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-120a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"protein targeting and secretion","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:120#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:120"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P2","passage":"NT3 sits in the plasma membrane facing the gut lumen. Protein Z, a membrane enzyme, is needed to place NT3 at that membrane. NT3 moves neutral amino acids from the gut into the cell.","q":"Based on the information in the passage, a mature NT3 mRNA is most likely to contain a sequence coding for which genetic factor(s)?","choices":["Introns","Enhancer","Signal sequence","Mitochondrial targeting signal"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Mature mRNA: introns removed","c":[0]},{"f":"Enhancers are DNA, not mRNA","c":[1]},{"f":"Plasma membrane, not mitochondria","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Membrane or secreted protein means its mRNA codes a signal sequence."},"exp":"**Answer:** Signal sequence\n\n**Incorrect: Introns** Introns are spliced out before mRNA is mature.\n\n**Incorrect: Enhancer** Enhancers are DNA control regions, not in mRNA.\n\n**Correct: Signal sequence** NT3 is a plasma membrane protein. Its mRNA codes a signal sequence that sends it to the rough ER.\n\n**Incorrect: Mitochondrial targeting signal** NT3 goes to the plasma membrane, not mitochondria.","how":{"ask":"What does a membrane protein's mRNA carry?","know":"Plasma membrane proteins start in the rough ER via a signal sequence.","steps":[{"text":"NT3 is a plasma membrane protein.","hl":"NT3 sits in the plasma membrane"},{"text":"Mature mRNA has no introns; enhancers stay in DNA.","strike":["Introns","Enhancer"]},{"text":"NT3 does not go to mitochondria.","strike":["Mitochondrial targeting signal"]},{"text":"Answer: Signal sequence."}],"trap":"Enhancer: it controls the gene, but it is never part of the mRNA."},"teach":[{"text":"Find where the protein lives.","marks":[{"t":"plasma membrane facing the gut lumen","note":"ER route"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Membrane and secreted proteins have mRNA coding a signal sequence."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-121a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:121#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:121"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P2","passage":"Researchers gave a gut-damaging chemical to wild-type (WT) mice and to mice lacking the X-linked Zx gene (Zx-null). The mice then ate either a normal diet or a diet with no protein. On the normal diet, WT mice lost 3% of body weight and Zx-null mice lost 12%. On the no-protein diet, WT mice lost 18% and Zx-null mice lost 21%.","q":"Which effect did a no-protein diet have on chemical-treated WT and Zx-null mice, respectively?","choices":["The WT mice lost weight, and the Zx-null mice gained weight.","Both the WT and the Zx-null mice lost weight.","Both the WT and the Zx-null mice gained weight.","The WT mice gained weight, and the Zx-null mice lost weight."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Both rows show weight loss","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the no-protein row; both numbers are losses."},"exp":"**Answer:** Both the WT and the Zx-null mice lost weight.\n\n**Incorrect: The WT mice lost weight, and the Zx-null mice gained weight.** Zx-null mice lost 21%, not gained.\n\n**Correct: Both the WT and the Zx-null mice lost weight.** On the no-protein diet, WT lost 18% and Zx-null lost 21%.\n\n**Incorrect: Both the WT and the Zx-null mice gained weight.** Both numbers are losses.\n\n**Incorrect: The WT mice gained weight, and the Zx-null mice lost weight.** WT mice lost 18%, not gained.","how":{"ask":"Did each group gain or lose on the no-protein diet?","know":"Just read the right row.","steps":[{"text":"Find the no-protein diet numbers.","hl":"On the no-protein diet, WT mice lost 18% and Zx-null mice lost 21%"},{"text":"Both are losses. Cut every choice with a gain.","strike":["The WT mice lost weight, and the Zx-null mice gained weight.","Both the WT and the Zx-null mice gained weight.","The WT mice gained weight, and the Zx-null mice lost weight."]},{"text":"Answer: Both the WT and the Zx-null mice lost weight."}],"trap":"WT lost, Zx-null gained: the Zx-null loss is only a bit bigger, but it is still a loss."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the diet to its row.","marks":[{"t":"WT mice lost 18% and Zx-null mice lost 21%","note":"both lost"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For a respectively question, read each group's value in the named row only."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-122a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:122#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:122"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P2","passage":"Tryptophan is used to make niacin. Niacin forms NAD+, the carrier that picks up electrons in respiration. Low tryptophan intake can therefore lower NAD+ levels in cells.","q":"Niacin deficiency also causes skin and gut disease. Niacin nucleotides are neither oxidized nor reduced during which step of cellular respiration?","choices":["Glycolysis","Pyruvate oxidation","Electron transport chain","Chemiosmosis"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Glycolysis, pyruvate step reduce NAD+","c":[0,1]},{"f":"ETC oxidizes NADH","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Chemiosmosis uses the H+ gradient only; no NAD+ or NADH."},"exp":"**Answer:** Chemiosmosis\n\n**Incorrect: Glycolysis** Glycolysis reduces NAD+ to NADH.\n\n**Incorrect: Pyruvate oxidation** Pyruvate oxidation reduces NAD+ to NADH.\n\n**Incorrect: Electron transport chain** The ETC oxidizes NADH back to NAD+.\n\n**Correct: Chemiosmosis** H+ flows through ATP synthase to make ATP. No NAD+ or NADH changes here.","how":{"ask":"Which step never touches NAD+ or NADH?","know":"NAD+ is reduced in glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation and Krebs; NADH is oxidized at the ETC.","steps":[{"text":"NAD+ is the electron carrier.","hl":"the carrier that picks up electrons in respiration"},{"text":"Glycolysis and pyruvate oxidation make NADH.","strike":["Glycolysis","Pyruvate oxidation"]},{"text":"The ETC turns NADH back into NAD+.","strike":["Electron transport chain"]},{"text":"Answer: Chemiosmosis."}],"trap":"Electron transport chain: it is the end stage, but it does oxidize NADH."},"teach":[{"text":"NAD+ carries electrons.","marks":[{"t":"picks up electrons","note":"gets reduced"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Chemiosmosis is the only respiration step where NAD+ and NADH do not change."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-123a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"x-linked cross probability","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:123#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:123"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P2","passage":"The Zx gene is on the X chromosome in mice. Zx is needed to place the NT3 transporter in the gut cell membrane. Mice with a loss-of-function Zx allele absorb less leucine.","q":"If a male mouse with a normal copy of Zx has pups with a female that is heterozygous for the mutant Zx allele, what is the probability that a pup will be male and hemizygous for the mutant Zx allele?","choices":["0","0.25","0.5","1"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Male 1/2 × mutant X 1/2","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sons get their only X from mom: 1/2 male × 1/2 mutant X."},"exp":"**Answer:** 0.25\n\n**Incorrect: 0** Sons get an X from the carrier mother, so a mutant son is possible.\n\n**Correct: 0.25** Half the pups are male. Each son has a 1/2 chance of getting the mutant X from mom. 1/2 × 1/2 = 0.25.\n\n**Incorrect: 0.5** This is the chance among sons only; it forgets the 1/2 chance of being male.\n\n**Incorrect: 1** Mom passes the normal X half the time.","how":{"ask":"Chance of a pup that is male AND carries mom's mutant X?","know":"A son's single X always comes from his mother.","steps":[{"text":"Zx is X-linked.","hl":"The Zx gene is on the X chromosome in mice"},{"text":"P(male) = 1/2. P(mom gives mutant X) = 1/2.","strike":["0","1"]},{"text":"Multiply: 1/2 × 1/2 = 0.25. 0.5 forgets the sex step.","strike":["0.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.25."}],"trap":"0.5: that is the chance for a son, not for any pup."},"teach":[{"text":"X-linked: track the X.","marks":[{"t":"on the X chromosome","note":"X-linked"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For sex AND genotype, multiply 1/2 for sex by the parent's chance of passing the allele."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-124a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"muscle contraction and fiber type","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:124#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:124"],"q":"During the initiation of muscle contraction, tropomyosin shifts to expose actin after troponin binds to which ion?","choices":["Mg2+","Ca2+","Cl–","K+"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Troponin binds only calcium","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Troponin's partner is always Ca2+."},"exp":"**Answer:** Ca2+\n\n**Incorrect: Mg2+** Mg2+ helps ATP bind myosin, not troponin.\n\n**Correct: Ca2+** Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin. Tropomyosin moves and myosin can bind actin.\n\n**Incorrect: Cl–** Cl– affects membrane potential, not troponin.\n\n**Incorrect: K+** K+ sets resting potential, not troponin binding.","how":{"ask":"Which ion binds troponin?","know":"Ca2+ binds troponin C and moves tropomyosin.","steps":[{"text":"Troponin is the calcium sensor of muscle.","strike":["Cl–","K+"]},{"text":"Mg2+ works with ATP, not troponin.","strike":["Mg2+"]},{"text":"Answer: Ca2+."}],"trap":"Mg2+: it is needed in muscle, but with ATP, not troponin."},"teach":[{"text":"Ca2+ is released from the SR when the muscle fires."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ca2+ binds troponin, tropomyosin moves, and myosin binds actin."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-125a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:125#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:125"],"q":"What is the total number of six-membered rings in the fused core of a steroid?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Steroid core: 6-6-6-5","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Steroid core is 6-6-6-5: three six-rings, one five-ring."},"exp":"**Answer:** 3\n\n**Incorrect: 1** A steroid has four fused rings, three of them six-membered.\n\n**Incorrect: 2** A steroid has three six-membered rings, not two.\n\n**Correct: 3** The steroid core is 6-6-6-5. That is three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring.\n\n**Incorrect: 4** Four is the total ring count; one ring has five members.","how":{"ask":"How many six-rings are in a steroid?","know":"Steroid core = 6-6-6-5.","steps":[{"text":"Picture the 6-6-6-5 core.","strike":["1","2"]},{"text":"Four rings total, but one is a five-ring.","strike":["4"]},{"text":"Answer: 3."}],"trap":"4: that counts all rings, including the five-ring."},"teach":[{"text":"All steroids, like cholesterol and cortisol, share one core."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A steroid has four fused rings: three six-membered and one five-membered."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-126a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:126#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:126"],"q":"Migraine aura may result from a wave of excess neuron firing that spreads across the visual cortex. A drug to prevent the aura would be most effective if it caused which of the following changes in the affected neurons?","choices":["An increase in extracellular K+ concentration","A more negative resting membrane potential","An increase in membrane permeability to Na+","A decrease in the length of the refractory period"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"High outside K+ depolarizes","c":[0]},{"f":"More Na+ entry = more firing","c":[2]},{"f":"Short refractory = faster refiring","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the change that pushes neurons further from threshold."},"exp":"**Answer:** A more negative resting membrane potential\n\n**Incorrect: An increase in extracellular K+ concentration** More K+ outside depolarizes neurons and makes firing easier.\n\n**Correct: A more negative resting membrane potential** The neuron sits further from threshold. It is harder to fire, so the wave spreads less.\n\n**Incorrect: An increase in membrane permeability to Na+** More Na+ entry depolarizes and boosts firing.\n\n**Incorrect: A decrease in the length of the refractory period** Neurons could fire again sooner, raising firing.","how":{"ask":"Which change makes neurons fire less?","know":"Further from threshold = less firing.","steps":[{"text":"High outside K+ and more Na+ entry both depolarize.","strike":["An increase in extracellular K+ concentration","An increase in membrane permeability to Na+"]},{"text":"A shorter refractory period lets neurons refire faster.","strike":["A decrease in the length of the refractory period"]},{"text":"Answer: A more negative resting membrane potential."}],"trap":"Extracellular K+: more ions sound calming, but high outside K+ depolarizes."},"teach":[{"text":"To calm a seizure-like wave, lower excitability."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A drug that moves neurons further from threshold reduces spreading firing."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-127a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nephron segment function","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:127#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:127"],"q":"When concentrated urine is being produced, in which of the following regions of the kidney will the tubular fluid have its lowest osmolarity?","choices":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Distal convoluted tubule","Bottom of the loop of Henle","Medullary portion of the collecting duct"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"PCT stays iso-osmotic, about 300","c":[0]},{"f":"Loop tip, medullary duct: concentrated","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Salt pumped out of the ascending limb leaves the early distal tubule most dilute."},"exp":"**Answer:** Distal convoluted tubule\n\n**Incorrect: Proximal convoluted tubule** Water and salt leave together here, so fluid stays near 300 mOsm.\n\n**Correct: Distal convoluted tubule** The thick ascending limb pumps out salt but not water. Fluid enters the distal tubule at its most dilute.\n\n**Incorrect: Bottom of the loop of Henle** The loop tip is very concentrated, in the deep medulla.\n\n**Incorrect: Medullary portion of the collecting duct** With ADH, water leaves here, so the fluid is concentrated.","how":{"ask":"Where is tubular fluid most dilute?","know":"Ascending limb removes salt only; fluid then is most dilute.","steps":[{"text":"The PCT keeps fluid iso-osmotic.","strike":["Proximal convoluted tubule"]},{"text":"The loop tip and medullary duct are the concentrated spots.","strike":["Bottom of the loop of Henle","Medullary portion of the collecting duct"]},{"text":"Answer: Distal convoluted tubule."}],"trap":"Proximal tubule: it is early, but early is not dilute."},"teach":[{"text":"Map: PCT about 300, loop tip high, early DCT low, duct high with ADH."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Fluid is most dilute in the early distal tubule and most concentrated at the loop tip and medullary duct."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-128a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:128#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:128"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P3","passage":"In Experiment 1, researchers placed a Ca2+ ATPase into liposomes made of saturated (16:0) or monounsaturated (16:1) PC. They measured pump activity from 10°C to 40°C, with and without 30 mol% cholesterol. Activity rose with temperature in both liposome types. Activity changed less with temperature in 16:1 liposomes than in 16:0 liposomes. At every temperature, activity was highest in 16:1 liposomes, and cholesterol raised activity.","q":"What aspect of Experiment 1 does NOT address whether membrane composition has an effect on Ca2+ ATPase activity? The activity of the Ca2+ ATPase:","choices":["showed less temperature dependence in the 16:1 liposome than the 16:0 liposome.","was highest in the 16:1 liposomes at all temperatures.","increased with temperature in both the 16:1 liposome and the 16:0 liposome.","was greater at all temperatures when cholesterol was present."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Compares lipids: tests composition","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Cholesterol is composition too","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the result that is the same in both membranes; it compares nothing."},"exp":"**Answer:** increased with temperature in both the 16:1 liposome and the 16:0 liposome.\n\n**Incorrect: showed less temperature dependence in the 16:1 liposome than the 16:0 liposome.** This compares two lipids, so it tests composition.\n\n**Incorrect: was highest in the 16:1 liposomes at all temperatures.** This compares two lipids, so it tests composition.\n\n**Correct: increased with temperature in both the 16:1 liposome and the 16:0 liposome.** Most enzymes speed up with heat. A result shared by both membranes says nothing about composition.\n\n**Incorrect: was greater at all temperatures when cholesterol was present.** Cholesterol changes the membrane, so this tests composition.","how":{"ask":"Which result does not compare membranes?","know":"A test of composition must compare different membranes.","steps":[{"text":"16:1 vs 16:0 results compare lipids.","hl":"Activity changed less with temperature in 16:1 liposomes than in 16:0 liposomes","strike":["showed less temperature dependence in the 16:1 liposome than the 16:0 liposome.","was highest in the 16:1 liposomes at all temperatures."]},{"text":"Cholesterol is part of membrane composition.","strike":["was greater at all temperatures when cholesterol was present."]},{"text":"Rising with heat in both is general enzyme behavior.","hl":"Activity rose with temperature in both liposome types"},{"text":"Answer: increased with temperature in both the 16:1 liposome and the 16:0 liposome."}],"trap":"Cholesterol: it seems like an added factor, but it is part of membrane composition."},"teach":[{"text":"Look for a difference between membranes.","marks":[{"t":"in both liposome types","note":"no comparison"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A result that is the same in every group cannot test the variable that differs between groups."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-129a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"activation energy vs free energy","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:129#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:129"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P3","passage":"A Ca2+ ATPase was placed into liposomes. In each cycle, the pump splits one ATP into ADP and Pi. The energy from that split moves Ca2+ into the liposome against its gradient.","q":"Which approach does NOT measure the activity of the Ca2+ ATPase?","choices":["Measuring the rate of phosphate release","Measuring the rate of Ca2+ uptake into the liposome","Measuring the ΔG of ATP hydrolysis","Measuring the rate of ATP use"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Rates track activity; ΔG does not","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Activity is a rate; ΔG is not a rate."},"exp":"**Answer:** Measuring the ΔG of ATP hydrolysis\n\n**Incorrect: Measuring the rate of phosphate release** Pi is a product, so its rate tracks activity.\n\n**Incorrect: Measuring the rate of Ca2+ uptake into the liposome** Moving Ca2+ is the pump's job, so this tracks activity.\n\n**Correct: Measuring the ΔG of ATP hydrolysis** ΔG tells if a reaction is favorable, not how fast it goes.\n\n**Incorrect: Measuring the rate of ATP use** ATP is the substrate, so its use rate tracks activity.","how":{"ask":"Which choice is not a rate?","know":"Enzyme activity = how fast substrate turns into product.","steps":[{"text":"The pump uses ATP, makes Pi and moves Ca2+.","hl":"the pump splits one ATP into ADP and Pi"},{"text":"Rates of any of these track activity.","strike":["Measuring the rate of phosphate release","Measuring the rate of Ca2+ uptake into the liposome","Measuring the rate of ATP use"]},{"text":"Answer: Measuring the ΔG of ATP hydrolysis."}],"trap":"Ca2+ uptake: it is not a chemical product, but it is the pump's work per time."},"teach":[{"text":"Every substrate or product change per time is a rate.","marks":[{"t":"moves Ca2+ into the liposome","note":"countable output"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Thermodynamics (ΔG) says whether, not how fast; it does not measure enzyme activity."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-130a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"na+/k+ pump direction","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:130#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:130"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P3","passage":"The Na+K+ ATPase sets up the Na+ and K+ gradients across the cell membrane. These gradients largely set the cell's membrane potential. Weak pump activity is linked to poor heart function.","q":"What is the function of the Na+K+ ATPase in a heart muscle cell after an action potential?","choices":["Triggering the next action potential","Depolarization of the membrane","Repolarization by opening K+ channels","Restoration of the ion gradients"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Triggers, depolarizes: Na+ channels","c":[0,1]},{"f":"K+ channels repolarize, not pump","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Channels make the spike; the pump resets the gradients."},"exp":"**Answer:** Restoration of the ion gradients\n\n**Incorrect: Triggering the next action potential** Triggering comes from channels opening, not the pump.\n\n**Incorrect: Depolarization of the membrane** Depolarization comes from Na+ (or Ca2+) channels.\n\n**Incorrect: Repolarization by opening K+ channels** K+ channels open on their own; the pump does not open them.\n\n**Correct: Restoration of the ion gradients** The pump moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in to reset the gradients.","how":{"ask":"What does the pump do, as opposed to channels?","know":"Channels change voltage fast; the pump maintains gradients.","steps":[{"text":"The pump sets up the gradients.","hl":"sets up the Na+ and K+ gradients across the cell membrane"},{"text":"Triggering and depolarizing come from channels.","strike":["Triggering the next action potential","Depolarization of the membrane"]},{"text":"Repolarization is K+ channel work.","strike":["Repolarization by opening K+ channels"]},{"text":"Answer: Restoration of the ion gradients."}],"trap":"Repolarization: it happens after the spike too, but K+ channels do it."},"teach":[{"text":"Pump = maintenance.","marks":[{"t":"sets up the Na+ and K+ gradients","note":"pump job"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The Na+K+ ATPase restores and keeps ion gradients; channels drive the action potential."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-131a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"na+/k+ pump direction","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:131#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:131"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P3","passage":"The Na+K+ ATPase sets up the Na+ and K+ gradients across the cell membrane. It moves both ions against their gradients. Each cycle uses the energy of one ATP.","q":"For each ATP hydrolyzed, how many ions does the Na+K+ ATPase move, and in which direction?","choices":["2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in","3 Na+ in; 2 K+ out","3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in","3 Na+ and 2 K+ both in"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Na+ goes out, K+ in","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Ratio: 3 Na+ per 2 K+","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in: both against their gradients."},"exp":"**Answer:** 3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in\n\n**Incorrect: 2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in** The directions are right but the numbers are swapped.\n\n**Incorrect: 3 Na+ in; 2 K+ out** That moves both ions down their gradients, the reverse of the pump.\n\n**Correct: 3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in** Na+ is high outside and K+ is high inside. The pump pushes each against its gradient, 3 to 2.\n\n**Incorrect: 3 Na+ and 2 K+ both in** Na+ moving in would be down its gradient, not against it.","how":{"ask":"Which way and how many?","know":"Na+ is high outside; K+ is high inside.","steps":[{"text":"The pump moves ions against their gradients.","hl":"It moves both ions against their gradients"},{"text":"So Na+ goes out and K+ goes in.","strike":["3 Na+ in; 2 K+ out","3 Na+ and 2 K+ both in"]},{"text":"The ratio is 3 Na+ to 2 K+.","strike":["2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in"]},{"text":"Answer: 3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in."}],"trap":"2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in: right directions, swapped numbers."},"teach":[{"text":"Against the gradient means uphill.","marks":[{"t":"against their gradients","note":"Na+ out, K+ in"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Each ATP moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-132a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:132#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:132"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P4","passage":"In some lymphomas, the transcription factor STAT3 stays active all the time. Once active, STAT3 moves into the nucleus. Active STAT3 raises IL-10 mRNA levels and helps the cancer cells survive.","q":"Upon activation, STAT3 controls the level of IL-10 mRNA by:","choices":["binding mRNA.","degrading mRNA.","binding DNA.","replicating DNA."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Transcription factors bind DNA","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Not copying the genome","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Transcription factor means it binds DNA at the gene."},"exp":"**Answer:** binding DNA.\n\n**Incorrect: binding mRNA.** Transcription factors act on DNA, not mRNA.\n\n**Incorrect: degrading mRNA.** STAT3 raises IL-10 mRNA; it does not destroy mRNA.\n\n**Correct: binding DNA.** STAT3 is a transcription factor. It binds DNA near the IL-10 gene and boosts transcription.\n\n**Incorrect: replicating DNA.** Transcription factors control genes; they do not copy the genome.","how":{"ask":"How does a transcription factor change mRNA levels?","know":"Transcription factors bind promoter or enhancer DNA.","steps":[{"text":"STAT3 is a transcription factor in the nucleus.","hl":"the transcription factor STAT3"},{"text":"It works on DNA, not mRNA.","strike":["binding mRNA.","degrading mRNA."]},{"text":"It controls genes; it does not copy the genome.","strike":["replicating DNA."]},{"text":"Answer: binding DNA."}],"trap":"binding mRNA: the question says mRNA, but the control happens at the DNA."},"teach":[{"text":"The protein class gives the answer.","marks":[{"t":"transcription factor","note":"binds DNA"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Transcription factors change mRNA levels by binding DNA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-133a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"membrane transport type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:133#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:133"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P4","passage":"Drug K blocks a kinase inside lymphoma cells. Drug K is a small molecule built from fused aromatic rings. It carries no charge and has few polar groups.","q":"Considering the structure of Drug K, what is the most likely mechanism for its entry into the cell?","choices":["Primary active transport","Pinocytosis","Passage through a gated channel","Diffusion directly through the membrane"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Small, nonpolar: crosses lipid","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Small and nonpolar means it diffuses straight through the bilayer."},"exp":"**Answer:** Diffusion directly through the membrane\n\n**Incorrect: Primary active transport** A nonpolar drug needs no pump or ATP to enter.\n\n**Incorrect: Pinocytosis** Small nonpolar molecules do not need vesicles.\n\n**Incorrect: Passage through a gated channel** Channels carry ions and water, not uncharged ring drugs.\n\n**Correct: Diffusion directly through the membrane** Drug K is small, uncharged and nonpolar. It dissolves into the lipid bilayer and crosses it.","how":{"ask":"Can this molecule cross lipid on its own?","know":"Small nonpolar molecules diffuse through the bilayer.","steps":[{"text":"Drug K is small and uncharged.","hl":"It carries no charge and has few polar groups"},{"text":"No pump, vesicle or channel is needed.","strike":["Primary active transport","Pinocytosis","Passage through a gated channel"]},{"text":"Answer: Diffusion directly through the membrane."}],"trap":"Active transport: drugs act inside cells, but entry here is downhill and through lipid."},"teach":[{"text":"Read structure for polarity.","marks":[{"t":"fused aromatic rings","note":"hydrophobic"},{"t":"no charge","note":"crosses lipid"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A small, uncharged, mostly nonpolar drug enters by simple diffusion."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-134a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:134#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:134"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P4","passage":"In this lymphoma pathway, a receptor turns on protein kinase D (PKD). PKD acts directly on the adapter protein M7. Active M7 then turns on IKK, which frees NF-κB to enter the nucleus.","q":"Assuming no mutations to the signaling pathway described in the passage, what event directly activates adapter M7?","choices":["Phosphorylation","Ubiquitin tagging","GTP binding","Proteolytic cleavage"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Kinase acts directly: adds phosphate","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The upstream enzyme is a kinase, so the event is phosphorylation."},"exp":"**Answer:** Phosphorylation\n\n**Correct: Phosphorylation** PKD is a kinase and acts directly on M7. Kinases activate targets by adding phosphate groups.\n\n**Incorrect: Ubiquitin tagging** No ubiquitin ligase acts on M7 in the passage.\n\n**Incorrect: GTP binding** GTP binding activates G proteins, not targets of a kinase.\n\n**Incorrect: Proteolytic cleavage** No protease acts on M7 in the passage.","how":{"ask":"What does the enzyme just upstream of M7 do?","know":"Kinases add phosphate groups.","steps":[{"text":"PKD acts directly on M7.","hl":"PKD acts directly on the adapter protein M7"},{"text":"PKD is a kinase, so no tagging, GTP or cutting.","strike":["Ubiquitin tagging","GTP binding","Proteolytic cleavage"]},{"text":"Answer: Phosphorylation."}],"trap":"Proteolytic cleavage: NF-κB release involves breakdown, but that is further down the path."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the upstream enzyme.","marks":[{"t":"protein kinase D","note":"kinase = phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** If a kinase directly activates a protein, the event is phosphorylation."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-135a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:135#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:135"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P4","passage":"In some lymphomas, the transcription factor STAT3 stays active all the time. Active STAT3 enters the nucleus and turns on target genes such as IL-10. Drug K blocks a kinase that turns STAT3 on.","q":"According to the passage, what is most likely correct about the genes that are expressed as a result of the constant activation of STAT3?","choices":["They cause swelling of the ER.","They have STAT3 binding sites in their promoter region.","They have gained mutations that alter function.","They bind to Drug K in the nucleus."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Passage never mentions the ER","c":[0]},{"f":"Targets are turned on, not mutated","c":[2]},{"f":"Drug K binds kinase, not genes","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Genes a transcription factor turns on carry its binding site."},"exp":"**Answer:** They have STAT3 binding sites in their promoter region.\n\n**Incorrect: They cause swelling of the ER.** The passage never links these genes to the ER.\n\n**Correct: They have STAT3 binding sites in their promoter region.** STAT3 turns these genes on. To do that, it must bind DNA sites near their promoters.\n\n**Incorrect: They have gained mutations that alter function.** The genes are normal; they are just turned on more.\n\n**Incorrect: They bind to Drug K in the nucleus.** Drug K blocks a kinase, not genes.","how":{"ask":"What do all target genes of STAT3 share?","know":"A transcription factor turns on genes that carry its DNA binding site.","steps":[{"text":"STAT3 turns on target genes.","hl":"turns on target genes such as IL-10"},{"text":"No ER link and no mutation in the targets.","strike":["They cause swelling of the ER.","They have gained mutations that alter function."]},{"text":"Drug K acts on a kinase.","hl":"Drug K blocks a kinase","strike":["They bind to Drug K in the nucleus."]},{"text":"Answer: They have STAT3 binding sites in their promoter region."}],"trap":"Mutations: the cancer has mutations, but in the pathway, not in the target genes."},"teach":[{"text":"Targets are switched on, not changed.","marks":[{"t":"turns on target genes","note":"binding site needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Genes controlled by a transcription factor contain its binding site in their promoter or enhancer."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-136a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"membrane transport type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:136#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:136"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P5","passage":"BCRP is an ABC transporter in the plasma membrane of some tumor cells. It splits ATP and moves many fat-soluble drugs. Tumor cells with extra BCRP keep drug levels too low to kill them.","q":"Which mechanism best describes how BCRP causes drug resistance?","choices":["BCRP binds anticancer drugs and breaks them down using ATP.","BCRP blocks the entry of anticancer drugs into the cell.","BCRP acts as a pump and uses ATP to move anticancer drugs out of the cell.","BCRP makes the membrane leaky, so anticancer drugs leak out of the cell."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"ABC plus ATP = efflux pump","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"ABC transporter with ATPase activity pumps drugs out."},"exp":"**Answer:** BCRP acts as a pump and uses ATP to move anticancer drugs out of the cell.\n\n**Incorrect: BCRP binds anticancer drugs and breaks them down using ATP.** BCRP is a transporter, not a drug-degrading enzyme.\n\n**Incorrect: BCRP blocks the entry of anticancer drugs into the cell.** Transporters carry molecules across; they do not block entry.\n\n**Correct: BCRP acts as a pump and uses ATP to move anticancer drugs out of the cell.** ABC transporters use ATP for active transport. BCRP pumps drugs out, so they never reach toxic levels.\n\n**Incorrect: BCRP makes the membrane leaky, so anticancer drugs leak out of the cell.** Transporters move specific molecules; they do not make membranes leaky.","how":{"ask":"What does an ATP-using ABC transporter do to drugs?","know":"ABC transporters are ATP-driven pumps.","steps":[{"text":"BCRP is an ABC transporter that splits ATP.","hl":"It splits ATP and moves many fat-soluble drugs"},{"text":"It moves drugs; it does not break them down or block them.","strike":["BCRP binds anticancer drugs and breaks them down using ATP.","BCRP blocks the entry of anticancer drugs into the cell."]},{"text":"Pumps are selective; they do not make leaks.","strike":["BCRP makes the membrane leaky, so anticancer drugs leak out of the cell."]},{"text":"Answer: BCRP acts as a pump and uses ATP to move anticancer drugs out of the cell."}],"trap":"Breaks them down using ATP: ATP use sounds like an enzyme, but here it powers pumping."},"teach":[{"text":"Transporter + ATP = active pump.","marks":[{"t":"ABC transporter","note":"ATP pump"},{"t":"keep drug levels too low","note":"pumped out"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ABC transporters cause drug resistance by pumping drugs out using ATP."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-137a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:137#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:137"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P5","passage":"Researchers removed cholesterol from tumor cells rich in BCRP. BCRP ATPase activity fell to 30% of normal. Adding back 80 µM cholesterol raised ATPase activity to 100% of normal.","q":"The formation of which reaction products is increased in cholesterol-depleted BCRP cells after exposure to 80 µM cholesterol?","choices":["ADP + Pi","AMP + PPi","ATP + H2O","AMP + ADP"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"ATPase: ATP → ADP + Pi","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"More ATPase activity means more ADP and Pi."},"exp":"**Answer:** ADP + Pi\n\n**Correct: ADP + Pi** Cholesterol restored ATPase activity. An ATPase splits ATP into ADP and Pi.\n\n**Incorrect: AMP + PPi** That split happens in other reactions, like tRNA charging, not ATPases.\n\n**Incorrect: ATP + H2O** ATP and water are the reactants, not products.\n\n**Incorrect: AMP + ADP** An ATPase makes ADP, not AMP.","how":{"ask":"What does an ATPase make?","know":"ATP + H2O → ADP + Pi.","steps":[{"text":"Cholesterol raised ATPase activity.","hl":"raised ATPase activity to 100% of normal"},{"text":"ATP and water go in; they are not products.","strike":["ATP + H2O"]},{"text":"ATPases make ADP, not AMP.","strike":["AMP + PPi","AMP + ADP"]},{"text":"Answer: ADP + Pi."}],"trap":"AMP + PPi: also an ATP split, but not the ATPase reaction."},"teach":[{"text":"ATPase = ATP hydrolysis.","marks":[{"t":"ATPase activity","note":"makes ADP + Pi"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rising ATPase activity means more ADP and Pi formed."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-138a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"membrane transport type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:138#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:138"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P5","passage":"Protein F is a transporter that spans the plasma membrane. Staining shows it gathers in small patches rich in cholesterol and sphingolipids. Removing cholesterol breaks up these patches and lowers Protein F activity.","q":"What is the most likely location of Protein F within the plasma membrane?","choices":["Within a lipid raft","Bound to the inner leaflet only","Bound to the outer leaflet only","Loosely attached outside the bilayer"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Cholesterol-rich patch = lipid raft","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Spans membrane: not peripheral","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cholesterol- and sphingolipid-rich patch is a lipid raft."},"exp":"**Answer:** Within a lipid raft\n\n**Correct: Within a lipid raft** Lipid rafts are patches rich in cholesterol and sphingolipids. Protein F gathers in them.\n\n**Incorrect: Bound to the inner leaflet only** Protein F spans the membrane, so it is not on one side only.\n\n**Incorrect: Bound to the outer leaflet only** Protein F spans the membrane, so it is not on one side only.\n\n**Incorrect: Loosely attached outside the bilayer** That is a peripheral protein; Protein F is a spanning transporter.","how":{"ask":"What is a cholesterol-rich membrane patch called?","know":"Lipid raft = cholesterol + sphingolipid domain.","steps":[{"text":"Protein F gathers in cholesterol-rich patches.","hl":"small patches rich in cholesterol and sphingolipids"},{"text":"It spans the membrane, so it is not one-sided.","hl":"spans the plasma membrane","strike":["Bound to the inner leaflet only","Bound to the outer leaflet only"]},{"text":"Not peripheral either.","strike":["Loosely attached outside the bilayer"]},{"text":"Answer: Within a lipid raft."}],"trap":"Outer leaflet: sphingolipids sit mostly outside, but Protein F spans both leaflets."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the patch.","marks":[{"t":"rich in cholesterol and sphingolipids","note":"lipid raft"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A protein found in cholesterol-rich membrane domains sits in a lipid raft."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-139a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:139#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:139"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P5","passage":"Agent D is a cyclic peptide made by a soil fungus. It binds cholesterol and pulls it out of cell membranes. Researchers used Agent D to lower membrane cholesterol in tumor cells.","q":"Agent D is composed of which type of molecule?","choices":["Monosaccharides","Amino acids","Fatty acids","Nucleotides"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Peptide = amino acid chain","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Peptide means amino acids."},"exp":"**Answer:** Amino acids\n\n**Incorrect: Monosaccharides** Sugars build oligosaccharides, not peptides.\n\n**Correct: Amino acids** Agent D is a cyclic peptide. Peptides are chains of amino acids.\n\n**Incorrect: Fatty acids** Fatty acids build lipids, not peptides.\n\n**Incorrect: Nucleotides** Nucleotides build nucleic acids, not peptides.","how":{"ask":"What monomer is in a peptide?","know":"Peptide = amino acids; -saccharide = sugars.","steps":[{"text":"Agent D is a peptide.","hl":"Agent D is a cyclic peptide"},{"text":"Sugars, fats and nucleotides do not make peptides.","strike":["Monosaccharides","Fatty acids","Nucleotides"]},{"text":"Answer: Amino acids."}],"trap":"Fatty acids: it binds cholesterol, but what it binds is not what it is made of."},"teach":[{"text":"The class name gives the monomer.","marks":[{"t":"cyclic peptide","note":"amino acids"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Read the class word: peptide = amino acids, saccharide = sugars, nucleic = nucleotides."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-140a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mitosis, meiosis and nondisjunction","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:140#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:140"],"q":"Nocodazole is a drug that blocks microtubule assembly. Which of the following mitotic processes would NOT occur after exposure to this drug?","choices":["Condensation of chromosomes during prophase","Replication of DNA during S phase","Breakdown of the nuclear envelope during prophase","Movement of chromatids to opposite poles during anaphase"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Only anaphase needs spindle microtubules","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No microtubules means no spindle, so no chromosome movement."},"exp":"**Answer:** Movement of chromatids to opposite poles during anaphase\n\n**Incorrect: Condensation of chromosomes during prophase** Condensation uses condensin proteins, not microtubules.\n\n**Incorrect: Replication of DNA during S phase** DNA polymerase copies DNA without microtubules.\n\n**Incorrect: Breakdown of the nuclear envelope during prophase** Lamin phosphorylation breaks the envelope, not microtubules.\n\n**Correct: Movement of chromatids to opposite poles during anaphase** Spindle microtubules pull chromatids apart. Without them, anaphase movement fails.","how":{"ask":"Which step needs microtubules?","know":"The spindle is made of microtubules.","steps":[{"text":"Condensation and envelope breakdown use other proteins.","strike":["Condensation of chromosomes during prophase","Breakdown of the nuclear envelope during prophase"]},{"text":"DNA replication does not use microtubules.","strike":["Replication of DNA during S phase"]},{"text":"Answer: Movement of chromatids to opposite poles during anaphase."}],"trap":"Nuclear envelope breakdown: it is in prophase near spindle setup, but lamins drive it."},"teach":[{"text":"Microtubule drugs stop the spindle."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Blocking microtubules stops chromosome movement in mitosis, not DNA copying or condensation."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-141a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"osmolarity and fluid movement","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:141#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:141"],"q":"Fluid leaves the arterial end of a systemic capillary into the tissue mainly by:","choices":["passive flow due to a hydrostatic pressure difference.","passive flow resulting from countercurrent exchange.","active transport of water, followed by movement of solutes down a gradient.","active transport of Na+, followed by passive flow of water along the osmotic gradient."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Filtration is pressure-driven, not active","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Countercurrent is a loop thing","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Filtration out of a capillary is pushed by blood pressure."},"exp":"**Answer:** passive flow due to a hydrostatic pressure difference.\n\n**Correct: passive flow due to a hydrostatic pressure difference.** Blood pressure at the arterial end beats the pull of plasma proteins. Fluid is pushed out passively.\n\n**Incorrect: passive flow resulting from countercurrent exchange.** Countercurrent exchange works in the loop of Henle and vasa recta, not here.\n\n**Incorrect: active transport of water, followed by movement of solutes down a gradient.** Water is never actively pumped.\n\n**Incorrect: active transport of Na+, followed by passive flow of water along the osmotic gradient.** That is reabsorption, not filtration.","how":{"ask":"What pushes fluid out of a capillary?","know":"Filtration = bulk flow from hydrostatic pressure.","steps":[{"text":"Filtration is pressure-driven, not active.","strike":["active transport of water, followed by movement of solutes down a gradient.","active transport of Na+, followed by passive flow of water along the osmotic gradient."]},{"text":"Countercurrent exchange belongs to the kidney loop.","strike":["passive flow resulting from countercurrent exchange."]},{"text":"Answer: passive flow due to a hydrostatic pressure difference."}],"trap":"Active Na+ then water: true for reabsorption, not for filtration."},"teach":[{"text":"Same rule as the glomerulus: pressure pushes fluid out."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Capillary and glomerular filtration are passive flow driven by a pressure difference."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-142a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"half-life count","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-bb:142#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:142"],"q":"The half-life of drug X is the time it takes for its concentration to fall by one-half. Plasma drug X falls from 400 ng/mL to 100 ng/mL in 1.6 h. If it takes four half-lives for drug X to be considered eliminated from the body, how long did it take to eliminate drug X?","choices":["1.6 h","2.4 h","3.2 h","4.0 h"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"400 → 100 is two halvings","c":[0]},{"f":"Half-life 0.8 h × 4","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count halvings to get one half-life, then multiply by four."},"exp":"**Answer:** 3.2 h\n\n**Incorrect: 1.6 h** That is only two half-lives (400 → 200 → 100).\n\n**Incorrect: 2.4 h** That is three half-lives, not four.\n\n**Correct: 3.2 h** 400 → 200 → 100 is two halvings in 1.6 h, so one half-life is 0.8 h. Four half-lives = 3.2 h.\n\n**Incorrect: 4.0 h** That is five half-lives, not four.","how":{"ask":"How long are four half-lives?","know":"Each halving = one half-life.","steps":[{"text":"400 → 200 → 100 is two halvings in 1.6 h.","strike":["1.6 h"]},{"text":"One half-life = 0.8 h.","strike":["2.4 h","4.0 h"]},{"text":"Four × 0.8 h = 3.2 h. Answer: 3.2 h."}],"trap":"1.6 h: that is the given time, but it covers only two half-lives."},"teach":[{"text":"Count steps, not math: 400, 200, 100 is two steps."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Find one half-life by counting halvings, then multiply by the number needed."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-143a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"activation energy vs free energy","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:143#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:143"],"q":"Enzymes speed up chemical reactions by all of the following methods EXCEPT:","choices":["stabilizing the transition state.","orienting substrates.","changing the reaction's ΔG.","straining substrate bonds."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Orient, strain, stabilize: real methods","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Enzymes lower activation energy only; ΔG never changes."},"exp":"**Answer:** changing the reaction's ΔG.\n\n**Incorrect: stabilizing the transition state.** This lowers activation energy, a real enzyme method.\n\n**Incorrect: orienting substrates.** Holding substrates in the right position is a real enzyme method.\n\n**Correct: changing the reaction's ΔG.** Enzymes lower activation energy. They do not change ΔG or the equilibrium.\n\n**Incorrect: straining substrate bonds.** Bending the substrate toward the transition state is a real enzyme method.","how":{"ask":"Which is NOT something enzymes do?","know":"Enzymes lower Ea; ΔG stays the same.","steps":[{"text":"Stabilizing, orienting and straining all lower Ea.","strike":["stabilizing the transition state.","orienting substrates.","straining substrate bonds."]},{"text":"ΔG depends only on reactants and products."},{"text":"Answer: changing the reaction's ΔG."}],"trap":"Straining bonds: it sounds like changing the substrate, but it is a real catalytic trick."},"teach":[{"text":"Enzymes change speed, not the energy difference."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Enzymes lower activation energy but never change ΔG or Keq."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-144a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:144#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:144"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P6","passage":"Homocysteine (R = –CH2CH2SH) is an amino acid made in cells from methionine. Unlike cysteine, it is not built into proteins. Cells can turn homocysteine into cysteine or back into methionine.","q":"Which of the following statements gives the most fundamental reason why homocysteine is unlikely to be found in proteins synthesized in vivo?","choices":["It cannot form a peptide bond.","It has no codon in the standard genetic code.","It is not available in the diet.","It has a thiol group in its side chain."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Any amino acid can peptide-bond","c":[0]},{"f":"Made in cells; diet irrelevant","c":[2]},{"f":"Cysteine has thiol, still used","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ribosomes only add amino acids that have a codon."},"exp":"**Answer:** It has no codon in the standard genetic code.\n\n**Incorrect: It cannot form a peptide bond.** It has an amino and a carboxyl group, so it can form peptide bonds.\n\n**Correct: It has no codon in the standard genetic code.** Translation adds only amino acids that have a codon and tRNA. Homocysteine has none.\n\n**Incorrect: It is not available in the diet.** Cells make it from methionine, so supply is not the issue.\n\n**Incorrect: It has a thiol group in its side chain.** Cysteine has a thiol and is built into proteins.","how":{"ask":"What decides which amino acids go into proteins?","know":"Only codon-encoded amino acids are used by ribosomes.","steps":[{"text":"It is an amino acid, so it can form peptide bonds.","strike":["It cannot form a peptide bond."]},{"text":"Cells make it, so diet does not matter.","hl":"made in cells from methionine","strike":["It is not available in the diet."]},{"text":"Cysteine has a thiol and is in proteins.","strike":["It has a thiol group in its side chain."]},{"text":"Answer: It has no codon in the standard genetic code."}],"trap":"Thiol group: it looks unusual, but cysteine shares it and is coded."},"teach":[{"text":"Being an amino acid is not enough.","marks":[{"t":"not built into proteins","note":"no codon"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An amino acid with no codon cannot be added during translation."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-145a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:145#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:145"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P6","passage":"In the liver, arginine is split into ornithine and urea by the arginase reaction (Equation 1). arginine + H2O → ornithine + urea. Arginase is not used up in the reaction.","q":"Which of the following terms best describes the role of arginase in the reaction shown in Equation 1?","choices":["Substrate","Catalyst","Coenzyme","Product"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"-ase names an enzyme","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A name ending in -ase is an enzyme, which is a catalyst."},"exp":"**Answer:** Catalyst\n\n**Incorrect: Substrate** Arginine is the substrate; arginase acts on it.\n\n**Correct: Catalyst** Arginase is an enzyme. It speeds the reaction and is not used up.\n\n**Incorrect: Coenzyme** A coenzyme is a small helper molecule; arginase is the enzyme itself.\n\n**Incorrect: Product** The products are ornithine and urea.","how":{"ask":"What is arginase's role?","know":"-ase = enzyme = catalyst.","steps":[{"text":"Arginine and water go in; ornithine and urea come out.","hl":"arginine + H2O → ornithine + urea","strike":["Substrate","Product"]},{"text":"Arginase is the enzyme, not a helper molecule.","hl":"Arginase is not used up in the reaction","strike":["Coenzyme"]},{"text":"Answer: Catalyst."}],"trap":"Coenzyme: it helps the reaction, but enzymes and coenzymes are different things."},"teach":[{"text":"Read the name ending.","marks":[{"t":"arginase reaction","note":"-ase = enzyme"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An enzyme named in a reaction plays the role of catalyst."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-146a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"passage purpose","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:146#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:146"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P6","passage":"Arginase splits arginine into ornithine and urea. The enzyme is found in the liver and in many other tissues. In many cell types, arginase activity rises sharply just before the cells divide.","q":"The arginase reaction has been studied extensively by cancer researchers. The most likely reason for the interest of these researchers is that the reaction is:","choices":["a good source of arginine.","unique to the liver.","linked to the start of cell division.","one that releases a nitrogen-containing product."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Arginine is used up, not made","c":[0]},{"f":"Found in many tissues","c":[1]},{"f":"True but not cancer-relevant","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cancer is uncontrolled division; pick the link to division."},"exp":"**Answer:** linked to the start of cell division.\n\n**Incorrect: a good source of arginine.** The reaction uses up arginine.\n\n**Incorrect: unique to the liver.** The passage says many tissues have it.\n\n**Correct: linked to the start of cell division.** Activity rises just before division. Cancer is runaway division, so researchers care.\n\n**Incorrect: one that releases a nitrogen-containing product.** Urea has nitrogen, but that does not explain cancer interest.","how":{"ask":"Why would cancer researchers care?","know":"Cancer = uncontrolled cell division.","steps":[{"text":"Arginine is the reactant, not a product.","strike":["a good source of arginine."]},{"text":"It is in many tissues.","hl":"found in the liver and in many other tissues","strike":["unique to the liver."]},{"text":"Nitrogen release is true but not about cancer.","strike":["one that releases a nitrogen-containing product."]},{"text":"Activity rises before division. Answer: linked to the start of cell division.","hl":"arginase activity rises sharply just before the cells divide"}],"trap":"Nitrogen product: a true fact, but it does not answer why."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the research field to the fact.","marks":[{"t":"just before the cells divide","note":"cancer link"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For why-researchers-care questions, pick the passage fact tied to the field's main problem."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-147a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:147#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:147"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P6","passage":"The arginase assay uses arginine with a radioactive label on its guanidino carbon. Arginase moves that carbon into urea. Urease then turns the labeled urea into CO2, which is counted.","q":"The statement that the arginase assay is highly specific means that it:","choices":["can tell arginase activity apart from the many other reactions in a cell.","requires labeled arginine of high specific activity.","produces urea of high specific activity.","can detect the tiny amount of arginine present in a cell."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Specific = measures one reaction","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Specific means it picks out one reaction; sensitive means it detects small amounts."},"exp":"**Answer:** can tell arginase activity apart from the many other reactions in a cell.\n\n**Correct: can tell arginase activity apart from the many other reactions in a cell.** A specific assay measures only its target reaction. Other reactions do not give signal.\n\n**Incorrect: requires labeled arginine of high specific activity.** Specific activity of a label is a different term from assay specificity.\n\n**Incorrect: produces urea of high specific activity.** This mixes up label strength with assay specificity.\n\n**Incorrect: can detect the tiny amount of arginine present in a cell.** Detecting small amounts is sensitivity, not specificity.","how":{"ask":"What does specific mean for an assay?","know":"Specific = only its target; sensitive = detects tiny amounts.","steps":[{"text":"The label only reaches CO2 through arginase.","hl":"Arginase moves that carbon into urea"},{"text":"Specific activity of a label is a different idea.","strike":["requires labeled arginine of high specific activity.","produces urea of high specific activity."]},{"text":"Tiny amounts is sensitivity.","strike":["can detect the tiny amount of arginine present in a cell."]},{"text":"Answer: can tell arginase activity apart from the many other reactions in a cell."}],"trap":"High specific activity: same word, different meaning."},"teach":[{"text":"The signal path runs through one enzyme.","marks":[{"t":"radioactive label","note":"tracks one carbon"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A highly specific assay measures one reaction and ignores the rest."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-148a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:148#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:148"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P7","passage":"The liver enzyme CYP3A4 breaks down many drugs so they can be excreted. Compound G, found in an herbal tea, competes with these drugs for the CYP3A4 active site. A patient took a normal dose of a blood pressure drug with several cups of the tea. Drug levels rose far too high, and blood pressure dropped dangerously.","q":"In the example of the patient in the passage, the herbal tea caused the drug overdose because Compound G:","choices":["denatured the CYP3A4.","inhibited the CYP3A4.","decreased the concentration of CYP3A4.","increased the concentration of CYP3A4."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Competes for site = inhibitor","c":[0,2]},{"f":"More enzyme clears drug faster","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Competes for the active site means competitive inhibitor."},"exp":"**Answer:** inhibited the CYP3A4.\n\n**Incorrect: denatured the CYP3A4.** Compound G competes for the site; it does not unfold the enzyme.\n\n**Correct: inhibited the CYP3A4.** Compound G blocks drug binding at the active site. The drug is broken down slower and builds up.\n\n**Incorrect: decreased the concentration of CYP3A4.** The passage says Compound G competes, not that it lowers enzyme amount.\n\n**Incorrect: increased the concentration of CYP3A4.** More enzyme would clear the drug faster, not cause overdose.","how":{"ask":"How did Compound G raise drug levels?","know":"Competitive inhibitors block the active site.","steps":[{"text":"Compound G competes for the active site.","hl":"competes with these drugs for the CYP3A4 active site","strike":["denatured the CYP3A4.","decreased the concentration of CYP3A4."]},{"text":"More enzyme would lower drug levels.","strike":["increased the concentration of CYP3A4."]},{"text":"Answer: inhibited the CYP3A4."}],"trap":"Decreased concentration: the effect looks the same, but the passage says competition."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the verb that names the mechanism.","marks":[{"t":"competes","note":"competitive inhibitor"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A compound that competes for a drug-clearing enzyme inhibits it and raises drug levels."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-149a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:149#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:149"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P7","passage":"Fat-soluble toxins and fuels are often changed by liver enzymes. Some are broken into small two-carbon units. These units then feed the cell's energy pathways.","q":"Fatty acids may be broken down by beta-oxidation into two-carbon units of acetyl coenzyme A. Acetyl coenzyme A may then enter:","choices":["glycolysis.","the citric acid cycle.","the electron transport chain.","the pentose phosphate pathway."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Acetyl-CoA feeds citric acid cycle","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Acetyl-CoA joins oxaloacetate to start the citric acid cycle."},"exp":"**Answer:** the citric acid cycle.\n\n**Incorrect: glycolysis.** Glycolysis starts with glucose, not acetyl-CoA.\n\n**Correct: the citric acid cycle.** Acetyl-CoA joins oxaloacetate to make citrate. That starts the cycle.\n\n**Incorrect: the electron transport chain.** The ETC takes NADH and FADH2, not acetyl-CoA.\n\n**Incorrect: the pentose phosphate pathway.** This pathway starts from glucose-6-phosphate.","how":{"ask":"Where does acetyl-CoA go?","know":"Acetyl-CoA + oxaloacetate → citrate.","steps":[{"text":"Two-carbon units feed energy pathways.","hl":"small two-carbon units"},{"text":"Glycolysis and the pentose path start from glucose.","strike":["glycolysis.","the pentose phosphate pathway."]},{"text":"The ETC takes electron carriers, not acetyl-CoA.","strike":["the electron transport chain."]},{"text":"Answer: the citric acid cycle."}],"trap":"Electron transport: it follows the cycle, but acetyl-CoA enters first at the cycle."},"teach":[{"text":"Two-carbon unit = acetyl group.","marks":[{"t":"two-carbon units","note":"acetyl-CoA"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Acetyl-CoA from any fuel enters the citric acid cycle."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-150a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:150#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:150"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P7","passage":"CYP2E1 is an inducible liver enzyme. When a toxin level rises, liver cells make more of the enzyme. Long-term exposure to Drug Q greatly raises CYP2E1 in liver cells.","q":"The liver cells of people who take Drug Q daily for months would be expected to have greatly increased concentrations of CYP2E1 and:","choices":["copies of the CYP2E1 gene.","mRNA that codes for CYP2E1.","tRNA that carries methionine.","rRNA in each ribosome."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Gene copies stay constant","c":[0]},{"f":"tRNA, rRNA not enzyme-specific","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"More enzyme made means more of its mRNA."},"exp":"**Answer:** mRNA that codes for CYP2E1.\n\n**Incorrect: copies of the CYP2E1 gene.** Induction turns the gene up; it does not copy the gene.\n\n**Correct: mRNA that codes for CYP2E1.** Induction raises transcription of the gene. More mRNA makes more enzyme.\n\n**Incorrect: tRNA that carries methionine.** tRNA levels are not tied to one enzyme.\n\n**Incorrect: rRNA in each ribosome.** Each ribosome has a fixed rRNA set; it does not rise for one enzyme.","how":{"ask":"What else rises when an enzyme is induced?","know":"Induction = more transcription = more mRNA.","steps":[{"text":"Cells make more CYP2E1 when toxins rise.","hl":"liver cells make more of the enzyme"},{"text":"The gene count is fixed.","strike":["copies of the CYP2E1 gene."]},{"text":"tRNA and rRNA serve all proteins.","strike":["tRNA that carries methionine.","rRNA in each ribosome."]},{"text":"Answer: mRNA that codes for CYP2E1."}],"trap":"Gene copies: more product sounds like more genes, but cells raise mRNA instead."},"teach":[{"text":"Inducible = turned up on demand.","marks":[{"t":"inducible","note":"more transcription"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An induced protein comes with more of its own mRNA, not more gene copies."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-151a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:151#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:151"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P7","passage":"CYP2E1 is an inducible liver enzyme. Long exposure to a toxin makes liver cells produce more of it. CYP2E1 breaks down both Compound H and the sedative Drug S. A heavy Compound H user took a normal dose of Drug S and felt no sleepiness.","q":"No sleepiness was felt by the user because the previous heavy use of Compound H had:","choices":["denatured the CYP2E1.","induced the CYP2E1.","inhibited the CYP2E1.","reduced the CYP2E1."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Less enzyme means stronger effect","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Drug had too little effect, so it was cleared fast: more enzyme."},"exp":"**Answer:** induced the CYP2E1.\n\n**Incorrect: denatured the CYP2E1.** Less working enzyme would make Drug S stronger, not weaker.\n\n**Correct: induced the CYP2E1.** Heavy use raised CYP2E1 levels. Drug S was broken down fast, so it had little effect.\n\n**Incorrect: inhibited the CYP2E1.** Inhibition would slow clearance and cause more sleepiness.\n\n**Incorrect: reduced the CYP2E1.** Less enzyme would make Drug S stronger.","how":{"ask":"Why did the sedative fail?","know":"More enzyme = faster clearance = weaker drug effect.","steps":[{"text":"Long toxin use raises the enzyme.","hl":"Long exposure to a toxin makes liver cells produce more of it"},{"text":"Any loss of enzyme would make the drug stronger.","strike":["denatured the CYP2E1.","inhibited the CYP2E1.","reduced the CYP2E1."]},{"text":"Answer: induced the CYP2E1."}],"trap":"Inhibited: true when both are present at once, but past heavy use induces."},"teach":[{"text":"Weak drug effect means fast clearance.","marks":[{"t":"felt no sleepiness","note":"drug cleared fast"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Chronic toxin use induces the clearing enzyme, so other drugs it clears work less."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-152a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mitosis, meiosis and nondisjunction","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:152#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:152"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P8","passage":"Researchers grew lung epithelial cells together with fibroblasts. The fibroblasts were treated so they could not divide but stayed metabolically active. All cells in each dish were counted by machine for 10 days.","q":"The fibroblasts used in these experiments were probably in which phase of the cell cycle?","choices":["Prophase","Metaphase","Interphase","Telophase"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Not dividing = no mitosis","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cells that cannot divide sit in interphase."},"exp":"**Answer:** Interphase\n\n**Incorrect: Prophase** Prophase is a mitosis stage, and these cells were not dividing.\n\n**Incorrect: Metaphase** Metaphase is a mitosis stage, and these cells were not dividing.\n\n**Correct: Interphase** The fibroblasts were blocked from dividing but stayed active. Non-dividing, working cells are in interphase.\n\n**Incorrect: Telophase** Telophase is a mitosis stage, and these cells were not dividing.","how":{"ask":"Where do non-dividing cells sit?","know":"Prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase are all mitosis.","steps":[{"text":"Fibroblasts could not divide.","hl":"treated so they could not divide but stayed metabolically active"},{"text":"All mitosis stages are out.","strike":["Prophase","Metaphase","Telophase"]},{"text":"Answer: Interphase."}],"trap":"Metaphase: drugs can trap cells there, but these cells were active and not in mitosis."},"teach":[{"text":"Growth-arrested means stopped before mitosis.","marks":[{"t":"could not divide","note":"interphase"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Growth-arrested but living cells are in interphase."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-153a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:153#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:153"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P8","passage":"Lung capillaries are lined by thin epithelial and endothelial cells. Red blood cells squeeze through these capillaries one at a time. Researchers tested which nearby cells change the growth of lung epithelial cells.","q":"A student hypothesized that the growth of lung epithelial cells might be affected by genes expressed in mature red blood cells. Is this student's hypothesis reasonable?","choices":["No; mature red blood cells have no nucleus or DNA.","No; red blood cell genes are needed to carry CO2.","Yes; red blood cell genes control division in most cells.","Yes; red blood cells deliver gene products to every tissue."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Mature RBCs lack a nucleus","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mature mammal red blood cells have no nucleus, so no genes to express."},"exp":"**Answer:** No; mature red blood cells have no nucleus or DNA.\n\n**Correct: No; mature red blood cells have no nucleus or DNA.** Mammal red blood cells lose their nucleus as they mature. With no DNA, they express no genes.\n\n**Incorrect: No; red blood cell genes are needed to carry CO2.** Proteins like hemoglobin carry gases; mature cells have no genes.\n\n**Incorrect: Yes; red blood cell genes control division in most cells.** Red blood cell genes do not control other cells, and mature ones have none.\n\n**Incorrect: Yes; red blood cells deliver gene products to every tissue.** Red blood cells carry gases, not gene products.","how":{"ask":"Do mature red blood cells have DNA?","know":"Mature mammal RBCs are enucleated.","steps":[{"text":"Red blood cells pass through the capillaries.","hl":"Red blood cells squeeze through these capillaries"},{"text":"Mature RBCs have no nucleus, so both yes choices fail.","strike":["Yes; red blood cell genes control division in most cells.","Yes; red blood cells deliver gene products to every tissue."]},{"text":"Gas carrying is done by proteins, not genes.","strike":["No; red blood cell genes are needed to carry CO2."]},{"text":"Answer: No; mature red blood cells have no nucleus or DNA."}],"trap":"No; needed to carry CO2: right verdict, wrong reason."},"teach":[{"text":"Check the cell before the claim.","marks":[{"t":"Red blood cells","note":"no nucleus"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Mature mammal red blood cells have no nucleus, so any claim about their DNA or genes fails."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-154a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:154#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:154"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P8","passage":"Researchers mixed lung epithelial cells directly with fibroblasts in the same dish. The fibroblasts were treated so they could not divide. All cells in each dish were counted together by machine for 10 days. The goal was to see how fibroblasts change epithelial cell growth.","q":"Fibroblasts were made unable to divide in the experiment so that the:","choices":["fibroblasts would not slow epithelial growth.","fibroblast division would not add to the epithelial cell count.","fibroblast wastes would not affect the cell count.","epithelial cells would not make the fibroblasts grow."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Slowing growth is the tested outcome","c":[0]},{"f":"Goal: clean count, not biology","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A shared machine count cannot tell cell types apart, so stop the helper cells from dividing."},"exp":"**Answer:** fibroblast division would not add to the epithelial cell count.\n\n**Incorrect: fibroblasts would not slow epithelial growth.** Whether fibroblasts change growth is what the study tests.\n\n**Correct: fibroblast division would not add to the epithelial cell count.** The machine counts all cells together. If fibroblasts divided, their new cells would look like epithelial growth.\n\n**Incorrect: fibroblast wastes would not affect the cell count.** The cells stay metabolically active, so wastes are still made.\n\n**Incorrect: epithelial cells would not make the fibroblasts grow.** The aim is a clean epithelial count, not fibroblast biology.","how":{"ask":"Why stop the fibroblasts from dividing?","know":"Mixed cells counted together need one group frozen.","steps":[{"text":"All cells are counted together.","hl":"All cells in each dish were counted together by machine"},{"text":"The effect on growth is what is tested.","strike":["fibroblasts would not slow epithelial growth."]},{"text":"Wastes and fibroblast growth are not the point.","strike":["fibroblast wastes would not affect the cell count.","epithelial cells would not make the fibroblasts grow."]},{"text":"Answer: fibroblast division would not add to the epithelial cell count."}],"trap":"Would not slow epithelial growth: that is the question being tested, not a control."},"teach":[{"text":"Look at how cells were counted.","marks":[{"t":"counted together by machine","note":"no cell-type sort"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** When mixed cells are counted together, stop the non-target cells from dividing."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-155a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"structure fits exchange function","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe1-bb:155#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:155"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P8","passage":"Brain capillaries are lined by endothelial cells that touch the blood directly. Pericytes wrap around the outside of these endothelial cells. Astrocyte end-feet cover the pericytes and capillary wall. Neurons sit further out in the brain tissue.","q":"Based on the passage, which of the following cells are most important in the exchange of glucose between the blood and the brain tissue?","choices":["Pericytes","Astrocytes","Endothelial cells","Neurons"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Only endothelium touches the blood","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The cell lining the lumen is the exchange cell."},"exp":"**Answer:** Endothelial cells\n\n**Incorrect: Pericytes** Pericytes sit outside the endothelium and do not touch blood.\n\n**Incorrect: Astrocytes** Astrocytes sit outside the pericytes, away from the blood.\n\n**Correct: Endothelial cells** Endothelial cells line the capillary and touch the blood. All exchange must pass through them first.\n\n**Incorrect: Neurons** Neurons use the glucose but sit far from the blood.","how":{"ask":"Which cell is in direct contact with blood?","know":"Capillary exchange crosses the endothelium.","steps":[{"text":"Endothelial cells touch the blood.","hl":"endothelial cells that touch the blood directly"},{"text":"Pericytes and astrocytes sit outside.","strike":["Pericytes","Astrocytes"]},{"text":"Neurons are further out still.","strike":["Neurons"]},{"text":"Answer: Endothelial cells."}],"trap":"Astrocytes: they help the barrier, but they do not touch blood."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the innermost layer.","marks":[{"t":"touch the blood directly","note":"exchange cell"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In a capillary, the endothelial cells are the main exchange surface."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-156a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"virus and bacteria traits","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:156#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:156"],"q":"Culturing bacteria often involves spreading cells on a noncellular, agar-based medium. Such a method would NOT grow bacteriophages placed alone on the agar because bacteriophages:","choices":["lack RNA.","fix nitrogen.","are obligate parasites.","need added amino acids to grow."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Viruses need host cells","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Viruses copy only inside living host cells."},"exp":"**Answer:** are obligate parasites.\n\n**Incorrect: lack RNA.** Genome type does not explain it; no virus grows without a host.\n\n**Incorrect: fix nitrogen.** Phages do not fix nitrogen, and that would not stop growth.\n\n**Correct: are obligate parasites.** Phages copy only inside bacteria. Agar alone has no host cells.\n\n**Incorrect: need added amino acids to grow.** Viruses have no metabolism; adding nutrients does not help.","how":{"ask":"Why can a virus not grow on plain agar?","know":"Viruses need a host cell's machinery.","steps":[{"text":"Plain agar has no living cells.","strike":["fix nitrogen."]},{"text":"Genome and nutrients do not matter; no host means no copying.","strike":["lack RNA.","need added amino acids to grow."]},{"text":"Answer: are obligate parasites."}],"trap":"Need added amino acids: true of some bacteria, but viruses have no metabolism."},"teach":[{"text":"Phages need a bacterial lawn, not plain agar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Viruses are obligate intracellular parasites; they grow only in living host cells."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-157a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:157#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:157"],"q":"In general, telomerase is NOT needed to copy mitochondrial DNA because most mitochondrial DNA molecules:","choices":["are single-stranded.","do not replicate.","are circular.","have no genes."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"No ends, no end problem","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Telomeres fix ends; circles have no ends."},"exp":"**Answer:** are circular.\n\n**Incorrect: are single-stranded.** Mitochondrial DNA is double-stranded.\n\n**Incorrect: do not replicate.** Mitochondrial DNA does replicate as mitochondria divide.\n\n**Correct: are circular.** Telomerase solves the end-replication problem. A circle has no ends to shorten.\n\n**Incorrect: have no genes.** Mitochondrial DNA codes rRNAs, tRNAs and some proteins.","how":{"ask":"Why are telomeres not needed?","know":"Telomeres protect linear chromosome ends.","steps":[{"text":"Mitochondrial DNA is double-stranded and replicates.","strike":["are single-stranded.","do not replicate."]},{"text":"It has genes too.","strike":["have no genes."]},{"text":"Answer: are circular."}],"trap":"do not replicate: mitochondria divide, so their DNA must copy."},"teach":[{"text":"Same logic as bacteria: circular DNA has no ends."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Circular DNA has no ends, so it needs no telomeres."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-158a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:158#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:158"],"q":"Which of the following best describes the bond that would form when a guanine nucleotide is added to the 3′ end of a DNA strand that ends in cytosine?","choices":["A bond between the phosphate of the cytosine and the sugar of the guanine","A bond between the phosphate of the cytosine and the phosphate of the guanine","A bond between the phosphate of the guanine and the sugar of the cytosine","A bond between a nitrogen in the cytosine base and an oxygen in the guanine base"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"New nucleotide brings 5′ phosphate","c":[0]},{"f":"Phosphodiester: phosphate to sugar","c":[1]},{"f":"Base bonds pair strands, not backbone","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The incoming nucleotide's 5′ phosphate bonds to the 3′ OH sugar of the strand end."},"exp":"**Answer:** A bond between the phosphate of the guanine and the sugar of the cytosine\n\n**Incorrect: A bond between the phosphate of the cytosine and the sugar of the guanine** This is backwards; the new nucleotide brings the phosphate.\n\n**Incorrect: A bond between the phosphate of the cytosine and the phosphate of the guanine** Backbone bonds join phosphate to sugar, not phosphate to phosphate.\n\n**Correct: A bond between the phosphate of the guanine and the sugar of the cytosine** The 5′ phosphate of the new G joins the 3′ OH on the sugar of C. That is a phosphodiester bond.\n\n**Incorrect: A bond between a nitrogen in the cytosine base and an oxygen in the guanine base** Base-to-base bonds are hydrogen bonds between strands, not along one strand.","how":{"ask":"Which groups join when DNA grows 5′ to 3′?","know":"New 5′ phosphate + old 3′ OH sugar.","steps":[{"text":"Bases bond across strands, not along one strand.","strike":["A bond between a nitrogen in the cytosine base and an oxygen in the guanine base"]},{"text":"A phosphodiester bond joins a phosphate to a sugar.","strike":["A bond between the phosphate of the cytosine and the phosphate of the guanine"]},{"text":"G is new, so G brings the phosphate.","strike":["A bond between the phosphate of the cytosine and the sugar of the guanine"]},{"text":"Answer: A bond between the phosphate of the guanine and the sugar of the cytosine."}],"trap":"Phosphate of C to sugar of G: right bond type, wrong direction."},"teach":[{"text":"DNA grows at the 3′ end."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The 5′ nucleotide gives its sugar; the 3′ (newer) nucleotide gives its phosphate."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-159a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:159#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:159"],"q":"During a cardiac pacemaker action potential, the movement of Ca2+ ions into a pacemaker cell causes the cell membrane to do which of the following?","choices":["Hyperpolarize","Depolarize","Repolarize","Inactivate"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Positive ions in → depolarize","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Positive charge flowing in makes the inside less negative."},"exp":"**Answer:** Depolarize\n\n**Incorrect: Hyperpolarize** Hyperpolarizing makes the inside more negative, the opposite of cation entry.\n\n**Correct: Depolarize** Ca2+ brings positive charge in. The inside gets less negative, so the membrane depolarizes.\n\n**Incorrect: Repolarize** Repolarizing comes from K+ leaving the cell.\n\n**Incorrect: Inactivate** Ca2+ entry drives the action potential; it does not shut the cell off.","how":{"ask":"What does positive charge entering do?","know":"Cations in = depolarize; K+ out = repolarize.","steps":[{"text":"Ca2+ is a cation moving in.","strike":["Hyperpolarize","Inactivate"]},{"text":"Repolarizing needs K+ out.","strike":["Repolarize"]},{"text":"Answer: Depolarize."}],"trap":"Repolarize: Ca2+ channels open late in some cells, but entry still depolarizes."},"teach":[{"text":"Track charge direction, not ion name."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Any positive ion moving into a cell depolarizes the membrane."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-160a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:160#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:160"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P9","passage":"A man had both adrenal glands removed because of tumors. Without treatment, his body made no cortisol and no aldosterone. Aldosterone normally makes the kidney keep Na+ and excrete K+ and H+.","q":"The aldosterone loss after removal of both adrenal glands will cause a decrease in the serum levels of all of the following ions EXCEPT:","choices":["Na+ ions.","K+ ions.","Cl– ions.","HCO3– ions."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"No aldosterone: Na+ lost","c":[0]},{"f":"Cl– follows Na+","c":[2]},{"f":"H+ kept, so HCO3– falls","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No aldosterone flips its job: Na+ lost, K+ kept."},"exp":"**Answer:** K+ ions.\n\n**Incorrect: Na+ ions.** Without aldosterone, Na+ is lost in urine, so serum Na+ falls.\n\n**Correct: K+ ions.** Aldosterone normally excretes K+. Without it, K+ builds up, so serum K+ rises.\n\n**Incorrect: Cl– ions.** Cl– follows Na+ out, so it falls too.\n\n**Incorrect: HCO3– ions.** H+ is kept, which uses up HCO3–, so it falls.","how":{"ask":"Which ion rises when aldosterone is gone?","know":"Aldosterone: keep Na+, dump K+ and H+.","steps":[{"text":"Aldosterone keeps Na+ and dumps K+ and H+.","hl":"keep Na+ and excrete K+ and H+"},{"text":"Na+ is lost, and Cl– follows it.","strike":["Na+ ions.","Cl– ions."]},{"text":"Kept H+ uses up HCO3–.","strike":["HCO3– ions."]},{"text":"K+ is kept, so it rises. Answer: K+ ions."}],"trap":"HCO3–: it seems unrelated, but acid buildup lowers it."},"teach":[{"text":"Reverse the hormone's job.","marks":[{"t":"excrete K+","note":"lost: K+ rises"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Aldosterone loss lowers Na+, Cl– and HCO3– but raises K+."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-161a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"negative feedback loop","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:161#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:161"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P9","passage":"In Hashimoto's disease, immune cells destroy the thyroid gland. The gland can no longer make thyroid hormone (T4). T4 normally raises the metabolic rate of most tissues.","q":"Normally, a hypothalamic factor (TRH) stimulates the release of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) from the pituitary gland. In a patient with Hashimoto's disease, the secretion of TRH will:","choices":["be lower than normal.","be unchanged.","be higher than normal.","rise before disease onset and fall thereafter."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Low T4 removes negative feedback","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"End hormone gone, so the brake is off and upstream hormones rise."},"exp":"**Answer:** be higher than normal.\n\n**Incorrect: be lower than normal.** Low T4 removes the brake on TRH, so it rises.\n\n**Incorrect: be unchanged.** Feedback changes when T4 is lost.\n\n**Correct: be higher than normal.** T4 normally inhibits TRH. With no T4, TRH secretion goes up.\n\n**Incorrect: rise before disease onset and fall thereafter.** TRH rises after T4 falls, and stays high.","how":{"ask":"What happens upstream when the end hormone is missing?","know":"End hormones inhibit the hypothalamus and pituitary.","steps":[{"text":"The thyroid cannot make T4.","hl":"The gland can no longer make thyroid hormone (T4)"},{"text":"No T4 means no negative feedback.","strike":["be lower than normal.","be unchanged."]},{"text":"TRH stays high after onset.","strike":["rise before disease onset and fall thereafter."]},{"text":"Answer: be higher than normal."}],"trap":"Lower than normal: the gland is sick, but the brain is not."},"teach":[{"text":"Missing end hormone = brake off.","marks":[{"t":"can no longer make thyroid hormone","note":"no feedback"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** When the end gland fails, its upstream releasing hormones rise."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-162a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:162#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:162"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P9","passage":"Cortisol raises blood glucose by driving gluconeogenesis in the liver. It supplies amino acids for this by breaking down muscle protein. Patients without adrenal glands must take cortisol-like drugs for life.","q":"If a patient without adrenal glands is given too high a replacement dose of glucocorticoids, the effect over time will be a decrease in:","choices":["blood glucose.","muscle mass.","body fat.","blood pressure."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Cortisol raises glucose","c":[0]},{"f":"Excess cortisol adds fat, pressure","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cortisol breaks down muscle protein, so too much shrinks muscle."},"exp":"**Answer:** muscle mass.\n\n**Incorrect: blood glucose.** Cortisol raises blood glucose, it does not lower it.\n\n**Correct: muscle mass.** Cortisol breaks down muscle protein for gluconeogenesis. Too much over time shrinks muscle.\n\n**Incorrect: body fat.** Excess cortisol adds fat, mainly in the trunk.\n\n**Incorrect: blood pressure.** Excess cortisol tends to raise blood pressure.","how":{"ask":"What does extra cortisol use up?","know":"Cortisol breaks down muscle to feed gluconeogenesis.","steps":[{"text":"Cortisol raises glucose.","hl":"Cortisol raises blood glucose","strike":["blood glucose."]},{"text":"Excess cortisol raises fat and blood pressure.","strike":["body fat.","blood pressure."]},{"text":"It breaks down muscle protein.","hl":"breaking down muscle protein"},{"text":"Answer: muscle mass."}],"trap":"Body fat: cortisol burns stores in fasting, but excess cortisol adds fat."},"teach":[{"text":"Follow cortisol's fuel source.","marks":[{"t":"breaking down muscle protein","note":"muscle loss"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Too much glucocorticoid wastes muscle and raises blood glucose."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-163a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:163#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:163"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P9","passage":"Cortisol turns on liver genes that drive gluconeogenesis. Gluconeogenesis makes new glucose from amino acids and other non-sugar sources. The liver uses it to keep blood glucose up when sugar is scarce.","q":"Gluconeogenesis in the liver will most likely be slowest when:","choices":["cortisol release is high.","blood glucose levels are high.","glycogen stores are low.","the person has fasted for a day."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Cortisol speeds gluconeogenesis up","c":[0]},{"f":"Low fuel speeds gluconeogenesis","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gluconeogenesis runs when sugar is short; plenty of sugar turns it down."},"exp":"**Answer:** blood glucose levels are high.\n\n**Incorrect: cortisol release is high.** Cortisol turns gluconeogenesis on, so it would be fast.\n\n**Correct: blood glucose levels are high.** The liver makes new glucose only when sugar is scarce. High blood glucose slows it.\n\n**Incorrect: glycogen stores are low.** Low glycogen means sugar is short, so gluconeogenesis speeds up.\n\n**Incorrect: the person has fasted for a day.** Fasting drives gluconeogenesis up.","how":{"ask":"When is new glucose least needed?","know":"Gluconeogenesis is a fasting pathway.","steps":[{"text":"Cortisol drives it.","hl":"Cortisol turns on liver genes that drive gluconeogenesis","strike":["cortisol release is high."]},{"text":"Low stores and fasting speed it up.","hl":"when sugar is scarce","strike":["glycogen stores are low.","the person has fasted for a day."]},{"text":"Answer: blood glucose levels are high."}],"trap":"Glycogen stores are low: that makes it fastest, not slowest."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the pathway to the fuel state.","marks":[{"t":"when sugar is scarce","note":"fasting pathway"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Gluconeogenesis is fastest when sugar stores are low and slowest when blood glucose is high."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-164a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:164#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:164"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P10","passage":"Insulin moves glucose transporters to the cell membrane in muscle and fat. These transporters work by facilitated diffusion. Glucose moves through them down its concentration gradient.","q":"Glucose transporters in pancreatic β cells do not require insulin to take up glucose. However, a rise in glucokinase activity speeds the first step of glycolysis in these cells. Therefore, in β cells, higher glucokinase activity most likely:","choices":["hinders glucose uptake by raising the cell's glucose concentration.","hinders glucose uptake by using the ATP the transporters need.","aids glucose uptake by supplying the ATP the transporters need.","aids glucose uptake by lowering the cell's glucose concentration."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Faster first step aids uptake","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Facilitated diffusion needs no ATP","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Using up free glucose inside keeps the gradient steep."},"exp":"**Answer:** aids glucose uptake by lowering the cell's glucose concentration.\n\n**Incorrect: hinders glucose uptake by raising the cell's glucose concentration.** The first step uses glucose, so inside glucose falls, not rises.\n\n**Incorrect: hinders glucose uptake by using the ATP the transporters need.** These transporters need no ATP.\n\n**Incorrect: aids glucose uptake by supplying the ATP the transporters need.** Facilitated diffusion uses no ATP.\n\n**Correct: aids glucose uptake by lowering the cell's glucose concentration.** Glucokinase turns glucose into glucose-6-phosphate. Free glucose inside drops, so more flows in down its gradient.","how":{"ask":"How does using glucose inside change uptake?","know":"Facilitated diffusion depends only on the gradient.","steps":[{"text":"The transporters use facilitated diffusion, down the gradient.","hl":"Glucose moves through them down its concentration gradient"},{"text":"No ATP is involved in this transport.","strike":["hinders glucose uptake by using the ATP the transporters need.","aids glucose uptake by supplying the ATP the transporters need."]},{"text":"The first step uses glucose, so inside levels drop.","strike":["hinders glucose uptake by raising the cell's glucose concentration."]},{"text":"Answer: aids glucose uptake by lowering the cell's glucose concentration."}],"trap":"Supplying ATP: glycolysis makes ATP, but these transporters do not use it."},"teach":[{"text":"Downhill transport needs a gradient, not energy.","marks":[{"t":"facilitated diffusion","note":"no ATP"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Trapping glucose as glucose-6-phosphate keeps inside glucose low and speeds uptake."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-165a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:165#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:165"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P10","passage":"In Type 1 diabetes, the body makes no insulin. In Type 2 diabetes, the body makes insulin but responds poorly to it. In both types, blood glucose rises.","q":"A drug blocks glucose reabsorption in the kidney, so glucose leaves in the urine with no role for insulin. Given this, the drug would most likely reduce blood glucose levels in patients with which type(s) of diabetes?","choices":["Type 2 only","Both Type 1 and Type 2","Type 1 only","Neither Type 1 nor Type 2"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Insulin-free path works in both","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"If insulin plays no role, the type of insulin problem does not matter."},"exp":"**Answer:** Both Type 1 and Type 2\n\n**Incorrect: Type 2 only** The drug does not need insulin, so it also works in Type 1.\n\n**Correct: Both Type 1 and Type 2** Both types have high blood glucose. The drug removes glucose without insulin, so it works in both.\n\n**Incorrect: Type 1 only** The drug does not need insulin action, so it also works in Type 2.\n\n**Incorrect: Neither Type 1 nor Type 2** The drug removes glucose in urine, so it lowers blood glucose.","how":{"ask":"Does the drug depend on insulin?","know":"Both types fail at insulin; a route around insulin helps both.","steps":[{"text":"Both types have high glucose from insulin problems.","hl":"In both types, blood glucose rises"},{"text":"The drug skips insulin, so it is not limited to one type.","strike":["Type 2 only","Type 1 only"]},{"text":"It does lower glucose.","strike":["Neither Type 1 nor Type 2"]},{"text":"Answer: Both Type 1 and Type 2."}],"trap":"Type 2 only: Type 2 is the common drug target, but this drug needs no insulin."},"teach":[{"text":"Find what each type lacks.","marks":[{"t":"makes no insulin","note":"Type 1"},{"t":"responds poorly to it","note":"Type 2"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A glucose-lowering effect that needs no insulin works in both Type 1 and Type 2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-166a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"protein targeting and secretion","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:166#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:166"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P10","passage":"Glucagon is a peptide hormone made in pancreatic α cells. It raises blood glucose between meals. α cells release glucagon into the blood by exocytosis.","q":"During the production of glucagon, the translated proglucagon is cleaved into the mature form and secreted from the cell. The cleavage most likely takes place in which of the following locations?","choices":["Nucleus","Secretory vesicles","Cytosol","Ribosomes"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Secreted proteins stay in endomembrane","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Ribosomes make, don't cut","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Secreted proteins are processed inside the endomembrane path."},"exp":"**Answer:** Secretory vesicles\n\n**Incorrect: Nucleus** Proteins for secretion never enter the nucleus.\n\n**Correct: Secretory vesicles** Secreted proteins travel ER → Golgi → vesicles. Prohormones are cut into mature form along this path.\n\n**Incorrect: Cytosol** Secreted proteins are inside the ER lumen, not the cytosol.\n\n**Incorrect: Ribosomes** Ribosomes make the chain; they do not cut it.","how":{"ask":"Where are secreted proteins processed?","know":"Secretion path: rough ER → Golgi → vesicles → exocytosis.","steps":[{"text":"Glucagon leaves by exocytosis.","hl":"release glucagon into the blood by exocytosis"},{"text":"It never sits in the nucleus or cytosol.","strike":["Nucleus","Cytosol"]},{"text":"Ribosomes only translate.","strike":["Ribosomes"]},{"text":"Answer: Secretory vesicles."}],"trap":"Ribosomes: the chain is made there, but cutting comes later."},"teach":[{"text":"Secreted = endomembrane path.","marks":[{"t":"by exocytosis","note":"vesicle route"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Prohormones for secretion are cleaved in the Golgi and secretory vesicles."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-167a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:167#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:167"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P10","passage":"In diabetes, many cell types cannot take up glucose well. Insulin normally moves glucose transporters to the membrane in these cells. Some tissues, though, keep their glucose transporters in the membrane at all times.","q":"Despite the effects of diabetes, the red blood cells of diabetic patients still take up glucose normally. This is most likely because red blood cells use:","choices":["fatty acids for energy instead of glucose.","insulin-dependent transporters for glucose uptake.","insulin-independent transporters for glucose uptake.","less glucose than do other body tissues."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"RBCs lack mitochondria: glucose only","c":[0]},{"f":"Insulin-dependent fails in diabetes","c":[1]},{"f":"Low use doesn't explain uptake","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tissue still gets glucose in diabetes, so its transporters need no insulin."},"exp":"**Answer:** insulin-independent transporters for glucose uptake.\n\n**Incorrect: fatty acids for energy instead of glucose.** Red blood cells have no mitochondria, so they cannot burn fatty acids.\n\n**Incorrect: insulin-dependent transporters for glucose uptake.** These fail in diabetes, so uptake would drop.\n\n**Correct: insulin-independent transporters for glucose uptake.** Red blood cells use GLUT1, which is always in the membrane. Uptake works without insulin.\n\n**Incorrect: less glucose than do other body tissues.** Using less does not explain normal uptake.","how":{"ask":"How do RBCs dodge the diabetes problem?","know":"Some tissues keep insulin-free transporters.","steps":[{"text":"Some tissues keep transporters in place always.","hl":"keep their glucose transporters in the membrane at all times"},{"text":"RBCs cannot use fat; insulin-dependent uptake fails.","strike":["fatty acids for energy instead of glucose.","insulin-dependent transporters for glucose uptake."]},{"text":"Low use is not a reason for normal uptake.","strike":["less glucose than do other body tissues."]},{"text":"Answer: insulin-independent transporters for glucose uptake."}],"trap":"Fatty acids: other tissues switch to fat, but RBCs have no mitochondria."},"teach":[{"text":"Find tissues that do not wait for insulin.","marks":[{"t":"at all times","note":"insulin-independent"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Tissues that still get glucose in diabetes use insulin-independent transporters."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-168a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:168#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:168"],"pid":"pe1-bb:P10","passage":"In diabetes, cells cannot take up glucose well, so blood glucose rises. Extra glucose spills into urine and pulls water with it. Tissues burn fat and protein instead, which can acidify the blood.","q":"Based on the passage, which of the following is LEAST likely to be a symptom of untreated Type 1 diabetes?","choices":["Frequent urination","Constant thirst","Weight gain","Fruity breath"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Glucose in urine: pee, thirst","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Fat burning makes ketones","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Body burns fat and protein, so weight drops, not rises."},"exp":"**Answer:** Weight gain\n\n**Incorrect: Frequent urination** Glucose in urine pulls water, so urination rises.\n\n**Incorrect: Constant thirst** Water loss in urine causes thirst.\n\n**Correct: Weight gain** Cells cannot use glucose, so the body burns fat and protein. That causes weight loss, not gain.\n\n**Incorrect: Fruity breath** Fat burning makes ketones like acetone, which smell fruity.","how":{"ask":"Which symptom does the passage NOT predict?","know":"Untreated diabetes: urine sugar, water loss, fat burning.","steps":[{"text":"Glucose in urine pulls water out.","hl":"Extra glucose spills into urine and pulls water with it","strike":["Frequent urination","Constant thirst"]},{"text":"Burning fat makes ketones.","hl":"Tissues burn fat and protein instead","strike":["Fruity breath"]},{"text":"Answer: Weight gain."}],"trap":"Fruity breath: not named in the passage, but it follows from fat burning."},"teach":[{"text":"Trace each symptom to a passage cause.","marks":[{"t":"burn fat and protein","note":"weight loss"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For LEAST likely, pick the choice that goes against a passage mechanism."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-169a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"dna replication and repair","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:169#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:169"],"q":"A certain bacterium was cultured for several generations in medium containing 14N, transferred to medium containing 15N, and allowed to complete two rounds of cell division. Given that the bacterium's genome mass is 4.8 fg when grown in 14N medium and 5.0 fg when grown in 15N medium, individual bacteria with which of the following genome masses would most likely be isolated from this culture?","choices":["4.8 fg and 4.9 fg","4.9 fg and 5.0 fg","4.8 fg and 5.0 fg","4.9 fg only"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"No all-14N genome after shift","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Round 2 makes all-15N genomes","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Round 1: all hybrid. Round 2: half hybrid, half new-only."},"exp":"**Answer:** 4.9 fg and 5.0 fg\n\n**Incorrect: 4.8 fg and 4.9 fg** Every new strand is 15N, so no all-14N genome remains.\n\n**Correct: 4.9 fg and 5.0 fg** After round 1, all genomes are hybrid (4.9 fg). In round 2, hybrids make one hybrid and one all-15N genome (5.0 fg).\n\n**Incorrect: 4.8 fg and 5.0 fg** All-14N genomes are gone after the first round.\n\n**Incorrect: 4.9 fg only** That is after one round; round 2 adds all-15N genomes.","how":{"ask":"What genomes exist after two rounds?","know":"Each daughter keeps one old strand and gets one new strand.","steps":[{"text":"Round 1: every genome is 14N/15N = 4.9 fg.","strike":["4.8 fg and 4.9 fg","4.8 fg and 5.0 fg"]},{"text":"Round 2: half stay 4.9 fg, half become 15N/15N = 5.0 fg.","strike":["4.9 fg only"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.9 fg and 5.0 fg."}],"trap":"4.8 fg and 4.9 fg: that is the answer if the shift went 15N to 14N."},"teach":[{"text":"Hybrid mass is halfway: (4.8 + 5.0) / 2 = 4.9."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** After two rounds in new medium, half the genomes are hybrid and half are all-new."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-170a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mendelian cross probability","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:170#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:170"],"q":"Assume that A and B are two unlinked genes that affect fur color. The dominant A allele is necessary for dark fur, and the dominant B allele causes white fur regardless of the other genes present. Given this, what fraction of the offspring of parents with the genotypes AaBb and aaBb will most likely have white fur?","choices":["1/8","3/8","5/8","7/8"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Dark = 1/2 × 1/4","c":[1,2]},{"f":"White = 1 − 1/8","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the one rare phenotype (dark), then subtract from 1."},"exp":"**Answer:** 7/8\n\n**Incorrect: 1/8** That is the dark fraction, not white.\n\n**Incorrect: 3/8** No gene split gives this ratio here.\n\n**Incorrect: 5/8** This would fit if both parents were Aa; one parent is aa.\n\n**Correct: 7/8** Dark needs A_ and bb. P(A_) = 1/2 and P(bb) = 1/4, so dark = 1/8. White = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8.","how":{"ask":"What fraction is white?","know":"Dark needs A_ AND bb; everything else is white.","steps":[{"text":"Aa × aa gives A_ = 1/2.","strike":["5/8"]},{"text":"Bb × Bb gives bb = 1/4. Dark = 1/2 × 1/4 = 1/8.","strike":["3/8"]},{"text":"White is everything else: 1 − 1/8.","strike":["1/8"]},{"text":"Answer: 7/8."}],"trap":"1/8: correct math, but it answers dark, not white."},"teach":[{"text":"The single good outcome is easier to count."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For one-needs-both traits, multiply the two needed chances, then subtract from 1 for the rest."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe1-171a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe1-bb:171#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-bb:171"],"q":"An mRNA molecule has 1200 bases. What is the maximum number of amino acids it can encode?","choices":["400","600","1200","3600"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"3 bases per amino acid","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Divide bases by 3."},"exp":"**Answer:** 400\n\n**Correct: 400** Each codon is 3 bases. 1200 / 3 = 400 amino acids at most.\n\n**Incorrect: 600** That uses 2 bases per codon.\n\n**Incorrect: 1200** That uses 1 base per amino acid.\n\n**Incorrect: 3600** That multiplies by 3 instead of dividing.","how":{"ask":"How many codons fit in 1200 bases?","know":"One codon = 3 bases = 1 amino acid.","steps":[{"text":"Divide, don't multiply.","strike":["3600"]},{"text":"Codons are 3 bases, not 1 or 2.","strike":["600","1200"]},{"text":"1200 / 3 = 400. Answer: 400."}],"trap":"3600: multiplying by 3 is the classic slip."},"teach":[{"text":"Max means every base is in a codon."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Maximum amino acids = bases / 3."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-113a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"apply the physics formula","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:113#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:113"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P1","passage":"A muscle's force depends on the cross-sectional area (CSA) of its fibers, not on its length. Thicker fibers hold more myofibrils, so they pull harder. A longer muscle shortens over a greater distance, and work equals force times distance. So with equal CSA, the longer muscle does more work. Power equals force times velocity, and fibers of equal diameter have equal force but differ in power by their myosin ATPase rate. Fast-twitch fibers use mostly glycolysis, while slow-twitch fibers rely on aerobic, oxidative ATP production.","q":"Swimmer P has longer arms and muscles than Swimmer Q. Both pull the same weight on a cable through a full arm stroke an equal number of times. If all pulls were completed in synchrony, which statement about the amount of work completed by the swimmers is true? At the end of the set:","choices":["both swimmers will have done the same amount of work because the pulls lasted the same amount of time.","Swimmer Q will have done more work because the weight was moved a shorter distance.","Swimmer P will have done more work because the weight was moved a greater distance.","Swimmer Q will have done more work because the muscle contracted faster to keep pace with Swimmer P."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Work = force times distance","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Speed changes power, not work","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same weight, longer path, more work."},"exp":"**Answer:** Swimmer P will have done more work because the weight was moved a greater distance.\n\n**Incorrect: both swimmers will have done the same amount of work because the pulls lasted the same amount of time.** Time is not part of work; distance is.\n\n**Incorrect: Swimmer Q will have done more work because the weight was moved a shorter distance.** A shorter distance gives less work, not more.\n\n**Correct: Swimmer P will have done more work because the weight was moved a greater distance.** The force is the same, but longer arms move the weight farther. Work is force times distance.\n\n**Incorrect: Swimmer Q will have done more work because the muscle contracted faster to keep pace with Swimmer P.** Speed affects power, not work.","how":{"ask":"Who did more work, and why?","know":"Work = force times distance moved.","steps":[{"text":"Work uses distance, not time.","hl":"work equals force times distance","strike":["both swimmers will have done the same amount of work because the pulls lasted the same amount of time."]},{"text":"Longer arms move the weight farther, so P does more, not Q.","hl":"the longer muscle does more work","strike":["Swimmer Q will have done more work because the weight was moved a shorter distance."]},{"text":"Faster contraction changes power, not work.","strike":["Swimmer Q will have done more work because the muscle contracted faster to keep pace with Swimmer P."]},{"text":"Answer: Swimmer P did more work."}],"trap":"Equal time sounds like equal work, but time is not in the work formula."},"teach":[{"text":"Same weight means same force. The longer limb moves it farther.","marks":[{"t":"the longer muscle does more work","note":"key rule"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Equal force over a longer distance means more work."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-114a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"muscle contraction and fiber type","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:114#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:114"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P1","passage":"A muscle's force depends on the cross-sectional area (CSA) of its fibers, not on its length. Thicker fibers hold more myofibrils, so they pull harder. A longer muscle shortens over a greater distance, and work equals force times distance. So with equal CSA, the longer muscle does more work. Power equals force times velocity, and fibers of equal diameter have equal force but differ in power by their myosin ATPase rate. Fast-twitch fibers use mostly glycolysis, while slow-twitch fibers rely on aerobic, oxidative ATP production.","q":"What are the relative potentials for force and power generation by a fiber with a high myosin ATPase rate and a fiber with a low rate, both of the same diameter?","choices":["The high-rate fiber is capable of generating more force than the low-rate fiber, while the potential for power generation is the same.","The two fibers possess the same potentials for force and power generation.","The high-rate fiber is capable of generating more power than the low-rate fiber, while the potential for force generation is the same.","The low-rate fiber is capable of generating more power than the high-rate fiber, while the potential for force generation is the same."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Same diameter, same force","c":[0]},{"f":"Faster ATPase, more power","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Diameter sets force; ATPase speed sets power."},"exp":"**Answer:** The high-rate fiber is capable of generating more power than the low-rate fiber, while the potential for force generation is the same.\n\n**Incorrect: The high-rate fiber is capable of generating more force than the low-rate fiber, while the potential for power generation is the same.** Equal diameter gives equal force.\n\n**Incorrect: The two fibers possess the same potentials for force and power generation.** Force is equal, but power differs with ATPase rate.\n\n**Correct: The high-rate fiber is capable of generating more power than the low-rate fiber, while the potential for force generation is the same.** Equal diameter means equal force. A faster ATPase gives faster shortening, so more power.\n\n**Incorrect: The low-rate fiber is capable of generating more power than the high-rate fiber, while the potential for force generation is the same.** The slower fiber has less power, not more.","how":{"ask":"Compare force and power at equal diameter.","know":"Power = force times velocity.","steps":[{"text":"Equal diameter means equal force.","hl":"fibers of equal diameter have equal force","strike":["The high-rate fiber is capable of generating more force than the low-rate fiber, while the potential for power generation is the same."]},{"text":"Power differs with ATPase rate.","hl":"differ in power by their myosin ATPase rate","strike":["The two fibers possess the same potentials for force and power generation."]},{"text":"Faster ATPase means faster shortening, so more power for the high-rate fiber.","strike":["The low-rate fiber is capable of generating more power than the high-rate fiber, while the potential for force generation is the same."]},{"text":"Answer: the high-rate fiber has more power, same force."}],"trap":"Thinking a fast fiber is also stronger. Strength comes from diameter only."},"teach":[{"text":"Force tracks size. Power adds speed.","marks":[{"t":"Power equals force times velocity","note":"power formula"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Same diameter, same force; faster ATPase, more power."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-115a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"atp yield and anaerobic pathways","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:115#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:115"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P1","passage":"A muscle's force depends on the cross-sectional area (CSA) of its fibers, not on its length. Thicker fibers hold more myofibrils, so they pull harder. A longer muscle shortens over a greater distance, and work equals force times distance. So with equal CSA, the longer muscle does more work. Power equals force times velocity, and fibers of equal diameter have equal force but differ in power by their myosin ATPase rate. Fast-twitch fibers use mostly glycolysis, while slow-twitch fibers rely on aerobic, oxidative ATP production.","q":"Which of the following is LEAST likely to occur in a sprinting muscle fiber that is metabolizing anaerobically?","choices":["Reduction of pyruvate to lactate","Transfer of electrons to O2 at Complex IV","Breakdown of stored glycogen","Drop in cytoplasmic pH"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"No O2: pyruvate becomes lactate","c":[0]},{"f":"Glycolysis burns glycogen fast","c":[2]},{"f":"Lactic acid lowers pH","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anaerobic means no O2, so anything using O2 is out."},"exp":"**Answer:** Transfer of electrons to O2 at Complex IV\n\n**Incorrect: Reduction of pyruvate to lactate** This is how anaerobic cells recycle NAD+.\n\n**Correct: Transfer of electrons to O2 at Complex IV** Anaerobic means oxygen is not the final electron acceptor. The electron transport chain stalls.\n\n**Incorrect: Breakdown of stored glycogen** Glycolysis runs fast and uses up glycogen.\n\n**Incorrect: Drop in cytoplasmic pH** Lactic acid builds up and lowers pH.","how":{"ask":"Which step needs oxygen?","know":"Without O2, the ETC stops; glycolysis and lactate rise.","steps":[{"text":"Anaerobic fibers lean on glycolysis.","hl":"Fast-twitch fibers use mostly glycolysis"},{"text":"Lactate forms to recycle NAD+.","strike":["Reduction of pyruvate to lactate"]},{"text":"Glycolysis eats glycogen, and lactic acid drops pH.","strike":["Breakdown of stored glycogen","Drop in cytoplasmic pH"]},{"text":"Answer: electron transfer to O2 at Complex IV needs oxygen."}],"trap":"Lactate sounds unusual, but it is the normal anaerobic product."},"teach":[{"text":"Anaerobic work runs on glycolysis, not oxygen.","marks":[{"t":"slow-twitch fibers rely on aerobic, oxidative ATP production","note":"aerobic needs O2"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** No oxygen means no Complex IV step."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-116a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"apply the physics formula","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:116#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:116"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P1","passage":"Work equals force times the distance a muscle shortens. Power equals force times velocity, so power is work done per unit time. On a power curve, Point W has force 90 units and velocity 0.1 units. Point X has force 10 units and velocity 9 units. Point Y has force 50 units and velocity 5 units, and Point Z has force 70 units and velocity 3 units.","q":"Assuming the duration of contraction is the same, which point on the power curve corresponds to the LEAST amount of work being done by the muscle?","choices":["Point W","Point X","Point Y","Point Z"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"X: 10 times 9 = 90","c":[1]},{"f":"Y and Z exceed 200","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal time: smallest force times velocity is least work."},"exp":"**Answer:** Point W\n\n**Correct: Point W** Power is 90 times 0.1 = 9, the smallest. With equal time, least power means least work.\n\n**Incorrect: Point X** Power is 10 times 9 = 90.\n\n**Incorrect: Point Y** Power is 50 times 5 = 250.\n\n**Incorrect: Point Z** Power is 70 times 3 = 210.","how":{"ask":"Which point has the smallest power?","know":"Work = power times time.","steps":[{"text":"Same time, so work tracks power.","hl":"Power equals force times velocity, so power is work done per unit time"},{"text":"W: 90 times 0.1 = 9.","hl":"Point W has force 90 units and velocity 0.1 units"},{"text":"X = 90, Y = 250, Z = 210. All bigger.","strike":["Point X","Point Y","Point Z"]},{"text":"Answer: Point W."}],"trap":"Point W has the biggest force, so it looks like the most work."},"teach":[{"text":"Multiply force by velocity for each point.","marks":[{"t":"Point W has force 90 units and velocity 0.1 units","note":"tiny velocity"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** At equal time, rank work by force times velocity."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-117a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"muscle contraction and fiber type","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:117#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:117"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P1","passage":"A muscle's force depends on the cross-sectional area (CSA) of its fibers, not on its length. Thicker fibers hold more myofibrils, so they pull harder. A longer muscle shortens over a greater distance, and work equals force times distance. So with equal CSA, the longer muscle does more work. Power equals force times velocity, and fibers of equal diameter have equal force but differ in power by their myosin ATPase rate. Fast-twitch fibers use mostly glycolysis, while slow-twitch fibers rely on aerobic, oxidative ATP production.","q":"According to the passage, relative to fast-twitch fibers, slow-twitch fibers are likely to exhibit which property?","choices":["Greater Ca2+-pumping capacity","More mitochondria","Higher glycolytic enzyme levels","Faster myosin ATPase activity"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Fast fibers pump Ca2+ faster","c":[0]},{"f":"Glycolysis and speed mark fast fibers","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Slow = aerobic, so pick the oxygen-using trait."},"exp":"**Answer:** More mitochondria\n\n**Incorrect: Greater Ca2+-pumping capacity** Fast fibers cycle Ca2+ faster to contract and relax quickly.\n\n**Correct: More mitochondria** Slow fibers make ATP aerobically. Oxidative ATP is made in mitochondria.\n\n**Incorrect: Higher glycolytic enzyme levels** Fast fibers rely on glycolysis.\n\n**Incorrect: Faster myosin ATPase activity** Fast fibers have the faster ATPase.","how":{"ask":"What trait fits an aerobic fiber?","know":"Aerobic ATP is made in mitochondria.","steps":[{"text":"Slow fibers are aerobic.","hl":"slow-twitch fibers rely on aerobic, oxidative ATP production"},{"text":"Fast Ca2+ cycling belongs to fast fibers.","strike":["Greater Ca2+-pumping capacity"]},{"text":"Glycolysis and fast ATPase are fast-fiber traits.","hl":"Fast-twitch fibers use mostly glycolysis","strike":["Higher glycolytic enzyme levels","Faster myosin ATPase activity"]},{"text":"Answer: more mitochondria."}],"trap":"Ca2+ pumping sounds like endurance, but it matches fast contraction."},"teach":[{"text":"Aerobic fibers need many mitochondria.","marks":[{"t":"aerobic, oxidative ATP production","note":"needs mitochondria"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Slow = oxidative = mitochondria; fast = glycolytic."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-118a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pedigree pattern elimination","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-bb:118#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:118"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P2","passage":"Factor N is a growth signal made by stromal cells that acts on nearby epithelial cells. Its receptor, NRK, is a receptor tyrosine kinase. A mutation that keeps NRK always on causes inherited thyroid tumors. In one family, an affected man and an affected woman have four children: two affected sons, one unaffected son, and one unaffected daughter. One affected son marries an unaffected woman, and one of their two daughters is affected.","q":"Based on the information in the passage, what is the most likely mechanism of inheritance for the inherited thyroid tumors?","choices":["Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant","Autosomal dominant","Mitochondrial"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Two affected parents, unaffected child","c":[0]},{"f":"Affected father, unaffected daughter","c":[1]},{"f":"Fathers never pass mitochondria","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Affected parents with an unaffected child means dominant."},"exp":"**Answer:** Autosomal dominant\n\n**Incorrect: Autosomal recessive** Two affected recessive parents could only have affected children.\n\n**Incorrect: X-linked dominant** An affected father gives his X to every daughter, but one daughter is unaffected.\n\n**Correct: Autosomal dominant** Two carrier parents can have unaffected children. Both sexes are affected, and a father passes it on.\n\n**Incorrect: Mitochondrial** Fathers do not pass mitochondria, yet an affected son has an affected child.","how":{"ask":"Which pattern fits every clue?","know":"Rule out patterns with one clue each.","steps":[{"text":"Affected parents had unaffected kids, so not recessive.","hl":"an affected man and an affected woman","strike":["Autosomal recessive"]},{"text":"The affected father has an unaffected daughter, so not X-linked dominant.","hl":"one unaffected daughter","strike":["X-linked dominant"]},{"text":"An affected father passed it to a daughter, so not mitochondrial.","hl":"one of their two daughters is affected","strike":["Mitochondrial"]},{"text":"Answer: autosomal dominant."}],"trap":"Recessive looks possible until you see two affected parents with an unaffected child."},"teach":[{"text":"Test each pattern against one clue.","marks":[{"t":"one unaffected daughter","note":"kills X-dominant"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Two affected parents plus an unaffected child means dominant."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-119a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:119#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:119"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P2","passage":"Factor N is a growth signal with two chains joined by a disulfide bond. Both chains are cut from one inactive precursor by proteases. Its receptor, NRK, is a receptor tyrosine kinase. A single-base change in NRK changes tyrosine 845 to cysteine and keeps the receptor always on. Sequencing this codon showed an A in normal cells but a G in tumor cells.","q":"Based on these results, the NRK mutation is what type of mutation?","choices":["Purine to purine","Purine to pyrimidine","Pyrimidine to purine","Pyrimidine to pyrimidine"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Start base A is a purine","c":[2,3]},{"f":"End base G is a purine","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A and G are purines; C, T, U are pyrimidines."},"exp":"**Answer:** Purine to purine\n\n**Correct: Purine to purine** A changed to G. Both are purines.\n\n**Incorrect: Purine to pyrimidine** G is a purine, not a pyrimidine.\n\n**Incorrect: Pyrimidine to purine** A is a purine, not a pyrimidine.\n\n**Incorrect: Pyrimidine to pyrimidine** Neither A nor G is a pyrimidine.","how":{"ask":"What class is each base?","know":"Purines: A, G. Pyrimidines: C, T, U.","steps":[{"text":"Normal base is A.","hl":"an A in normal cells"},{"text":"A is a purine, so it did not start as a pyrimidine.","strike":["Pyrimidine to purine","Pyrimidine to pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Tumor base G is also a purine.","hl":"a G in tumor cells","strike":["Purine to pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Answer: purine to purine."}],"trap":"Mixing up which letters are purines."},"teach":[{"text":"Pure As Gold: A and G are purines.","marks":[{"t":"an A in normal cells but a G in tumor cells","note":"A to G"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Name each base's class, then read old to new."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-120a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:120#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:120"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P2","passage":"Factor N is a growth signal with two chains joined by a disulfide bond. Both chains are cut from one inactive precursor by proteases. Its receptor, NRK, is a receptor tyrosine kinase. A single-base change in NRK changes tyrosine 845 to cysteine and keeps the receptor always on. Sequencing this codon showed an A in normal cells but a G in tumor cells.","q":"What is the structure of the amino acid residue that is replaced at position 845 as a result of the NRK mutation?","choices":["CH2 attached to a benzene ring","CH2 attached to an imidazole ring","CH2 attached to a phenol (ring-OH) group","Side chain looped back to the backbone N"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Tyrosine has ring plus OH","c":[0]},{"f":"Imidazole is histidine","c":[1]},{"f":"Loop to backbone N: proline","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the old residue, then match its side chain."},"exp":"**Answer:** CH2 attached to a phenol (ring-OH) group\n\n**Incorrect: CH2 attached to a benzene ring** That is phenylalanine; it lacks the OH.\n\n**Incorrect: CH2 attached to an imidazole ring** That is histidine.\n\n**Correct: CH2 attached to a phenol (ring-OH) group** Tyrosine is the residue replaced. Its side chain is a benzene ring with an OH.\n\n**Incorrect: Side chain looped back to the backbone N** That is proline.","how":{"ask":"Which residue was lost, and what does it look like?","know":"Tyrosine = CH2 + ring + OH.","steps":[{"text":"Tyrosine is the old residue; cysteine is new.","hl":"changes tyrosine 845 to cysteine"},{"text":"A ring with no OH is phenylalanine.","strike":["CH2 attached to a benzene ring"]},{"text":"Imidazole is histidine; a loop to the N is proline.","strike":["CH2 attached to an imidazole ring","Side chain looped back to the backbone N"]},{"text":"Answer: CH2 attached to a phenol group."}],"trap":"Phenylalanine looks almost the same; check for the OH."},"teach":[{"text":"Read 'changes X to Y': X is replaced.","marks":[{"t":"changes tyrosine 845 to cysteine","note":"tyrosine lost"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Tyrosine is phenylalanine plus an OH."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-121a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:121#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:121"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P2","passage":"Factor N is a growth signal with two chains joined by a disulfide bond. Both chains are cut from one inactive precursor by proteases. Its receptor, NRK, is a receptor tyrosine kinase. A single-base change in NRK changes tyrosine 845 to cysteine and keeps the receptor always on. Sequencing this codon showed an A in normal cells but a G in tumor cells.","q":"Which amino acid residue is involved in the bond that joins the two chains of Factor N?","choices":["M","T","C","K"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Met sulfur is capped (S-CH3)","c":[0]},{"f":"T and K lack sulfur","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Disulfide bond means cysteine (C)."},"exp":"**Answer:** C\n\n**Incorrect: M** Methionine has sulfur, but it is capped with a methyl, so it cannot form S-S bonds.\n\n**Incorrect: T** Threonine has an OH, not an SH.\n\n**Correct: C** Cysteine has a free SH group. Two cysteines join into a disulfide bond.\n\n**Incorrect: K** Lysine has an amine, not an SH.","how":{"ask":"Which residue forms S-S bonds?","know":"Only cysteine has a free thiol (SH).","steps":[{"text":"The chains are joined by a disulfide bond.","hl":"two chains joined by a disulfide bond"},{"text":"Methionine's sulfur is blocked by a methyl.","strike":["M"]},{"text":"Threonine and lysine have no sulfur.","strike":["T","K"]},{"text":"Answer: C, cysteine."}],"trap":"M has sulfur, but it cannot make a disulfide."},"teach":[{"text":"Disulfide = two cysteine SH groups joined.","marks":[{"t":"disulfide bond","note":"cysteine only"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** S-S bridge means cysteine, never methionine."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-122a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"evolution and conservation","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:122#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:122"],"q":"Which of the following animal pairs best illustrates the outcome of convergent evolution?","choices":["The wolf and the domestic dog","The bat and the sparrow","The lion and the tiger","The dark and the light forms of the rock pocket mouse"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Close kin share traits by descent","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Color forms: one species","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Convergent = distant groups, same trait, same need."},"exp":"**Answer:** The bat and the sparrow\n\n**Incorrect: The wolf and the domestic dog** Dogs came from wolves; they share traits by descent.\n\n**Correct: The bat and the sparrow** A mammal and a bird each evolved flight wings on their own.\n\n**Incorrect: The lion and the tiger** They are close relatives in one genus.\n\n**Incorrect: The dark and the light forms of the rock pocket mouse** These are variants within one species.","how":{"ask":"Which pair is distant but looks alike?","know":"Convergent: unrelated groups evolve similar traits.","steps":[{"text":"Wolf and dog are ancestor and descendant.","strike":["The wolf and the domestic dog"]},{"text":"Lion and tiger are close cousins.","strike":["The lion and the tiger"]},{"text":"Mouse color forms are one species.","strike":["The dark and the light forms of the rock pocket mouse"]},{"text":"Answer: the bat and the sparrow, mammal and bird with wings."}],"trap":"Mouse color forms show selection, not convergence."},"teach":[{"text":"Look for different classes with the same tool, like wings."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Convergent means distant relatives, similar trait."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-123a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"x inactivation and dosage","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-bb:123#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:123"],"q":"Assume that a certain species with sex chromosomes M and N exists such that MN individuals develop as males and MM individuals develop as females. Which of the following mechanisms would most likely compensate for the potential imbalance of sex-chromosome gene products between males and females of this species?","choices":["Doubling transcription from the N chromosome in males","Inactivation of the M chromosome in males","Inactivation of one M chromosome in females","Doubling transcription from the M chromosomes in females"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Imbalance is in M genes","c":[0]},{"f":"Males need their one M","c":[1]},{"f":"Doubling female M widens gap","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Silence one copy in the sex with two."},"exp":"**Answer:** Inactivation of one M chromosome in females\n\n**Incorrect: Doubling transcription from the N chromosome in males** Only males have N, so N is not the imbalance.\n\n**Incorrect: Inactivation of the M chromosome in males** Males would have no M genes left.\n\n**Correct: Inactivation of one M chromosome in females** Females have two M; males have one. Silencing one female M evens it out.\n\n**Incorrect: Doubling transcription from the M chromosomes in females** Females already have more M output.","how":{"ask":"Which sex has extra copies, and of what?","know":"Silence the extra copy, like X inactivation.","steps":[{"text":"MM females have two M; MN males have one.","strike":["Doubling transcription from the M chromosomes in females"]},{"text":"N is only in males, so it has no imbalance.","strike":["Doubling transcription from the N chromosome in males"]},{"text":"Males cannot lose their only M.","strike":["Inactivation of the M chromosome in males"]},{"text":"Answer: inactivate one M in females."}],"trap":"Thinking males must be fixed because they look different."},"teach":[{"text":"Count copies of the shared chromosome in each sex."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The sex with two copies silences one."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-124a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:124#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:124"],"q":"Syndrome V is a disease in which corticotropin-releasing hormone producing neurons fail to mature in the hypothalamus during embryonic development. Which endocrine axis is disrupted in individuals with Syndrome V?","choices":["The reproductive axis","The stress axis","The thyroid axis","The growth hormone axis"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"CRH to ACTH to cortisol","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name the hormone's target: CRH drives cortisol."},"exp":"**Answer:** The stress axis\n\n**Incorrect: The reproductive axis** That axis starts with GnRH, not CRH.\n\n**Correct: The stress axis** CRH makes the pituitary release ACTH. ACTH makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol.\n\n**Incorrect: The thyroid axis** That axis starts with TRH.\n\n**Incorrect: The growth hormone axis** That axis starts with GHRH.","how":{"ask":"Which axis does CRH start?","know":"CRH, ACTH, cortisol = stress axis.","steps":[{"text":"The missing neurons make CRH.","strike":["The reproductive axis"]},{"text":"TRH, not CRH, runs the thyroid axis.","strike":["The thyroid axis"]},{"text":"GHRH, not CRH, runs growth hormone.","strike":["The growth hormone axis"]},{"text":"Answer: the stress axis."}],"trap":"Corticotropin sounds unfamiliar; link cortico to cortisol."},"teach":[{"text":"Match each releasing hormone to its gland."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Read the releasing hormone name to find its axis."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-125a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"dna replication and repair","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:125#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:125"],"q":"Which statement correctly identifies an enzyme involved in DNA replication and describes its primary function?","choices":["Helicase catalyzes the joining of adjacent Okazaki fragments via phosphodiester bonds.","Primase catalyzes the synthesis of a short RNA primer on the template strand.","Ligase catalyzes the unwinding of the parent DNA strands at the replication fork.","Topoisomerase catalyzes the removal of RNA primers and their replacement with DNA."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Helicase unwinds; ligase joins","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Topoisomerase relieves supercoils","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check each enzyme name against its one job."},"exp":"**Answer:** Primase catalyzes the synthesis of a short RNA primer on the template strand.\n\n**Incorrect: Helicase catalyzes the joining of adjacent Okazaki fragments via phosphodiester bonds.** Ligase joins fragments; helicase unwinds.\n\n**Correct: Primase catalyzes the synthesis of a short RNA primer on the template strand.** DNA polymerase needs a primer to start. Primase makes it from RNA.\n\n**Incorrect: Ligase catalyzes the unwinding of the parent DNA strands at the replication fork.** Helicase unwinds; ligase seals.\n\n**Incorrect: Topoisomerase catalyzes the removal of RNA primers and their replacement with DNA.** Topoisomerase relieves supercoiling.","how":{"ask":"Which name matches its real job?","know":"Helicase unwinds, primase primes, ligase seals, topoisomerase untwists.","steps":[{"text":"Joining fragments is ligase, not helicase.","strike":["Helicase catalyzes the joining of adjacent Okazaki fragments via phosphodiester bonds."]},{"text":"Unwinding is helicase, not ligase.","strike":["Ligase catalyzes the unwinding of the parent DNA strands at the replication fork."]},{"text":"Topoisomerase relieves twisting, not primers.","strike":["Topoisomerase catalyzes the removal of RNA primers and their replacement with DNA."]},{"text":"Answer: primase makes the RNA primer."}],"trap":"The jobs are real; only the enzyme names are swapped."},"teach":[{"text":"Each wrong choice pairs a real job with the wrong enzyme."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Match enzyme to job before reading the details."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-126a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:126#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:126"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P3","passage":"Receptor KR7 sits on the cell surface and can signal cell death. When KR7 is stimulated, it recruits the adaptor protein KADD. KADD then recruits pro-Casp-X, which becomes the protease Casp-X and triggers apoptosis. Protein vFLP looks like Casp-X but has no catalytic activity, and it binds KADD at the same site as Casp-X. In these cells, cytokine IL-Z signals through a pathway that does not use KADD. Phosphorylated kinase SK3 marks normal apoptotic signaling in these cells.","q":"Overexpression of vFLP in other cell types will most likely result in:","choices":["increased apoptosis in response to IL-Z.","decreased apoptosis in response to IL-Z.","decreased apoptosis in response to KR7 ligand.","increased apoptosis in response to KR7 ligand."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"IL-Z skips KADD","c":[0,1]},{"f":"vFLP takes the Casp-X seat","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A dead look-alike that takes the seat blocks the signal."},"exp":"**Answer:** decreased apoptosis in response to KR7 ligand.\n\n**Incorrect: increased apoptosis in response to IL-Z.** IL-Z does not use KADD, so vFLP cannot change it.\n\n**Incorrect: decreased apoptosis in response to IL-Z.** IL-Z does not use KADD, so vFLP cannot change it.\n\n**Correct: decreased apoptosis in response to KR7 ligand.** Extra vFLP crowds Casp-X off KADD. Less Casp-X means less apoptosis.\n\n**Incorrect: increased apoptosis in response to KR7 ligand.** vFLP has no catalytic activity, so it cannot add death signaling.","how":{"ask":"What does more vFLP do to each pathway?","know":"An inactive look-alike competes for the same site.","steps":[{"text":"IL-Z does not go through KADD, so vFLP does not touch it.","hl":"IL-Z signals through a pathway that does not use KADD","strike":["increased apoptosis in response to IL-Z.","decreased apoptosis in response to IL-Z."]},{"text":"vFLP sits where Casp-X should bind, but cannot cut.","hl":"binds KADD at the same site as Casp-X","strike":["increased apoptosis in response to KR7 ligand."]},{"text":"Answer: less apoptosis after KR7 ligand."}],"trap":"Looks like Casp-X, so it seems it would add death. It has no activity."},"teach":[{"text":"Same seat, no activity means a blocker.","marks":[{"t":"has no catalytic activity","note":"dead copy"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An inactive look-alike lowers the pathway it competes in."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-127a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:127#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:127"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P3","passage":"Receptor KR7 sits on the cell surface and can signal cell death. When KR7 is stimulated, it recruits the adaptor protein KADD. KADD then recruits pro-Casp-X, which becomes the protease Casp-X and triggers apoptosis. Protein vFLP looks like Casp-X but has no catalytic activity, and it binds KADD at the same site as Casp-X. In these cells, cytokine IL-Z signals through a pathway that does not use KADD. Phosphorylated kinase SK3 marks normal apoptotic signaling in these cells.","q":"Which type of catalytic activity is most likely missing from vFLP?","choices":["Transferase activity","Ligase activity","Hydrolase activity","Isomerase activity"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Casp-X is a protease","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Proteases cut, ligases join","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Protease cuts peptide bonds with water: hydrolase."},"exp":"**Answer:** Hydrolase activity\n\n**Incorrect: Transferase activity** Transferases move groups; proteases do not.\n\n**Incorrect: Ligase activity** Ligases join molecules; proteases cut them.\n\n**Correct: Hydrolase activity** vFLP copies the protease Casp-X but cannot cut. Proteases break peptide bonds with water.\n\n**Incorrect: Isomerase activity** Isomerases rearrange a molecule; they do not cut it.","how":{"ask":"What activity would the look-alike have had?","know":"Proteases are hydrolases.","steps":[{"text":"vFLP is a copy of the protease Casp-X.","hl":"becomes the protease Casp-X"},{"text":"Proteases do not move groups or rearrange molecules.","strike":["Transferase activity","Isomerase activity"]},{"text":"Proteases cut; ligases join.","strike":["Ligase activity"]},{"text":"Answer: hydrolase activity."}],"trap":"Ligase sounds like a bond enzyme, but it makes bonds."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the parent enzyme's job, then its class.","marks":[{"t":"has no catalytic activity","note":"lost cutting"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Protease = hydrolase."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-128a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"germ layer and cell fate","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-bb:128#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:128"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P3","passage":"Receptor KR7 sits on the cell surface and can signal cell death. When KR7 is stimulated, it recruits the adaptor protein KADD. KADD then recruits pro-Casp-X, which becomes the protease Casp-X and triggers apoptosis. Protein vFLP looks like Casp-X but has no catalytic activity, and it binds KADD at the same site as Casp-X. In these cells, cytokine IL-Z signals through a pathway that does not use KADD. Phosphorylated kinase SK3 marks normal apoptotic signaling in these cells.","q":"Cells that contain a large amount of phosphorylated SK3 are most likely:","choices":["increasing in mass.","copying their chromosomes.","entering mitosis.","halting the cell cycle."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"SK3 marks death signaling","c":[0]},{"f":"Dying cells skip S and M","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Death marker means the cell stops growing and dividing."},"exp":"**Answer:** halting the cell cycle.\n\n**Incorrect: increasing in mass.** Apoptotic cells shrink, not grow.\n\n**Incorrect: copying their chromosomes.** Cells signaling death stop DNA replication.\n\n**Incorrect: entering mitosis.** Cells signaling death stop dividing.\n\n**Correct: halting the cell cycle.** Phosphorylated SK3 marks apoptotic signaling. Such cells stop growing and dividing.","how":{"ask":"What is a cell with this marker doing?","know":"Apoptotic signaling stops growth.","steps":[{"text":"Phosphorylated SK3 marks apoptotic signaling.","hl":"Phosphorylated kinase SK3 marks normal apoptotic signaling","strike":["increasing in mass."]},{"text":"Dying cells do not replicate DNA or divide.","strike":["copying their chromosomes.","entering mitosis."]},{"text":"Answer: halting the cell cycle."}],"trap":"Kinase activity sounds like growth signaling."},"teach":[{"text":"Find what the marker means, then pick the matching fate.","marks":[{"t":"marks normal apoptotic signaling","note":"death marker"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Death signal means arrest, not growth."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-129a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:129#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:129"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P3","passage":"Receptor KR7 sits on the cell surface and can signal cell death. When KR7 is stimulated, it recruits the adaptor protein KADD. KADD then recruits pro-Casp-X, which becomes the protease Casp-X and triggers apoptosis. Protein vFLP looks like Casp-X but has no catalytic activity, and it binds KADD at the same site as Casp-X. In these cells, cytokine IL-Z signals through a pathway that does not use KADD. Phosphorylated kinase SK3 marks normal apoptotic signaling in these cells.","q":"Based on the passage, the overexpression of which protein is most likely associated with these cells becoming cancerous?","choices":["KADD","KR7","vFLP","Casp-X"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"KR7 and KADD drive death","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Casp-X carries out apoptosis","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cancer favors whatever blocks apoptosis."},"exp":"**Answer:** vFLP\n\n**Incorrect: KADD** More KADD would favor death signaling.\n\n**Incorrect: KR7** More KR7 would favor death signaling.\n\n**Correct: vFLP** vFLP blocks Casp-X at KADD. Cells that cannot die can become cancerous.\n\n**Incorrect: Casp-X** More Casp-X would cause more apoptosis.","how":{"ask":"Which protein blocks cell death?","know":"Blocking apoptosis favors cancer.","steps":[{"text":"KR7 and KADD start the death signal.","hl":"it recruits the adaptor protein KADD","strike":["KADD","KR7"]},{"text":"Casp-X triggers apoptosis.","hl":"triggers apoptosis","strike":["Casp-X"]},{"text":"Answer: vFLP, which blocks Casp-X."}],"trap":"Any signaling protein can sound cancer-linked; ask which one stops death."},"teach":[{"text":"vFLP takes the Casp-X seat without cutting.","marks":[{"t":"binds KADD at the same site as Casp-X","note":"blocks death"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Overexpressed death blockers favor cancer."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-130a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:130#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:130"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P4","passage":"Drug V is a plant glycoside that strengthens heart contraction by blocking the Na+/K+ ATPase in heart muscle cells. Some patients inactivate drug V because gut bacteria reduce it to dihydro-V. The gut bacterium Bacterium Q can carry out this reduction. An operon holding two genes, dvrA and dvrB, was turned on when Bacterium Q grew with drug V. High levels of lysine block the reduction.","q":"The DvrA and DvrB proteins are produced from:","choices":["separate mRNAs transcribed from different promoters in front of each gene.","one RNA transcribed from one promoter and spliced into two separate mRNAs.","a single mRNA transcribed from a single promoter in front of the operon.","separate mRNAs translated from a single promoter in front of the operon."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Operon = one promoter","c":[0]},{"f":"Bacteria do not splice","c":[1]},{"f":"Promoters drive transcription, not translation","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Operon: one promoter, one mRNA, many proteins."},"exp":"**Answer:** a single mRNA transcribed from a single promoter in front of the operon.\n\n**Incorrect: separate mRNAs transcribed from different promoters in front of each gene.** Genes in one operon share one promoter.\n\n**Incorrect: one RNA transcribed from one promoter and spliced into two separate mRNAs.** Bacteria do not splice their mRNA.\n\n**Correct: a single mRNA transcribed from a single promoter in front of the operon.** An operon is read into one mRNA. Ribosomes make both proteins from it.\n\n**Incorrect: separate mRNAs translated from a single promoter in front of the operon.** A promoter starts transcription, not translation.","how":{"ask":"How is an operon expressed?","know":"One promoter gives one polycistronic mRNA.","steps":[{"text":"The two genes sit in one operon.","hl":"An operon holding two genes","strike":["separate mRNAs transcribed from different promoters in front of each gene."]},{"text":"Bacteria do not splice RNA.","hl":"The gut bacterium Bacterium Q","strike":["one RNA transcribed from one promoter and spliced into two separate mRNAs."]},{"text":"Promoters control transcription, not translation.","strike":["separate mRNAs translated from a single promoter in front of the operon."]},{"text":"Answer: one mRNA from one promoter."}],"trap":"Splicing sounds plausible, but it is a eukaryote process."},"teach":[{"text":"Operon genes share one promoter and one mRNA.","marks":[{"t":"An operon holding two genes","note":"one promoter"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Bacterial operon = one promoter, one mRNA, no splicing."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-131a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"na+/k+ pump direction","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:131#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:131"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P4","passage":"Drug V is a plant glycoside that strengthens heart contraction by blocking the Na+/K+ ATPase in heart muscle cells. Stronger contraction needs more Ca2+ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger uses the inward Na+ gradient to move Ca2+ out of the cell.","q":"Drug V most likely strengthens heart contraction because it does which of the following? I. Raises cytosolic Na+ levels II. Slows Ca2+ removal by the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger III. Loads more Ca2+ into the sarcoplasmic reticulum","choices":["I only","III only","II and III only","I, II, and III"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Blocked pump traps Na+ inside","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Weak Na+ gradient slows Ca2+ exit","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Follow the chain: Na+ up, Ca2+ exit down, SR Ca2+ up."},"exp":"**Answer:** I, II, and III\n\n**Incorrect: I only** Higher Na+ also slows the exchanger and fills the SR.\n\n**Incorrect: III only** SR loading happens because Na+ rises first.\n\n**Incorrect: II and III only** Raised Na+ is the first step of the chain.\n\n**Correct: I, II, and III** The blocked pump lets Na+ build up. Less Na+ gradient slows Ca2+ exit, so the SR stores more Ca2+.","how":{"ask":"Which steps follow from blocking the pump?","know":"Na+ pump block, then Na+/Ca2+ exchanger slows.","steps":[{"text":"Blocking the pump keeps Na+ inside, so I is true.","hl":"blocking the Na+/K+ ATPase in heart muscle cells","strike":["III only","II and III only"]},{"text":"Less Na+ gradient slows Ca2+ exit, so II is true.","hl":"uses the inward Na+ gradient to move Ca2+ out of the cell","strike":["I only"]},{"text":"More Ca2+ inside fills the SR, so III is true.","hl":"more Ca2+ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)"},{"text":"Answer: I, II, and III."}],"trap":"Stopping at step I and missing the Ca2+ chain."},"teach":[{"text":"One blocked pump sets off a chain of ion changes.","marks":[{"t":"uses the inward Na+ gradient to move Ca2+ out of the cell","note":"link Na+ to Ca2+"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Na+/K+ pump block raises cell Ca2+ through the exchanger."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-132a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"membrane transport type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:132#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:132"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P4","passage":"Drug V is a plant glycoside that strengthens heart contraction by blocking the Na+/K+ ATPase in heart muscle cells. Some patients inactivate drug V because gut bacteria reduce it to dihydro-V. The gut bacterium Bacterium Q can carry out this reduction. An operon holding two genes, dvrA and dvrB, was turned on when Bacterium Q grew with drug V. High levels of lysine block the reduction.","q":"Which type of membrane transport is directly affected by drug V?","choices":["Secondary active transport","Primary active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Diffusion uses no ATP","c":[2,3]},{"f":"The pump splits ATP itself","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"ATPase in the name means primary active transport."},"exp":"**Answer:** Primary active transport\n\n**Incorrect: Secondary active transport** That uses an ion gradient, not ATP directly. It is only hit indirectly.\n\n**Correct: Primary active transport** The Na+/K+ ATPase splits ATP to move ions. Drug V blocks it directly.\n\n**Incorrect: Facilitated diffusion** That moves solutes down their gradient with no energy.\n\n**Incorrect: Simple diffusion** That needs no protein and no energy.","how":{"ask":"What kind of transporter is the drug target?","know":"ATP used directly = primary active.","steps":[{"text":"Drug V blocks an ATPase pump.","hl":"blocking the Na+/K+ ATPase"},{"text":"Diffusion uses no ATP.","strike":["Facilitated diffusion","Simple diffusion"]},{"text":"The pump uses ATP itself, not a gradient.","strike":["Secondary active transport"]},{"text":"Answer: primary active transport."}],"trap":"The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger is affected too, but only indirectly."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask: does the protein split ATP itself?","marks":[{"t":"Na+/K+ ATPase","note":"splits ATP"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ATPase pump = primary; gradient-driven = secondary."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-133a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:133#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:133"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P4","passage":"Bacterium Q reduces drug V to inactive dihydro-V using the enzyme made by gene dvrB. High levels of lysine block this reduction. Cells were grown under four conditions, and dvrB mRNA was measured during growth. Set 1 had drug V and no lysine, with dvrB at 20 units. Set 3 had no drug V, with dvrB at 2 units. Set 5 had drug V plus high lysine, with dvrB at 25 units, and Set 7 had drug V and no lysine, with dvrB at 8 units.","q":"Dihydro-V levels in the culture medium of Bacterium Q cells would most likely be highest in which data set?","choices":["1","3","5","7"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"No drug, no product","c":[1]},{"f":"Lysine blocks the reduction","c":[2]},{"f":"Set 7 has less dvrB","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Need drug present, no blocker, most enzyme."},"exp":"**Answer:** 1\n\n**Correct: 1** It has drug V, no lysine and high dvrB. That gives the most reduction.\n\n**Incorrect: 3** With no drug V, there is nothing to reduce.\n\n**Incorrect: 5** dvrB is highest, but lysine blocks the reaction.\n\n**Incorrect: 7** It has drug V, but much less dvrB than Set 1.","how":{"ask":"Which set makes the most product?","know":"Product needs substrate, enzyme and no inhibitor.","steps":[{"text":"Set 3 has no drug V to reduce.","hl":"Set 3 had no drug V","strike":["3"]},{"text":"Set 5 has lysine, which blocks the reaction.","hl":"High levels of lysine block this reduction","strike":["5"]},{"text":"Set 7 has less dvrB than Set 1.","hl":"Set 7 had drug V and no lysine, with dvrB at 8 units","strike":["7"]},{"text":"Answer: Set 1."}],"trap":"Set 5 has the highest dvrB, but lysine stops the reaction."},"teach":[{"text":"High gene level is not enough if an inhibitor is there.","marks":[{"t":"Set 5 had drug V plus high lysine","note":"blocked"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Check substrate, enzyme and inhibitor for each set."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-134a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"na+/k+ pump direction","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:134#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:134"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P4","passage":"Drug V is a plant glycoside that strengthens heart contraction by blocking the Na+/K+ ATPase in heart muscle cells. Some patients inactivate drug V because gut bacteria reduce it to dihydro-V. The gut bacterium Bacterium Q can carry out this reduction. An operon holding two genes, dvrA and dvrB, was turned on when Bacterium Q grew with drug V. High levels of lysine block the reduction.","q":"In the absence of drug V treatment, hydrolysis of one molecule of ATP results in which combination of ion movements across the heart cell membrane?","choices":["2 Na+ in; 3 K+ out","3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in","2 Na+ out; 2 K+ in","2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Na+ goes out, K+ in","c":[0]},{"f":"3 out, 2 in","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pump: 3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in per ATP."},"exp":"**Answer:** 3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in\n\n**Incorrect: 2 Na+ in; 3 K+ out** The directions are backward.\n\n**Correct: 3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in** Each ATP moves 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in. This builds the Na+ and K+ gradients.\n\n**Incorrect: 2 Na+ out; 2 K+ in** Three Na+ leave, not two.\n\n**Incorrect: 2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in** The numbers are swapped.","how":{"ask":"What does one pump cycle move?","know":"3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in.","steps":[{"text":"The target is the Na+/K+ ATPase.","hl":"Na+/K+ ATPase"},{"text":"Na+ leaves the cell; K+ enters.","strike":["2 Na+ in; 3 K+ out"]},{"text":"The ratio is 3 out to 2 in.","strike":["2 Na+ out; 2 K+ in","2 Na+ out; 3 K+ in"]},{"text":"Answer: 3 Na+ out; 2 K+ in."}],"trap":"Swapping the 3 and the 2."},"teach":[{"text":"Remember: 3 sodium out, 2 potassium in.","marks":[{"t":"Na+/K+ ATPase","note":"3 out, 2 in"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Na+/K+ pump = 3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in per ATP."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-135a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:135#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:135"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P4","passage":"Stool samples from 16 people were tested for how much drug V they reduced and for their dvr ratio (dvr operon copies per Bacterium Q marker gene). Group A samples reduced 90% of drug V and had a dvr ratio of 1.2. Group B samples reduced 15% and had a ratio of 0.4. Group A acted like strain Q1, and Group B acted like strain Q2. Adding strain Q1 to Group B samples raised reduction to 80%, but adding strain Q2 left it at 18%.","q":"Which statement is LEAST supported by the experimental data?","choices":["A high dvr ratio correlates with more inactivation of drug V.","A low dvr ratio correlates with less reduction of drug V.","The dvr operon is functionally more active in strain Q2 than in strain Q1.","The amount of dvr operon in gut flora may predict how much active drug V a patient keeps."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Group A: high ratio, 90%","c":[0]},{"f":"Group B: low ratio, 15%","c":[1]},{"f":"Ratio tracks reduction, so predicts","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the statement the data contradicts."},"exp":"**Answer:** The dvr operon is functionally more active in strain Q2 than in strain Q1.\n\n**Incorrect: A high dvr ratio correlates with more inactivation of drug V.** Group A has a high ratio and 90% reduction.\n\n**Incorrect: A low dvr ratio correlates with less reduction of drug V.** Group B has a low ratio and 15% reduction.\n\n**Correct: The dvr operon is functionally more active in strain Q2 than in strain Q1.** Adding Q1 raised reduction to 80%; Q2 left it at 18%. So Q1 is more active, not Q2.\n\n**Incorrect: The amount of dvr operon in gut flora may predict how much active drug V a patient keeps.** The ratio tracks reduction, so it could predict drug levels.","how":{"ask":"Which claim goes against the data?","know":"LEAST supported = contradicted.","steps":[{"text":"High ratio with high reduction supports the first claim.","hl":"Group A samples reduced 90% of drug V and had a dvr ratio of 1.2","strike":["A high dvr ratio correlates with more inactivation of drug V."]},{"text":"Low ratio with low reduction supports the second.","hl":"Group B samples reduced 15% and had a ratio of 0.4","strike":["A low dvr ratio correlates with less reduction of drug V."]},{"text":"Since the ratio tracks reduction, it could predict drug levels.","strike":["The amount of dvr operon in gut flora may predict how much active drug V a patient keeps."]},{"text":"Answer: Q2 is not more active; Q1 is.","hl":"Adding strain Q1 to Group B samples raised reduction to 80%"}],"trap":"The prediction claim sounds like a stretch, but the data back it."},"teach":[{"text":"Check each claim against one number.","marks":[{"t":"adding strain Q2 left it at 18%","note":"Q2 weak"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For LEAST supported, find the claim the data flip."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-136a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:136#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:136"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P5","passage":"Compound P is a short fatty acid made by gut bacteria that changes gene expression. It blocks histone deacetylases (HDACs), which undo the work of histone acetyltransferases (HATs). Both enzyme types act on the basic lysine side chains of histones and other proteins. Ketone K, a ketone body the liver makes during long fasts, looks like Compound P. Cells given Ketone K showed more acetylated histone H3 at two lysines. An antibody to acetylated tubulin showed no change with Ketone K.","q":"What is the structure of an amino acid side chain that can be modified by HATs?","choices":["-CH2-CH2-COO-","-CH2-OH","-(CH2)4-NH3+","-CH2-CH2-CONH2"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"COO- side chain is acidic","c":[0]},{"f":"Need a basic NH3+ end","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"HATs act on lysine: four CH2 then NH3+."},"exp":"**Answer:** -(CH2)4-NH3+\n\n**Incorrect: -CH2-CH2-COO-** That is glutamate, an acidic residue.\n\n**Incorrect: -CH2-OH** That is serine, a polar neutral residue.\n\n**Correct: -(CH2)4-NH3+** HATs acetylate lysine. Lysine has four CH2 groups ending in a charged amine.\n\n**Incorrect: -CH2-CH2-CONH2** That is glutamine, an amide with no charge.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain is lysine?","know":"Lysine = -(CH2)4-NH3+.","steps":[{"text":"HATs and HDACs act on lysine.","hl":"the basic lysine side chains"},{"text":"A COO- group is acidic, not basic.","strike":["-CH2-CH2-COO-"]},{"text":"OH and amide groups are not basic.","strike":["-CH2-OH","-CH2-CH2-CONH2"]},{"text":"Answer: -(CH2)4-NH3+."}],"trap":"Glutamine's amide has N, but it is not basic."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the residue named, then picture its side chain.","marks":[{"t":"the basic lysine side chains","note":"lysine"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Basic side chain ends in a charged N, like lysine's NH3+."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-137a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:137#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:137"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P5","passage":"Compound P is a short fatty acid made by gut bacteria that changes gene expression. It blocks histone deacetylases (HDACs), which undo the work of histone acetyltransferases (HATs). Both enzyme types act on the basic lysine side chains of histones and other proteins. Ketone K, a ketone body the liver makes during long fasts, looks like Compound P. Cells given Ketone K showed more acetylated histone H3 at two lysines. An antibody to acetylated tubulin showed no change with Ketone K.","q":"HATs change chromatin by:","choices":["increasing its coiling and blocking DNA replication.","decreasing its condensation and promoting transcription.","increasing charge attraction between acetyl groups and DNA, which boosts transcription.","tightening the attachment of DNA to nucleosome core particles."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Acetyl cancels lysine's + charge","c":[2]},{"f":"Less + charge loosens DNA","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Acetylation opens chromatin and turns genes on."},"exp":"**Answer:** decreasing its condensation and promoting transcription.\n\n**Incorrect: increasing its coiling and blocking DNA replication.** Acetylation loosens chromatin, not coils it.\n\n**Correct: decreasing its condensation and promoting transcription.** Acetyl groups cancel lysine's positive charge. Histones grip the negative DNA less, so genes open up.\n\n**Incorrect: increasing charge attraction between acetyl groups and DNA, which boosts transcription.** Acetyl groups are neutral; they lower attraction.\n\n**Incorrect: tightening the attachment of DNA to nucleosome core particles.** Acetylation loosens that attachment.","how":{"ask":"What does adding acetyl to lysine do?","know":"Acetyl removes the + charge, so DNA loosens.","steps":[{"text":"HATs add acetyl groups to lysine.","hl":"act on the basic lysine side chains of histones"},{"text":"Acetyl is neutral, so attraction drops.","strike":["increasing charge attraction between acetyl groups and DNA, which boosts transcription."]},{"text":"Looser grip means less coiling, not tighter binding.","strike":["increasing its coiling and blocking DNA replication.","tightening the attachment of DNA to nucleosome core particles."]},{"text":"Answer: less condensed chromatin, more transcription."}],"trap":"Mixing up HATs (open) with HDACs (close)."},"teach":[{"text":"HATs open chromatin; HDACs close it.","marks":[{"t":"undo the work of histone acetyltransferases (HATs)","note":"opposite enzymes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Acetyl on = open chromatin = genes on."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-138a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:138#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:138"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P5","passage":"Compound P is a short fatty acid made by gut bacteria that changes gene expression. It blocks histone deacetylases (HDACs), which undo the work of histone acetyltransferases (HATs). Both enzyme types act on the basic lysine side chains of histones and other proteins. Ketone K, a ketone body the liver makes during long fasts, looks like Compound P. Cells given Ketone K showed more acetylated histone H3 at two lysines. An antibody to acetylated tubulin showed no change with Ketone K.","q":"If HDAC inhibition by Ketone K is a physiological response in living animals, the information in the passage indicates it is likely to occur when:","choices":["glycogen synthesis is active.","fatty acid synthesis is active.","there is prolonged fasting with fat breakdown.","insulin levels are high."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Fed state makes no ketones","c":[0,1]},{"f":"High insulin means fed","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ketone bodies come from fat burned during fasting."},"exp":"**Answer:** there is prolonged fasting with fat breakdown.\n\n**Incorrect: glycogen synthesis is active.** That happens after a meal, when ketones are low.\n\n**Incorrect: fatty acid synthesis is active.** That is a fed-state process; ketones need fat breakdown.\n\n**Correct: there is prolonged fasting with fat breakdown.** The liver makes ketone bodies from fatty acid oxidation. This happens during long fasts.\n\n**Incorrect: insulin levels are high.** High insulin blocks ketone production.","how":{"ask":"When is Ketone K made?","know":"Ketones = fasting + fatty acid oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"Ketone K is made during long fasts.","hl":"a ketone body the liver makes during long fasts"},{"text":"Building glycogen or fat is fed-state work.","strike":["glycogen synthesis is active.","fatty acid synthesis is active."]},{"text":"High insulin means the fed state.","strike":["insulin levels are high."]},{"text":"Answer: prolonged fasting with fat breakdown."}],"trap":"Fatty acid synthesis sounds related, but it is the opposite process."},"teach":[{"text":"Ketone bodies rise when the body burns fat for fuel.","marks":[{"t":"during long fasts","note":"fasting state"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ketone bodies signal fasting and fat oxidation."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-139a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:139#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:139"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P5","passage":"Ketone K may also protect proteins from oxidative stress. Stress can damage the side chains of amino acids. In this study, researchers tracked damage to Asp, Glu, Ser, and Thr residues in kidney proteins. Mice given Ketone K showed less damage after a toxin that raises reactive oxygen species.","q":"Which word CANNOT be used to classify any of the amino acids mentioned in the passage that are tracked for damage?","choices":["Acidic","Hydrophilic","Basic","Polar"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Asp and Glu are acidic","c":[0]},{"f":"All four are polar, hydrophilic","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Label each residue; pick the word none of them fit."},"exp":"**Answer:** Basic\n\n**Incorrect: Acidic** Asp and Glu are acidic.\n\n**Incorrect: Hydrophilic** All four have polar or charged side chains.\n\n**Correct: Basic** The basic residues are Lys, Arg and His. None of them are on the list.\n\n**Incorrect: Polar** Ser and Thr have OH groups, so they are polar.","how":{"ask":"Which label fits none of the four?","know":"Basic = Lys, Arg, His.","steps":[{"text":"The list is Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr.","hl":"Asp, Glu, Ser, and Thr"},{"text":"Asp and Glu are acidic.","strike":["Acidic"]},{"text":"All four are polar and water-loving.","strike":["Hydrophilic","Polar"]},{"text":"Answer: basic."}],"trap":"CANNOT flips the question; you want the word that fits nobody."},"teach":[{"text":"Label every residue before reading choices.","marks":[{"t":"Asp, Glu, Ser, and Thr","note":"2 acidic, 2 OH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Basic residues are only Lys, Arg and His."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-140a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:140#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:140"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P5","passage":"Compound P is a short fatty acid made by gut bacteria that changes gene expression. It blocks histone deacetylases (HDACs), which undo the work of histone acetyltransferases (HATs). Both enzyme types act on the basic lysine side chains of histones and other proteins. Ketone K, a ketone body the liver makes during long fasts, looks like Compound P. Cells given Ketone K showed more acetylated histone H3 at two lysines. An antibody to acetylated tubulin showed no change with Ketone K.","q":"Which statement is best supported by the data using the anti-acetyl-tubulin antibody?","choices":["Ketone K is not a general deacetylase inhibitor.","HDACs remove acetyl groups from many non-histone proteins.","Ketone K enters the cytoplasm but not the nucleus.","Ketone K blocks the same deacetylases as Compound P."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Only one non-histone protein tested","c":[1]},{"f":"Histone change proves nuclear entry","c":[2]},{"f":"Compound P not tested here","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Histones changed, tubulin did not: the inhibitor is selective."},"exp":"**Answer:** Ketone K is not a general deacetylase inhibitor.\n\n**Correct: Ketone K is not a general deacetylase inhibitor.** Histone acetylation rose, but tubulin acetylation did not. So Ketone K does not block every deacetylase.\n\n**Incorrect: HDACs remove acetyl groups from many non-histone proteins.** Only one non-histone protein was tested.\n\n**Incorrect: Ketone K enters the cytoplasm but not the nucleus.** Histone changes show it reached the nucleus.\n\n**Incorrect: Ketone K blocks the same deacetylases as Compound P.** Compound P was not tested on tubulin.","how":{"ask":"What does no change in tubulin tell us?","know":"A selective inhibitor spares some targets.","steps":[{"text":"Tubulin acetylation did not change.","hl":"An antibody to acetylated tubulin showed no change with Ketone K"},{"text":"One protein cannot speak for many.","strike":["HDACs remove acetyl groups from many non-histone proteins."]},{"text":"Histones changed, so Ketone K reached the nucleus.","hl":"more acetylated histone H3","strike":["Ketone K enters the cytoplasm but not the nucleus."]},{"text":"Compound P was never compared, so drop that claim. Answer: not a general inhibitor.","strike":["Ketone K blocks the same deacetylases as Compound P."]}],"trap":"Choices that go beyond the data sound smart but are not tested."},"teach":[{"text":"A no-change control shows what the drug does NOT do.","marks":[{"t":"showed no change with Ketone K","note":"spares tubulin"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Pick the claim the tested data directly show."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-141a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nervous system pathways","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:141#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:141"],"q":"The nerve fibers that drive sweat gland secretion are part of which branch(es) of the peripheral nervous system?","choices":["The somatic nervous system only","The parasympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system only","The sympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system only","Both the sympathetic and the parasympathetic branches of the autonomic nervous system"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Glands are autonomic, not somatic","c":[0]},{"f":"Sweat glands lack parasympathetic supply","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sweating is fight-or-flight: sympathetic only."},"exp":"**Answer:** The sympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system only\n\n**Incorrect: The somatic nervous system only** Somatic nerves control skeletal muscle, not glands.\n\n**Incorrect: The parasympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system only** Sweat glands get no parasympathetic nerves.\n\n**Correct: The sympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system only** Sweat glands are controlled by sympathetic fibers. They have no parasympathetic supply.\n\n**Incorrect: Both the sympathetic and the parasympathetic branches of the autonomic nervous system** Only the sympathetic branch reaches them.","how":{"ask":"Which branch controls sweat glands?","know":"Glands are autonomic; sweating is sympathetic.","steps":[{"text":"Glands are not skeletal muscle, so not somatic.","strike":["The somatic nervous system only"]},{"text":"Sweat glands get no parasympathetic fibers.","strike":["The parasympathetic branch of the autonomic nervous system only","Both the sympathetic and the parasympathetic branches of the autonomic nervous system"]},{"text":"Answer: sympathetic only."}],"trap":"Many organs get both branches, so 'both' feels safe."},"teach":[{"text":"Sweat glands, like the adrenal medulla, are sympathetic only."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Fight-or-flight targets with one supply are sympathetic."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-142a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:142#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:142"],"q":"Allosteric activation of an enzyme involves which of the following events?","choices":["Binding of an activator competitively in place of the substrate","Binding of an activator to a site other than the substrate binding site","Binding of an activator covalently to the active site","Binding of an activator only to free substrate"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Allosteric = other site","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Effectors bind enzyme, not substrate","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Allo means other: binds away from the active site."},"exp":"**Answer:** Binding of an activator to a site other than the substrate binding site\n\n**Incorrect: Binding of an activator competitively in place of the substrate** That would block the active site, not activate.\n\n**Correct: Binding of an activator to a site other than the substrate binding site** Allosteric effectors bind a separate regulatory site. This changes the enzyme's shape.\n\n**Incorrect: Binding of an activator covalently to the active site** Allosteric binding is away from the active site.\n\n**Incorrect: Binding of an activator only to free substrate** Allosteric effectors bind the enzyme, not the substrate.","how":{"ask":"Where does an allosteric effector bind?","know":"Allosteric = a site other than the active site.","steps":[{"text":"Allo means other, so not the substrate site.","strike":["Binding of an activator competitively in place of the substrate","Binding of an activator covalently to the active site"]},{"text":"Effectors bind the enzyme itself.","strike":["Binding of an activator only to free substrate"]},{"text":"Answer: binding at a site other than the substrate site."}],"trap":"Competitive sounds like a binding word, but it means the active site."},"teach":[{"text":"Allosteric activators and inhibitors both bind elsewhere."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Allosteric = other site, shape change."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-143a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:143#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:143"],"q":"Which of the following best describes the chemical energy that is derived from the succinyl-CoA to fumarate steps of the Krebs cycle? Energy is produced in the forms of:","choices":["FAD, which directly powers many cell processes, and ATP, which feeds the electron transport chain.","ATP, which directly powers many cell processes, and FAD, which feeds the electron transport chain.","FADH2, which directly powers many cell processes, and ATP, which feeds the electron transport chain.","ATP, which directly powers many cell processes, and FADH2, which feeds the electron transport chain."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"FAD is spent, not made","c":[0,1]},{"f":"ATP powers work; FADH2 feeds ETC","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"ATP does work; reduced carriers feed the ETC."},"exp":"**Answer:** ATP, which directly powers many cell processes, and FADH2, which feeds the electron transport chain.\n\n**Incorrect: FAD, which directly powers many cell processes, and ATP, which feeds the electron transport chain.** FAD is the empty carrier, and ATP does not feed the ETC.\n\n**Incorrect: ATP, which directly powers many cell processes, and FAD, which feeds the electron transport chain.** FAD has no electrons to give; FADH2 does.\n\n**Incorrect: FADH2, which directly powers many cell processes, and ATP, which feeds the electron transport chain.** The roles are swapped.\n\n**Correct: ATP, which directly powers many cell processes, and FADH2, which feeds the electron transport chain.** Succinyl-CoA gives GTP, which makes ATP. Succinate to fumarate makes FADH2, which gives electrons to the ETC.","how":{"ask":"Which forms carry energy, and what does each do?","know":"Reduced carriers (NADH, FADH2) feed the ETC.","steps":[{"text":"FAD is the empty form; it carries no energy.","strike":["FAD, which directly powers many cell processes, and ATP, which feeds the electron transport chain.","ATP, which directly powers many cell processes, and FAD, which feeds the electron transport chain."]},{"text":"ATP powers work; FADH2 gives electrons to the ETC.","strike":["FADH2, which directly powers many cell processes, and ATP, which feeds the electron transport chain."]},{"text":"Answer: ATP for work, FADH2 for the ETC."}],"trap":"Swapped roles look right if you only check the names."},"teach":[{"text":"H on the carrier means it is loaded with electrons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ATP spends; NADH and FADH2 feed the ETC."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-144a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"osmolarity and fluid movement","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:144#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:144"],"q":"The average osmotic pressure of water in a salt lake is 56 atm, corresponding to a concentration of 1.0 M solutes (approximated as NaCl). What is the approximate concentration of solutes (also approximated as NaCl) present in fish blood with an osmotic pressure of 14 atm?","choices":["0.12 M","0.25 M","0.50 M","4.0 M"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Lower pressure, lower concentration","c":[3]},{"f":"14 is one fourth of 56","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pressure ratio = concentration ratio."},"exp":"**Answer:** 0.25 M\n\n**Incorrect: 0.12 M** That is one eighth, not one fourth.\n\n**Correct: 0.25 M** Osmotic pressure is proportional to concentration. 14 is one fourth of 56, so 1.0 M becomes 0.25 M.\n\n**Incorrect: 0.50 M** That is one half, not one fourth.\n\n**Incorrect: 4.0 M** Lower pressure must mean lower concentration.","how":{"ask":"What fraction of the pressure is left?","know":"Osmotic pressure is proportional to M.","steps":[{"text":"Lower pressure means lower concentration.","strike":["4.0 M"]},{"text":"14 / 56 = 1/4.","strike":["0.12 M","0.50 M"]},{"text":"1.0 M times 1/4 = 0.25 M. Answer: 0.25 M."}],"trap":"Flipping the ratio and multiplying by 4."},"teach":[{"text":"Same solute, so just scale by the pressure ratio."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Concentration scales with osmotic pressure."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-145a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"atp yield and anaerobic pathways","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:145#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:145"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P6","passage":"In low oxygen (hypoxia), cells raise glycolysis and slow oxidative phosphorylation to save oxygen. Researchers asked whether subunit R9 of Complex I helps cells cut oxygen use in hypoxia. In hypoxia, aldolase mRNA rose 4-fold over normal oxygen. R9 mRNA and protein both rose 3-fold, while actin protein was equal in all lanes. In wild-type cells, hypoxia cut oxygen use by 40%, but in R9-knockout cells oxygen use did not change. Complex IV activity was the same in all groups.","q":"What are the effects of hypoxia on energy metabolism? In hypoxia:","choices":["the citric acid cycle is induced to produce more FADH2.","oxygen accepts more electrons at Complex IV.","pyruvate is reduced to lactate to regenerate NAD+.","glycogen synthesis is induced to regenerate NAD+."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Hypoxia slows oxidative steps","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Glycogen synthesis uses no NADH","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Low O2: glycolysis up, lactate recycles NAD+."},"exp":"**Answer:** pyruvate is reduced to lactate to regenerate NAD+.\n\n**Incorrect: the citric acid cycle is induced to produce more FADH2.** With the ETC slowed, the cycle slows too.\n\n**Incorrect: oxygen accepts more electrons at Complex IV.** There is less oxygen, so fewer electrons reach it.\n\n**Correct: pyruvate is reduced to lactate to regenerate NAD+.** Glycolysis needs NAD+. Without the ETC, lactate formation recycles NADH to NAD+.\n\n**Incorrect: glycogen synthesis is induced to regenerate NAD+.** Glycogen synthesis does not use NADH.","how":{"ask":"How do cells keep glycolysis going in low O2?","know":"Lactate formation regenerates NAD+.","steps":[{"text":"Hypoxia slows oxidative phosphorylation.","hl":"slow oxidative phosphorylation to save oxygen","strike":["the citric acid cycle is induced to produce more FADH2.","oxygen accepts more electrons at Complex IV."]},{"text":"Glycolysis rises and needs NAD+.","hl":"cells raise glycolysis"},{"text":"Glycogen synthesis does not touch NADH.","strike":["glycogen synthesis is induced to regenerate NAD+."]},{"text":"Answer: pyruvate to lactate regenerates NAD+."}],"trap":"The glycogen choice reuses the right phrase, regenerate NAD+."},"teach":[{"text":"Glycolysis stalls without NAD+; lactate fixes that.","marks":[{"t":"cells raise glycolysis","note":"needs NAD+"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Low oxygen means lactate makes NAD+ for glycolysis."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-146a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:146#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:146"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P6","passage":"In low oxygen (hypoxia), cells raise glycolysis and slow oxidative phosphorylation to save oxygen. Researchers asked whether subunit R9 of Complex I helps cells cut oxygen use in hypoxia. In hypoxia, aldolase mRNA rose 4-fold over normal oxygen. R9 mRNA and protein both rose 3-fold, while actin protein was equal in all lanes. In wild-type cells, hypoxia cut oxygen use by 40%, but in R9-knockout cells oxygen use did not change. Complex IV activity was the same in all groups.","q":"Based on the passage, hypoxia induces an enzyme of:","choices":["the citric acid cycle.","fatty acid oxidation.","the electron transport chain.","glycolysis."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Aldolase splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name the induced enzyme's pathway."},"exp":"**Answer:** glycolysis.\n\n**Incorrect: the citric acid cycle.** Aldolase is not a citric acid cycle enzyme.\n\n**Incorrect: fatty acid oxidation.** Aldolase does not break down fatty acids.\n\n**Incorrect: the electron transport chain.** Aldolase is a soluble cytosolic enzyme, not an ETC complex.\n\n**Correct: glycolysis.** Aldolase splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate in glycolysis. Hypoxia raised its mRNA 4-fold.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme rose, and where does it work?","know":"Aldolase is a glycolysis enzyme.","steps":[{"text":"Aldolase rose in hypoxia.","hl":"aldolase mRNA rose 4-fold"},{"text":"Aldolase is not in the citric acid cycle, fat oxidation or the ETC.","strike":["the citric acid cycle.","fatty acid oxidation.","the electron transport chain."]},{"text":"Answer: glycolysis."}],"trap":"Complex I is in the passage, so the ETC feels tempting."},"teach":[{"text":"Know a few glycolysis enzymes by name: hexokinase, aldolase, PFK.","marks":[{"t":"aldolase mRNA rose 4-fold","note":"glycolysis up"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Hypoxia induces glycolysis enzymes."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-147a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:147#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:147"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P6","passage":"In low oxygen (hypoxia), cells raise glycolysis and slow oxidative phosphorylation to save oxygen. Researchers asked whether subunit R9 of Complex I helps cells cut oxygen use in hypoxia. In hypoxia, aldolase mRNA rose 4-fold over normal oxygen. R9 mRNA and protein both rose 3-fold, while actin protein was equal in all lanes. In wild-type cells, hypoxia cut oxygen use by 40%, but in R9-knockout cells oxygen use did not change. Complex IV activity was the same in all groups.","q":"If the R9 gene is amplified inside cells leading to overexpression of the protein, what event is likely to occur under hypoxic conditions?","choices":["Complex IV will further decrease its activity.","Oxygen consumption within the mitochondria will decrease further.","Oxygen consumption within the mitochondria will increase further.","Complex IV will further increase its activity."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Complex IV never changed","c":[0,3]},{"f":"R9 lowers O2 use","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Knockout loses the effect, so extra protein boosts it."},"exp":"**Answer:** Oxygen consumption within the mitochondria will decrease further.\n\n**Incorrect: Complex IV will further decrease its activity.** Complex IV did not change in any group.\n\n**Correct: Oxygen consumption within the mitochondria will decrease further.** Without R9, hypoxia did not lower oxygen use. So more R9 should lower it even more.\n\n**Incorrect: Oxygen consumption within the mitochondria will increase further.** R9 lowers oxygen use, not raises it.\n\n**Incorrect: Complex IV will further increase its activity.** Complex IV did not change in any group.","how":{"ask":"What does R9 do, and what would more of it do?","know":"Knockout shows what the protein is needed for.","steps":[{"text":"No R9, no drop in oxygen use.","hl":"in R9-knockout cells oxygen use did not change"},{"text":"So R9 lowers oxygen use; more R9 lowers it more.","strike":["Oxygen consumption within the mitochondria will increase further."]},{"text":"Complex IV stayed flat in every group.","hl":"Complex IV activity was the same in all groups","strike":["Complex IV will further decrease its activity.","Complex IV will further increase its activity."]},{"text":"Answer: oxygen use falls further."}],"trap":"Complex IV uses oxygen, so it seems like it should change too."},"teach":[{"text":"Loss of the protein removes the effect; more protein adds to it.","marks":[{"t":"in R9-knockout cells oxygen use did not change","note":"R9 needed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Overexpression pushes the knockout's lost effect further."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-148a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:148#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:148"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P6","passage":"In low oxygen (hypoxia), cells raise glycolysis and slow oxidative phosphorylation to save oxygen. Researchers asked whether subunit R9 of Complex I helps cells cut oxygen use in hypoxia. In hypoxia, aldolase mRNA rose 4-fold over normal oxygen. R9 mRNA and protein both rose 3-fold, while actin protein was equal in all lanes. In wild-type cells, hypoxia cut oxygen use by 40%, but in R9-knockout cells oxygen use did not change. Complex IV activity was the same in all groups.","q":"Why is the level of actin included in the protein blot?","choices":["Actin controls the expression of R9 in hypoxia and normal oxygen.","Actin is used as a control to check total protein in each sample.","Actin is induced by hypoxia at the same time as R9.","Actin expression is regulated by R9."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Actin was equal in all lanes","c":[2]},{"f":"No data link actin to R9","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A steady housekeeping protein is a loading control."},"exp":"**Answer:** Actin is used as a control to check total protein in each sample.\n\n**Incorrect: Actin controls the expression of R9 in hypoxia and normal oxygen.** Nothing in the passage links actin to R9 control.\n\n**Correct: Actin is used as a control to check total protein in each sample.** Actin levels stay steady in most cells. Equal actin shows each lane had the same protein load.\n\n**Incorrect: Actin is induced by hypoxia at the same time as R9.** Actin was equal in all lanes.\n\n**Incorrect: Actin expression is regulated by R9.** Nothing in the passage shows R9 controls actin.","how":{"ask":"What job does actin do on the blot?","know":"Loading controls stay constant.","steps":[{"text":"Actin was equal in every lane.","hl":"actin protein was equal in all lanes","strike":["Actin is induced by hypoxia at the same time as R9."]},{"text":"No data tie actin to R9 either way.","strike":["Actin controls the expression of R9 in hypoxia and normal oxygen.","Actin expression is regulated by R9."]},{"text":"Answer: actin is a loading control."}],"trap":"Thinking every protein on the blot is part of the result."},"teach":[{"text":"Actin, tubulin and GAPDH are common loading controls.","marks":[{"t":"actin protein was equal in all lanes","note":"loading control"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A steady protein on a blot checks equal loading."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-149a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:149#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:149"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P7","passage":"Fumarate, a citric acid cycle intermediate, also acts as an oxygen-sensing signal in cells. Nuclear factor TRX turns on genes for cell division and new blood vessels in low oxygen. TRX binds the DNA target sequence GGAATTCA.","q":"Which type(s) of restriction enzyme(s) can recognize the TRX binding sequence? A restriction enzyme that has: I. a four-base recognition sequence II. a six-base recognition sequence III. an eight-base recognition sequence","choices":["I only","II only","III only","I and II only"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"AATT is a palindrome","c":[1]},{"f":"GAATTC is a palindrome","c":[0]},{"f":"GGAATTCA is not a palindrome","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Restriction sites are palindromes; check each length."},"exp":"**Answer:** I and II only\n\n**Incorrect: I only** GAATTC is also a palindrome, so a six-base enzyme fits.\n\n**Incorrect: II only** AATT is also a palindrome, so a four-base enzyme fits.\n\n**Incorrect: III only** GGAATTCA reads TGAATTCC on the other strand, so it is not a palindrome.\n\n**Correct: I and II only** Restriction sites read the same on both strands. AATT and GAATTC do; the full eight bases do not.","how":{"ask":"Which lengths inside the sequence are palindromes?","know":"A site reads the same 5' to 3' on both strands.","steps":[{"text":"The sequence is GGAATTCA.","hl":"GGAATTCA"},{"text":"Middle four, AATT, is a palindrome.","strike":["II only"]},{"text":"Middle six, GAATTC, is a palindrome.","strike":["I only"]},{"text":"All eight read TGAATTCC on the other strand, so no. Answer: I and II only.","strike":["III only"]}],"trap":"Thinking the enzyme must match the whole eight bases."},"teach":[{"text":"Work from the center out and check each length.","marks":[{"t":"GGAATTCA","note":"check 4, 6, 8"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Restriction sites are palindromes; test each centered length."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-150a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme location by pathway","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:150#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:150"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P7","passage":"Some inherited tumors arise from loss of a mitochondrial enzyme. In one family, a germline mutation inactivated NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase. This enzyme passes electrons from NADH to ubiquinone in the inner mitochondrial membrane.","q":"Which component of the electron transport chain is defective in cells from this family's tumors?","choices":["Complex I","Complex II","Complex III","Complex IV"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Complex II takes succinate","c":[1]},{"f":"III and IV handle cytochrome c","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NADH enters at Complex I."},"exp":"**Answer:** Complex I\n\n**Correct: Complex I** Complex I is NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase. It moves electrons from NADH to ubiquinone.\n\n**Incorrect: Complex II** Complex II is succinate dehydrogenase; it takes electrons from succinate.\n\n**Incorrect: Complex III** Complex III moves electrons from ubiquinol to cytochrome c.\n\n**Incorrect: Complex IV** Complex IV moves electrons from cytochrome c to oxygen.","how":{"ask":"Which complex uses NADH?","know":"I = NADH, II = succinate, III = to cyt c, IV = to O2.","steps":[{"text":"The lost enzyme takes electrons from NADH.","hl":"passes electrons from NADH to ubiquinone"},{"text":"Complex II starts from succinate, not NADH.","strike":["Complex II"]},{"text":"III and IV act after ubiquinone.","strike":["Complex III","Complex IV"]},{"text":"Answer: Complex I."}],"trap":"Complex II also passes electrons to ubiquinone."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the electron donor to its complex.","marks":[{"t":"NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase","note":"Complex I"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** NADH donor = Complex I; succinate = Complex II."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-151a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:151#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:151"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P7","passage":"Fumarate, a citric acid cycle intermediate, also acts as an oxygen-sensing signal in cells. It competitively blocks HIF hydroxylase, an enzyme that tags HIF for breakdown. HIF turns on genes for cell division and new blood vessels in low oxygen.","q":"Overexpression of which enzyme is likely to result in increased levels of HIF?","choices":["Fumarate hydratase","Malate dehydrogenase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Fumarate decarboxylase"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"These use up fumarate's path","c":[0,1]},{"f":"No fumarate decarboxylase in Krebs","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"More HIF needs more fumarate: pick the enzyme that makes it."},"exp":"**Answer:** Succinate dehydrogenase\n\n**Incorrect: Fumarate hydratase** It turns fumarate into malate, so fumarate falls.\n\n**Incorrect: Malate dehydrogenase** It acts after fumarate and does not make it.\n\n**Correct: Succinate dehydrogenase** It turns succinate into fumarate. More fumarate blocks HIF hydroxylase, so HIF builds up.\n\n**Incorrect: Fumarate decarboxylase** This is not a citric acid cycle enzyme.","how":{"ask":"What raises fumarate?","know":"More inhibitor means less HIF breakdown.","steps":[{"text":"Fumarate blocks the enzyme that breaks down HIF.","hl":"It competitively blocks HIF hydroxylase"},{"text":"Fumarate hydratase and malate dehydrogenase act after fumarate.","strike":["Fumarate hydratase","Malate dehydrogenase"]},{"text":"Decarboxylase is not a Krebs step here.","strike":["Fumarate decarboxylase"]},{"text":"Answer: succinate dehydrogenase makes fumarate."}],"trap":"Fumarate hydratase has fumarate in its name, but it removes it."},"teach":[{"text":"Chain: more fumarate, less HIF breakdown, more HIF.","marks":[{"t":"an enzyme that tags HIF for breakdown","note":"blocked by fumarate"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To raise a product, boost the enzyme that makes it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-152a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"dominance and inheritance rules","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:152#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:152"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P7","passage":"A germline mutation in gene PGX is linked to a benign nerve tumor. Both mothers and fathers carry and pass on the mutant allele. Fathers pass it to sons and daughters alike. Tumors appear only in children who got the allele from their mother.","q":"Which statement best accounts for the hereditary transmission of PGX-linked tumors in a parent-specific manner? PGX is:","choices":["an X-linked gene.","an imprinted gene.","a mitochondrial gene.","an oncogene."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Fathers pass it to sons","c":[0]},{"f":"Fathers pass it at all","c":[2]},{"f":"Oncogenes show no parent bias","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Effect depends on which parent gave it: imprinting."},"exp":"**Answer:** an imprinted gene.\n\n**Incorrect: an X-linked gene.** Fathers do not pass an X to sons, yet they pass PGX to sons.\n\n**Correct: an imprinted gene.** In imprinting, one parent's copy is silenced. Only the mother's copy acts, so only maternal alleles cause tumors.\n\n**Incorrect: a mitochondrial gene.** Fathers do not pass mitochondria, but they pass PGX.\n\n**Incorrect: an oncogene.** Being an oncogene does not explain a parent-of-origin effect.","how":{"ask":"Why does the parent matter?","know":"Imprinting silences one parent's copy.","steps":[{"text":"Fathers pass it to sons, so not X-linked.","hl":"Fathers pass it to sons and daughters alike","strike":["an X-linked gene."]},{"text":"Fathers pass it at all, so not mitochondrial.","hl":"Both mothers and fathers carry and pass on the mutant allele","strike":["a mitochondrial gene."]},{"text":"Oncogene status does not explain the parent bias.","strike":["an oncogene."]},{"text":"Answer: imprinted gene.","hl":"Tumors appear only in children who got the allele from their mother"}],"trap":"Mother-only effect looks like mitochondrial inheritance."},"teach":[{"text":"Both parents pass it, but only one parent's copy matters.","marks":[{"t":"only in children who got the allele from their mother","note":"parent of origin"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Parent-of-origin effect = imprinting."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-153a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"pulse-chase label tracking","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:153#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:153"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P8","passage":"Yeast cells make Protein Y. At 0 s, radiolabeled amino acids were added to the culture, and at 40 s a large excess of unlabeled amino acids was added. Every 20 s, cells were removed, broken open, and treated with an antibody that precipitates only fully made Protein Y. Labeled Protein Y in the cells rose to 6000 cpm and stayed level until about 900 s. It then fell to 3000 cpm at 1050 s, 1500 cpm at 1200 s, and 750 cpm at 1350 s. Hypothesis 1 says the drop comes from random protein breakdown, and Hypothesis 2 says a precursor is matured and then secreted from the cell.","q":"Assuming that protein synthesis was under way when the radioactive amino acids were added, which of the following best describes how the radioactivity was distributed in one of the first molecules of Protein Y that was completely translated?","choices":["Radioactive amino acids were spread evenly through the molecule.","Radioactive amino acids were found only near the N-terminal end.","Radioactive amino acids were found only near the C-terminal end.","Radioactive amino acids were found only in the middle of the molecule."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Chains grow N to C","c":[1,3]},{"f":"First finished chains were half made","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Late-added label goes on the last end made: C-terminal."},"exp":"**Answer:** Radioactive amino acids were found only near the C-terminal end.\n\n**Incorrect: Radioactive amino acids were spread evenly through the molecule.** That needs labeling from the very start of the chain.\n\n**Incorrect: Radioactive amino acids were found only near the N-terminal end.** The N-terminal end was already made before the label came.\n\n**Correct: Radioactive amino acids were found only near the C-terminal end.** Ribosomes build chains from N to C. The first chains to finish got label only on their last part.\n\n**Incorrect: Radioactive amino acids were found only in the middle of the molecule.** Label added late keeps going to the end of the chain.","how":{"ask":"Which part was made after the label arrived?","know":"Translation runs N-terminus to C-terminus.","steps":[{"text":"Synthesis was already under way at time 0.","hl":"At 0 s, radiolabeled amino acids were added"},{"text":"The first finished chains were already partly made.","strike":["Radioactive amino acids were spread evenly through the molecule."]},{"text":"Chains grow N to C, so the new part is the C end.","strike":["Radioactive amino acids were found only near the N-terminal end.","Radioactive amino acids were found only in the middle of the molecule."]},{"text":"Answer: label only near the C-terminal end."}],"trap":"Picking the N-terminus because it is the start of the protein."},"teach":[{"text":"The first chains done were almost done when label came.","marks":[{"t":"precipitates only fully made Protein Y","note":"finished chains"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Label added mid-synthesis ends up at the C-terminus."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-154a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pulse-chase label tracking","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:154#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:154"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P8","passage":"Yeast cells make Protein Y. At 0 s, radiolabeled amino acids were added to the culture, and at 40 s a large excess of unlabeled amino acids was added. Every 20 s, cells were removed, broken open, and treated with an antibody that precipitates only fully made Protein Y. Labeled Protein Y in the cells rose to 6000 cpm and stayed level until about 900 s. It then fell to 3000 cpm at 1050 s, 1500 cpm at 1200 s, and 750 cpm at 1350 s. Hypothesis 1 says the drop comes from random protein breakdown, and Hypothesis 2 says a precursor is matured and then secreted from the cell.","q":"Which of the following best describes the radioactivity of the antibody-Protein Y precipitate in the extracellular growth medium if Hypothesis 2 is correct?","choices":["It rises from 40 s to a peak, then falls after 900 s.","It stays near zero until about 900 s, then rises as cell levels fall.","It stays near zero at all times.","It rises from about 60 s, then levels off after 900 s."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"That curve is the inside level","c":[0]},{"f":"Secretion means outside label rises","c":[2]},{"f":"Release starts when inside drops","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Outside level is the mirror of the inside drop."},"exp":"**Answer:** It stays near zero until about 900 s, then rises as cell levels fall.\n\n**Incorrect: It rises from 40 s to a peak, then falls after 900 s.** That describes the inside of the cell, not the medium.\n\n**Correct: It stays near zero until about 900 s, then rises as cell levels fall.** Under Hypothesis 2, labeled protein leaves the cell. The medium gains what the cell loses, starting near 900 s.\n\n**Incorrect: It stays near zero at all times.** That would fit Hypothesis 1, not secretion.\n\n**Incorrect: It rises from about 60 s, then levels off after 900 s.** Nothing is secreted before the cell level drops.","how":{"ask":"When does labeled protein appear outside?","know":"Secretion moves the inside loss to the outside.","steps":[{"text":"Inside level drops only after 900 s.","hl":"stayed level until about 900 s"},{"text":"Rise then fall is the inside curve.","strike":["It rises from 40 s to a peak, then falls after 900 s."]},{"text":"Secretion must raise outside label.","hl":"a precursor is matured and then secreted from the cell","strike":["It stays near zero at all times."]},{"text":"Release cannot start before 900 s. Answer: near zero, then rising after 900 s.","strike":["It rises from about 60 s, then levels off after 900 s."]}],"trap":"Choosing the familiar inside curve."},"teach":[{"text":"What leaves the cell shows up in the medium.","marks":[{"t":"secreted from the cell","note":"goes outside"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Outside rises exactly when inside falls by secretion."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-155a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"half-life count","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-bb:155#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:155"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P8","passage":"Yeast cells make Protein Y. At 0 s, radiolabeled amino acids were added to the culture, and at 40 s a large excess of unlabeled amino acids was added. Every 20 s, cells were removed, broken open, and treated with an antibody that precipitates only fully made Protein Y. Labeled Protein Y in the cells rose to 6000 cpm and stayed level until about 900 s. It then fell to 3000 cpm at 1050 s, 1500 cpm at 1200 s, and 750 cpm at 1350 s. Hypothesis 1 says the drop comes from random protein breakdown, and Hypothesis 2 says a precursor is matured and then secreted from the cell.","q":"Once the radioactivity began to decline (at about 900 s), how many seconds elapsed until 1/2 of the radioactive Protein Y was lost from the cell?","choices":["75 s","150 s","300 s","450 s"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"6000 to 3000 takes 150 s","c":[0]},{"f":"Longer times leave under half","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find when the peak value halves; subtract times."},"exp":"**Answer:** 150 s\n\n**Incorrect: 75 s** At 75 s the level is still above 3000 cpm.\n\n**Correct: 150 s** The level falls from 6000 cpm at 900 s to 3000 cpm at 1050 s. That is 150 s.\n\n**Incorrect: 300 s** By 300 s only one quarter is left.\n\n**Incorrect: 450 s** By 450 s only one eighth is left.","how":{"ask":"When does 6000 become 3000?","know":"Half lost = value drops to half.","steps":[{"text":"Start: 6000 cpm at 900 s.","hl":"rose to 6000 cpm and stayed level until about 900 s"},{"text":"Half: 3000 cpm at 1050 s, so 150 s.","hl":"fell to 3000 cpm at 1050 s","strike":["75 s"]},{"text":"300 s and 450 s go past half.","strike":["300 s","450 s"]},{"text":"Answer: 150 s."}],"trap":"Reading the clock time (1050 s) instead of the elapsed time."},"teach":[{"text":"Elapsed time = end time minus start time.","marks":[{"t":"3000 cpm at 1050 s","note":"half point"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Half-life = time for the value to halve from its start."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-156a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pulse-chase label tracking","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:156#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:156"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P8","passage":"Yeast cells make Protein Y. At 0 s, radiolabeled amino acids were added to the culture, and at 40 s a large excess of unlabeled amino acids was added. Every 20 s, cells were removed, broken open, and treated with an antibody that precipitates only fully made Protein Y. Labeled Protein Y in the cells rose to 6000 cpm and stayed level until about 900 s. It then fell to 3000 cpm at 1050 s, 1500 cpm at 1200 s, and 750 cpm at 1350 s. Hypothesis 1 says the drop comes from random protein breakdown, and Hypothesis 2 says a precursor is matured and then secreted from the cell.","q":"Assume that the rate of protein synthesis is lower when the culture is grown at 20 C than at 30 C. Which of the following best describes how temperature will influence the results of the experiment?","choices":["At 20 C, the curve reaches its plateau sooner.","At 20 C, the curve takes longer to reach its plateau.","At 20 C, the curve rises at the same rate to a lower plateau.","At 20 C, the curve rises at the same rate to a higher plateau."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Slower synthesis, slower rise","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Rate changes the slope, so the plateau comes later."},"exp":"**Answer:** At 20 C, the curve takes longer to reach its plateau.\n\n**Incorrect: At 20 C, the curve reaches its plateau sooner.** Slower synthesis cannot finish sooner.\n\n**Correct: At 20 C, the curve takes longer to reach its plateau.** Lower synthesis rate means labeled chains finish more slowly. The curve rises more slowly.\n\n**Incorrect: At 20 C, the curve rises at the same rate to a lower plateau.** The rate is what changed, so the slope must change.\n\n**Incorrect: At 20 C, the curve rises at the same rate to a higher plateau.** The rate is what changed, so the slope must change.","how":{"ask":"What does a slower rate do to the curve?","know":"Rate sets the slope of the rise.","steps":[{"text":"The curve rises as labeled chains finish.","hl":"rose to 6000 cpm"},{"text":"Slower synthesis cannot finish sooner.","strike":["At 20 C, the curve reaches its plateau sooner."]},{"text":"The rate changed, so the slope cannot stay the same.","strike":["At 20 C, the curve rises at the same rate to a lower plateau.","At 20 C, the curve rises at the same rate to a higher plateau."]},{"text":"Answer: it takes longer to reach the plateau."}],"trap":"Changing the plateau height instead of the slope."},"teach":[{"text":"Rate is the slope; a lower rate means a flatter rise.","marks":[{"t":"rose to 6000 cpm and stayed level","note":"rise then plateau"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Slower rate means same curve shape, reached later."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-157a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"virus and bacteria traits","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:157#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:157"],"q":"Certain plant viruses contain RNA as their genetic material. One of the ways these RNA viruses copy themselves is to:","choices":["infect only bacteria that use RNA as their genetic material.","alter the host's DNA polymerase so it copies the viral RNA template.","code for or carry a polymerase that copies viral RNA.","turn on host DNA sequences that direct the assembly of viral particles."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"No cells use RNA genomes","c":[0]},{"f":"Host lacks RNA-copying polymerase","c":[1]},{"f":"Host DNA lacks virus genes","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hosts cannot copy RNA from RNA, so the virus brings its own enzyme."},"exp":"**Answer:** code for or carry a polymerase that copies viral RNA.\n\n**Incorrect: infect only bacteria that use RNA as their genetic material.** All cells, bacteria too, use DNA as genetic material.\n\n**Incorrect: alter the host's DNA polymerase so it copies the viral RNA template.** RNA viruses do not rebuild host enzymes; they bring their own.\n\n**Correct: code for or carry a polymerase that copies viral RNA.** Host cells have no enzyme that copies RNA from RNA. The virus must encode or carry one.\n\n**Incorrect: turn on host DNA sequences that direct the assembly of viral particles.** Viral parts are coded by viral genes, not host DNA.","how":{"ask":"How does an RNA genome get copied?","know":"Cells have no RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.","steps":[{"text":"No cells use RNA as their genome.","strike":["infect only bacteria that use RNA as their genetic material."]},{"text":"The virus brings its own polymerase rather than rebuilding the host's.","strike":["alter the host's DNA polymerase so it copies the viral RNA template."]},{"text":"Host DNA does not hold viral genes.","strike":["turn on host DNA sequences that direct the assembly of viral particles."]},{"text":"Answer: the virus codes for or carries its own polymerase."}],"trap":"Hijacking host enzymes sounds viral, but hosts have no RNA copier."},"teach":[{"text":"RNA viruses carry or encode RNA-dependent polymerases."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** If the host lacks the enzyme, the virus supplies it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-158a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nephron segment function","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:158#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:158"],"q":"Large amounts of glucose are found in the urine of a patient with normal blood glucose. Based on this information, which portion of the nephron is most likely malfunctioning?","choices":["Collecting duct","Proximal tubule","Glomerulus","Loop of Henle"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Glucose is reabsorbed in proximal tubule","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Glomerulus filters glucose normally","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Glucose is reclaimed in the proximal tubule."},"exp":"**Answer:** Proximal tubule\n\n**Incorrect: Collecting duct** It adjusts water and salt, not glucose.\n\n**Correct: Proximal tubule** Filtered glucose is fully reabsorbed here. If it fails, glucose stays in the urine.\n\n**Incorrect: Glomerulus** It always filters glucose; that is normal.\n\n**Incorrect: Loop of Henle** It handles salt and water, not glucose.","how":{"ask":"Where is filtered glucose taken back?","know":"Proximal tubule reabsorbs all glucose.","steps":[{"text":"Blood glucose is normal, so the filter load is normal.","strike":["Glomerulus"]},{"text":"The loop and collecting duct do not reclaim glucose.","strike":["Collecting duct","Loop of Henle"]},{"text":"Answer: proximal tubule."}],"trap":"Glomerulus is the answer for protein in urine, not glucose."},"teach":[{"text":"Protein in urine points to the glomerulus; glucose points to the proximal tubule."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Match the lost substance to where it is filtered or reclaimed."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-159a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"km, kd and rate terms","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:159#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:159"],"q":"The rate of a typical human enzymatic reaction is increased by which of the following changes?","choices":["Addition of a competitive inhibitor","Decrease in temperature from 37 C to 25 C","Increase in substrate concentration below saturation","Increase in the energy of activation"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Inhibitors and cold slow enzymes","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Higher Ea means slower rate","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Below saturation, more substrate means faster rate."},"exp":"**Answer:** Increase in substrate concentration below saturation\n\n**Incorrect: Addition of a competitive inhibitor** Inhibitors slow the reaction.\n\n**Incorrect: Decrease in temperature from 37 C to 25 C** Cooling below body temperature slows human enzymes.\n\n**Correct: Increase in substrate concentration below saturation** Below Vmax, more substrate fills more active sites. The rate goes up.\n\n**Incorrect: Increase in the energy of activation** A higher barrier slows the reaction.","how":{"ask":"Which change speeds the enzyme?","know":"Rate rises with substrate until saturation.","steps":[{"text":"Inhibitors and cooling slow enzymes.","strike":["Addition of a competitive inhibitor","Decrease in temperature from 37 C to 25 C"]},{"text":"Higher activation energy slows reactions.","strike":["Increase in the energy of activation"]},{"text":"Answer: more substrate below saturation."}],"trap":"Thinking higher activation energy means more energy, so faster."},"teach":[{"text":"Below saturation, rate tracks substrate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Faster: more substrate, warmer to optimum, lower Ea."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-160a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:160#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:160"],"q":"Hydrochloric acid in the stomach kills swallowed microbes. This acid is produced by which of the following tissue types found in the stomach?","choices":["Connective","Smooth muscle","Epithelial","Nervous"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Secreting glands are epithelium","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Secretions come from epithelial cells."},"exp":"**Answer:** Epithelial\n\n**Incorrect: Connective** Connective tissue supports; it does not secrete acid.\n\n**Incorrect: Smooth muscle** Smooth muscle churns food; it does not secrete.\n\n**Correct: Epithelial** Parietal cells in the stomach lining make HCl. They are epithelial cells.\n\n**Incorrect: Nervous** Nerves signal; they do not secrete acid.","how":{"ask":"Which tissue makes secretions?","know":"Glands are built from epithelium.","steps":[{"text":"Connective tissue supports.","strike":["Connective"]},{"text":"Muscle moves; nerves signal.","strike":["Smooth muscle","Nervous"]},{"text":"Answer: epithelial."}],"trap":"Nerves trigger acid release, but they do not make it."},"teach":[{"text":"Mucus, acid and enzymes come from epithelial cells."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Secretion = epithelial tissue."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-161a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"synaptic transmission","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:161#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:161"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P9","passage":"Low dopamine signaling is thought to cause Condition D in some people. Reuptake blockers raise dopamine in the synapse by stopping its transport back into the presynaptic neuron. Dopamine is made in two steps: tyrosine hydroxylase turns tyrosine into L-DOPA, and aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase turns L-DOPA into dopamine. Monoamine oxidase B then breaks dopamine down to an aldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase turns into an acid. A variant of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, was found in 12 of 90 patients but in only 4 of 200 controls. Cells with the variant made 20% as much dopamine as wild-type cells.","q":"Based on the passage, in which of the following ways is the action of dopamine on postsynaptic receptors most likely terminated?","choices":["Lowering of the receptor's affinity for dopamine","Transport of released dopamine back into the presynaptic terminal","Production of variant enzymes for dopamine synthesis","Uptake of the dopamine-receptor complex into the postsynaptic cell"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Passage names reuptake as brake","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Synthesis acts before release","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage's reuptake step is what ends the signal."},"exp":"**Answer:** Transport of released dopamine back into the presynaptic terminal\n\n**Incorrect: Lowering of the receptor's affinity for dopamine** The passage says nothing about receptor affinity.\n\n**Correct: Transport of released dopamine back into the presynaptic terminal** Reuptake removes dopamine from the synapse. Blocking it is how the drugs work.\n\n**Incorrect: Production of variant enzymes for dopamine synthesis** Synthesis happens before release; it does not end the signal.\n\n**Incorrect: Uptake of the dopamine-receptor complex into the postsynaptic cell** The passage does not describe this.","how":{"ask":"What clears dopamine from the synapse?","know":"Reuptake ends the signal.","steps":[{"text":"The passage names transport back into the presynaptic neuron.","hl":"stopping its transport back into the presynaptic neuron","strike":["Lowering of the receptor's affinity for dopamine","Uptake of the dopamine-receptor complex into the postsynaptic cell"]},{"text":"Synthesis enzymes act before release.","strike":["Production of variant enzymes for dopamine synthesis"]},{"text":"Answer: reuptake into the presynaptic terminal."}],"trap":"The variant enzyme is in the passage, but it does not end signaling."},"teach":[{"text":"Whatever the drug blocks is the normal off-switch.","marks":[{"t":"Reuptake blockers raise dopamine in the synapse","note":"off-switch blocked"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Reuptake ends monoamine signals."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-162a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:162#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:162"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P9","passage":"Low dopamine signaling is thought to cause Condition D in some people. Reuptake blockers raise dopamine in the synapse by stopping its transport back into the presynaptic neuron. Dopamine is made in two steps: tyrosine hydroxylase turns tyrosine into L-DOPA, and aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase turns L-DOPA into dopamine. Monoamine oxidase B then breaks dopamine down to an aldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase turns into an acid. A variant of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, was found in 12 of 90 patients but in only 4 of 200 controls. Cells with the variant made 20% as much dopamine as wild-type cells.","q":"Which of the following is the most likely reason that 10 of the 12 patients with the variant allele failed to respond to even the highest doses of reuptake blockers? These ten patients:","choices":["also have a mutation in the postsynaptic dopamine receptor.","make an enzyme that breaks down the drug before it acts.","have postsynaptic dopamine receptors that are always active.","do not release enough dopamine into the synapse for the drug to make a difference."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Passage shows low synthesis only","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Always-on receptors mean no deficit","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A blocker can only save dopamine that was released."},"exp":"**Answer:** do not release enough dopamine into the synapse for the drug to make a difference.\n\n**Incorrect: also have a mutation in the postsynaptic dopamine receptor.** Possible, but the passage gives no data for it.\n\n**Incorrect: make an enzyme that breaks down the drug before it acts.** The passage gives no data for it.\n\n**Incorrect: have postsynaptic dopamine receptors that are always active.** Always-on receptors would not cause low dopamine signaling.\n\n**Correct: do not release enough dopamine into the synapse for the drug to make a difference.** The variant cells make only 20% of normal dopamine. Blocking reuptake cannot help if little is released.","how":{"ask":"Why would a reuptake blocker fail here?","know":"Blockers need released transmitter to work.","steps":[{"text":"The variant cuts dopamine output to 20%.","hl":"Cells with the variant made 20% as much dopamine as wild-type cells"},{"text":"Receptor and drug-breakdown ideas have no support.","strike":["also have a mutation in the postsynaptic dopamine receptor.","make an enzyme that breaks down the drug before it acts."]},{"text":"Always-active receptors would not cause low signaling.","strike":["have postsynaptic dopamine receptors that are always active."]},{"text":"Answer: too little dopamine is released."}],"trap":"Extra mutations sound possible but are not in the data."},"teach":[{"text":"Tie the answer to the data you were given.","marks":[{"t":"made 20% as much dopamine","note":"low supply"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A reuptake blocker fails when transmitter supply is low."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-163a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:163#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:163"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P9","passage":"Low dopamine signaling is thought to cause Condition D in some people. Reuptake blockers raise dopamine in the synapse by stopping its transport back into the presynaptic neuron. Dopamine is made in two steps: tyrosine hydroxylase turns tyrosine into L-DOPA, and aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase turns L-DOPA into dopamine. Monoamine oxidase B then breaks dopamine down to an aldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase turns into an acid. A variant of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, was found in 12 of 90 patients but in only 4 of 200 controls. Cells with the variant made 20% as much dopamine as wild-type cells.","q":"A drug that inhibits the activity of which of the following enzymes would be most likely to relieve Condition D in some people, assuming the reactions in the passage are irreversible?","choices":["Tyrosine hydroxylase","Aldehyde dehydrogenase","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase","Monoamine oxidase B"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Blocking synthesis lowers dopamine","c":[0,2]},{"f":"ALDH acts after dopamine is gone","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Block the first breakdown step to keep more dopamine."},"exp":"**Answer:** Monoamine oxidase B\n\n**Incorrect: Tyrosine hydroxylase** Blocking it lowers dopamine synthesis.\n\n**Incorrect: Aldehyde dehydrogenase** It acts after dopamine is already broken down.\n\n**Incorrect: Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase** Blocking it lowers dopamine synthesis.\n\n**Correct: Monoamine oxidase B** It is the first enzyme that breaks down dopamine. Blocking it keeps dopamine levels higher.","how":{"ask":"Which block raises dopamine?","know":"Block breakdown, not synthesis.","steps":[{"text":"Hydroxylase and decarboxylase make dopamine.","hl":"tyrosine hydroxylase turns tyrosine into L-DOPA","strike":["Tyrosine hydroxylase","Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase"]},{"text":"ALDH works on the aldehyde, after dopamine is gone.","hl":"which aldehyde dehydrogenase turns into an acid","strike":["Aldehyde dehydrogenase"]},{"text":"Answer: monoamine oxidase B, the first breakdown step."}],"trap":"Aldehyde dehydrogenase is a breakdown enzyme, but it acts too late."},"teach":[{"text":"With irreversible steps, only the first breakdown enzyme protects dopamine.","marks":[{"t":"Monoamine oxidase B then breaks dopamine down","note":"first breakdown"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To raise a molecule, block the enzyme that first uses it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-164a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"synaptic transmission","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:164#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:164"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P9","passage":"Low dopamine signaling is thought to cause Condition D in some people. Reuptake blockers raise dopamine in the synapse by stopping its transport back into the presynaptic neuron. Dopamine is made in two steps: tyrosine hydroxylase turns tyrosine into L-DOPA, and aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase turns L-DOPA into dopamine. Monoamine oxidase B then breaks dopamine down to an aldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase turns into an acid. A variant of tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, was found in 12 of 90 patients but in only 4 of 200 controls. Cells with the variant made 20% as much dopamine as wild-type cells.","q":"Reuptake blockers most likely relieve Condition D by increasing the:","choices":["rate of dopamine synthesis in the presynaptic neuron.","amount of time that dopamine stays in the synaptic space.","release of dopamine by the presynaptic neuron.","amount of dopamine taken up by the postsynaptic neuron."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Blockers act after release","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Postsynaptic cell does not absorb it","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Blocking removal makes dopamine stay longer."},"exp":"**Answer:** amount of time that dopamine stays in the synaptic space.\n\n**Incorrect: rate of dopamine synthesis in the presynaptic neuron.** The drug does not touch synthesis.\n\n**Correct: amount of time that dopamine stays in the synaptic space.** Blocking reuptake slows removal. Dopamine lingers and keeps acting on receptors.\n\n**Incorrect: release of dopamine by the presynaptic neuron.** The drug acts after release.\n\n**Incorrect: amount of dopamine taken up by the postsynaptic neuron.** Dopamine binds receptors; it is not absorbed by the postsynaptic cell.","how":{"ask":"What does blocking reuptake change?","know":"Less removal = longer time in the synapse.","steps":[{"text":"The drug blocks transport back into the presynaptic cell.","hl":"stopping its transport back into the presynaptic neuron"},{"text":"It does not change synthesis or release.","strike":["rate of dopamine synthesis in the presynaptic neuron.","release of dopamine by the presynaptic neuron."]},{"text":"The postsynaptic cell binds dopamine, not absorbs it.","strike":["amount of dopamine taken up by the postsynaptic neuron."]},{"text":"Answer: dopamine stays longer in the synapse."}],"trap":"More release sounds like more dopamine, but the drug acts later."},"teach":[{"text":"Block the exit and the molecule stays longer.","marks":[{"t":"raise dopamine in the synapse","note":"lingers"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Reuptake blockers extend synaptic time, not synthesis or release."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-165a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"structure fits exchange function","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe3-bb:165#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:165"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P10","passage":"A 5-year-old girl had poor growth, weight loss, bloating, and pale, fatty stools. A biopsy showed flattened villi in her small intestine. Her disease is triggered by a cereal protein that the gut cannot fully digest. The undigested protein pieces enter the intestinal lining, where an enzyme modifies them and sets off an immune attack. The attack damages the lining and shrinks the villi. On a diet free of the protein, her villi recovered and she began to grow.","q":"Based on the passage, which of the following conditions most likely contributed to the girl's weight loss?","choices":["A decrease in the ability of the villi to push food into the large intestine","A loss of small intestine surface area, leading to less nutrient absorption","An increase in enzyme release by the lining, leading to over-digestion of nutrients","A decrease in stomach acid release, leading to poor protein digestion"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Smooth muscle moves food, not villi","c":[0]},{"f":"More digestion would add calories","c":[2]},{"f":"Damage is in intestine, not stomach","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Flat villi mean less surface, less absorption."},"exp":"**Answer:** A loss of small intestine surface area, leading to less nutrient absorption\n\n**Incorrect: A decrease in the ability of the villi to push food into the large intestine** Smooth muscle moves food, not villi.\n\n**Correct: A loss of small intestine surface area, leading to less nutrient absorption** Villi give the gut its huge absorbing surface. Flattened villi absorb less, so she lost weight.\n\n**Incorrect: An increase in enzyme release by the lining, leading to over-digestion of nutrients** More digestion would give her more nutrients, not fewer.\n\n**Incorrect: A decrease in stomach acid release, leading to poor protein digestion** The passage shows damage in the small intestine, not the stomach.","how":{"ask":"What does damage to the villi cause?","know":"Villi = surface area for absorption.","steps":[{"text":"Her villi were flattened.","hl":"A biopsy showed flattened villi in her small intestine"},{"text":"Villi absorb; they do not push food.","strike":["A decrease in the ability of the villi to push food into the large intestine"]},{"text":"Over-digestion would add nutrients, and the stomach was not involved.","strike":["An increase in enzyme release by the lining, leading to over-digestion of nutrients","A decrease in stomach acid release, leading to poor protein digestion"]},{"text":"Answer: less surface area, less absorption."}],"trap":"Thinking villi move food along like muscle."},"teach":[{"text":"Flat villi mean a smaller absorbing surface.","marks":[{"t":"shrinks the villi","note":"less surface"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Villus damage causes malabsorption."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-166a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"osmolarity and fluid movement","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:166#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:166"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P10","passage":"A 5-year-old girl had poor growth, weight loss, bloating, and pale, fatty stools. A biopsy showed flattened villi in her small intestine. Her disease is triggered by a cereal protein that the gut cannot fully digest. The undigested protein pieces enter the intestinal lining, where an enzyme modifies them and sets off an immune attack. The attack damages the lining and shrinks the villi. On a diet free of the protein, her villi recovered and she began to grow.","q":"One consequence of severe protein malnutrition is reduced amounts of albumin in the blood. This condition would most likely cause the osmotic pressure of the blood to:","choices":["increase, resulting in less fluid in the body tissues.","decrease, resulting in more fluid in the body tissues.","decrease, resulting in less fluid in the body tissues.","increase, resulting in more fluid in the body tissues."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Fewer proteins, lower pressure","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Weak pull lets fluid leak out","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Less blood protein, less pull, fluid leaks into tissues."},"exp":"**Answer:** decrease, resulting in more fluid in the body tissues.\n\n**Incorrect: increase, resulting in less fluid in the body tissues.** Less albumin lowers osmotic pressure, not raises it.\n\n**Correct: decrease, resulting in more fluid in the body tissues.** Albumin holds water in the blood. With less of it, fluid leaks into tissues and causes swelling.\n\n**Incorrect: decrease, resulting in less fluid in the body tissues.** Lower pull means fluid leaves the blood, so tissues gain fluid.\n\n**Incorrect: increase, resulting in more fluid in the body tissues.** Less albumin lowers osmotic pressure, not raises it.","how":{"ask":"What do fewer blood proteins do?","know":"Plasma proteins pull water into capillaries.","steps":[{"text":"Less albumin means lower blood osmotic pressure.","strike":["increase, resulting in less fluid in the body tissues.","increase, resulting in more fluid in the body tissues."]},{"text":"Weaker pull lets fluid stay in the tissues.","strike":["decrease, resulting in less fluid in the body tissues."]},{"text":"Answer: decrease, more fluid in tissues."}],"trap":"Getting the pressure right but the fluid direction backward."},"teach":[{"text":"Low protein in blood leads to swelling (edema).","marks":[{"t":"weight loss","note":"malnutrition"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Low plasma protein = low oncotic pressure = edema."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-167a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"osmolarity and fluid movement","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:167#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:167"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P10","passage":"A 5-year-old girl had poor growth, weight loss, bloating, and pale, fatty stools. A biopsy showed flattened villi in her small intestine. Her disease is triggered by a cereal protein that the gut cannot fully digest. The undigested protein pieces enter the intestinal lining, where an enzyme modifies them and sets off an immune attack. The attack damages the lining and shrinks the villi. On a diet free of the protein, her villi recovered and she began to grow.","q":"Within the intestines, undigested lactose is fermented by bacteria into small acids and gas. Given this, excess undigested lactose most likely has which of the following effects within the intestines?","choices":["It decreases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to constipation.","It increases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to constipation.","It decreases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to diarrhea.","It increases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to diarrhea."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"More particles, more osmotic pressure","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Water follows solute into gut","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Extra solute in the gut pulls water in: diarrhea."},"exp":"**Answer:** It increases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to diarrhea.\n\n**Incorrect: It decreases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to constipation.** More small molecules raise osmotic pressure.\n\n**Incorrect: It increases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to constipation.** High gut osmotic pressure holds water, so stool is loose.\n\n**Incorrect: It decreases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to diarrhea.** More small molecules raise osmotic pressure.\n\n**Correct: It increases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to diarrhea.** Fermenting lactose makes many small molecules. They hold water in the gut, causing diarrhea.","how":{"ask":"What do extra solutes in the gut do?","know":"Water follows solute.","steps":[{"text":"One lactose becomes many small acids, so pressure rises.","strike":["It decreases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to constipation.","It decreases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to diarrhea."]},{"text":"Water stays in the gut, so stool is loose.","strike":["It increases the osmotic pressure within the intestines, leading to constipation."]},{"text":"Answer: higher pressure, diarrhea.","hl":"bloating"}],"trap":"Getting the pressure right but picking constipation."},"teach":[{"text":"Unabsorbed solute keeps water in the gut.","marks":[{"t":"pale, fatty stools","note":"malabsorption"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More gut solute = more water = diarrhea."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-168a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:168#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:168"],"pid":"pe3-bb:P10","passage":"A 5-year-old girl had poor growth, weight loss, bloating, and pale, fatty stools. A biopsy showed flattened villi in her small intestine. Her disease is triggered by a cereal protein that the gut cannot fully digest. The undigested protein pieces enter the intestinal lining, where an enzyme modifies them and sets off an immune attack. The attack damages the lining and shrinks the villi. On a diet free of the protein, her villi recovered and she began to grow.","q":"As the girl recovers, her growing bones take up calcium from her blood. The most effective way for the body to move calcium from the blood into bone would be to simultaneously:","choices":["increase osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast activity.","decrease osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast activity.","increase osteoblast activity and decrease osteoclast activity.","decrease osteoblast activity and decrease osteoclast activity."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Osteoclasts release calcium","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Osteoblasts build bone; raise them","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Blasts build, clasts chew: more blasts, fewer clasts."},"exp":"**Answer:** increase osteoblast activity and decrease osteoclast activity.\n\n**Incorrect: increase osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast activity.** More osteoclasts would release calcium back to the blood.\n\n**Incorrect: decrease osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast activity.** This moves calcium out of bone, the opposite.\n\n**Correct: increase osteoblast activity and decrease osteoclast activity.** Osteoblasts lay down calcium in bone. Osteoclasts break bone down, so slowing them keeps calcium in.\n\n**Incorrect: decrease osteoblast activity and decrease osteoclast activity.** Fewer osteoblasts would store less calcium.","how":{"ask":"Which cells put calcium into bone?","know":"Osteoblasts build; osteoclasts break down.","steps":[{"text":"The goal is calcium into bone.","hl":"she began to grow"},{"text":"More osteoclast activity frees calcium.","strike":["increase osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast activity.","decrease osteoblast activity and increase osteoclast activity."]},{"text":"Lower osteoblast activity stores less.","strike":["decrease osteoblast activity and decrease osteoclast activity."]},{"text":"Answer: raise osteoblasts, lower osteoclasts."}],"trap":"Mixing up blast (build) and clast (break)."},"teach":[{"text":"Blasts build bone; clasts chew bone.","marks":[{"t":"she began to grow","note":"bone building"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To store calcium, raise osteoblasts and lower osteoclasts."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-169a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:169#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:169"],"q":"The number of aspartate transcarbamoylase molecules in the cell remains constant regardless of CTP concentration, yet enzyme activity drops when CTP is high. These observations are consistent with a mechanism of:","choices":["transcriptional control.","CTP degradation.","feedback inhibition.","translational control."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Enzyme count fixed: not expression","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Breaking CTP would not lower activity","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same enzyme amount, less activity: the product inhibits it."},"exp":"**Answer:** feedback inhibition.\n\n**Incorrect: transcriptional control.** That would change how many enzyme molecules exist.\n\n**Incorrect: CTP degradation.** Breaking down CTP would not slow the enzyme.\n\n**Correct: feedback inhibition.** CTP is the end product of the pathway. It slows the enzyme directly, without changing its amount.\n\n**Incorrect: translational control.** That would also change the number of enzyme molecules.","how":{"ask":"Did the amount change, or the activity?","know":"Activity change without amount change = direct regulation.","steps":[{"text":"Enzyme number stays the same, so expression is not changing.","strike":["transcriptional control.","translational control."]},{"text":"Breaking down CTP does not explain lower activity.","strike":["CTP degradation."]},{"text":"Answer: feedback inhibition by the end product."}],"trap":"Thinking any regulation must change gene expression."},"teach":[{"text":"Fixed amount but lower activity points to an inhibitor."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** End product slows its own pathway's enzyme = feedback inhibition."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-170a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:170#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:170"],"q":"A certain drug blocks the activity of enzyme B by reversibly binding a site away from the enzyme's active site, lowering Vmax without changing Km. Given this, the drug most likely inhibits enzyme B's activity by:","choices":["lowering the enzyme's activation energy.","competitive inhibition.","noncompetitive inhibition.","feedback inhibition."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Lower Ea speeds reactions","c":[0]},{"f":"Not the active site: not competitive","c":[1]},{"f":"Drug is not pathway product","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Other site, Vmax down, Km same = noncompetitive."},"exp":"**Answer:** noncompetitive inhibition.\n\n**Incorrect: lowering the enzyme's activation energy.** That would speed the reaction, not block it.\n\n**Incorrect: competitive inhibition.** Competitive inhibitors bind the active site and raise Km.\n\n**Correct: noncompetitive inhibition.** The drug binds away from the active site. Vmax falls while Km stays the same.\n\n**Incorrect: feedback inhibition.** The drug is not a product of the enzyme's pathway.","how":{"ask":"Where does it bind, and what changes?","know":"Noncompetitive: other site, Vmax down, Km same.","steps":[{"text":"Lower activation energy speeds reactions.","strike":["lowering the enzyme's activation energy."]},{"text":"It binds away from the active site, so not competitive.","strike":["competitive inhibition."]},{"text":"A drug is not the pathway's own product.","strike":["feedback inhibition."]},{"text":"Answer: noncompetitive inhibition."}],"trap":"Feedback inhibition can be allosteric, but it needs the pathway's product."},"teach":[{"text":"Competitive: Km up. Noncompetitive: Vmax down."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Read binding site plus Km and Vmax to name the inhibitor."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe3-171a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"trace air and blood flow order","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe3-bb:171#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-bb:171"],"q":"Which series shows the order in which inhaled air passes through the regions of the respiratory tract?","choices":["Larynx → pharynx → trachea → bronchioles → bronchi","Pharynx → larynx → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles","Trachea → pharynx → larynx → bronchi → bronchioles","Pharynx → trachea → larynx → bronchioles → bronchi"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Pharynx comes before larynx","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Larynx sits above trachea","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anchor the first and last steps, then check the middle."},"exp":"**Answer:** Pharynx → larynx → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles\n\n**Incorrect: Larynx → pharynx → trachea → bronchioles → bronchi** Air hits the pharynx first, and bronchi come before bronchioles.\n\n**Correct: Pharynx → larynx → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles** Air goes from throat to voice box to windpipe. Then it branches into bronchi and smaller bronchioles.\n\n**Incorrect: Trachea → pharynx → larynx → bronchi → bronchioles** The trachea comes after the larynx, not first.\n\n**Incorrect: Pharynx → trachea → larynx → bronchioles → bronchi** The larynx comes before the trachea.","how":{"ask":"What order does air travel?","know":"Pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles.","steps":[{"text":"Air enters the pharynx first.","strike":["Larynx → pharynx → trachea → bronchioles → bronchi","Trachea → pharynx → larynx → bronchi → bronchioles"]},{"text":"The larynx sits on top of the trachea.","strike":["Pharynx → trachea → larynx → bronchioles → bronchi"]},{"text":"Answer: pharynx → larynx → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles."}],"trap":"Swapping bronchi and bronchioles at the end."},"teach":[{"text":"Tubes get smaller as they go deeper."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For order questions, fix the first step and kill choices fast."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-113a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:113#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:113"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P1","passage":"In mice, the pituitary releases hormone Z in a pattern that differs by sex. Males release hormone Z in short bursts about every 4 hours, with almost none in between. Females release it often, so blood levels stay high all day. Hormone Z binds a membrane receptor that turns on the tyrosine kinase JK3. JK3 adds phosphates to tyrosines on the receptor tail and on protein TF7. Phosphorylated TF7 pairs with another TF7, enters the nucleus and turns on genes. In the liver, steady hormone Z raises the level of some cytochrome P450 enzymes, which break down drugs.","q":"Most cytochrome P450 enzymes change the activity of drugs by:","choices":["methylating them.","oxidizing them.","hydrolyzing them.","reducing them."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"P450 adds oxygen to drugs","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"P450 = monooxygenase, so it oxidizes."},"exp":"**Answer:** oxidizing them.\n\n**Incorrect: methylating them.** Methyl transfer is done by methyltransferases, not P450s.\n\n**Correct: oxidizing them.** P450s are monooxygenases. They add an O atom (often a hydroxyl) to the drug.\n\n**Incorrect: hydrolyzing them.** Hydrolysis is done by esterases and amidases.\n\n**Incorrect: reducing them.** P450s add oxygen; that is oxidation, not reduction.","how":{"ask":"What reaction do P450s run?","know":"P450 = heme monooxygenase: adds O.","steps":[{"text":"The passage says P450s break down drugs.","hl":"cytochrome P450 enzymes, which break down drugs"},{"text":"P450s add oxygen. Not a methyl group, not water, not electrons gained.","strike":["methylating them.","hydrolyzing them.","reducing them."]},{"text":"Answer: oxidizing them."}],"trap":"Reducing them sounds possible because P450 uses NADPH. But the drug itself is oxidized."},"teach":[{"text":"P450s make drugs more polar by adding O.","marks":[{"t":"cytochrome P450 enzymes","note":"monooxygenases"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** P450 = oxidation (hydroxylation) of the drug."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-114a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:114#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:114"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P1","passage":"In mice, the pituitary releases hormone Z in a pattern that differs by sex. Males release hormone Z in short bursts about every 4 hours, with almost none in between. Females release it often, so blood levels stay high all day. Hormone Z binds a membrane receptor that turns on the tyrosine kinase JK3. JK3 adds phosphates to tyrosines on the receptor tail and on protein TF7. Phosphorylated TF7 pairs with another TF7, enters the nucleus and turns on genes. In the liver, steady hormone Z raises the level of some cytochrome P450 enzymes, which break down drugs.","q":"During phosphorylation of the receptor tail, phosphate groups are exchanged for what atoms on tyrosine residues?","choices":["Hydrogen atoms of amino groups","Hydrogen atoms of hydroxyl groups","Oxygen atoms of hydroxyl groups","Carbon atoms of the ring"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Kinases target side-chain OH","c":[0,3]},{"f":"The O stays; H leaves","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphate replaces the H of the side-chain OH."},"exp":"**Answer:** Hydrogen atoms of hydroxyl groups\n\n**Incorrect: Hydrogen atoms of amino groups** Tyrosine kinases attach phosphate to the side-chain OH, not an amine.\n\n**Correct: Hydrogen atoms of hydroxyl groups** The phenol O–H loses its H. The O then bonds to phosphorus, making a phosphate ester.\n\n**Incorrect: Oxygen atoms of hydroxyl groups** The oxygen stays and links to the phosphate.\n\n**Incorrect: Carbon atoms of the ring** Ring carbons are not replaced in phosphorylation.","how":{"ask":"Which atom does phosphate replace on tyrosine?","know":"R–OH becomes R–O–PO3: H is swapped out.","steps":[{"text":"JK3 phosphorylates tyrosines.","hl":"JK3 adds phosphates to tyrosines"},{"text":"Kinases use the side-chain OH, not an amine or ring carbon.","strike":["Hydrogen atoms of amino groups","Carbon atoms of the ring"]},{"text":"The O stays and bonds P. Answer: Hydrogen atoms of hydroxyl groups.","strike":["Oxygen atoms of hydroxyl groups"]}],"trap":"Oxygen atoms of hydroxyl groups: the O is kept, only the H leaves."},"teach":[{"text":"Ser, Thr, Tyr all have an OH. That H is what phosphate replaces.","marks":[{"t":"tyrosines","note":"phenol OH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Phosphorylation swaps the H of an OH for PO3."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-115a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:115#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:115"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P1","passage":"In mice, the pituitary releases hormone Z in a pattern that differs by sex. Males release hormone Z in short bursts about every 4 hours, with almost none in between. Females release it often, so blood levels stay high all day. Hormone Z binds a membrane receptor that turns on the tyrosine kinase JK3. JK3 adds phosphates to tyrosines on the receptor tail and on protein TF7. Phosphorylated TF7 pairs with another TF7, enters the nucleus and turns on genes. In the liver, steady hormone Z raises the level of some cytochrome P450 enzymes, which break down drugs.","q":"Given that drug Q is broken down by sex-sensitive P450 enzymes in the liver, which result best predicts the half-life of drug Q in adult males versus adult females?","choices":["Longer in females than in males","Shorter in females than in males","Equal in males and females","Zero in males, long in females"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Females have more P450","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Sexes differ, so not equal","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Steady hormone in females → more P450 → faster clearance → shorter half-life."},"exp":"**Answer:** Shorter in females than in males\n\n**Incorrect: Longer in females than in males** Females have more P450, so they clear the drug faster, not slower.\n\n**Correct: Shorter in females than in males** Steady hormone Z in females raises P450 levels. More enzyme breaks the drug down faster, so its half-life is shorter.\n\n**Incorrect: Equal in males and females** The passage gives a sex difference in P450 levels.\n\n**Incorrect: Zero in males, long in females** This reverses the direction and no drug has zero half-life.","how":{"ask":"Which sex clears drug Q faster?","know":"More enzyme → faster breakdown → shorter half-life.","steps":[{"text":"Females have steady high hormone Z.","hl":"Females release it often, so blood levels stay high all day"},{"text":"Steady hormone Z raises P450. So females break the drug down faster.","hl":"steady hormone Z raises the level of some cytochrome P450 enzymes","strike":["Longer in females than in males","Zero in males, long in females"]},{"text":"There is a sex difference. Answer: Shorter in females than in males.","strike":["Equal in males and females"]}],"trap":"Longer in females: mixing up more enzyme with longer drug life."},"teach":[{"text":"Chain it: pattern → enzyme level → clearance → half-life.","marks":[{"t":"blood levels stay high all day","note":"females steady"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More metabolizing enzyme means shorter drug half-life."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-116a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:116#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:116"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P1","passage":"In mice, the pituitary releases hormone Z in a pattern that differs by sex. Males release hormone Z in short bursts about every 4 hours, with almost none in between. Females release it often, so blood levels stay high all day. Hormone Z binds a membrane receptor that turns on the tyrosine kinase JK3. JK3 adds phosphates to tyrosines on the receptor tail and on protein TF7. Phosphorylated TF7 pairs with another TF7, enters the nucleus and turns on genes. In the liver, steady hormone Z raises the level of some cytochrome P450 enzymes, which break down drugs.","q":"A defining feature of proteins that act as transcription factors (such as TF7) is that they:","choices":["can form dimers.","can be phosphorylated.","contain a DNA binding domain.","are found in the nucleus."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Many proteins do this too","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Defining = true of all TFs and only TFs: they bind DNA."},"exp":"**Answer:** contain a DNA binding domain.\n\n**Incorrect: can form dimers.** Many non-TF proteins dimerize, and some TFs act alone.\n\n**Incorrect: can be phosphorylated.** Almost any protein can be phosphorylated.\n\n**Correct: contain a DNA binding domain.** A transcription factor must bind DNA to control a gene. That is what defines the class.\n\n**Incorrect: are found in the nucleus.** Histones and polymerases are in the nucleus too, and TFs may sit in the cytosol until active.","how":{"ask":"What do all transcription factors share?","know":"A TF binds DNA to control transcription.","steps":[{"text":"TF7 enters the nucleus and turns on genes.","hl":"enters the nucleus and turns on genes"},{"text":"Dimers, phosphates and nuclear location are TF7 details, not defining traits.","strike":["can form dimers.","can be phosphorylated.","are found in the nucleus."]},{"text":"Answer: contain a DNA binding domain."}],"trap":"can form dimers: true of TF7, but not a defining trait of all TFs."},"teach":[{"text":"Defining means every member has it, and it sets the group apart.","marks":[{"t":"turns on genes","note":"needs DNA binding"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Transcription factor = has a DNA binding domain."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-117a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme location by pathway","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:117#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:117"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P2","passage":"Enzymes CBX1 and CBX2 both add a carboxyl group to acetyl-CoA to make malonyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA from CBX1 is the building block for fatty acid synthesis. Malonyl-CoA from CBX2 blocks CPT1, the carrier that moves fatty acids into mitochondria to be burned. Keeping the two enzymes in separate places stops the cell from building and burning fat at the same time. The kinase AMPK turns off CBX2 by adding a phosphate to serine 221. Mice lacking Cbx2 stay sensitive to insulin on a rich diet.","q":"Based on the passage, CBX2 is most likely compartmentalized to:","choices":["the nucleus.","the mitochondria.","the cytosol.","the lysosome."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"CPT1 sits on mitochondria","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Cytosol is CBX1's synthesis site","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"CBX2's product blocks CPT1, so CBX2 sits next to CPT1: mitochondria."},"exp":"**Answer:** the mitochondria.\n\n**Incorrect: the nucleus.** Fat burning does not happen in the nucleus.\n\n**Correct: the mitochondria.** CBX2's malonyl-CoA blocks CPT1. CPT1 is on the outer mitochondrial membrane, so CBX2 works there.\n\n**Incorrect: the cytosol.** Fatty acid synthesis is cytosolic; that is CBX1's job, kept apart from CBX2.\n\n**Incorrect: the lysosome.** Lysosomes digest; they do not burn fatty acids.","how":{"ask":"Where must CBX2 be to block CPT1?","know":"CPT1 = outer mitochondrial membrane carrier.","steps":[{"text":"CBX2's product blocks CPT1, the mitochondrial carrier.","hl":"blocks CPT1, the carrier that moves fatty acids into mitochondria"},{"text":"Not nucleus or lysosome. Cytosol is the synthesis side, kept apart.","strike":["the nucleus.","the lysosome.","the cytosol."]},{"text":"Answer: the mitochondria."}],"trap":"the cytosol: that is where fat is built, which CBX1 serves."},"teach":[{"text":"Put an enzyme where its product acts.","marks":[{"t":"separate places","note":"compartments"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** FA synthesis = cytosol; FA oxidation = mitochondria."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-118a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:118#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:118"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P2","passage":"Enzymes CBX1 and CBX2 both add a carboxyl group to acetyl-CoA to make malonyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA from CBX1 is the building block for fatty acid synthesis. Malonyl-CoA from CBX2 blocks CPT1, the carrier that moves fatty acids into mitochondria to be burned. Keeping the two enzymes in separate places stops the cell from building and burning fat at the same time. The kinase AMPK turns off CBX2 by adding a phosphate to serine 221. Mice lacking Cbx2 stay sensitive to insulin on a rich diet.","q":"Based on the passage, compared to a wild-type mouse, a Cbx2–/–/Cpt1–/– double knockout mouse is most likely to show:","choices":["increased glucagon secretion.","decreased fatty acid synthesis.","decreased fatty acid oxidation.","increased malonyl-CoA production."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"No CPT1, no fat burning","c":[1]},{"f":"Losing CBX2 lowers malonyl-CoA","c":[3]},{"f":"Passage says nothing about glucagon","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Knock out the downstream step: no CPT1 means no fatty acid oxidation."},"exp":"**Answer:** decreased fatty acid oxidation.\n\n**Incorrect: increased glucagon secretion.** The passage gives no link from these genes to glucagon.\n\n**Incorrect: decreased fatty acid synthesis.** CBX1 still makes malonyl-CoA for synthesis.\n\n**Correct: decreased fatty acid oxidation.** CPT1 moves fatty acids into mitochondria. Without CPT1, fat cannot be burned, whatever CBX2 does.\n\n**Incorrect: increased malonyl-CoA production.** Losing CBX2 removes a malonyl-CoA source.","how":{"ask":"What happens with no CBX2 and no CPT1?","know":"The last step in a pathway decides the outcome.","steps":[{"text":"CPT1 is needed to burn fat.","hl":"the carrier that moves fatty acids into mitochondria to be burned"},{"text":"CBX1 still builds fat. Losing CBX2 lowers malonyl-CoA.","strike":["decreased fatty acid synthesis.","increased malonyl-CoA production."]},{"text":"Glucagon is not in the passage. Answer: decreased fatty acid oxidation.","strike":["increased glucagon secretion."]}],"trap":"Cbx2 loss alone raises oxidation. But no CPT1 overrides it."},"teach":[{"text":"In a double knockout, the downstream block wins.","marks":[{"t":"blocks CPT1","note":"CBX2 acts upstream"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Removing the final carrier stops the pathway, regardless of its inhibitor."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-119a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:119#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:119"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P2","passage":"Enzymes CBX1 and CBX2 both add a carboxyl group to acetyl-CoA to make malonyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA from CBX1 is the building block for fatty acid synthesis. Malonyl-CoA from CBX2 blocks CPT1, the carrier that moves fatty acids into mitochondria to be burned. Keeping the two enzymes in separate places stops the cell from building and burning fat at the same time. The kinase AMPK turns off CBX2 by adding a phosphate to serine 221. Mice lacking Cbx2 stay sensitive to insulin on a rich diet.","q":"During CBX1- or CBX2-catalyzed formation of malonyl-CoA, what functional group is added to acetyl-CoA?","choices":["–OPO3²⁻ (phosphate)","–CH3 (methyl)","–COO⁻ (carboxylate)","–OH (hydroxyl)"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Carboxylase adds COO⁻","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The enzyme name says it: carboxylase adds a carboxyl."},"exp":"**Answer:** –COO⁻ (carboxylate)\n\n**Incorrect: –OPO3²⁻ (phosphate)** Kinases add phosphate; carboxylases do not.\n\n**Incorrect: –CH3 (methyl)** Methyltransferases add methyl groups.\n\n**Correct: –COO⁻ (carboxylate)** A carboxylase adds CO2 as a carboxyl group. Acetyl-CoA (2 C) becomes malonyl-CoA (3 C).\n\n**Incorrect: –OH (hydroxyl)** Hydroxylases add OH.","how":{"ask":"What group turns acetyl-CoA into malonyl-CoA?","know":"Carboxylase = adds COO⁻ (from bicarbonate).","steps":[{"text":"The passage says a carboxyl group is added.","hl":"add a carboxyl group to acetyl-CoA"},{"text":"Phosphate, methyl and hydroxyl belong to other enzyme types.","strike":["–OPO3²⁻ (phosphate)","–CH3 (methyl)","–OH (hydroxyl)"]},{"text":"Answer: –COO⁻ (carboxylate)."}],"trap":"Phosphate: ATP is used, but no phosphate stays on the product."},"teach":[{"text":"Enzyme names tell the group: kinase = P, carboxylase = COO⁻.","marks":[{"t":"carboxyl group","note":"COO⁻ added"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Read the enzyme's name to find what it adds."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-120a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:120#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:120"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P2","passage":"Enzymes CBX1 and CBX2 both add a carboxyl group to acetyl-CoA to make malonyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA from CBX1 is the building block for fatty acid synthesis. Malonyl-CoA from CBX2 blocks CPT1, the carrier that moves fatty acids into mitochondria to be burned. Keeping the two enzymes in separate places stops the cell from building and burning fat at the same time. The kinase AMPK turns off CBX2 by adding a phosphate to serine 221. Mice lacking Cbx2 stay sensitive to insulin on a rich diet.","q":"The researchers want a mouse strain with a constantly active CBX2 made by replacing the amino acid at position 221 with a different residue. Which residue is the best choice as a replacement?","choices":["Threonine","Tyrosine","Alanine","Serine"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"OH residues still get phosphorylated","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Serine is already there","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To stop an off-switch phosphate, swap in a residue with no OH: alanine."},"exp":"**Answer:** Alanine\n\n**Incorrect: Threonine** Threonine has an OH and can still be phosphorylated.\n\n**Incorrect: Tyrosine** Tyrosine has an OH and can still be phosphorylated.\n\n**Correct: Alanine** Alanine is small like serine but has no OH. AMPK cannot add a phosphate, so CBX2 stays on.\n\n**Incorrect: Serine** Serine is the original residue, so nothing changes.","how":{"ask":"Which residue cannot be phosphorylated?","know":"Phosphate needs an OH (Ser, Thr, Tyr).","steps":[{"text":"Phosphate on serine 221 turns CBX2 off.","hl":"turns off CBX2 by adding a phosphate to serine 221"},{"text":"Threonine and tyrosine have OH; they can still be switched off.","strike":["Threonine","Tyrosine"]},{"text":"Serine is unchanged. Answer: Alanine.","strike":["Serine"]}],"trap":"Threonine looks like a safe swap, but its OH keeps the site."},"teach":[{"text":"Ser→Ala removes the OH but keeps size.","marks":[{"t":"serine 221","note":"OH target"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Phosphate turns it off → swap to Ala to keep it on."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-121a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pulse-chase label tracking","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:121#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:121"],"q":"Phages were grown with ³H-thymidine and ³⁵S-methionine. The labeled phages then infected host cells. At the end, ³H was found only inside the host cells and ³⁵S only outside the host cells. This experiment shows that:","choices":["both DNA and protein enter the host cell.","phages contain only DNA and protein.","only protein, not DNA, enters the host cell.","only DNA, not protein, enters the host cell."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Thymidine labels DNA; methionine, protein","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Shows entry, not full makeup","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Match label to molecule, then see which label went inside."},"exp":"**Answer:** only DNA, not protein, enters the host cell.\n\n**Incorrect: both DNA and protein enter the host cell.** The protein label stayed outside.\n\n**Incorrect: phages contain only DNA and protein.** The test tracks where labels go, not every part of the phage.\n\n**Incorrect: only protein, not DNA, enters the host cell.** This is backward; the DNA label went in.\n\n**Correct: only DNA, not protein, enters the host cell.** Thymidine is only in DNA and it ended up inside. Methionine (protein) stayed outside.","how":{"ask":"Which molecule entered the cell?","know":"Thymidine → DNA. Methionine (S) → protein.","steps":[{"text":"³H-thymidine marks DNA. ³⁵S-methionine marks protein."},{"text":"Protein stayed out. DNA went in.","strike":["both DNA and protein enter the host cell.","only protein, not DNA, enters the host cell."]},{"text":"The test does not list all parts. Answer: only DNA, not protein, enters the host cell.","strike":["phages contain only DNA and protein."]}],"trap":"phages contain only DNA and protein: likely true, but not what this test shows."},"teach":[{"text":"Label → molecule → location → conclusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The label found inside names what entered."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-122a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"atp yield and anaerobic pathways","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:122#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:122"],"q":"For two glucose molecules, which statement describes ATP consumption and production during the preparatory and payoff phases of glycolysis, respectively?","choices":["Two ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced","Four ATP molecules consumed, eight ATP molecules produced","Four ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced","Eight ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Per glucose: use 2, make 4","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Per glucose: 2 in, 4 out. Then multiply by the number of glucoses."},"exp":"**Answer:** Four ATP molecules consumed, eight ATP molecules produced\n\n**Incorrect: Two ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced** That is for one glucose, not two.\n\n**Correct: Four ATP molecules consumed, eight ATP molecules produced** Each glucose uses 2 ATP (hexokinase, PFK-1) and makes 4 ATP in the payoff phase. Two glucoses double both.\n\n**Incorrect: Four ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced** Payoff makes twice what prep uses.\n\n**Incorrect: Eight ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced** This swaps used and made.","how":{"ask":"ATP used, then made, for 2 glucose?","know":"1 glucose: 2 used, 4 made, net 2.","steps":[{"text":"One glucose: 2 used, 4 made."},{"text":"Two glucoses: 4 used, 8 made. Cut the single-glucose and swapped counts.","strike":["Two ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced","Eight ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced"]},{"text":"Payoff doubles prep. Answer: Four ATP molecules consumed, eight ATP molecules produced.","strike":["Four ATP molecules consumed, four ATP molecules produced"]}],"trap":"Two consumed, four produced: right for one glucose, not two."},"teach":[{"text":"Prep: hexokinase and PFK-1 each use 1 ATP. Payoff: 2 per 3-carbon × 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Glycolysis per glucose = −2, +4, net +2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-123a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"oxygen–hemoglobin curve shift","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-bb:123#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:123"],"q":"As blood passes through actively contracting skeletal muscle tissue, the affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen in the muscle tissue:","choices":["increases as a result of an increase in plasma temperature.","decreases as a result of an increase in plasma PCO2.","increases as a result of a decrease in plasma pH.","decreases as a result of a decrease in 2,3-BPG."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Working muscle lowers affinity","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Less BPG raises affinity","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Active muscle = hot, acidic, high CO2 → curve shifts right → lower affinity."},"exp":"**Answer:** decreases as a result of an increase in plasma PCO2.\n\n**Incorrect: increases as a result of an increase in plasma temperature.** Heat lowers affinity, not raises it.\n\n**Correct: decreases as a result of an increase in plasma PCO2.** Working muscle makes CO2. More CO2 shifts the curve right, so hemoglobin lets go of O2.\n\n**Incorrect: increases as a result of a decrease in plasma pH.** Lower pH lowers affinity (Bohr effect).\n\n**Incorrect: decreases as a result of a decrease in 2,3-BPG.** Less BPG would raise affinity.","how":{"ask":"Does affinity rise or fall in working muscle, and why?","know":"CADET right: CO2, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temp.","steps":[{"text":"Working muscle is hot, acidic, CO2-rich. Affinity falls."},{"text":"Increases is the wrong direction.","strike":["increases as a result of an increase in plasma temperature.","increases as a result of a decrease in plasma pH."]},{"text":"Less BPG raises affinity. Answer: decreases as a result of an increase in plasma PCO2.","strike":["decreases as a result of a decrease in 2,3-BPG."]}],"trap":"decreases ... 2,3-BPG: right direction, wrong cause."},"teach":[{"text":"Check both parts: direction and cause."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More CO2, acid, BPG or heat → lower O2 affinity."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-124a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"apply the physics formula","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:124#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:124"],"q":"A researcher measures the partial pressure of O2 above a solution at equilibrium. Which additional quantity must be measured in order to calculate the Henry's Law constant kH for the gas?","choices":["Atmospheric pressure","Concentration of dissolved O2","Volume of the solvent","Vapor pressure of water"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"kH = C / P(gas)","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Write C = kH·P; you have P, so you need C."},"exp":"**Answer:** Concentration of dissolved O2\n\n**Incorrect: Atmospheric pressure** Only the gas's own partial pressure is in Henry's law.\n\n**Correct: Concentration of dissolved O2** Henry's law is C = kH·P. With P known, kH = C / P, so C must be measured.\n\n**Incorrect: Volume of the solvent** Concentration already accounts for volume.\n\n**Incorrect: Vapor pressure of water** Water's vapor pressure is not in the equation.","how":{"ask":"What is missing to solve kH = C/P?","know":"Henry's law: C = kH × P(gas).","steps":[{"text":"Write C = kH·P. P is known."},{"text":"Total pressure, volume and water vapor are not in the formula.","strike":["Atmospheric pressure","Volume of the solvent","Vapor pressure of water"]},{"text":"Answer: Concentration of dissolved O2."}],"trap":"Atmospheric pressure: only the gas's partial pressure counts."},"teach":[{"text":"List the formula's terms. Measure what's not given."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Henry's law needs only dissolved concentration and partial pressure."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-125a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:125#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:125"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P3","passage":"The hypothalamus controls appetite and is linked to some causes of obesity. Syndrome W comes from losing genes on the father's copy of Chromosome 11. Children with Syndrome W lack growth hormone (GH), have weak muscles and feed poorly at first, then later overeat. Surgery near the hypothalamus can also cause GH loss and obesity. Hormone G is a 23-amino-acid peptide cut from a longer protein made by the stomach; it acts on the hypothalamus to raise appetite. Fasting hormone G was 800 units in lean people, 400 in obese people with no other condition, and 1,600 in people with Syndrome W.","q":"Which of the following hypotheses best accounts for the difference in circulating hormone G between people who are obese with no underlying condition and people who are lean?","choices":["The hypothalamus releases less hormone G in lean people because their energy needs are greater than those of obese people.","Hormone G release is suppressed in obese people because their bodies hold more stored energy than the bodies of lean people.","The hypothalamus releases more hormone G in lean people because their energy needs are smaller than those of obese people.","Hormone G release is suppressed in obese people because their bodies hold less stored energy than the bodies of lean people."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Stomach makes it, not hypothalamus","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Obese bodies store more energy","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check the source organ, then the direction of energy stores."},"exp":"**Answer:** Hormone G release is suppressed in obese people because their bodies hold more stored energy than the bodies of lean people.\n\n**Incorrect: The hypothalamus releases less hormone G in lean people because their energy needs are greater than those of obese people.** The stomach makes hormone G, and lean people have more of it, not less.\n\n**Correct: Hormone G release is suppressed in obese people because their bodies hold more stored energy than the bodies of lean people.** Obese people have lower hormone G. Large energy stores mean less need to drive hunger.\n\n**Incorrect: The hypothalamus releases more hormone G in lean people because their energy needs are smaller than those of obese people.** The hypothalamus is the target, not the source.\n\n**Incorrect: Hormone G release is suppressed in obese people because their bodies hold less stored energy than the bodies of lean people.** Obese bodies hold more stored energy, not less.","how":{"ask":"Why is hormone G lower in obese people?","know":"Hunger hormone falls when energy stores are full.","steps":[{"text":"Hormone G comes from the stomach.","hl":"made by the stomach","strike":["The hypothalamus releases less hormone G in lean people because their energy needs are greater than those of obese people.","The hypothalamus releases more hormone G in lean people because their energy needs are smaller than those of obese people."]},{"text":"Obese people have less hormone G than lean people.","hl":"400 in obese people with no other condition"},{"text":"Obese bodies store more energy. Answer: suppressed in obese people because of more stored energy.","strike":["Hormone G release is suppressed in obese people because their bodies hold less stored energy than the bodies of lean people."]}],"trap":"The two suppressed choices differ only in more vs less stored energy."},"teach":[{"text":"Long choices: find the one word that differs.","marks":[{"t":"made by the stomach","note":"source organ"},{"t":"acts on the hypothalamus","note":"target"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Source ≠ target; check both before reading the reason."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-126a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:126#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:126"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P3","passage":"The hypothalamus controls appetite and is linked to some causes of obesity. Syndrome W comes from losing genes on the father's copy of Chromosome 11. Children with Syndrome W lack growth hormone (GH), have weak muscles and feed poorly at first, then later overeat. Surgery near the hypothalamus can also cause GH loss and obesity. Hormone G is a 23-amino-acid peptide cut from a longer protein made by the stomach; it acts on the hypothalamus to raise appetite. Fasting hormone G was 800 units in lean people, 400 in obese people with no other condition, and 1,600 in people with Syndrome W.","q":"Hormone G is cut from a longer precursor protein. The mRNA that codes for this precursor is composed of:","choices":["more than 23 amino acids.","exactly 69 nucleotides.","exactly 72 nucleotides.","more than 72 nucleotides."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"mRNA is nucleotides, not amino acids","c":[0]},{"f":"Precursor is longer than 23","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"23 × 3 + stop = 72 for hormone G alone; the precursor is longer, so more than 72."},"exp":"**Answer:** more than 72 nucleotides.\n\n**Incorrect: more than 23 amino acids.** mRNA is made of nucleotides, not amino acids.\n\n**Incorrect: exactly 69 nucleotides.** That codes only the 23-residue piece, with no stop codon.\n\n**Incorrect: exactly 72 nucleotides.** That is the 23-residue piece plus a stop codon, but the precursor is longer.\n\n**Correct: more than 72 nucleotides.** The precursor has more than 23 residues. Plus a stop codon and UTRs, the mRNA must be longer than 72 nucleotides.","how":{"ask":"How long is the precursor's mRNA?","know":"3 nucleotides per amino acid, plus a stop codon.","steps":[{"text":"Hormone G is cut from a longer protein.","hl":"cut from a longer protein"},{"text":"mRNA is counted in nucleotides.","strike":["more than 23 amino acids."]},{"text":"23 × 3 = 69; + stop = 72. The precursor is longer. Answer: more than 72 nucleotides.","strike":["exactly 69 nucleotides.","exactly 72 nucleotides."]}],"trap":"exactly 72: right math for hormone G alone, not its precursor."},"teach":[{"text":"Cut from a longer protein means the gene codes more than the final peptide.","marks":[{"t":"23-amino-acid peptide","note":"final piece only"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mRNA length > 3 × amino acids + 3."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-127a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:127#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:127"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P3","passage":"The hypothalamus controls appetite and is linked to some causes of obesity. Syndrome W comes from losing genes on the father's copy of Chromosome 11. Children with Syndrome W lack growth hormone (GH), have weak muscles and feed poorly at first, then later overeat. Surgery near the hypothalamus can also cause GH loss and obesity. Hormone G is a 23-amino-acid peptide cut from a longer protein made by the stomach; it acts on the hypothalamus to raise appetite. Fasting hormone G was 800 units in lean people, 400 in obese people with no other condition, and 1,600 in people with Syndrome W.","q":"Hormone replacement therapy is often given to children who have Syndrome W once they reach the overeating stage. Based on the passage, the most likely reason for this therapy would be to prevent:","choices":["excess height.","weight loss.","short stature.","joint stiffness."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Low GH means short stature","c":[0]},{"f":"Overeating children gain weight","c":[1]},{"f":"GH not tied to stiff joints","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the missing hormone (GH), then its lack: short height."},"exp":"**Answer:** short stature.\n\n**Incorrect: excess height.** Excess height comes from too much GH, not too little.\n\n**Incorrect: weight loss.** These children overeat and gain weight.\n\n**Correct: short stature.** Children with Syndrome W lack GH. Replacing GH lets them grow to normal height.\n\n**Incorrect: joint stiffness.** The passage links Syndrome W to weak muscles, not stiff joints.","how":{"ask":"What does missing GH cause in children?","know":"Low GH in childhood → short stature.","steps":[{"text":"Syndrome W children lack GH.","hl":"lack growth hormone (GH)"},{"text":"Too much GH causes excess height; overeaters do not lose weight.","strike":["excess height.","weight loss."]},{"text":"Stiff joints are not linked. Answer: short stature.","strike":["joint stiffness."]}],"trap":"excess height: that is GH excess, the opposite problem."},"teach":[{"text":"Replacement therapy fixes what is missing.","marks":[{"t":"lack growth hormone (GH)","note":"GH missing"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Child GH lack → short; GH excess → giant."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-128a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:128#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:128"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P3","passage":"The hypothalamus controls appetite and is linked to some causes of obesity. Syndrome W comes from losing genes on the father's copy of Chromosome 11. Children with Syndrome W lack growth hormone (GH), have weak muscles and feed poorly at first, then later overeat. Surgery near the hypothalamus can also cause GH loss and obesity. Hormone G is a 23-amino-acid peptide cut from a longer protein made by the stomach; it acts on the hypothalamus to raise appetite. Fasting hormone G was 800 units in lean people, 400 in obese people with no other condition, and 1,600 in people with Syndrome W.","q":"Eating suppresses hormone G release. However, this drop is not caused by food in the stomach or duodenum, nor by stomach stretch. Given this, which of the following physiological conditions is likely to suppress hormone G release following a meal?","choices":["Increased levels of circulating glucagon","Increased levels of circulating insulin","Decreased osmolarity in the jejunum","Decreased levels of circulating insulin"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"After meals insulin rises","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Gut contents ruled out","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gut contents are ruled out, so pick the blood signal of the fed state: insulin up."},"exp":"**Answer:** Increased levels of circulating insulin\n\n**Incorrect: Increased levels of circulating glucagon** Glucagon rises in fasting, not after a meal.\n\n**Correct: Increased levels of circulating insulin** Insulin rises after a meal. It is a blood signal, so it fits a drop not caused by gut contents.\n\n**Incorrect: Decreased osmolarity in the jejunum** Gut contents are ruled out, and meals raise gut osmolarity.\n\n**Incorrect: Decreased levels of circulating insulin** Insulin goes up, not down, after eating.","how":{"ask":"What fed-state signal is not in the gut?","know":"Fed state = insulin high, glucagon low.","steps":[{"text":"Hormone G drives hunger, so a fed signal should lower it.","hl":"acts on the hypothalamus to raise appetite"},{"text":"After a meal insulin rises and glucagon falls.","strike":["Increased levels of circulating glucagon","Decreased levels of circulating insulin"]},{"text":"Gut contents are ruled out. Answer: Increased levels of circulating insulin.","strike":["Decreased osmolarity in the jejunum"]}],"trap":"Decreased osmolarity in the jejunum: the stem rules out gut contents."},"teach":[{"text":"The stem rules out the gut, so look to the blood.","marks":[{"t":"raise appetite","note":"hunger hormone"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** After a meal: insulin up, glucagon down."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-129a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:129#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:129"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P3","passage":"The hypothalamus controls appetite and is linked to some causes of obesity. Syndrome W comes from losing genes on the father's copy of Chromosome 11. Children with Syndrome W lack growth hormone (GH), have weak muscles and feed poorly at first, then later overeat. Surgery near the hypothalamus can also cause GH loss and obesity. Hormone G is a 23-amino-acid peptide cut from a longer protein made by the stomach; it acts on the hypothalamus to raise appetite. Fasting hormone G was 800 units in lean people, 400 in obese people with no other condition, and 1,600 in people with Syndrome W.","q":"A lean person with no other condition gains a lot of weight, then diets back to the starting weight. The fasting level of circulating hormone G would:","choices":["increase as the person gained weight, then decrease as the person lost weight.","decrease as the person gained weight, then increase as the person lost weight.","increase as the person gained weight and stay high as the person lost weight.","decrease as the person gained weight and stay low as the person lost weight."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"More fat, less hormone G","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Hormone G tracks energy stores","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hormone G moves opposite to body fat, both ways."},"exp":"**Answer:** decrease as the person gained weight, then increase as the person lost weight.\n\n**Incorrect: increase as the person gained weight, then decrease as the person lost weight.** Obese people have less hormone G, so gaining weight lowers it.\n\n**Correct: decrease as the person gained weight, then increase as the person lost weight.** Hormone G is lower with more stored fat. It falls with weight gain and rises again with weight loss.\n\n**Incorrect: increase as the person gained weight and stay high as the person lost weight.** Wrong direction on gain.\n\n**Incorrect: decrease as the person gained weight and stay low as the person lost weight.** The level follows energy stores, so it rises again with loss.","how":{"ask":"How does hormone G change with weight gain, then loss?","know":"Hunger hormone is inverse to fat stores.","steps":[{"text":"Lean 800 vs obese 400: more fat, less hormone G.","hl":"800 units in lean people, 400 in obese people with no other condition"},{"text":"Gaining weight lowers hormone G.","strike":["increase as the person gained weight, then decrease as the person lost weight.","increase as the person gained weight and stay high as the person lost weight."]},{"text":"Losing weight raises it back. Answer: decrease, then increase.","strike":["decrease as the person gained weight and stay low as the person lost weight."]}],"trap":"stay low: the level follows current stores, so it reverses."},"teach":[{"text":"Use the data's direction, then run it backward for weight loss.","marks":[{"t":"400 in obese people","note":"fat ↑, G ↓"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An inverse link flips both ways."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-130a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"immune branch and cell type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:130#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:130"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P4","passage":"Bacterium Bx causes a lung infection by making toxin TX. TX is an A+B exotoxin. The A part, subunit T1, is an enzyme that adds ADP-ribose to a G protein inside host cells. The B part holds subunits T2, T3, T4 and T5 in a 1:1:2:1 ratio and binds the host cell surface. After B binds, T1 enters the cytoplasm. The TX operon turns on only when activator RgA binds its promoter, and RgA binds only after kinase RgS phosphorylates its aspartate at position 61. Drug Y treats Bx by binding the 23S rRNA of the large ribosomal subunit and blocking tRNA movement between sites.","q":"Based on the passage, which subunit of TX is LEAST suitable for generation of a vaccine against Bx?","choices":["T2","T1","T4","T5"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"B subunits bind cell surface","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Antibodies work outside cells: target the binding B part, not the enzyme that goes inside."},"exp":"**Answer:** T1\n\n**Incorrect: T2** T2 is in the B part. Antibodies to it can block toxin binding.\n\n**Correct: T1** T1 is the toxic enzyme that acts inside the cell. Antibodies to it would not stop the toxin from binding and entering.\n\n**Incorrect: T4** T4 is in the B part and has two copies, a good antibody target.\n\n**Incorrect: T5** T5 is in the B part. Antibodies to it can block binding.","how":{"ask":"Which subunit is the worst vaccine target?","know":"Block the toxin at binding: aim at B subunits.","steps":[{"text":"The B part binds the cell surface.","hl":"binds the host cell surface"},{"text":"T2, T4, T5 are all B subunits; antibodies there block entry.","strike":["T2","T4","T5"]},{"text":"T1 enters the cytoplasm and acts inside. Answer: T1.","hl":"T1 enters the cytoplasm"}],"trap":"T4: it looks special (2 copies), but that makes it a better target, not worse."},"teach":[{"text":"A+B toxin: B binds, A acts inside.","marks":[{"t":"binds the host cell surface","note":"B part"},{"t":"T1 enters the cytoplasm","note":"A part inside"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Vaccines aim at the part exposed outside the cell."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-131a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:131#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:131"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P4","passage":"Bacterium Bx causes a lung infection by making toxin TX. TX is an A+B exotoxin. The A part, subunit T1, is an enzyme that adds ADP-ribose to a G protein inside host cells. The B part holds subunits T2, T3, T4 and T5 in a 1:1:2:1 ratio and binds the host cell surface. After B binds, T1 enters the cytoplasm. The TX operon turns on only when activator RgA binds its promoter, and RgA binds only after kinase RgS phosphorylates its aspartate at position 61. Drug Y treats Bx by binding the 23S rRNA of the large ribosomal subunit and blocking tRNA movement between sites.","q":"Drug Y interferes with protein synthesis by binding to which ribosomal subunit?","choices":["30S","50S","70S","80S"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"30S is the small subunit","c":[0]},{"f":"70S/80S are whole ribosomes","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Bacteria: 30S + 50S = 70S. 23S rRNA sits in the 50S."},"exp":"**Answer:** 50S\n\n**Incorrect: 30S** 30S is the bacterial small subunit; it holds 16S rRNA.\n\n**Correct: 50S** The drug binds 23S rRNA in the large subunit. In bacteria the large subunit is 50S.\n\n**Incorrect: 70S** 70S is the whole bacterial ribosome, not a subunit.\n\n**Incorrect: 80S** 80S is the whole eukaryotic ribosome.","how":{"ask":"What is the bacterial large subunit?","know":"Bacteria: 30S small, 50S large, 70S whole.","steps":[{"text":"Drug Y binds the large subunit's 23S rRNA.","hl":"binding the 23S rRNA of the large ribosomal subunit"},{"text":"30S is small. 70S and 80S are whole ribosomes.","strike":["30S","70S","80S"]},{"text":"Answer: 50S."}],"trap":"70S: the whole ribosome, not a subunit."},"teach":[{"text":"S values do not add simply: 30 + 50 = 70.","marks":[{"t":"large ribosomal subunit","note":"50S"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Bacteria 30S/50S/70S; eukaryotes 40S/60S/80S."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-132a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:132#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:132"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P4","passage":"Bacterium Bx causes a lung infection by making toxin TX. TX is an A+B exotoxin. The A part, subunit T1, is an enzyme that adds ADP-ribose to a G protein inside host cells. The B part holds subunits T2, T3, T4 and T5 in a 1:1:2:1 ratio and binds the host cell surface. After B binds, T1 enters the cytoplasm. The TX operon turns on only when activator RgA binds its promoter, and RgA binds only after kinase RgS phosphorylates its aspartate at position 61. Drug Y treats Bx by binding the 23S rRNA of the large ribosomal subunit and blocking tRNA movement between sites.","q":"Substituting the residue at position 61 of RgA with which amino acid will LEAST likely prevent the expression of the TX operon?","choices":["Glycine","Glutamine","Valine","Glutamate"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"No negative charge, no mimic","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phospho-Asp is negative; glutamate's negative side chain mimics it best."},"exp":"**Answer:** Glutamate\n\n**Incorrect: Glycine** Glycine has no side chain and no charge, so it cannot mimic a phosphate.\n\n**Incorrect: Glutamine** Glutamine is neutral; its amide carries no negative charge.\n\n**Incorrect: Valine** Valine is nonpolar and uncharged.\n\n**Correct: Glutamate** Glutamate is acidic and negative like phospho-aspartate. It best keeps RgA in its active, DNA-binding form.","how":{"ask":"Which swap keeps RgA active?","know":"Asp/Glu mimic a phosphorylated residue (negative charge).","steps":[{"text":"Phosphorylated Asp 61 is needed for RgA to bind.","hl":"phosphorylates its aspartate at position 61"},{"text":"Glycine, glutamine and valine lack a negative charge.","strike":["Glycine","Glutamine","Valine"]},{"text":"Answer: Glutamate."}],"trap":"Glutamine: looks like glutamate, but its amide is neutral."},"teach":[{"text":"LEAST likely to block = most likely to keep it working.","marks":[{"t":"aspartate at position 61","note":"needs negative"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Phosphomimetic = Asp or Glu."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-133a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:133#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:133"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P4","passage":"Bacterium Bx causes a lung infection by making toxin TX. TX is an A+B exotoxin. The A part, subunit T1, is an enzyme that adds ADP-ribose to a G protein inside host cells. The B part holds subunits T2, T3, T4 and T5 in a 1:1:2:1 ratio and binds the host cell surface. After B binds, T1 enters the cytoplasm. The TX operon turns on only when activator RgA binds its promoter, and RgA binds only after kinase RgS phosphorylates its aspartate at position 61. Drug Y treats Bx by binding the 23S rRNA of the large ribosomal subunit and blocking tRNA movement between sites.","q":"Binding of drug Y to the large subunit of the bacterial ribosome most likely blocks the transfer of the tRNA bound at:","choices":["the E site to the P site of the ribosome.","the A site to the P site of the ribosome.","the E site to the A site of the ribosome.","the P site to the A site of the ribosome."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"tRNAs move A → P → E","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"tRNA order is A, P, E; the only forward move listed is A to P."},"exp":"**Answer:** the A site to the P site of the ribosome.\n\n**Incorrect: the E site to the P site of the ribosome.** The E site is the exit; tRNA leaves from there, it does not move back.\n\n**Correct: the A site to the P site of the ribosome.** During translocation, the tRNA in the A site moves to the P site. Blocking tRNA movement stops this step.\n\n**Incorrect: the E site to the A site of the ribosome.** tRNA never moves from exit back to entry.\n\n**Incorrect: the P site to the A site of the ribosome.** This is backward; tRNAs move A → P.","how":{"ask":"Which tRNA move does the drug block?","know":"tRNA enters A, moves to P, exits from E.","steps":[{"text":"Drug Y blocks tRNA movement between sites.","hl":"blocking tRNA movement between sites"},{"text":"All moves that go backward are wrong.","strike":["the E site to the P site of the ribosome.","the E site to the A site of the ribosome.","the P site to the A site of the ribosome."]},{"text":"Answer: the A site to the P site of the ribosome."}],"trap":"P to A: the peptide moves P→A, but the tRNA moves A→P."},"teach":[{"text":"Say it in order: A (arrive), P (peptide), E (exit).","marks":[{"t":"tRNA movement","note":"translocation"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** tRNAs only move forward: A → P → E."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-134a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:134#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:134"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P5","passage":"Muscle cells can sense stretch and turn it into chemical signals. Transcription factor MDX drives muscle differentiation by turning on gene Ckn2. Ckn2 makes the cell leave the cell cycle for good and commit to becoming muscle. MDX has a nuclear localization sequence, KRSDLAGTVQ, at its C-terminus. The cytokine IL-x binds its receptor and turns on NOS, which makes NO; L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS. In myoblasts, IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%, while stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal. Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","q":"Which compound is predicted to mimic the effect of exercise in undifferentiated muscle cells in vivo?","choices":["An MDX-specific siRNA","An activator of NOS expression","A blocker of IL-x signaling","An inhibitor of Ckn2 expression"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Stretch protects MDX from IL-x","c":[1]},{"f":"Lowering MDX or Ckn2 blocks differentiation","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stretch undoes IL-x, so anything that blocks IL-x mimics stretch."},"exp":"**Answer:** A blocker of IL-x signaling\n\n**Incorrect: An MDX-specific siRNA** This lowers MDX, the opposite of stretch.\n\n**Incorrect: An activator of NOS expression** More NOS copies IL-x's harmful effect.\n\n**Correct: A blocker of IL-x signaling** Stretch keeps MDX normal even with IL-x. Blocking IL-x does the same thing.\n\n**Incorrect: An inhibitor of Ckn2 expression** Ckn2 drives differentiation; blocking it works against exercise.","how":{"ask":"What does exercise (stretch) do here?","know":"A mimic gives the same net effect.","steps":[{"text":"Stretch brings MDX back despite IL-x.","hl":"stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal"},{"text":"siRNA and Ckn2 block lower differentiation.","strike":["An MDX-specific siRNA","An inhibitor of Ckn2 expression"]},{"text":"NOS is IL-x's tool. Answer: A blocker of IL-x signaling.","strike":["An activator of NOS expression"]}],"trap":"An activator of NOS: NOS is downstream of IL-x, so it lowers MDX."},"teach":[{"text":"Draw the arrows: IL-x → NOS → MDX down. Stretch blocks this.","marks":[{"t":"turns on NOS, which makes NO","note":"IL-x pathway"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To mimic a blocker, block the same pathway."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-135a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:135#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:135"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P5","passage":"Muscle cells can sense stretch and turn it into chemical signals. Transcription factor MDX drives muscle differentiation by turning on gene Ckn2. Ckn2 makes the cell leave the cell cycle for good and commit to becoming muscle. MDX has a nuclear localization sequence, KRSDLAGTVQ, at its C-terminus. The cytokine IL-x binds its receptor and turns on NOS, which makes NO; L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS. In myoblasts, IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%, while stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal. Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","q":"Which Lineweaver–Burk plot best illustrates the kinetics of NOS in the presence (+I) or absence (–I) of L-NAME?","choices":["Lines meet on the x-axis","Lines meet on the y-axis","Parallel lines","Lines meet left of the y-axis"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Competitive: same Vmax, same y-intercept","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Competitive keeps Vmax, so both lines share the y-intercept."},"exp":"**Answer:** Lines meet on the y-axis\n\n**Incorrect: Lines meet on the x-axis** That is pure noncompetitive: Km same, Vmax down.\n\n**Correct: Lines meet on the y-axis** A competitive inhibitor raises Km but not Vmax. Same 1/Vmax means the lines cross on the y-axis.\n\n**Incorrect: Parallel lines** That is uncompetitive inhibition.\n\n**Incorrect: Lines meet left of the y-axis** That is mixed inhibition.","how":{"ask":"What LB pattern fits a competitive inhibitor?","know":"Competitive: y-intercept (1/Vmax) unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"L-NAME is competitive.","hl":"L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS"},{"text":"x-axis = noncompetitive, parallel = uncompetitive, left = mixed.","strike":["Lines meet on the x-axis","Parallel lines","Lines meet left of the y-axis"]},{"text":"Answer: Lines meet on the y-axis."}],"trap":"Lines meet on the x-axis: that is noncompetitive, the common mix-up."},"teach":[{"text":"Competitive = Y-axis cross. Noncompetitive = X-axis cross.","marks":[{"t":"competitive inhibitor","note":"Vmax same"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Competitive lines share the y-intercept."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-136a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:136#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:136"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P5","passage":"Muscle cells can sense stretch and turn it into chemical signals. Transcription factor MDX drives muscle differentiation by turning on gene Ckn2. Ckn2 makes the cell leave the cell cycle for good and commit to becoming muscle. MDX has a nuclear localization sequence, KRSDLAGTVQ, at its C-terminus. The cytokine IL-x binds its receptor and turns on NOS, which makes NO; L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS. In myoblasts, IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%, while stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal. Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","q":"Which experiment can be used to investigate the transcriptional regulation of Ckn2?","choices":["Measuring Ckn2 protein levels by Southern blotting","Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by Western blotting","Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by RT-qPCR","Measuring Ckn2 protein levels by RT-qPCR"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Transcription → measure mRNA","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Western detects protein, not mRNA","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Transcription makes mRNA, so measure mRNA with an RNA method."},"exp":"**Answer:** Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by RT-qPCR\n\n**Incorrect: Measuring Ckn2 protein levels by Southern blotting** Southern blots detect DNA, and protein is not the transcription readout.\n\n**Incorrect: Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by Western blotting** Western blots detect proteins, not mRNA.\n\n**Correct: Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by RT-qPCR** Transcription makes mRNA. RT-qPCR turns mRNA into cDNA and counts it.\n\n**Incorrect: Measuring Ckn2 protein levels by RT-qPCR** RT-qPCR measures RNA, not protein.","how":{"ask":"How do you measure transcription?","know":"Transcription → mRNA. RT-qPCR/Northern measure mRNA.","steps":[{"text":"MDX controls Ckn2 at the gene level.","hl":"turning on gene Ckn2"},{"text":"Protein levels do not measure transcription.","strike":["Measuring Ckn2 protein levels by Southern blotting","Measuring Ckn2 protein levels by RT-qPCR"]},{"text":"Western is for protein. Answer: Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by RT-qPCR.","strike":["Measuring Ckn2 mRNA levels by Western blotting"]}],"trap":"mRNA by Western: right molecule, wrong method."},"teach":[{"text":"Check both: right molecule, and a method that detects it.","marks":[{"t":"gene Ckn2","note":"transcription target"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SNoW DRoP: Southern-DNA, Northern-RNA, Western-Protein."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-137a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:137#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:137"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P5","passage":"Muscle cells can sense stretch and turn it into chemical signals. Transcription factor MDX drives muscle differentiation by turning on gene Ckn2. Ckn2 makes the cell leave the cell cycle for good and commit to becoming muscle. MDX has a nuclear localization sequence, KRSDLAGTVQ, at its C-terminus. The cytokine IL-x binds its receptor and turns on NOS, which makes NO; L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS. In myoblasts, IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%, while stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal. Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","q":"If cleaved, what is the net charge of the nuclear localization sequence described in the passage at physiological pH?","choices":["–1","0","+1","+2"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"K, R, N-term: +3","c":[3]},{"f":"D, C-term: −2","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count +: K, R, H*, N-term. Count −: D, E, C-term. Subtract."},"exp":"**Answer:** +1\n\n**Incorrect: –1** That ignores the positive N-terminus or a basic residue.\n\n**Incorrect: 0** That misses one positive group.\n\n**Correct: +1** Positives: N-terminus, K, R = +3. Negatives: D and C-terminus = −2. Net = +1.\n\n**Incorrect: +2** That forgets the negative C-terminus.","how":{"ask":"Net charge of free KRSDLAGTVQ at pH 7.4?","know":"Free peptide: N-term +1, C-term −1, plus side chains.","steps":[{"text":"Read the sequence.","hl":"KRSDLAGTVQ"},{"text":"Positives: N-term, K, R = +3. Not +2 total, so +2 needs the C-term ignored.","strike":["+2"]},{"text":"Negatives: D, C-term = −2. Net +1. Answer: +1.","strike":["–1","0"]}],"trap":"+2: forgetting the free C-terminal carboxylate once cleaved."},"teach":[{"text":"If cleaved means both ends are free and charged.","marks":[{"t":"KRSDLAGTVQ","note":"K,R + ; D −"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Net = (N-term + K + R) − (C-term + D + E)."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-138a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:138#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:138"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P5","passage":"Muscle cells can sense stretch and turn it into chemical signals. Transcription factor MDX drives muscle differentiation by turning on gene Ckn2. Ckn2 makes the cell leave the cell cycle for good and commit to becoming muscle. MDX has a nuclear localization sequence, KRSDLAGTVQ, at its C-terminus. The cytokine IL-x binds its receptor and turns on NOS, which makes NO; L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS. In myoblasts, IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%, while stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal. Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","q":"In myoblast cells:","choices":["stretch reverses the effects of IL-x.","NOS activity reverses the effects of IL-x.","IL-x reverses the effects of L-NAME.","NOS activity is required for MDX expression."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"NOS carries out IL-x's effect","c":[1]},{"f":"L-NAME alone did nothing","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Test each claim against one data row."},"exp":"**Answer:** stretch reverses the effects of IL-x.\n\n**Correct: stretch reverses the effects of IL-x.** IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%. Adding stretch brought it back to normal.\n\n**Incorrect: NOS activity reverses the effects of IL-x.** NOS carries out IL-x's effect; blocking NOS is what reverses it.\n\n**Incorrect: IL-x reverses the effects of L-NAME.** L-NAME alone had no effect to reverse.\n\n**Incorrect: NOS activity is required for MDX expression.** Blocking NOS with L-NAME alone did not lower MDX.","how":{"ask":"Which claim do the data support?","know":"A claim needs a matching data row.","steps":[{"text":"IL-x lowers MDX; stretch restores it.","hl":"stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal"},{"text":"Blocking NOS undoes IL-x, so NOS drives IL-x's effect.","strike":["NOS activity reverses the effects of IL-x."]},{"text":"L-NAME alone did nothing. Answer: stretch reverses the effects of IL-x.","hl":"Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","strike":["IL-x reverses the effects of L-NAME.","NOS activity is required for MDX expression."]}],"trap":"NOS activity is required: L-NAME alone left MDX normal, so no."},"teach":[{"text":"Use the alone controls to test required claims.","marks":[{"t":"L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA","note":"NOS not required"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Restores to normal = reverses the effect."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-139a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:139#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:139"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P5","passage":"Muscle cells can sense stretch and turn it into chemical signals. Transcription factor MDX drives muscle differentiation by turning on gene Ckn2. Ckn2 makes the cell leave the cell cycle for good and commit to becoming muscle. MDX has a nuclear localization sequence, KRSDLAGTVQ, at its C-terminus. The cytokine IL-x binds its receptor and turns on NOS, which makes NO; L-NAME is a competitive inhibitor of NOS. In myoblasts, IL-x cut MDX mRNA by 60%, while stretch or L-NAME added with IL-x brought MDX mRNA back to normal. Stretch alone or L-NAME alone did not change MDX mRNA.","q":"The nuclear localization sequence of MDX carries how many hydroxyl-containing phosphorylation site(s)?","choices":["None","One","Two","Three"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"S and T both count","c":[0,1]},{"f":"No Y in sequence","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count S, T and Y letters."},"exp":"**Answer:** Two\n\n**Incorrect: None** S and T both have OH groups.\n\n**Incorrect: One** That counts only serine and misses threonine (T).\n\n**Correct: Two** KRSDLAGTVQ has one S and one T. Both have side-chain OH groups.\n\n**Incorrect: Three** There is no Y, the third OH residue.","how":{"ask":"How many S, T, Y in the sequence?","know":"Hydroxyl phospho sites: Ser (S), Thr (T), Tyr (Y).","steps":[{"text":"Scan the sequence.","hl":"KRSDLAGTVQ"},{"text":"Find S (position 3) and T (position 8).","strike":["None","One"]},{"text":"No Y. Answer: Two.","strike":["Three"]}],"trap":"One: missing T, threonine."},"teach":[{"text":"Q is glutamine, not an OH residue.","marks":[{"t":"KRSDLAGTVQ","note":"S and T"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** OH sites = S, T, Y only."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-140a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:140#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:140"],"q":"Which technique separates proteins based only on their size?","choices":["Ion exchange chromatography","Size-exclusion chromatography","Isoelectric focusing","Native PAGE"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Ion exchange and IEF use charge","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Native PAGE: size, shape, charge","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name each method's basis; only gel filtration uses size alone."},"exp":"**Answer:** Size-exclusion chromatography\n\n**Incorrect: Ion exchange chromatography** It separates by charge.\n\n**Correct: Size-exclusion chromatography** Also called gel filtration. Small proteins enter the beads and come out later; big ones pass straight through.\n\n**Incorrect: Isoelectric focusing** It separates by pI, a charge property.\n\n**Incorrect: Native PAGE** It depends on size, shape and charge together.","how":{"ask":"Which method uses size alone?","know":"Gel filtration = size only.","steps":[{"text":"Match each method to what it sorts by."},{"text":"Ion exchange and IEF sort by charge. Native PAGE mixes in charge.","strike":["Ion exchange chromatography","Isoelectric focusing","Native PAGE"]},{"text":"Answer: Size-exclusion chromatography."}],"trap":"Native PAGE: size matters, but charge does too."},"teach":[{"text":"Native PAGE keeps native charge; SDS-PAGE masks it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Size alone = gel filtration (or SDS-PAGE)."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-141a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"order peptide fragments","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe4-bb:141#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:141"],"q":"A peptide of twelve amino acids was partially hydrolyzed. Four different tetrapeptides were isolated. None of the tetrapeptides share a common amino acid. Based on the data, what is the total number of possible structures for the full-length peptide?","choices":["4","12","24","81"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Arrange 4 blocks: 4!","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Treat each piece as a block: n blocks give n! orders."},"exp":"**Answer:** 24\n\n**Incorrect: 4** That is the number of pieces, not the number of orders.\n\n**Incorrect: 12** That is the number of residues.\n\n**Correct: 24** The four tetrapeptides are fixed blocks. They can be ordered 4! = 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24 ways.\n\n**Incorrect: 81** That is 3⁴, not a block ordering.","how":{"ask":"How many orders of 4 fixed blocks?","know":"n distinct blocks → n! orders.","steps":[{"text":"Four blocks, none overlapping."},{"text":"4 and 12 just restate counts. 81 is the wrong formula.","strike":["4","12","81"]},{"text":"4! = 24. Answer: 24."}],"trap":"4: counting the pieces, not their orders."},"teach":[{"text":"Blocks keep their inside order; only block order changes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Orders of n pieces = n!."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-142a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mendelian cross probability","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:142#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:142"],"q":"A man with sickle cell disease considers having children with an asymptomatic woman who is heterozygous for the sickle cell allele. What is the probability of having a child with sickle cell disease?","choices":["25%","50%","67%","100%"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"aa × Aa → half aa","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"aa × Aa: every child gets a; half get the mom's a."},"exp":"**Answer:** 50%\n\n**Incorrect: 25%** That is a carrier × carrier cross (Aa × Aa).\n\n**Correct: 50%** The man is aa and gives a to every child. The woman is Aa, so half the children get a from her: aa.\n\n**Incorrect: 67%** That is a conditional ratio from Aa × Aa, not this cross.\n\n**Incorrect: 100%** Only if both parents were aa.","how":{"ask":"Chance of aa from aa × Aa?","know":"Asymptomatic carrier = recessive trait.","steps":[{"text":"Man = aa. Woman = Aa."},{"text":"Punnett: Aa, Aa, aa, aa = 2 of 4.","strike":["25%","100%"]},{"text":"67% is a different setup. Answer: 50%.","strike":["67%"]}],"trap":"25%: that is for two carriers."},"teach":[{"text":"First set each parent's genotype from their phenotype."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** aa × Aa → 50% affected."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-143a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"x inactivation and dosage","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["pe4-bb:143#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:143"],"q":"Which of the following statements best applies to the Barr body in the cells of a female cat?","choices":["It is not copied in S phase.","It is less condensed than the active X chromosome.","It is one of the last chromosomes to replicate.","It is highly transcribed."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Barr body = condensed, silent","c":[1,3]},{"f":"It still replicates each cycle","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Barr body = tight heterochromatin: silent and late to replicate."},"exp":"**Answer:** It is one of the last chromosomes to replicate.\n\n**Incorrect: It is not copied in S phase.** Both X chromosomes are copied every cycle.\n\n**Incorrect: It is less condensed than the active X chromosome.** It is more condensed (heterochromatin).\n\n**Correct: It is one of the last chromosomes to replicate.** Tightly packed heterochromatin replicates late in S phase.\n\n**Incorrect: It is highly transcribed.** It is mostly silent.","how":{"ask":"What is true of the inactive X?","know":"Heterochromatin: condensed, silent, late-replicating.","steps":[{"text":"The Barr body is the inactive X: heterochromatin."},{"text":"Not loose, not active, and it is still copied.","strike":["It is less condensed than the active X chromosome.","It is highly transcribed.","It is not copied in S phase."]},{"text":"Answer: It is one of the last chromosomes to replicate."}],"trap":"Not copied: inactive does not mean skipped in S phase."},"teach":[{"text":"Packed DNA is hard to reach, so it replicates late."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Heterochromatin = condensed, silent, late."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-144a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"le châtelier shift","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:144#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:144"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P6","passage":"Bacterium Sx causes lung infections. Its enzyme xanthine oxidase makes hydrogen peroxide in Reaction 1: xanthine + O2 + H2O ⇌ urate + H2O2. H2O2 can cause double-strand breaks (DSBs) in host DNA; DSB sites are marked by γH2AX and 53BP1. In Experiment 1, three Sx strains grew in medium for 6 hours and were filtered out, and lung cells sat in this conditioned medium for 6 hours. About 40% of these cells showed DSB markers, compared with 5% of control cells. In Experiment 2, lung cells in conditioned medium had 30% apoptosis without catalase, which breaks down H2O2, and 8% apoptosis with catalase.","q":"Which of the following explains why increasing the initial concentration of xanthine will cause an increase in the equilibrium concentration of urate?","choices":["Reaction rate","Heat absorbed by the reaction","Le Châtelier's principle","The decrease in free energy"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Adding reactant shifts equilibrium right","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Add reactant → system shifts toward products: Le Châtelier."},"exp":"**Answer:** Le Châtelier's principle\n\n**Incorrect: Reaction rate** Rate tells how fast equilibrium is reached, not where it lies.\n\n**Incorrect: Heat absorbed by the reaction** Heat matters for temperature changes, not added reactant.\n\n**Correct: Le Châtelier's principle** Adding xanthine stresses the system. It shifts right to use it up, making more urate.\n\n**Incorrect: The decrease in free energy** ΔG° sets K; it does not change when you add reactant.","how":{"ask":"Why does more reactant give more product?","know":"Le Châtelier: system offsets an added stress.","steps":[{"text":"Xanthine is a reactant.","hl":"xanthine + O2 + H2O ⇌ urate + H2O2"},{"text":"Rate, heat and ΔG do not explain a concentration shift.","strike":["Reaction rate","Heat absorbed by the reaction","The decrease in free energy"]},{"text":"Answer: Le Châtelier's principle."}],"trap":"The decrease in free energy: ΔG° fixes K, it does not shift with concentration."},"teach":[{"text":"K stays fixed; concentrations rearrange to match it.","marks":[{"t":"⇌","note":"equilibrium"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Concentration change at equilibrium = Le Châtelier."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-145a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"le châtelier shift","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:145#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:145"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P6","passage":"Bacterium Sx causes lung infections. Its enzyme xanthine oxidase makes hydrogen peroxide in Reaction 1: xanthine + O2 + H2O ⇌ urate + H2O2. H2O2 can cause double-strand breaks (DSBs) in host DNA; DSB sites are marked by γH2AX and 53BP1. In Experiment 1, three Sx strains grew in medium for 6 hours and were filtered out, and lung cells sat in this conditioned medium for 6 hours. About 40% of these cells showed DSB markers, compared with 5% of control cells. In Experiment 2, lung cells in conditioned medium had 30% apoptosis without catalase, which breaks down H2O2, and 8% apoptosis with catalase.","q":"What is the equilibrium constant expression for Reaction 1?","choices":["[Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2][H2O]","[Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2]","[Xanthine][O2]/[Urate][H2O2]","[Xanthine][O2][H2O]/[Urate][H2O2]"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Products over reactants","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Leave out solvent water","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Products on top, reactants below, drop the solvent water."},"exp":"**Answer:** [Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2]\n\n**Incorrect: [Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2][H2O]** Water is the solvent; its activity is 1, so it is left out.\n\n**Correct: [Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2]** K is products over reactants. Water in a water solution is left out.\n\n**Incorrect: [Xanthine][O2]/[Urate][H2O2]** This is upside down.\n\n**Incorrect: [Xanthine][O2][H2O]/[Urate][H2O2]** Upside down and includes water.","how":{"ask":"Write K for Reaction 1.","know":"K = [products]/[reactants]; skip pure liquids and solvent.","steps":[{"text":"Find products and reactants.","hl":"xanthine + O2 + H2O ⇌ urate + H2O2"},{"text":"Products go on top.","strike":["[Xanthine][O2]/[Urate][H2O2]","[Xanthine][O2][H2O]/[Urate][H2O2]"]},{"text":"Drop water. Answer: [Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2].","strike":["[Urate][H2O2]/[Xanthine][O2][H2O]"]}],"trap":"Keeping [H2O]: solvent water is not in K."},"teach":[{"text":"Water in the equation does not mean water in K.","marks":[{"t":"H2O ⇌","note":"solvent, omit"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** K omits solvent water and pure solids."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-146a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:146#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:146"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P6","passage":"Bacterium Sx causes lung infections. Its enzyme xanthine oxidase makes hydrogen peroxide in Reaction 1: xanthine + O2 + H2O ⇌ urate + H2O2. H2O2 can cause double-strand breaks (DSBs) in host DNA; DSB sites are marked by γH2AX and 53BP1. In Experiment 1, three Sx strains grew in medium for 6 hours and were filtered out, and lung cells sat in this conditioned medium for 6 hours. About 40% of these cells showed DSB markers, compared with 5% of control cells. In Experiment 2, lung cells in conditioned medium had 30% apoptosis without catalase, which breaks down H2O2, and 8% apoptosis with catalase.","q":"The negative control used in Experiment 1 was most likely:","choices":["fresh medium without lung cells.","conditioned medium with lung cells.","fresh medium with lung cells.","conditioned medium without lung cells."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Need cells to measure DSBs","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Conditioned with cells is the test","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Negative control = same setup minus the one tested factor."},"exp":"**Answer:** fresh medium with lung cells.\n\n**Incorrect: fresh medium without lung cells.** With no cells there are no DSBs to count.\n\n**Incorrect: conditioned medium with lung cells.** That is the test group, not the control.\n\n**Correct: fresh medium with lung cells.** It keeps the cells but removes the bacterial factors. Any DSBs then are background.\n\n**Incorrect: conditioned medium without lung cells.** Again, no cells to measure.","how":{"ask":"What is the test condition minus the tested factor?","know":"Negative control: change only the variable.","steps":[{"text":"The tested factor is conditioned medium; cells are measured.","hl":"lung cells sat in this conditioned medium for 6 hours"},{"text":"No cells means nothing to measure.","strike":["fresh medium without lung cells.","conditioned medium without lung cells."]},{"text":"Conditioned with cells is the test. Answer: fresh medium with lung cells.","strike":["conditioned medium with lung cells."]}],"trap":"conditioned medium with lung cells: that is the experimental group."},"teach":[{"text":"Keep what you measure (cells); remove what you test (bacterial factors).","marks":[{"t":"compared with 5% of control cells","note":"background"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Negative control = test setup minus one variable."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-147a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:147#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:147"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P6","passage":"Bacterium Sx causes lung infections. Its enzyme xanthine oxidase makes hydrogen peroxide in Reaction 1: xanthine + O2 + H2O ⇌ urate + H2O2. H2O2 can cause double-strand breaks (DSBs) in host DNA; DSB sites are marked by γH2AX and 53BP1. In Experiment 1, three Sx strains grew in medium for 6 hours and were filtered out, and lung cells sat in this conditioned medium for 6 hours. About 40% of these cells showed DSB markers, compared with 5% of control cells. In Experiment 2, lung cells in conditioned medium had 30% apoptosis without catalase, which breaks down H2O2, and 8% apoptosis with catalase.","q":"Which experiment would best link the results of Experiments 1 and 2 to show that Sx can cause apoptosis of host cells by contributing to DSBs?","choices":["Measure levels of H2O2 made by each strain.","Test whether each strain secretes other toxins.","Test whether other Sx strains make H2O2.","Count DSBs in lung cells grown with each live strain."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"H2O2 is the shared link","c":[1,2]},{"f":"Exp 1 already counted DSBs","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the factor in both experiments (H2O2) and measure it."},"exp":"**Answer:** Measure levels of H2O2 made by each strain.\n\n**Correct: Measure levels of H2O2 made by each strain.** Experiment 2 ties H2O2 to apoptosis; Experiment 1 ties the strains to DSBs. Measuring each strain's H2O2 connects the two.\n\n**Incorrect: Test whether each strain secretes other toxins.** Other toxins do not link H2O2 to DSBs.\n\n**Incorrect: Test whether other Sx strains make H2O2.** New strains do not connect these two experiments.\n\n**Incorrect: Count DSBs in lung cells grown with each live strain.** Experiment 1 already showed DSBs; this adds no link to H2O2.","how":{"ask":"What ties DSBs (Exp 1) to apoptosis (Exp 2)?","know":"Link experiments through their shared variable.","steps":[{"text":"Exp 2: removing H2O2 lowers apoptosis.","hl":"30% apoptosis without catalase, which breaks down H2O2, and 8% apoptosis with catalase"},{"text":"Other toxins and other strains drift off topic.","strike":["Test whether each strain secretes other toxins.","Test whether other Sx strains make H2O2."]},{"text":"DSBs were already counted. Answer: Measure levels of H2O2 made by each strain.","strike":["Count DSBs in lung cells grown with each live strain."]}],"trap":"Count DSBs: repeats Exp 1 and never touches H2O2."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask: what does Exp 1 lack that Exp 2 has?","marks":[{"t":"About 40% of these cells showed DSB markers","note":"Exp 1 result"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To link two results, measure the variable they share."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-148a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:148#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:148"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P7","passage":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gut hormone released from the small intestine when food arrives. CCK empties bile from the gallbladder and enzyme-rich pancreatic juice into the duodenum. CCK also signals fullness by stimulating vagal sensory fibers that tell the brain to stop eating. Giving extra CCK makes animals eat smaller, shorter meals. Researchers tested people with binge-eating disorder, who eat very large meals. After a meal, CCK rose from 1 to 6 pmol/L in controls but only from 1 to 2 pmol/L in binge eaters.","q":"The information in the passage best supports the hypothesis that which of the following defects accounts for excessive eating by people with binge-eating disorder?","choices":["Fewer CCK-sensitive vagal afferent fibers","Decreased release of CCK in response to food","Excess bile release in response to CCK","Hypersensitivity to the presence of CCK"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Data measured CCK release only","c":[0,2]},{"f":"More sensitivity means less eating","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the choice the data actually measured: lower CCK rise."},"exp":"**Answer:** Decreased release of CCK in response to food\n\n**Incorrect: Fewer CCK-sensitive vagal afferent fibers** Possible, but the study measured CCK levels, not nerves.\n\n**Correct: Decreased release of CCK in response to food** Binge eaters' CCK rose only to 2 vs 6 pmol/L. Less CCK means a weaker fullness signal.\n\n**Incorrect: Excess bile release in response to CCK** Bile does not control meal size.\n\n**Incorrect: Hypersensitivity to the presence of CCK** That would cause less eating, not more.","how":{"ask":"What do the data show about binge eaters?","know":"CCK = fullness signal; less CCK → bigger meals.","steps":[{"text":"Binge eaters release much less CCK.","hl":"only from 1 to 2 pmol/L in binge eaters"},{"text":"Nerves and bile were not measured.","strike":["Fewer CCK-sensitive vagal afferent fibers","Excess bile release in response to CCK"]},{"text":"Hypersensitivity would cut eating. Answer: Decreased release of CCK in response to food.","strike":["Hypersensitivity to the presence of CCK"]}],"trap":"Fewer vagal fibers: plausible, but not supported by these data."},"teach":[{"text":"Best supported = matches what was measured.","marks":[{"t":"1 to 6 pmol/L in controls","note":"normal rise"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Choose the hypothesis the data directly measured."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-149a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:149#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:149"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P7","passage":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gut hormone released from the small intestine when food arrives. CCK empties bile from the gallbladder and enzyme-rich pancreatic juice into the duodenum. CCK also signals fullness by stimulating vagal sensory fibers that tell the brain to stop eating. Giving extra CCK makes animals eat smaller, shorter meals. Researchers tested people with binge-eating disorder, who eat very large meals. After a meal, CCK rose from 1 to 6 pmol/L in controls but only from 1 to 2 pmol/L in binge eaters.","q":"Which of the following events does the release of CCK likely trigger?","choices":["Relaxation of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter","Relaxation of gallbladder wall muscle and contraction of the hepatopancreatic sphincter","Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and contraction of the hepatopancreatic sphincter","Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Squeeze bag to empty it","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Open the exit valve","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To empty bile: squeeze the bag, open the door."},"exp":"**Answer:** Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter\n\n**Incorrect: Relaxation of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter** A relaxed gallbladder cannot push bile out.\n\n**Incorrect: Relaxation of gallbladder wall muscle and contraction of the hepatopancreatic sphincter** That stores bile; it is the opposite of CCK.\n\n**Incorrect: Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and contraction of the hepatopancreatic sphincter** A closed sphincter blocks bile flow.\n\n**Correct: Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter** CCK empties bile into the duodenum. That needs the gallbladder to squeeze and the sphincter to open.","how":{"ask":"How does CCK move bile into the duodenum?","know":"Empty a sac = contract sac + relax outlet.","steps":[{"text":"CCK empties the gallbladder.","hl":"CCK empties bile from the gallbladder"},{"text":"The gallbladder must contract.","strike":["Relaxation of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter","Relaxation of gallbladder wall muscle and contraction of the hepatopancreatic sphincter"]},{"text":"The sphincter must open. Answer: Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and relaxation of the hepatopancreatic sphincter.","strike":["Contraction of gallbladder wall muscle and contraction of the hepatopancreatic sphincter"]}],"trap":"Contract both: sounds active, but a closed sphincter traps bile."},"teach":[{"text":"Sphincters relax to let things pass.","marks":[{"t":"into the duodenum","note":"bile exits"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Emptying = squeeze the wall, open the sphincter."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-150a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:150#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:150"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P7","passage":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gut hormone released from the small intestine when food arrives. CCK empties bile from the gallbladder and enzyme-rich pancreatic juice into the duodenum. CCK also signals fullness by stimulating vagal sensory fibers that tell the brain to stop eating. Giving extra CCK makes animals eat smaller, shorter meals. Researchers tested people with binge-eating disorder, who eat very large meals. After a meal, CCK rose from 1 to 6 pmol/L in controls but only from 1 to 2 pmol/L in binge eaters.","q":"Which graph best illustrates the change in food consumption that would result from treating rats with a CCK receptor blocker?","choices":["Treated rats eat less than control rats","Treated rats eat more than control rats","Treated and control rats eat the same","Treated rats eat less, then the same"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Blocking fullness signal → more eating","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Block the stop-eating signal → rats eat more."},"exp":"**Answer:** Treated rats eat more than control rats\n\n**Incorrect: Treated rats eat less than control rats** That is the effect of extra CCK, not a blocker.\n\n**Correct: Treated rats eat more than control rats** CCK tells the brain to stop eating. Blocking its receptor removes that signal, so meals get bigger.\n\n**Incorrect: Treated and control rats eat the same** CCK clearly affects meal size, so a blocker should change intake.\n\n**Incorrect: Treated rats eat less, then the same** Nothing here would lower intake.","how":{"ask":"What happens to intake without CCK signaling?","know":"Blocker = opposite of the hormone's effect.","steps":[{"text":"CCK tells the brain to stop eating.","hl":"tell the brain to stop eating"},{"text":"A blocker removes the stop signal, so intake rises.","strike":["Treated rats eat less than control rats","Treated rats eat less, then the same"]},{"text":"Intake does change. Answer: Treated rats eat more than control rats.","strike":["Treated and control rats eat the same"]}],"trap":"eat less: true for CCK itself, but this is a blocker."},"teach":[{"text":"Notice if the drug adds or blocks the hormone.","marks":[{"t":"Giving extra CCK makes animals eat smaller, shorter meals","note":"CCK effect"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A receptor blocker gives the opposite of the hormone."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-151a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:151#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:151"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P7","passage":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gut hormone released from the small intestine when food arrives. CCK empties bile from the gallbladder and enzyme-rich pancreatic juice into the duodenum. CCK also signals fullness by stimulating vagal sensory fibers that tell the brain to stop eating. Giving extra CCK makes animals eat smaller, shorter meals. Researchers tested people with binge-eating disorder, who eat very large meals. After a meal, CCK rose from 1 to 6 pmol/L in controls but only from 1 to 2 pmol/L in binge eaters.","q":"By stimulating secretion of lipase from the pancreas into the duodenum, CCK facilitates the digestion of:","choices":["protein.","fats.","nucleotides.","carbohydrates."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Lipase digests lipids (fats)","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Enzyme name → substrate: lip-ase → lipids."},"exp":"**Answer:** fats.\n\n**Incorrect: protein.** Proteins are broken down by proteases like trypsin.\n\n**Correct: fats.** Lipase splits triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides.\n\n**Incorrect: nucleotides.** Nucleic acids are broken down by nucleases.\n\n**Incorrect: carbohydrates.** Starches are broken down by amylase.","how":{"ask":"What does lipase digest?","know":"-ase names the substrate: lipase → lipid.","steps":[{"text":"CCK releases pancreatic enzymes.","hl":"enzyme-rich pancreatic juice into the duodenum"},{"text":"Protease, nuclease and amylase handle the others.","strike":["protein.","nucleotides.","carbohydrates."]},{"text":"Answer: fats."}],"trap":"protein: CCK also releases trypsin, but lipase is named."},"teach":[{"text":"Read the enzyme named in the stem.","marks":[{"t":"enzyme-rich pancreatic juice","note":"many enzymes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lipase-fat, protease-protein, amylase-starch, nuclease-DNA/RNA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-152a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"osmolarity and fluid movement","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:152#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:152"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P8","passage":"Three sensors track body water. Osmoreceptors in the brain sense blood osmolarity. Volume receptors in the atria and great veins sense blood volume. Oral receptors sense a dry mouth. In a study, eight healthy adults ate and drank water freely from 8 AM to 6 PM and rated their thirst. In the hour before drinking, blood osmolarity was 2% higher than at other times, blood volume did not change, and thirst ratings were higher.","q":"On the day of the study, the subjects drank about 1.5 L of water on average and excreted about 600 mL of urine. The most likely explanation for the difference between water intake and urine excretion is that:","choices":["the extra water was stored in fat.","the extra water was excreted in the feces.","water was lost via the skin and the lungs.","water was made by cell metabolism."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Healthy body keeps water steady","c":[0]},{"f":"Feces lose little water","c":[1]},{"f":"Making water widens the gap","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Intake > urine: the rest leaves by insensible loss (sweat, breath)."},"exp":"**Answer:** water was lost via the skin and the lungs.\n\n**Incorrect: the extra water was stored in fat.** Fat holds little water, and healthy adults stay in balance.\n\n**Incorrect: the extra water was excreted in the feces.** Feces lose only about 100–200 mL per day.\n\n**Correct: water was lost via the skin and the lungs.** Sweat and moist breath lose a lot of water each day. That covers the gap.\n\n**Incorrect: water was made by cell metabolism.** Metabolic water adds water, making the gap bigger.","how":{"ask":"Where did the missing 900 mL go?","know":"Outputs: urine, skin, lungs, feces.","steps":[{"text":"Healthy adults kept drinking freely, so they stayed in balance.","hl":"eight healthy adults ate and drank water freely"},{"text":"Storage and feces cannot hold 900 mL.","strike":["the extra water was stored in fat.","the extra water was excreted in the feces."]},{"text":"Metabolism adds water. Answer: water was lost via the skin and the lungs.","strike":["water was made by cell metabolism."]}],"trap":"water was made by cell metabolism: that adds input, it does not explain lost water."},"teach":[{"text":"Balance: in = out. Find the missing output.","marks":[{"t":"healthy adults","note":"in balance"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Intake beyond urine leaves by sweat and breath."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-153a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nephron segment function","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:153#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:153"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P8","passage":"Three sensors track body water. Osmoreceptors in the brain sense blood osmolarity. Volume receptors in the atria and great veins sense blood volume. Oral receptors sense a dry mouth. In a study, eight healthy adults ate and drank water freely from 8 AM to 6 PM and rated their thirst. In the hour before drinking, blood osmolarity was 2% higher than at other times, blood volume did not change, and thirst ratings were higher.","q":"The osmolarity of urine in these subjects was 900 milliosmoles/L, compared with an average blood osmolarity of 290 milliosmoles/L. The higher osmolarity of urine suggests that:","choices":["the kidneys are excreting very little urea.","the subjects have kidney failure.","the kidneys are acting to conserve water.","the subjects are on a high-water diet."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Concentrated urine = water kept","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Concentrated urine has much urea","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Urine more concentrated than blood → kidneys are saving water."},"exp":"**Answer:** the kidneys are acting to conserve water.\n\n**Incorrect: the kidneys are excreting very little urea.** Urea is a main solute in concentrated urine.\n\n**Incorrect: the subjects have kidney failure.** Failing kidneys cannot concentrate urine; these subjects are healthy.\n\n**Correct: the kidneys are acting to conserve water.** Urine 3× blood osmolarity means water was pulled back into the blood (ADH at work).\n\n**Incorrect: the subjects are on a high-water diet.** Lots of water makes dilute urine, not concentrated.","how":{"ask":"What does urine above blood osmolarity mean?","know":"High urine osmolarity = water reabsorbed.","steps":[{"text":"Subjects are healthy.","hl":"eight healthy adults"},{"text":"Failing kidneys and heavy drinking both give dilute urine.","strike":["the subjects have kidney failure.","the subjects are on a high-water diet."]},{"text":"Urea is a big part of concentrated urine. Answer: the kidneys are acting to conserve water.","strike":["the kidneys are excreting very little urea."]}],"trap":"Kidney failure: failing kidneys make urine near blood osmolarity."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare urine to blood: higher = concentrating.","marks":[{"t":"healthy adults","note":"normal kidneys"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Urine osm > blood osm → kidneys conserving water."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-154a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nervous system pathways","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:154#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:154"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P8","passage":"Three sensors track body water. Osmoreceptors in the brain sense blood osmolarity. Volume receptors in the atria and great veins sense blood volume. Oral receptors sense a dry mouth. In a study, eight healthy adults ate and drank water freely from 8 AM to 6 PM and rated their thirst. In the hour before drinking, blood osmolarity was 2% higher than at other times, blood volume did not change, and thirst ratings were higher.","q":"In a different experiment, healthy adults received an intravenous infusion of 150 mL of hyperosmotic NaCl solution, which induced thirst and a desire to drink. In this experiment, thirst probably was stimulated via:","choices":["osmoreceptors, as a result of increased blood osmolarity after the infusion.","volume receptors, as a result of increased blood volume after the infusion.","a sensation of dryness in the mouth by oral receptors.","the NaCl itself, because salt makes a person thirsty regardless of osmolarity."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Higher volume would reduce thirst","c":[1]},{"f":"IV skips the mouth","c":[2]},{"f":"Salt acts through osmolarity","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hyperosmotic IV raises osmolarity → osmoreceptors fire."},"exp":"**Answer:** osmoreceptors, as a result of increased blood osmolarity after the infusion.\n\n**Correct: osmoreceptors, as a result of increased blood osmolarity after the infusion.** Hyperosmotic salt raises blood osmolarity. Brain osmoreceptors sense this and trigger thirst.\n\n**Incorrect: volume receptors, as a result of increased blood volume after the infusion.** More volume would lower thirst, not raise it.\n\n**Incorrect: a sensation of dryness in the mouth by oral receptors.** An IV bypasses the mouth.\n\n**Incorrect: the NaCl itself, because salt makes a person thirsty regardless of osmolarity.** Salt causes thirst by raising osmolarity.","how":{"ask":"Which sensor responds to a salty IV?","know":"Osmoreceptors sense blood osmolarity.","steps":[{"text":"Osmoreceptors sense osmolarity.","hl":"Osmoreceptors in the brain sense blood osmolarity."},{"text":"More volume reduces thirst; IV skips the mouth.","strike":["volume receptors, as a result of increased blood volume after the infusion.","a sensation of dryness in the mouth by oral receptors."]},{"text":"Salt works through osmolarity. Answer: osmoreceptors.","strike":["the NaCl itself, because salt makes a person thirsty regardless of osmolarity."]}],"trap":"volume receptors: volume did rise, but that signals stop drinking."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the change (osmolarity up) to the sensor for it.","marks":[{"t":"sense blood osmolarity","note":"salt IV"},{"t":"sense blood volume","note":"blood loss"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Hyperosmotic load → osmoreceptors → thirst and ADH."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-155a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"apply the physics formula","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:155#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:155"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P8","passage":"Three sensors track body water. Osmoreceptors in the brain sense blood osmolarity. Volume receptors in the atria and great veins sense blood volume. Oral receptors sense a dry mouth. In a study, eight healthy adults ate and drank water freely from 8 AM to 6 PM and rated their thirst. In the hour before drinking, blood osmolarity was 2% higher than at other times, blood volume did not change, and thirst ratings were higher.","q":"Which of the following responses could maintain cardiac output under blood-loss conditions that lower stroke volume?","choices":["A decrease in heart rate","An increase in blood viscosity","An increase in heart rate","A decrease in venous return"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"CO = HR × SV","c":[0]},{"f":"These lower output further","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"CO = HR × SV; if SV falls, HR must rise."},"exp":"**Answer:** An increase in heart rate\n\n**Incorrect: A decrease in heart rate** Lower HR with lower SV drops output more.\n\n**Incorrect: An increase in blood viscosity** Thicker blood raises resistance and does not restore output.\n\n**Correct: An increase in heart rate** Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. A faster heart makes up for a smaller stroke.\n\n**Incorrect: A decrease in venous return** Less venous return lowers stroke volume further.","how":{"ask":"What offsets a lower stroke volume?","know":"CO = HR × SV.","steps":[{"text":"Low volume is sensed by volume receptors.","hl":"Volume receptors in the atria and great veins sense blood volume."},{"text":"Slower heart, thicker blood, less return all hurt output.","strike":["A decrease in heart rate","An increase in blood viscosity","A decrease in venous return"]},{"text":"Answer: An increase in heart rate."}],"trap":"blood viscosity: changes resistance, not the CO formula's terms."},"teach":[{"text":"Volume loss → baroreflex → faster heart.","marks":[{"t":"sense blood volume","note":"triggers reflex"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SV down → HR up to keep CO."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-156a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"osmolarity and fluid movement","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:156#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:156"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P8","passage":"Three sensors track body water. Osmoreceptors in the brain sense blood osmolarity. Volume receptors in the atria and great veins sense blood volume. Oral receptors sense a dry mouth. In a study, eight healthy adults ate and drank water freely from 8 AM to 6 PM and rated their thirst. In the hour before drinking, blood osmolarity was 2% higher than at other times, blood volume did not change, and thirst ratings were higher.","q":"During exercise, the osmolarity of venous blood from active muscles will increase as a result of an increase in:","choices":["O2 concentration in plasma.","oxyhemoglobin in red blood cells.","pressure in systemic arteries.","lactate concentration in plasma."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Muscle uses O2, not adds","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Pressure is not a solute","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Osmolarity rises when solute is added: working muscle dumps lactate."},"exp":"**Answer:** lactate concentration in plasma.\n\n**Incorrect: O2 concentration in plasma.** Active muscle takes O2 out of the blood.\n\n**Incorrect: oxyhemoglobin in red blood cells.** Hemoglobin unloads O2 in muscle, so oxyhemoglobin falls.\n\n**Incorrect: pressure in systemic arteries.** Pressure is not a dissolved particle.\n\n**Correct: lactate concentration in plasma.** Hard-working muscle makes lactate and releases it. More solute raises osmolarity.","how":{"ask":"Which added solute raises venous osmolarity?","know":"Osmolarity = dissolved particles per liter.","steps":[{"text":"Osmolarity counts dissolved particles.","hl":"blood osmolarity"},{"text":"O2 and oxyhemoglobin fall in muscle veins.","strike":["O2 concentration in plasma.","oxyhemoglobin in red blood cells."]},{"text":"Pressure is not a particle. Answer: lactate concentration in plasma.","strike":["pressure in systemic arteries."]}],"trap":"Pressure: changes flow, not solute count."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask what muscle adds to blood: lactate, CO2, K+.","marks":[{"t":"blood osmolarity","note":"solute count"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Osmolarity rises only when particles are added."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-157a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:157#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:157"],"q":"Like other negative-strand RNA viruses, influenza carries an RNA-dependent RNA polymerase, an enzyme that copies the viral genome from:","choices":["DNA to RNA.","RNA to RNA.","RNA to DNA.","protein to RNA."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"RNA-dependent = RNA template","c":[0,3]},{"f":"RNA polymerase makes RNA","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read the name: X-dependent Y polymerase copies X into Y."},"exp":"**Answer:** RNA to RNA.\n\n**Incorrect: DNA to RNA.** That is DNA-dependent RNA polymerase (normal transcription).\n\n**Correct: RNA to RNA.** RNA-dependent means RNA is the template. RNA polymerase means RNA is made.\n\n**Incorrect: RNA to DNA.** That is reverse transcriptase.\n\n**Incorrect: protein to RNA.** No enzyme copies protein into RNA.","how":{"ask":"Template and product of RdRp?","know":"Name = template-dependent product polymerase.","steps":[{"text":"RNA-dependent: template is RNA."},{"text":"DNA and protein templates are out.","strike":["DNA to RNA.","protein to RNA."]},{"text":"It makes RNA, not DNA. Answer: RNA to RNA.","strike":["RNA to DNA."]}],"trap":"RNA to DNA: that is reverse transcriptase in retroviruses."},"teach":[{"text":"Reverse transcriptase = RNA-dependent DNA polymerase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Polymerase name gives template, then product."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-158a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"activation energy vs free energy","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:158#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:158"],"q":"Given that the solid line in each graph represents an uncatalyzed chemical reaction, in which of the following graphs does the dashed line best represent the same chemical reaction when catalyzed by an enzyme?","choices":["Lower peak, lower product energy","Higher peak, same start and end","Lower peak, same start and end","Same peak, higher product energy"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Enzymes never change ΔG","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Enzymes lower the peak","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Enzyme lowers only the hump; start and end stay put."},"exp":"**Answer:** Lower peak, same start and end\n\n**Incorrect: Lower peak, lower product energy** An enzyme does not change product energy (ΔG).\n\n**Incorrect: Higher peak, same start and end** Enzymes lower activation energy, not raise it.\n\n**Correct: Lower peak, same start and end** An enzyme lowers activation energy only. Reactant and product energies stay the same.\n\n**Incorrect: Same peak, higher product energy** Neither the peak nor ΔG would act this way.","how":{"ask":"What does an enzyme change on an energy diagram?","know":"Enzymes lower Ea; ΔG unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"Enzymes lower the peak only."},{"text":"Changing end energy changes ΔG: not allowed.","strike":["Lower peak, lower product energy","Same peak, higher product energy"]},{"text":"Peak must drop. Answer: Lower peak, same start and end.","strike":["Higher peak, same start and end"]}],"trap":"Lower peak, lower product energy: right peak, but ΔG must not change."},"teach":[{"text":"Look at three points: start, peak, end. Only the peak moves."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Catalysts lower Ea and never change ΔG."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-159a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"na+/k+ pump direction","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:159#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:159"],"q":"The function of the H+/K+ ATPase in stomach parietal cells is to transport: I. H+ out of cells against its concentration gradient. II. H+ into cells with its concentration gradient. III. K+ out of cells with its concentration gradient. IV. K+ into cells against its concentration gradient.","choices":["I and III only","I and IV only","II and III only","II and IV only"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Pumps go against gradients","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"ATPase pumps = against gradient both ways: H+ out, K+ in."},"exp":"**Answer:** I and IV only\n\n**Incorrect: I and III only** K+ moves in, not out, and pumps do not move ions down their gradient.\n\n**Correct: I and IV only** The pump uses ATP to push H+ out into the stomach and bring K+ in, both against their gradients.\n\n**Incorrect: II and III only** Both moves are downhill; an ATPase pumps uphill.\n\n**Incorrect: II and IV only** H+ goes out, not in.","how":{"ask":"Which way does each ion go, and uphill or down?","know":"ATP pump = against gradient.","steps":[{"text":"Pumps use ATP to move ions uphill. Cut any with its gradient."},{"text":"II and III are downhill.","strike":["I and III only","II and III only","II and IV only"]},{"text":"Answer: I and IV only."}],"trap":"I and III: H+ out is right, but K+ goes in."},"teach":[{"text":"The word ATPase signals active, uphill transport."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ATPase pumps move both ions against their gradients."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-160a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:160#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:160"],"q":"A graph plots log10 of cell number over time in a fish embryo. As the embryo begins gastrulation, the plotted value is 4.3. According to the graph, as the embryo begins gastrulation, the number of cells is between:","choices":["4 and 43.","430 and 4,300.","4,300 and 10,000.","10,000 and 100,000."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Log 4.3 = 10^4.3","c":[0,1]},{"f":"10^4 = 10,000","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Log value 4.x means 10^4 to 10^5."},"exp":"**Answer:** 10,000 and 100,000.\n\n**Incorrect: 4 and 43.** That reads the log value as the count.\n\n**Incorrect: 430 and 4,300.** That multiplies the log value instead of using it as a power.\n\n**Incorrect: 4,300 and 10,000.** 10^4.3 is above 10^4 = 10,000.\n\n**Correct: 10,000 and 100,000.** 10^4.3 ≈ 20,000. It sits between 10^4 and 10^5.","how":{"ask":"What count gives log10 = 4.3?","know":"log10 = 4.x → 10^4 to 10^5.","steps":[{"text":"Turn the log back into a power: 10^4.3."},{"text":"The count is not 4.3 or 430.","strike":["4 and 43.","430 and 4,300."]},{"text":"10^4.3 > 10,000. Answer: 10,000 and 100,000.","strike":["4,300 and 10,000."]}],"trap":"4 and 43: reading the log axis as the raw count."},"teach":[{"text":"The digit before the decimal = number of zeros."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Log axis value n.x → between 10^n and 10^(n+1)."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-161a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:161#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:161"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P9","passage":"Bacterium Nx lives harmlessly in the throat of some people. Sometimes it enters the blood, reaches the brain, crosses the blood–brain barrier and inflames the meninges. Nx first sticks to throat cells with hair-like pili built from pilin subunits. After sticking, Nx turns up gene pgtA. Enzyme PgtA adds phosphoglycerol groups onto pilin, mostly at residue 71.","q":"Which change in a property of pilin will be observed after modification by PgtA?","choices":["Increase in number of disulfide bonds","Decrease in molecular weight","Increase in isoelectric point","Decrease in isoelectric point"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Phosphate adds negative charge","c":[2]},{"f":"Adding groups adds mass","c":[1]},{"f":"No cysteines involved","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Adding a phosphate adds negative charge → lower pI."},"exp":"**Answer:** Decrease in isoelectric point\n\n**Incorrect: Increase in number of disulfide bonds** Phosphoglycerol does not link cysteines.\n\n**Incorrect: Decrease in molecular weight** Adding a group makes the protein heavier.\n\n**Incorrect: Increase in isoelectric point** Extra negative charge lowers pI, not raises it.\n\n**Correct: Decrease in isoelectric point** Phosphoglycerol carries a negative phosphate. More negative charge means a lower pH is needed to reach net zero.","how":{"ask":"How does adding phosphoglycerol change pilin?","know":"More negative groups → lower pI.","steps":[{"text":"PgtA adds phosphate-containing groups.","hl":"adds phosphoglycerol groups onto pilin"},{"text":"Adding mass and no cysteine link.","strike":["Increase in number of disulfide bonds","Decrease in molecular weight"]},{"text":"Negative charge lowers pI. Answer: Decrease in isoelectric point.","strike":["Increase in isoelectric point"]}],"trap":"Increase in isoelectric point: direction flipped."},"teach":[{"text":"Phospho = negative at body pH.","marks":[{"t":"phosphoglycerol","note":"negative group"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Add negative charge → pI falls."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-162a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:162#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:162"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P9","passage":"Bacterium Nx lives harmlessly in the throat of some people. Sometimes it enters the blood, reaches the brain, crosses the blood–brain barrier and inflames the meninges. Nx first sticks to throat cells with hair-like pili built from pilin subunits. After sticking, Nx turns up gene pgtA. Enzyme PgtA adds phosphoglycerol groups onto pilin, mostly at residue 71.","q":"Which amino acid residue in the pilin subunit is most likely modified by PgtA?","choices":["Leucine","Threonine","Methionine","Proline"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Phosphoesters need a side-chain OH","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phospho groups attach to OH: pick Ser, Thr or Tyr."},"exp":"**Answer:** Threonine\n\n**Incorrect: Leucine** Leucine is a hydrocarbon side chain with no OH.\n\n**Correct: Threonine** Threonine has a side-chain OH. The phosphate forms an ester with it.\n\n**Incorrect: Methionine** Methionine has a thioether, not an OH.\n\n**Incorrect: Proline** Proline has a ring with no OH.","how":{"ask":"Which residue can take a phosphate group?","know":"Phospho groups bond to OH (Ser, Thr, Tyr).","steps":[{"text":"PgtA adds a phosphate-linked group at one residue.","hl":"mostly at residue 71"},{"text":"No OH on leucine, methionine or proline.","strike":["Leucine","Methionine","Proline"]},{"text":"Answer: Threonine."}],"trap":"Methionine: has sulfur, but no OH."},"teach":[{"text":"Phosphoglycerol links through phosphate, so same rule as kinases.","marks":[{"t":"phosphoglycerol groups","note":"needs OH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Phosphate targets = S, T, Y."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-163a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"immune branch and cell type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:163#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:163"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P9","passage":"Bacterium Nx lives harmlessly in the throat of some people. Sometimes it enters the blood, reaches the brain, crosses the blood–brain barrier and inflames the meninges. Nx first sticks to throat cells with hair-like pili built from pilin subunits. After sticking, Nx turns up gene pgtA. Enzyme PgtA adds phosphoglycerol groups onto pilin, mostly at residue 71.","q":"Based on the information in the passage, which immune cells would mount the initial immune response to Nx that results in meningitis?","choices":["Plasma cells","Cytotoxic T cells","Microglia","Helper T cells"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Initial = innate, not adaptive","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"First response in the brain = its resident innate cells: microglia."},"exp":"**Answer:** Microglia\n\n**Incorrect: Plasma cells** They are adaptive and come later.\n\n**Incorrect: Cytotoxic T cells** Adaptive cells that kill infected host cells, later.\n\n**Correct: Microglia** Microglia are the brain's resident macrophages. They are the first to meet bacteria that cross the barrier.\n\n**Incorrect: Helper T cells** Adaptive; they need days to activate.","how":{"ask":"Who responds first in the brain?","know":"Initial = innate; brain macrophages = microglia.","steps":[{"text":"Nx reaches the brain and inflames the meninges.","hl":"crosses the blood–brain barrier and inflames the meninges"},{"text":"Plasma, cytotoxic T and helper T cells are adaptive.","strike":["Plasma cells","Cytotoxic T cells","Helper T cells"]},{"text":"Answer: Microglia."}],"trap":"Helper T cells: important, but not first."},"teach":[{"text":"Initial response means innate cells already in the tissue.","marks":[{"t":"inflames the meninges","note":"brain site"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Brain's first immune responder = microglia."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-164a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:164#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:164"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P9","passage":"Bacterium Nx lives harmlessly in the throat of some people. Sometimes it enters the blood, reaches the brain, crosses the blood–brain barrier and inflames the meninges. Nx first sticks to throat cells with hair-like pili built from pilin subunits. After sticking, Nx turns up gene pgtA. Enzyme PgtA adds phosphoglycerol groups onto pilin, mostly at residue 71.","q":"The presence of which type of intercellular connection between endothelial cells of brain capillaries results in the blood–brain barrier?","choices":["Gap junctions","Desmosomes","Tight junctions","Intercalated discs"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Barrier = seal between cells","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Intercalated discs are heart only","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Barrier → seal → tight junctions."},"exp":"**Answer:** Tight junctions\n\n**Incorrect: Gap junctions** They open channels between cells, the opposite of a barrier.\n\n**Incorrect: Desmosomes** They anchor cells for strength but do not seal.\n\n**Correct: Tight junctions** They seal the space between endothelial cells, so solutes must cross through the cells.\n\n**Incorrect: Intercalated discs** They join heart muscle cells.","how":{"ask":"Which junction seals cells together?","know":"Tight = seal; desmosome = anchor; gap = channel.","steps":[{"text":"The blood–brain barrier blocks leaks between cells.","hl":"blood–brain barrier"},{"text":"Gap junctions leak; desmosomes only anchor.","strike":["Gap junctions","Desmosomes"]},{"text":"Discs are cardiac. Answer: Tight junctions.","strike":["Intercalated discs"]}],"trap":"Desmosomes: strong, but not watertight."},"teach":[{"text":"Barrier words point to tight junctions.","marks":[{"t":"blood–brain barrier","note":"tight junctions"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Seal = tight; anchor = desmosome; pass = gap."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-165a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:165#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:165"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P10","passage":"GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. In insects, GABA opens receptor channels that let chloride ions flow into neurons. The insecticide Xr is an allosteric blocker of these receptors. Insects in fields sprayed with Xr for years became resistant. A resistant receptor gene, Rxr, carries one base change that swaps one amino acid in one receptor subunit. Frog oocytes injected with wild-type or Rxr receptor cDNA both showed a chloride current with GABA; Xr blocked the current only in wild-type oocytes.","q":"When GABA is added to frog oocytes expressing either wild-type or Rxr GABA receptors, how does the membrane potential change?","choices":["Oocytes become depolarized and produce an action potential from sodium ion entry.","Oocytes become hyperpolarized as chloride ions exit them.","Oocytes become hyperpolarized as chloride ions enter them.","No change occurs in potential; chloride ions enter and sodium ions exit the oocytes."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"GABA receptor passes Cl⁻, not Na⁺","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Cl⁻ flows in","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Negative ion in → inside more negative → hyperpolarized."},"exp":"**Answer:** Oocytes become hyperpolarized as chloride ions enter them.\n\n**Incorrect: Oocytes become depolarized and produce an action potential from sodium ion entry.** The channel passes chloride, not sodium.\n\n**Incorrect: Oocytes become hyperpolarized as chloride ions exit them.** Chloride flows in; negative ions leaving would depolarize.\n\n**Correct: Oocytes become hyperpolarized as chloride ions enter them.** Negative Cl⁻ entering makes the inside more negative.\n\n**Incorrect: No change occurs in potential; chloride ions enter and sodium ions exit the oocytes.** The receptor does not move sodium.","how":{"ask":"What does Cl⁻ entry do to the potential?","know":"Anion in → hyperpolarize.","steps":[{"text":"GABA opens chloride channels; Cl⁻ flows in.","hl":"let chloride ions flow into neurons"},{"text":"No sodium path.","strike":["Oocytes become depolarized and produce an action potential from sodium ion entry.","No change occurs in potential; chloride ions enter and sodium ions exit the oocytes."]},{"text":"Cl⁻ enters, not exits. Answer: hyperpolarized as chloride ions enter them.","strike":["Oocytes become hyperpolarized as chloride ions exit them."]}],"trap":"chloride ions exit: right result word, wrong direction."},"teach":[{"text":"Inhibitory = harder to fire = more negative.","marks":[{"t":"inhibitory neurotransmitter","note":"hyperpolarizes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Cl⁻ in or K⁺ out → hyperpolarization."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-166a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:166#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:166"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P10","passage":"GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. In insects, GABA opens receptor channels that let chloride ions flow into neurons. The insecticide Xr is an allosteric blocker of these receptors. Insects in fields sprayed with Xr for years became resistant. A resistant receptor gene, Rxr, carries one base change that swaps one amino acid in one receptor subunit. Frog oocytes injected with wild-type or Rxr receptor cDNA both showed a chloride current with GABA; Xr blocked the current only in wild-type oocytes.","q":"If the fourth codon in the coding region of the Rxr receptor cDNA is replaced with a stop codon (for example, TGA) and the modified Rxr receptor cDNA is expressed in frog oocytes, functional full-length receptors will:","choices":["accumulate in the nucleus.","not be synthesized.","accumulate in the cytosol.","lose resistance to Xr."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Early stop ends translation","c":[0,2]},{"f":"No protein, no drug response","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stop codon near the start → ribosome quits → no full-length protein at all."},"exp":"**Answer:** not be synthesized.\n\n**Incorrect: accumulate in the nucleus.** No full-length protein is made, so none can pile up anywhere.\n\n**Correct: not be synthesized.** A stop codon at codon 4 ends translation after 3 amino acids. No full-length receptor is ever made.\n\n**Incorrect: accumulate in the cytosol.** Only a 3-amino-acid stub forms, not a full receptor.\n\n**Incorrect: lose resistance to Xr.** There is no receptor left to resist or respond to Xr.","how":{"ask":"Does a full-length receptor even get made?","know":"Stop codon = translation ends there.","steps":[{"text":"The receptor comes from the injected cDNA.","hl":"Frog oocytes injected with wild-type or Rxr receptor cDNA"},{"text":"Stop at codon 4 → ribosome quits after 3 amino acids. Nothing full-length to store.","strike":["accumulate in the nucleus.","accumulate in the cytosol."]},{"text":"No receptor means no drug question.","strike":["lose resistance to Xr."]},{"text":"Answer: not be synthesized."}],"trap":"accumulate in the cytosol: sounds like a stuck protein, but the protein never exists."},"teach":[{"text":"Early stop codon = nonsense mutation = short stub, not the full protein.","marks":[{"t":"Rxr receptor cDNA","note":"gets early stop"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A stop codon near the start means the full protein is never made."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-167a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"protein targeting and secretion","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:167#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:167"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P10","passage":"GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. In insects, GABA opens receptor channels that let chloride ions flow into neurons. The insecticide Xr is an allosteric blocker of these receptors. Insects in fields sprayed with Xr for years became resistant. A resistant receptor gene, Rxr, carries one base change that swaps one amino acid in one receptor subunit. Frog oocytes injected with wild-type or Rxr receptor cDNA both showed a chloride current with GABA; Xr blocked the current only in wild-type oocytes.","q":"Which pathway depicts the sequence of cellular compartments traversed by newly synthesized Rxr receptor subunits as they move to the cell surface?","choices":["Golgi complex, rough endoplasmic reticulum, and plasma membrane","Rough endoplasmic reticulum, lysosome, and Golgi complex","Rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, and secretory transport vesicle","Nucleus, endosome, and lysosome"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Membrane proteins start in rough ER","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Lysosomes degrade; not surface route","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Surface proteins go rough ER → Golgi → vesicle → membrane."},"exp":"**Answer:** Rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, and secretory transport vesicle\n\n**Incorrect: Golgi complex, rough endoplasmic reticulum, and plasma membrane** The order is backward; proteins enter the rough ER first.\n\n**Incorrect: Rough endoplasmic reticulum, lysosome, and Golgi complex** Lysosomes break proteins down; they are not a stop on the way out.\n\n**Correct: Rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, and secretory transport vesicle** A channel protein is made on rough ER ribosomes. The Golgi sorts it, and a vesicle carries it to the membrane.\n\n**Incorrect: Nucleus, endosome, and lysosome** Proteins are not made in the nucleus, and this route ends in breakdown.","how":{"ask":"Order of stops for a membrane protein.","know":"Rough ER → Golgi → vesicle → surface.","steps":[{"text":"The receptor is a channel in the cell membrane.","hl":"GABA opens receptor channels"},{"text":"Membrane proteins start in the rough ER.","strike":["Golgi complex, rough endoplasmic reticulum, and plasma membrane","Nucleus, endosome, and lysosome"]},{"text":"Lysosomes are for breakdown, not delivery.","strike":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum, lysosome, and Golgi complex"]},{"text":"Answer: Rough ER, Golgi complex, and secretory transport vesicle."}],"trap":"Golgi first: right parts, wrong order."},"teach":[{"text":"Channels sit in the membrane, so they ride the secretory path.","marks":[{"t":"receptor channels","note":"membrane protein"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Membrane and secreted proteins go rough ER → Golgi → vesicle."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-168a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:168#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:168"],"pid":"pe4-bb:P10","passage":"GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. In insects, GABA opens receptor channels that let chloride ions flow into neurons. The insecticide Xr is an allosteric blocker of these receptors. Insects in fields sprayed with Xr for years became resistant. A resistant receptor gene, Rxr, carries one base change that swaps one amino acid in one receptor subunit. Frog oocytes injected with wild-type or Rxr receptor cDNA both showed a chloride current with GABA; Xr blocked the current only in wild-type oocytes.","q":"Which procedure would be the best negative control for the frog oocytes' own native GABA-receptor function?","choices":["Addition of Xr to wild-type-injected oocytes","Addition of GABA to mock-injected frog oocytes","Injection of wild-type receptor cDNA into insect neurons","Addition of extra GABA to Rxr-injected oocytes"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Control lacks the injected gene","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Must test frog oocytes themselves","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same cells, same GABA, but no receptor cDNA: any current is the oocyte's own."},"exp":"**Answer:** Addition of GABA to mock-injected frog oocytes\n\n**Incorrect: Addition of Xr to wild-type-injected oocytes** These oocytes carry the added receptor, so they cannot show native function alone.\n\n**Correct: Addition of GABA to mock-injected frog oocytes** Mock-injected oocytes get no receptor cDNA. Any current they show with GABA comes from their own receptors.\n\n**Incorrect: Injection of wild-type receptor cDNA into insect neurons** This changes the cell type and tests nothing about frog oocytes.\n\n**Incorrect: Addition of extra GABA to Rxr-injected oocytes** These oocytes carry the added receptor, so the signal mixes both sources.","how":{"ask":"How do you see the oocyte's own GABA response?","know":"Negative control = everything but the added variable.","steps":[{"text":"The variable is the injected receptor cDNA.","hl":"Frog oocytes injected with wild-type or Rxr receptor cDNA"},{"text":"Any oocyte holding the added receptor can't isolate native function.","strike":["Addition of Xr to wild-type-injected oocytes","Addition of extra GABA to Rxr-injected oocytes"]},{"text":"The question is about frog oocytes, not insect cells.","strike":["Injection of wild-type receptor cDNA into insect neurons"]},{"text":"Answer: Addition of GABA to mock-injected frog oocytes."}],"trap":"Extra GABA to Rxr oocytes: sounds like a test, but the added receptor is still there."},"teach":[{"text":"Mock-injected = same handling, no cDNA.","marks":[{"t":"showed a chloride current with GABA","note":"could be native"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A negative control keeps every step but drops the one thing you added."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-169a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:169#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:169"],"q":"Assume that a mutant allele is the result of the deletion of three entire codons. The base pair length of the mutant allele, compared to that of the wild-type allele, would have:","choices":["one fewer nucleotide.","three fewer nucleotides.","nine fewer nucleotides.","six fewer nucleotides."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Count bases, not codons","c":[1]},{"f":"3 codons × 3 = 9","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Codons deleted × 3 = nucleotides lost."},"exp":"**Answer:** nine fewer nucleotides.\n\n**Incorrect: one fewer nucleotide.** One base is far less than even one codon.\n\n**Incorrect: three fewer nucleotides.** That is the codon count, not the base count.\n\n**Correct: nine fewer nucleotides.** Each codon is 3 nucleotides. Three codons × 3 = 9.\n\n**Incorrect: six fewer nucleotides.** That is only 2 codons.","how":{"ask":"How many bases do 3 codons hold?","know":"1 codon = 3 nucleotides.","steps":[{"text":"The answer counts nucleotides, not codons.","strike":["three fewer nucleotides."]},{"text":"Multiply: 3 codons × 3 = 9.","strike":["one fewer nucleotide.","six fewer nucleotides."]},{"text":"Answer: nine fewer nucleotides."}],"trap":"three fewer: copies the codon count straight into the answer."},"teach":[{"text":"A codon is a 3-base word for one amino acid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Nucleotides = codons × 3."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-170a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"x-linked cross probability","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:170#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:170"],"q":"In a mouse strain, gray fur is recessive to black fur, and kinked tails are recessive to straight tails; both genes are on the X chromosome. The genotype of a black-furred, straight-tailed female (XX) could be determined by mating it with a male (XY) that has which of the following phenotypes?","choices":["Gray fur, kinked tail","Black fur, straight tail","Black fur, kinked tail","Gray fur, straight tail"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Test cross: male shows both recessives","c":[1]},{"f":"One dominant trait masks that gene","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Test cross = mate with the all-recessive phenotype."},"exp":"**Answer:** Gray fur, kinked tail\n\n**Correct: Gray fur, kinked tail** This male gives only recessive alleles. Every pup then shows the mother's alleles directly.\n\n**Incorrect: Black fur, straight tail** His dominant alleles hide the mother's alleles in daughters for both genes.\n\n**Incorrect: Black fur, kinked tail** His black-fur allele hides the mother's fur alleles in daughters.\n\n**Incorrect: Gray fur, straight tail** His straight-tail allele hides the mother's tail alleles in daughters.","how":{"ask":"Which male reveals the female's hidden alleles?","know":"Test cross = cross with the homozygous recessive.","steps":[{"text":"Both her traits are dominant, so either could hide a recessive allele.","strike":["Black fur, straight tail"]},{"text":"A male dominant for one gene masks that gene in daughters.","strike":["Black fur, kinked tail","Gray fur, straight tail"]},{"text":"Answer: Gray fur, kinked tail."}],"trap":"Black fur, straight tail: matches her, but shows nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"A male has one X, so his phenotype is his genotype."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To find a dominant-looking genotype, cross with the all-recessive phenotype."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe4-171a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"mitosis, meiosis and nondisjunction","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe4-bb:171#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-bb:171"],"q":"Which process is most likely responsible for an egg cell receiving an extra copy of a particular chromosome?","choices":["Crossing over","Non-disjunction","Gene linkage","Independent assortment"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Crossing over swaps, keeps number","c":[0]},{"f":"Linkage, assortment keep chromosome counts","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Wrong chromosome number in a gamete = non-disjunction."},"exp":"**Answer:** Non-disjunction\n\n**Incorrect: Crossing over** It swaps pieces between homologs but keeps the number the same.\n\n**Correct: Non-disjunction** Chromosomes fail to separate in meiosis. One gamete gets an extra copy and another gets none.\n\n**Incorrect: Gene linkage** Linkage means nearby genes travel together; counts do not change.\n\n**Incorrect: Independent assortment** It mixes which homolog goes where, one per gamete.","how":{"ask":"What changes chromosome number in a gamete?","know":"Failed separation = non-disjunction.","steps":[{"text":"Crossing over swaps parts, not whole chromosomes.","strike":["Crossing over"]},{"text":"Linkage and assortment still give one copy each.","strike":["Gene linkage","Independent assortment"]},{"text":"Answer: Non-disjunction."}],"trap":"Crossing over: a meiosis word, but it never changes counts."},"teach":[{"text":"Extra or missing chromosome = separation failed."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Too many or too few chromosomes in a gamete → non-disjunction."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-113a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"immune branch and cell type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:113#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:113"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P1","passage":"Scientists measured six microRNAs (miRNAs) in heart biopsies from transplant patients with normal hearts or with acute rejection (AR). In AR hearts, miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204 rose about 4-fold, while miR-305, miR-412 and miR-519 fell to half of normal. The first three are made mostly by lymphocytes, and the last three are made mostly by heart muscle cells. In AR samples, CD8 (T cell) mRNA rose with miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204, but CD19 (B cell) and CD14 (monocyte) mRNA tracked no miRNA. Four sequences are miR-71 5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3', miR-88 5'-UGCAUAGCUUAGCAUUGACU-3', miR-305 5'-GCCGUAGCGGAUCCGUGCAG-3' and miR-412 5'-AGCUUACGAUGCAUAGCGUU-3'.","q":"The link observed between miRNA and mRNA levels in AR heart transplants indicates that miRNAs regulate genes in which type(s) of immune response(s)?","choices":["Humoral immunity only","Innate immunity only","Cell-mediated immunity only","Innate and cell-mediated immunity only"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"B cell mRNA tracked nothing","c":[0]},{"f":"Monocyte mRNA tracked nothing","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name the cell whose mRNA tracked the miRNAs, then name its immune branch."},"exp":"**Answer:** Cell-mediated immunity only\n\n**Incorrect: Humoral immunity only** B cell (CD19) mRNA did not track any miRNA.\n\n**Incorrect: Innate immunity only** Monocyte (CD14) mRNA, the innate marker, tracked no miRNA.\n\n**Correct: Cell-mediated immunity only** Only CD8 T cell mRNA rose with the miRNAs. T cells run cell-mediated immunity.\n\n**Incorrect: Innate and cell-mediated immunity only** The innate marker did not track, so innate is out.","how":{"ask":"Which immune branch do the linked genes belong to?","know":"T cells = cell-mediated; B cells = humoral; monocytes = innate.","steps":[{"text":"Find which mRNA moved with the miRNAs. Only the T cell marker did.","hl":"CD8 (T cell) mRNA rose with miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204"},{"text":"B cell and monocyte markers did not move. Humoral and innate are out.","hl":"CD19 (B cell) and CD14 (monocyte) mRNA tracked no miRNA","strike":["Humoral immunity only","Innate immunity only","Innate and cell-mediated immunity only"]},{"text":"T cells drive cell-mediated immunity. Answer: Cell-mediated immunity only."}],"trap":"Innate and cell-mediated tempts because rejection feels broad, but the monocyte marker never moved."},"teach":[{"text":"Match each marker to its cell, then its branch.","marks":[{"t":"CD8 (T cell)","note":"cell-mediated"},{"t":"CD19 (B cell)","note":"humoral"},{"t":"CD14 (monocyte)","note":"innate"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Only the markers that track the data count; map each to its immune branch."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-114a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:114#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:114"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P1","passage":"Scientists measured six microRNAs (miRNAs) in heart biopsies from transplant patients with normal hearts or with acute rejection (AR). In AR hearts, miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204 rose about 4-fold, while miR-305, miR-412 and miR-519 fell to half of normal. The first three are made mostly by lymphocytes, and the last three are made mostly by heart muscle cells. In AR samples, CD8 (T cell) mRNA rose with miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204, but CD19 (B cell) and CD14 (monocyte) mRNA tracked no miRNA. Four sequences are miR-71 5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3', miR-88 5'-UGCAUAGCUUAGCAUUGACU-3', miR-305 5'-GCCGUAGCGGAUCCGUGCAG-3' and miR-412 5'-AGCUUACGAUGCAUAGCGUU-3'.","q":"A patient whose transplanted heart shows which miRNA pattern is LEAST likely to benefit from immunosuppressive drugs?","choices":["high levels of miR-88","low levels of miR-519","high levels of miR-412","low levels of miR-305"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"High immune miRNA = rejection","c":[0]},{"f":"Low heart miRNA = rejection","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the pattern that looks like a normal heart, not rejection."},"exp":"**Answer:** high levels of miR-412\n\n**Incorrect: high levels of miR-88** High miR-88 marks rejection, so drugs would help.\n\n**Incorrect: low levels of miR-519** Low miR-519 marks rejection, so drugs would help.\n\n**Correct: high levels of miR-412** miR-412 falls in rejection. High levels look like a normal heart, so drugs add little.\n\n**Incorrect: low levels of miR-305** Low miR-305 marks rejection, so drugs would help.","how":{"ask":"Which pattern looks normal, not rejected?","know":"Drugs help only rejection.","steps":[{"text":"Rejection raises the immune miRNAs. High miR-88 = rejection.","hl":"miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204 rose about 4-fold","strike":["high levels of miR-88"]},{"text":"Rejection lowers the heart miRNAs. Low miR-519 or low miR-305 = rejection.","hl":"miR-305, miR-412 and miR-519 fell to half of normal","strike":["low levels of miR-519","low levels of miR-305"]},{"text":"High miR-412 is the normal pattern. Answer: high levels of miR-412."}],"trap":"Any 'high' choice can feel like rejection; check which miRNA group it is."},"teach":[{"text":"Sort the miRNAs into two groups and note each group's direction.","marks":[{"t":"rose about 4-fold","note":"up in rejection"},{"t":"fell to half of normal","note":"down in rejection"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** LEAST likely to benefit = the choice that matches the healthy pattern."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-115a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:115#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:115"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P1","passage":"Scientists measured six microRNAs (miRNAs) in heart biopsies from transplant patients with normal hearts or with acute rejection (AR). In AR hearts, miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204 rose about 4-fold, while miR-305, miR-412 and miR-519 fell to half of normal. The first three are made mostly by lymphocytes, and the last three are made mostly by heart muscle cells. In AR samples, CD8 (T cell) mRNA rose with miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204, but CD19 (B cell) and CD14 (monocyte) mRNA tracked no miRNA. Four sequences are miR-71 5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3', miR-88 5'-UGCAUAGCUUAGCAUUGACU-3', miR-305 5'-GCCGUAGCGGAUCCGUGCAG-3' and miR-412 5'-AGCUUACGAUGCAUAGCGUU-3'.","q":"The curve with the highest melting temperature belongs to which miRNA, assuming 100% base complementarity with its target sequence?","choices":["miR-71","miR-88","miR-412","miR-305"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"More G+C, higher Tm","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count G and C in each sequence; the most G+C melts highest."},"exp":"**Answer:** miR-305\n\n**Incorrect: miR-71** Only 5 G+C, the fewest.\n\n**Incorrect: miR-88** Only 8 G+C.\n\n**Incorrect: miR-412** Only 9 G+C.\n\n**Correct: miR-305** It has 14 G+C. G-C pairs have 3 hydrogen bonds, so this duplex melts highest.","how":{"ask":"Which duplex is most stable?","know":"G-C pairs (3 H-bonds) raise Tm more than A-U pairs (2).","steps":[{"text":"Count G+C: miR-71 has 5, miR-88 has 8.","hl":"miR-71 5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3'","strike":["miR-71","miR-88"]},{"text":"miR-412 has 9.","hl":"miR-412 5'-AGCUUACGAUGCAUAGCGUU-3'","strike":["miR-412"]},{"text":"miR-305 has 14, the most. Answer: miR-305.","hl":"miR-305 5'-GCCGUAGCGGAUCCGUGCAG-3'"}],"trap":"Picking by length; all four are 20 nt, so only G+C count matters."},"teach":[{"text":"Same length, so just count G and C.","marks":[{"t":"GCCGUAGCGGAUCCGUGCAG","note":"14 G+C"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Highest G+C count = highest melting temperature."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-116a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:116#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:116"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P1","passage":"Scientists measured six microRNAs (miRNAs) in heart biopsies from transplant patients with normal hearts or with acute rejection (AR). In AR hearts, miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204 rose about 4-fold, while miR-305, miR-412 and miR-519 fell to half of normal. The first three are made mostly by lymphocytes, and the last three are made mostly by heart muscle cells. In AR samples, CD8 (T cell) mRNA rose with miR-71, miR-88 and miR-204, but CD19 (B cell) and CD14 (monocyte) mRNA tracked no miRNA. Four sequences are miR-71 5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3', miR-88 5'-UGCAUAGCUUAGCAUUGACU-3', miR-305 5'-GCCGUAGCGGAUCCGUGCAG-3' and miR-412 5'-AGCUUACGAUGCAUAGCGUU-3'.","q":"Individual nucleotides within the backbone of the target mRNAs discussed in the passage are held together by:","choices":["peptide bonds.","hydrogen bonds.","phosphodiester linkages.","glycosidic linkages."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Peptide bonds join amino acids","c":[0]},{"f":"H-bonds join paired bases","c":[1]},{"f":"Glycosidic links join sugars","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Backbone = sugar-phosphate chain = phosphodiester."},"exp":"**Answer:** phosphodiester linkages.\n\n**Incorrect: peptide bonds.** These join amino acids in proteins.\n\n**Incorrect: hydrogen bonds.** These pair bases across two strands, not along a backbone.\n\n**Correct: phosphodiester linkages.** A phosphate links one sugar's 3' carbon to the next sugar's 5' carbon.\n\n**Incorrect: glycosidic linkages.** These join sugars, or a base to its sugar, not one nucleotide to the next.","how":{"ask":"What bond links one nucleotide to the next?","know":"Nucleic acid backbone = phosphodiester bonds.","steps":[{"text":"The strand runs 5' to 3' along a sugar-phosphate backbone.","hl":"5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3'"},{"text":"Peptide bonds are protein bonds. Hydrogen bonds pair bases between strands.","strike":["peptide bonds.","hydrogen bonds."]},{"text":"Glycosidic bonds join sugars. Answer: phosphodiester linkages.","strike":["glycosidic linkages."]}],"trap":"Hydrogen bonds tempt, but they hold base pairs, not the backbone."},"teach":[{"text":"The 5' and 3' ends name the sugar carbons that phosphates link.","marks":[{"t":"5'-UAUAGCAUUAAGUCAAUUGA-3'","note":"5' to 3' backbone"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Backbone = phosphodiester; base pairs = hydrogen bonds."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-117a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:117#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:117"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P2","passage":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate. These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria, and butyrate is also a main fuel for colon cells. Healthy guts are rich in Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes and have few Proteobacteria and Actinobacteria, but in bowel disease BD, Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall. Four species fall in BD: Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers to make butyrate; Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate; Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate; Kolbia (gram-negative) uses succinate to make propionate. One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate.","q":"From the passage, which bacterium can use mannose as an energy source?","choices":["Tarsella","Brennia","Velloria","Kolbia"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Mannose is a glucose isomer","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mannose is a glucose isomer, so find who eats glucose isomers."},"exp":"**Answer:** Velloria\n\n**Incorrect: Tarsella** It uses fiber plus H+.\n\n**Incorrect: Brennia** It uses acetate.\n\n**Correct: Velloria** It uses glucose and its isomers. Mannose is a glucose isomer.\n\n**Incorrect: Kolbia** It uses succinate.","how":{"ask":"Whose fuel list covers mannose?","know":"Mannose = C6H12O6, an isomer of glucose.","steps":[{"text":"Mannose has glucose's formula, so it is a glucose isomer."},{"text":"Velloria uses glucose and its isomers.","hl":"Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers"},{"text":"The others use fiber, acetate or succinate.","strike":["Tarsella","Brennia","Kolbia"]},{"text":"Answer: Velloria."}],"trap":"Tarsella eats fiber, a sugar polymer, but no isomer of glucose is named."},"teach":[{"text":"Read each species' fuel.","marks":[{"t":"glucose and its isomers","note":"covers mannose"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Glucose isomers include galactose, mannose and fructose."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-118a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:118#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:118"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P2","passage":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate. These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria, and butyrate is also a main fuel for colon cells. Healthy guts are rich in Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes and have few Proteobacteria and Actinobacteria, but in bowel disease BD, Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall. Four species fall in BD: Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers to make butyrate; Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate; Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate; Kolbia (gram-negative) uses succinate to make propionate. One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate.","q":"From the passage, in which metabolic process are gut bacteria directly involved?","choices":["Absorption of fatty acids","Conversion of fiber into short chain fatty acids","Fermentation of dietary fiber into amino acids","Absorption of disaccharides"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Absorption is done by host","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Products are fatty acids, not amino","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the choice that matches the listed inputs and products."},"exp":"**Answer:** Conversion of fiber into short chain fatty acids\n\n**Incorrect: Absorption of fatty acids** The passage gives bacteria no absorbing role; host gut cells absorb.\n\n**Correct: Conversion of fiber into short chain fatty acids** Bacteria ferment fiber into butyrate, propionate and acetate.\n\n**Incorrect: Fermentation of dietary fiber into amino acids** The products are fatty acids, not amino acids.\n\n**Incorrect: Absorption of disaccharides** No bacterium is said to absorb sugars for the host.","how":{"ask":"What do the bacteria actually make?","know":"Butyrate, propionate and acetate are short chain fatty acids.","steps":[{"text":"The bacteria turn fiber into three short chain fatty acids.","hl":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate"},{"text":"Absorption is the host's job, not described for bacteria.","strike":["Absorption of fatty acids","Absorption of disaccharides"]},{"text":"No amino acids are made. Answer: Conversion of fiber into short chain fatty acids.","strike":["Fermentation of dietary fiber into amino acids"]}],"trap":"Fermentation sounds right, but the product named is wrong."},"teach":[{"text":"Inputs and outputs are given right in the first line.","marks":[{"t":"short chain fatty acids","note":"the products"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Check both the input and the product word in each choice."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-119a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:119#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:119"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P2","passage":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate. These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria, and butyrate is also a main fuel for colon cells. Healthy guts are rich in Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes and have few Proteobacteria and Actinobacteria, but in bowel disease BD, Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall. Four species fall in BD: Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers to make butyrate; Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate; Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate; Kolbia (gram-negative) uses succinate to make propionate. One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate.","q":"Based on the passage, what is most likely associated with a reduction in gram-positive bacteria?","choices":["Increase in butyrate production","Increase in use of acetate","Decrease in pH","Decrease in LPS production"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Fewer makers, less product","c":[0]},{"f":"Fewer users, less use","c":[1]},{"f":"LPS maker is gram-negative","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"List what the gram-positives use and make, then reverse each."},"exp":"**Answer:** Decrease in pH\n\n**Incorrect: Increase in butyrate production** Brennia makes butyrate; fewer Brennia means less.\n\n**Incorrect: Increase in use of acetate** Brennia uses acetate; fewer Brennia means less use.\n\n**Correct: Decrease in pH** Tarsella eats H+. Fewer Tarsella leaves more H+, so pH drops.\n\n**Incorrect: Decrease in LPS production** Ostrobacter makes LPS, and it is gram-negative.","how":{"ask":"What changes when gram-positives disappear?","know":"Fewer H+ consumers = more H+ = lower pH.","steps":[{"text":"The gram-positives are Tarsella and Brennia.","hl":"Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate"},{"text":"Brennia uses acetate and makes butyrate. Losing it lowers both.","hl":"Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate","strike":["Increase in butyrate production","Increase in use of acetate"]},{"text":"LPS comes from gram-negative Ostrobacter.","strike":["Decrease in LPS production"]},{"text":"Losing Tarsella leaves extra H+. Answer: Decrease in pH."}],"trap":"Increase choices tempt, but losing bacteria lowers what they do."},"teach":[{"text":"H+ listed as a fuel means the bug removes acid.","marks":[{"t":"fiber plus H+","note":"eats acid"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Remove a group, then reverse every input and output it had."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-120a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:120#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:120"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P2","passage":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate. These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria, and butyrate is also a main fuel for colon cells. Healthy guts are rich in Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes and have few Proteobacteria and Actinobacteria, but in bowel disease BD, Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall. Four species fall in BD: Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers to make butyrate; Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate; Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate; Kolbia (gram-negative) uses succinate to make propionate. One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate.","q":"Based on the passage, the microbiome of BD patients will result in which physiological change?","choices":["Decreased immune tolerance","Increased protein digestion","Faster dietary fiber absorption","Increased amount of propionate"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"No bug digests protein","c":[1]},{"f":"Humans cannot absorb fiber","c":[2]},{"f":"Propionate maker falls in BD","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fewer acid makers means less calming of the immune system."},"exp":"**Answer:** Decreased immune tolerance\n\n**Correct: Decreased immune tolerance** The fatty acid makers fall in BD. Less fatty acid means less calming, so the gut reacts to normal bacteria.\n\n**Incorrect: Increased protein digestion** No species is linked to protein.\n\n**Incorrect: Faster dietary fiber absorption** Humans do not absorb fiber.\n\n**Incorrect: Increased amount of propionate** Kolbia makes propionate and falls in BD.","how":{"ask":"What happens when fatty acid makers drop?","know":"Short chain fatty acids calm gut immunity.","steps":[{"text":"The fatty acids calm the immune response.","hl":"These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria"},{"text":"The fatty acid makers fall in BD, including Kolbia, the propionate maker.","hl":"Four species fall in BD","strike":["Increased amount of propionate"]},{"text":"Nothing digests protein, and fiber is not absorbed.","strike":["Increased protein digestion","Faster dietary fiber absorption"]},{"text":"Less calming = less tolerance. Answer: Decreased immune tolerance."}],"trap":"Increased propionate tempts if you forget its maker falls in BD."},"teach":[{"text":"Link the product to its job.","marks":[{"t":"calm the immune response","note":"tolerance"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Fewer calming molecules = more inflammation = less tolerance."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-121a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:121#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:121"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P2","passage":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate. These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria, and butyrate is also a main fuel for colon cells. Healthy guts are rich in Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes and have few Proteobacteria and Actinobacteria, but in bowel disease BD, Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall. Four species fall in BD: Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers to make butyrate; Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate; Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate; Kolbia (gram-negative) uses succinate to make propionate. One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate.","q":"Based on the passage, to which phylum does Ostrobacter most likely belong?","choices":["Firmicutes","Proteobacteria","Actinobacteria","Bacteroidetes"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Those phyla fall in BD","c":[0,3]},{"f":"No BD data for Actinobacteria","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ostrobacter rises in BD; pick the phylum that rises in BD."},"exp":"**Answer:** Proteobacteria\n\n**Incorrect: Firmicutes** Firmicutes fall in BD; Ostrobacter rises.\n\n**Correct: Proteobacteria** Proteobacteria rise sharply in BD, just like Ostrobacter. Both are gram-negative.\n\n**Incorrect: Actinobacteria** The passage gives no BD trend for them, and they are gram-positive.\n\n**Incorrect: Bacteroidetes** Bacteroidetes fall in BD.","how":{"ask":"Which phylum moves the same way as Ostrobacter?","know":"A member follows its group's trend.","steps":[{"text":"Ostrobacter rises in BD.","hl":"One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative)"},{"text":"Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall in BD.","hl":"Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall","strike":["Firmicutes","Bacteroidetes"]},{"text":"Actinobacteria have no BD trend given.","strike":["Actinobacteria"]},{"text":"Answer: Proteobacteria."}],"trap":"Actinobacteria tempt because they are rare in healthy guts, but rare does not mean rising."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the species' direction to a phylum's direction.","marks":[{"t":"Proteobacteria rise sharply","note":"same as Ostrobacter"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Only a group with a stated matching trend can be chosen."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-122a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:122#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:122"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P2","passage":"Gut bacteria ferment dietary fiber into the short chain fatty acids butyrate, propionate and acetate. These acids calm the immune response to normal gut bacteria, and butyrate is also a main fuel for colon cells. Healthy guts are rich in Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes and have few Proteobacteria and Actinobacteria, but in bowel disease BD, Proteobacteria rise sharply while Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes fall. Four species fall in BD: Velloria (gram-negative) uses glucose and its isomers to make butyrate; Tarsella (gram-positive) uses fiber plus H+ to make acetate; Brennia (gram-positive) uses acetate to make butyrate; Kolbia (gram-negative) uses succinate to make propionate. One species rises in BD: Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate.","q":"Which product is particularly reduced in BD patients?","choices":["Lactate","Butyrate","LPS","Fructose"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Rising Ostrobacter makes those","c":[0,2]},{"f":"No species makes fructose","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the product whose makers all fall in BD."},"exp":"**Answer:** Butyrate\n\n**Incorrect: Lactate** Ostrobacter makes it, and Ostrobacter rises in BD.\n\n**Correct: Butyrate** Both butyrate makers, Velloria and Brennia, fall in BD.\n\n**Incorrect: LPS** Ostrobacter makes it, and Ostrobacter rises.\n\n**Incorrect: Fructose** No species makes fructose.","how":{"ask":"Which product loses its makers?","know":"Fewer makers = less product.","steps":[{"text":"Velloria and Brennia make butyrate, and both fall in BD.","hl":"Four species fall in BD"},{"text":"Lactate and LPS come from Ostrobacter, which rises.","hl":"Ostrobacter (gram-negative) uses fiber to make LPS and lactate","strike":["Lactate","LPS"]},{"text":"No one makes fructose. Answer: Butyrate.","strike":["Fructose"]}],"trap":"Lactate tempts if you miss that its maker rises."},"teach":[{"text":"Tag each product with its maker's direction.","marks":[{"t":"to make butyrate","note":"maker falls"},{"t":"to make LPS and lactate","note":"maker rises"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A product drops when its makers drop."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-123a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:123#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:123"],"q":"Which amino acid exhibits a side chain branched at the beta carbon?","choices":["Leucine","Threonine","Serine","Methionine"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Leucine branches at gamma","c":[0]},{"f":"Serine, methionine are unbranched","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Beta-branched = Val, Ile, Thr."},"exp":"**Answer:** Threonine\n\n**Incorrect: Leucine** Its branch is one carbon further out, at gamma.\n\n**Correct: Threonine** Its beta carbon holds both an OH and a CH3, so it branches there.\n\n**Incorrect: Serine** Its side chain is just CH2OH, no branch.\n\n**Incorrect: Methionine** Its side chain is a straight chain with sulfur.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain splits at the first side-chain carbon?","know":"Beta-branched: valine, isoleucine, threonine.","steps":[{"text":"Recall the beta-branched trio: Val, Ile, Thr."},{"text":"Leucine branches at gamma, not beta.","strike":["Leucine"]},{"text":"Serine and methionine do not branch.","strike":["Serine","Methionine"]},{"text":"Answer: Threonine."}],"trap":"Leucine tempts because it is branched, but the branch is at gamma."},"teach":[{"text":"Beta carbon = first carbon after the alpha carbon."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Beta-branched = Val, Ile, Thr; Leu is gamma-branched."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-124a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"receptor type and second messenger","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:124#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:124"],"q":"In contrast to hormones in classical endocrine signaling systems, neurotransmitters at a synapse are:","choices":["secreted by glands only.","not likely to bind cell receptors.","not transported by blood vessels.","usually transported by ducts."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Neurons release neurotransmitters","c":[0]},{"f":"All signals bind receptors","c":[1]},{"f":"Ducts carry exocrine products","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Endocrine = blood; synapse = crosses a tiny gap."},"exp":"**Answer:** not transported by blood vessels.\n\n**Incorrect: secreted by glands only.** Neurons release neurotransmitters.\n\n**Incorrect: not likely to bind cell receptors.** Neurotransmitters work by binding receptors.\n\n**Correct: not transported by blood vessels.** They diffuse across the synaptic cleft. Hormones travel in blood.\n\n**Incorrect: usually transported by ducts.** Ducts carry exocrine products like sweat.","how":{"ask":"How does a neurotransmitter reach its target?","know":"Endocrine signals ride the blood; synaptic signals cross the cleft.","steps":[{"text":"Neurotransmitters come from neurons, not glands.","strike":["secreted by glands only."]},{"text":"Every chemical signal must bind a receptor.","strike":["not likely to bind cell receptors."]},{"text":"Ducts are for exocrine glands.","strike":["usually transported by ducts."]},{"text":"They cross the cleft, not the blood. Answer: not transported by blood vessels."}],"trap":"Ducts tempt as a 'local' route, but ducts mean exocrine."},"teach":[{"text":"Sort signals by route: blood (endocrine), ducts (exocrine), short gap (synaptic, paracrine)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Only endocrine signals travel in the blood."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-125a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:125#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:125"],"q":"Which membrane transporter is electrogenic and translocates a net charge across the membrane?","choices":["Cl−–HCO3− exchanger","Na+–K+–2Cl− cotransporter","GLUT4 facilitative glucose transporter","Na+–K+ ATPase"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Charges cancel out","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Glucose carries no charge","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Add up the charges moved each cycle; nonzero = electrogenic."},"exp":"**Answer:** Na+–K+ ATPase\n\n**Incorrect: Cl−–HCO3− exchanger** One minus charge swaps for one minus charge. Net zero.\n\n**Incorrect: Na+–K+–2Cl− cotransporter** Two plus and two minus move together. Net zero.\n\n**Incorrect: GLUT4 facilitative glucose transporter** Glucose is uncharged.\n\n**Correct: Na+–K+ ATPase** It pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in. One net plus charge leaves each cycle.","how":{"ask":"Which transporter moves a net charge?","know":"Electrogenic = net charge moved per cycle is not zero.","steps":[{"text":"Exchanger: −1 one way, −1 the other. Net zero.","strike":["Cl−–HCO3− exchanger"]},{"text":"Cotransporter: +1 +1 −2 the same way. Net zero.","strike":["Na+–K+–2Cl− cotransporter"]},{"text":"Glucose has no charge.","strike":["GLUT4 facilitative glucose transporter"]},{"text":"3 Na+ out vs 2 K+ in leaves +1 out. Answer: Na+–K+ ATPase."}],"trap":"The cotransporter moves many ions, but its charges cancel."},"teach":[{"text":"Count charges, not ions."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Sum each ion's charge times its count; nonzero means electrogenic."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-126a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:126#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:126"],"q":"Which type of cells have a plasma membrane, lack a nucleus, are small fragments of a larger cell, and release granules that help blood clot?","choices":["Neutrophils","Erythrocytes","Platelets","Lymphocytes"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"White cells have nuclei","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Red cells carry oxygen, not granules","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No nucleus plus clotting = platelets."},"exp":"**Answer:** Platelets\n\n**Incorrect: Neutrophils** They have a lobed nucleus.\n\n**Incorrect: Erythrocytes** They lack a nucleus but are whole cells that carry oxygen, not clotting granules.\n\n**Correct: Platelets** They are fragments of megakaryocytes, lack a nucleus and release clotting granules.\n\n**Incorrect: Lymphocytes** They have a large nucleus.","how":{"ask":"Which cell matches every feature?","know":"Platelets = anucleate fragments that drive clotting.","steps":[{"text":"Neutrophils and lymphocytes have nuclei.","strike":["Neutrophils","Lymphocytes"]},{"text":"Red cells lack a nucleus but carry oxygen, not clotting granules.","strike":["Erythrocytes"]},{"text":"Answer: Platelets."}],"trap":"Erythrocytes tempt because they also lack a nucleus."},"teach":[{"text":"Check every feature, not just the first one that fits."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Anucleate in blood = red cells or platelets; clotting = platelets."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-127a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"negative feedback loop","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:127#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:127"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P3","passage":"Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low. EPO binds its receptor, EPOR, on precursor cells in red bone marrow and makes them mature into erythrocytes. As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO. Heart muscle cells also express EPOR, and in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow. Recombinant human EPO made in cultured hamster cells is given to patients with anemia from kidney failure.","q":"An increase in which of the following physiological variables is most likely to cause an increase in the amount of EPO released by the kidneys in a healthy human adult?","choices":["Blood oxygen saturation","Total number of mature erythrocytes","Altitude at which the person lives","Rate of erythrocyte maturation"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"More oxygen means less EPO","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the change that lowers tissue oxygen."},"exp":"**Answer:** Altitude at which the person lives\n\n**Incorrect: Blood oxygen saturation** More oxygen lowers EPO release.\n\n**Incorrect: Total number of mature erythrocytes** More red cells carry more oxygen, so EPO falls.\n\n**Correct: Altitude at which the person lives** Higher altitude means less oxygen, so tissue oxygen drops and EPO rises.\n\n**Incorrect: Rate of erythrocyte maturation** Faster maturation adds red cells and oxygen, so EPO falls.","how":{"ask":"What lowers tissue oxygen?","know":"Low oxygen raises EPO; more oxygen lowers it.","steps":[{"text":"Low tissue oxygen triggers EPO.","hl":"made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low"},{"text":"More oxygen or more red cells shuts EPO down.","hl":"As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO","strike":["Blood oxygen saturation","Total number of mature erythrocytes","Rate of erythrocyte maturation"]},{"text":"High altitude lowers oxygen. Answer: Altitude at which the person lives."}],"trap":"More red cells sounds like 'more EPO', but it is EPO's result, which shuts it off."},"teach":[{"text":"EPO runs on negative feedback.","marks":[{"t":"the kidneys release less EPO","note":"feedback off"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Anything that lowers oxygen raises EPO; anything that raises oxygen lowers it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-128a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:128#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:128"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P3","passage":"Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low. EPO binds its receptor, EPOR, on precursor cells in red bone marrow and makes them mature into erythrocytes. As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO. Heart muscle cells also express EPOR, and in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow. Recombinant human EPO made in cultured hamster cells is given to patients with anemia from kidney failure.","q":"Interested in treating heart attacks, researchers are developing forms of EPO that act on heart muscle cells without affecting erythrocyte production in the bone marrow. One benefit of such a form of EPO would be to:","choices":["promote apoptosis of damaged heart cells without affecting blood oxygen levels.","limit heart cell death without causing an immediate drop in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood.","prevent apoptosis in the heart without causing a harmful increase in blood viscosity.","promote heart healing without lowering the number of white blood cells."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"EPO blocks apoptosis, not promotes","c":[0]},{"f":"Normal EPO raises oxygen capacity","c":[1]},{"f":"EPO does not affect white cells","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Keep EPO's good effect; avoid its usual side effect, too many red cells."},"exp":"**Answer:** prevent apoptosis in the heart without causing a harmful increase in blood viscosity.\n\n**Incorrect: promote apoptosis of damaged heart cells without affecting blood oxygen levels.** EPO blocks apoptosis; more cell death would harm the heart.\n\n**Incorrect: limit heart cell death without causing an immediate drop in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood.** Normal EPO raises oxygen capacity, so there is no drop to avoid.\n\n**Correct: prevent apoptosis in the heart without causing a harmful increase in blood viscosity.** It keeps the anti-apoptosis benefit. It avoids extra red cells that thicken the blood.\n\n**Incorrect: promote heart healing without lowering the number of white blood cells.** EPO does not lower white cells, so there is nothing to avoid.","how":{"ask":"What good effect stays, and what side effect goes?","know":"Extra red cells make blood thicker.","steps":[{"text":"In heart cells, EPO blocks apoptosis.","hl":"in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow","strike":["promote apoptosis of damaged heart cells without affecting blood oxygen levels."]},{"text":"In marrow, EPO adds red cells, which raises oxygen capacity, not lowers it.","hl":"makes them mature into erythrocytes","strike":["limit heart cell death without causing an immediate drop in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood."]},{"text":"EPO is not linked to white cells.","strike":["promote heart healing without lowering the number of white blood cells."]},{"text":"Extra red cells thicken blood. Answer: prevent apoptosis in the heart without causing a harmful increase in blood viscosity."}],"trap":"The 'drop in oxygen capacity' choice flips EPO's real effect."},"teach":[{"text":"Find EPO's two effects in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"EPO blocks apoptosis","note":"keep this"},{"t":"mature into erythrocytes","note":"avoid this"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The benefit = keep the wanted effect and avoid the real side effect of the unwanted one."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-129a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"protein targeting and secretion","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:129#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:129"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P3","passage":"Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low. EPO binds its receptor, EPOR, on precursor cells in red bone marrow and makes them mature into erythrocytes. As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO. Heart muscle cells also express EPOR, and in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow. Recombinant human EPO made in cultured hamster cells is given to patients with anemia from kidney failure.","q":"Expression of the human EPO gene in Bacillus subtilis bacteria produced an EPO protein that did not raise erythrocyte counts when injected into humans. The most likely reason for this observation is that:","choices":["B. subtilis cannot glycosylate EPO in the same way that eukaryotic cells do.","bacterial ribosomes read the genetic code with different codon meanings.","bacteria are unable to make any protein longer than 100 amino acids.","only yeast cells have the machinery to translate human mRNA."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"The genetic code is universal","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Bacteria make long proteins","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Glycoprotein made in bacteria = missing sugars."},"exp":"**Answer:** B. subtilis cannot glycosylate EPO in the same way that eukaryotic cells do.\n\n**Correct: B. subtilis cannot glycosylate EPO in the same way that eukaryotic cells do.** EPO is a glycoprotein. Bacteria lack the ER and Golgi that add its sugars, so it may fold or work poorly.\n\n**Incorrect: bacterial ribosomes read the genetic code with different codon meanings.** The code is nearly universal.\n\n**Incorrect: bacteria are unable to make any protein longer than 100 amino acids.** Bacteria make many long proteins.\n\n**Incorrect: only yeast cells have the machinery to translate human mRNA.** Bacteria can translate human coding sequences too.","how":{"ask":"What step does a bacterium skip?","know":"Glycosylation happens in the ER and Golgi, which bacteria lack.","steps":[{"text":"EPO is a glycoprotein.","hl":"glycoprotein hormone"},{"text":"The genetic code is universal, so translation is not the problem.","strike":["bacterial ribosomes read the genetic code with different codon meanings.","only yeast cells have the machinery to translate human mRNA."]},{"text":"Bacteria make long proteins.","strike":["bacteria are unable to make any protein longer than 100 amino acids."]},{"text":"Answer: B. subtilis cannot glycosylate EPO in the same way that eukaryotic cells do."}],"trap":"Ribosome differences tempt, but codon meanings are the same."},"teach":[{"text":"Note why the working drug is made in hamster cells.","marks":[{"t":"made in cultured hamster cells","note":"eukaryote adds sugars"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Bacteria cannot add eukaryotic sugars; glycoproteins need eukaryotic hosts."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-130a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"dominance and inheritance rules","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:130#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:130"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P3","passage":"Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low. EPO binds its receptor, EPOR, on precursor cells in red bone marrow and makes them mature into erythrocytes. As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO. Heart muscle cells also express EPOR, and in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow. Recombinant human EPO made in cultured hamster cells is given to patients with anemia from kidney failure.","q":"Assume that a certain dominant mutation in the EPOR gene exists such that a person who carries this mutant EPOR allele has a higher-than-normal number of circulating erythrocytes. Which of the following best describes a mechanism by which this mutation could have its effect?","choices":["The mutant EPOR allele has a defective promoter and is not transcribed.","The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that cannot bind EPO.","The mRNA from the mutant EPOR allele is degraded before translation.","The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that stays active without EPO."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Loss of receptor lowers red cells","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"More red cells = more signaling = a gain of function."},"exp":"**Answer:** The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that stays active without EPO.\n\n**Incorrect: The mutant EPOR allele has a defective promoter and is not transcribed.** No receptor means less signaling and fewer red cells.\n\n**Incorrect: The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that cannot bind EPO.** No binding means less signaling.\n\n**Incorrect: The mRNA from the mutant EPOR allele is degraded before translation.** No receptor made means fewer red cells.\n\n**Correct: The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that stays active without EPO.** A receptor that is always on keeps telling precursors to mature, so red cells rise.","how":{"ask":"What change makes EPOR signal more?","know":"Dominant + more output = gain of function.","steps":[{"text":"EPOR signaling makes precursors mature into red cells.","hl":"makes them mature into erythrocytes"},{"text":"Three choices remove or block the receptor. That lowers red cells.","strike":["The mutant EPOR allele has a defective promoter and is not transcribed.","The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that cannot bind EPO.","The mRNA from the mutant EPOR allele is degraded before translation."]},{"text":"Answer: The mutant EPOR allele makes a receptor that stays active without EPO."}],"trap":"Loss choices look like 'mutations', but they cause the opposite effect."},"teach":[{"text":"Link receptor activity to the red cell count.","marks":[{"t":"EPO binds its receptor, EPOR","note":"signal start"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A dominant mutation that raises output is usually a gain of function."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-131a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"receptor type and second messenger","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:131#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:131"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P3","passage":"Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low. EPO binds its receptor, EPOR, on precursor cells in red bone marrow and makes them mature into erythrocytes. As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO. Heart muscle cells also express EPOR, and in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow. Recombinant human EPO made in cultured hamster cells is given to patients with anemia from kidney failure.","q":"In which of the following cellular locations does EPO most likely initially bind EPOR in heart muscle cells?","choices":["Nucleus","Golgi apparatus","Plasma membrane","Cytosol"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Glycoproteins cannot cross membranes","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Big polar hormone = receptor on the cell surface."},"exp":"**Answer:** Plasma membrane\n\n**Incorrect: Nucleus** EPO cannot cross the membrane to reach the nucleus.\n\n**Incorrect: Golgi apparatus** The Golgi processes proteins; it is not where outside hormones first bind.\n\n**Correct: Plasma membrane** EPO is a large glycoprotein. It cannot cross the lipid bilayer, so it binds a surface receptor.\n\n**Incorrect: Cytosol** EPO cannot enter the cytosol to bind.","how":{"ask":"Can EPO get inside the cell?","know":"Peptide and glycoprotein hormones bind surface receptors.","steps":[{"text":"EPO is a glycoprotein, large and polar.","hl":"glycoprotein hormone"},{"text":"It cannot cross the bilayer, so all inside spots are out.","strike":["Nucleus","Golgi apparatus","Cytosol"]},{"text":"Answer: Plasma membrane."}],"trap":"Nucleus tempts if you mix it up with steroid hormones."},"teach":[{"text":"The word glycoprotein tells you it stays outside.","marks":[{"t":"glycoprotein","note":"cannot cross membrane"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Protein hormones bind at the plasma membrane; steroids bind inside."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-132a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"negative feedback loop","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:132#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:132"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P3","passage":"Erythropoietin (EPO) is a glycoprotein hormone made mainly by the kidneys when tissue oxygen is low. EPO binds its receptor, EPOR, on precursor cells in red bone marrow and makes them mature into erythrocytes. As the blood carries more oxygen, the kidneys release less EPO. Heart muscle cells also express EPOR, and in these cells EPO blocks apoptosis after a loss of blood flow. Recombinant human EPO made in cultured hamster cells is given to patients with anemia from kidney failure.","q":"Certain types of liver tumors continuously produce and release EPO. Such tumors most likely have which of the following effects, if any, on erythrocyte production?","choices":["The spleen will take over the process of producing erythrocytes.","Constant stimulation of erythrocyte production will occur within the bone marrow.","Erythrocyte production within the bone marrow will stop.","There is no effect; the kidneys will keep regulating erythrocyte production based on oxygen levels."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Marrow makes red cells","c":[0]},{"f":"More EPO means more red cells","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"EPO from any source hits marrow EPOR; constant EPO = constant red cell production."},"exp":"**Answer:** Constant stimulation of erythrocyte production will occur within the bone marrow.\n\n**Incorrect: The spleen will take over the process of producing erythrocytes.** Adult red cells are made in red bone marrow.\n\n**Correct: Constant stimulation of erythrocyte production will occur within the bone marrow.** Tumor EPO reaches marrow EPOR no matter its source. It does not respond to oxygen feedback.\n\n**Incorrect: Erythrocyte production within the bone marrow will stop.** More EPO raises production.\n\n**Incorrect: There is no effect; the kidneys will keep regulating erythrocyte production based on oxygen levels.** The tumor's EPO ignores the kidney's feedback.","how":{"ask":"What does nonstop EPO do to marrow?","know":"A hormone acts wherever its receptor is, whatever made it.","steps":[{"text":"EPO acts on marrow precursors through EPOR.","hl":"on precursor cells in red bone marrow"},{"text":"The spleen is not the adult site.","strike":["The spleen will take over the process of producing erythrocytes."]},{"text":"More EPO raises, not stops, production; tumor EPO bypasses kidney feedback.","strike":["Erythrocyte production within the bone marrow will stop.","There is no effect; the kidneys will keep regulating erythrocyte production based on oxygen levels."]},{"text":"Answer: Constant stimulation of erythrocyte production will occur within the bone marrow."}],"trap":"'No effect' tempts if you think the kidneys still control things; the tumor ignores feedback."},"teach":[{"text":"Feedback only shuts off the kidneys, not the tumor.","marks":[{"t":"the kidneys release less EPO","note":"tumor ignores this"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An unregulated hormone source causes nonstop target stimulation."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-133a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"receptor type and second messenger","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:133#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:133"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P4","passage":"Drug R eases depression by blocking reuptake of dopamine from the synaptic cleft, so dopamine stays active longer. Drug R also raises brain levels of the cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6), which induces protein q7 in nearby neurons. Protein q7 binds DRX, a dopamine receptor that spans the plasma membrane. In cells with DRX alone, dopamine raised cAMP 4-fold; in cells with DRX and q7, dopamine raised cAMP 8-fold. In mice given Drug R for weeks, the anti-inflammatory drug prednisone blocked the rise in IL-6 and q7 and blocked the antidepressant behavior.","q":"According to the data presented in the passage, DRX is a:","choices":["ligand-gated ion channel.","G protein-coupled receptor.","receptor tyrosine kinase.","nuclear hormone receptor."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"cAMP rise = G protein","c":[0,2]},{"f":"DRX spans the plasma membrane","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Membrane receptor + cAMP rise = GPCR."},"exp":"**Answer:** G protein-coupled receptor.\n\n**Incorrect: ligand-gated ion channel.** These pass ions; they do not raise cAMP.\n\n**Correct: G protein-coupled receptor.** DRX is in the membrane and raises cAMP. Gs proteins turn on adenylyl cyclase to make cAMP.\n\n**Incorrect: receptor tyrosine kinase.** These signal by phosphorylating tyrosines, not by making cAMP.\n\n**Incorrect: nuclear hormone receptor.** DRX is in the plasma membrane, not the nucleus.","how":{"ask":"Which receptor type raises cAMP?","know":"cAMP is a G protein second messenger.","steps":[{"text":"DRX sits in the plasma membrane.","hl":"a dopamine receptor that spans the plasma membrane","strike":["nuclear hormone receptor."]},{"text":"Dopamine on DRX raised cAMP.","hl":"dopamine raised cAMP 4-fold"},{"text":"Channels and kinases do not make cAMP. Answer: G protein-coupled receptor.","strike":["ligand-gated ion channel.","receptor tyrosine kinase."]}],"trap":"Ion channel tempts because neurotransmitters often open channels."},"teach":[{"text":"The data point to the second messenger.","marks":[{"t":"raised cAMP","note":"G protein sign"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** cAMP change after ligand binding = GPCR."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-134a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"synaptic transmission","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:134#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:134"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P4","passage":"Drug R eases depression by blocking reuptake of dopamine from the synaptic cleft, so dopamine stays active longer. Drug R also raises brain levels of the cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6), which induces protein q7 in nearby neurons. Protein q7 binds DRX, a dopamine receptor that spans the plasma membrane. In cells with DRX alone, dopamine raised cAMP 4-fold; in cells with DRX and q7, dopamine raised cAMP 8-fold. In mice given Drug R for weeks, the anti-inflammatory drug prednisone blocked the rise in IL-6 and q7 and blocked the antidepressant behavior.","q":"From which amino acid is dopamine synthesized?","choices":["Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Histidine","Glutamate"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Tryptophan makes serotonin","c":[0]},{"f":"Histidine makes histamine","c":[2]},{"f":"Glutamate makes GABA","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Catecholamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine) come from tyrosine."},"exp":"**Answer:** Tyrosine\n\n**Incorrect: Tryptophan** It makes serotonin, with an indole ring.\n\n**Correct: Tyrosine** Tyrosine is hydroxylated to L-DOPA, then decarboxylated to dopamine. Both keep the phenol ring.\n\n**Incorrect: Histidine** It makes histamine.\n\n**Incorrect: Glutamate** It makes GABA.","how":{"ask":"Which amino acid has dopamine's ring?","know":"Tyrosine → L-DOPA → dopamine.","steps":[{"text":"Dopamine is the transmitter Drug R keeps active.","hl":"blocking reuptake of dopamine"},{"text":"Match each amino acid to its transmitter.","strike":["Tryptophan","Histidine","Glutamate"]},{"text":"Dopamine has a phenol-based ring, like tyrosine. Answer: Tyrosine."}],"trap":"Tryptophan tempts because it makes serotonin, the other mood transmitter."},"teach":[{"text":"Know the four precursor pairs.","marks":[{"t":"dopamine","note":"from tyrosine"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Tyr → catecholamines; Trp → serotonin; His → histamine; Glu → GABA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-135a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:135#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:135"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P4","passage":"Drug R eases depression by blocking reuptake of dopamine from the synaptic cleft, so dopamine stays active longer. Drug R also raises brain levels of the cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6), which induces protein q7 in nearby neurons. Protein q7 binds DRX, a dopamine receptor that spans the plasma membrane. In cells with DRX alone, dopamine raised cAMP 4-fold; in cells with DRX and q7, dopamine raised cAMP 8-fold. In mice given Drug R for weeks, the anti-inflammatory drug prednisone blocked the rise in IL-6 and q7 and blocked the antidepressant behavior.","q":"In an experiment in which prednisone alone was given to normal mice to determine whether long-term treatment with this drug can cause behavioral symptoms of depression, what would be the appropriate control group?","choices":["Normal mice treated with a placebo","Normal mice treated with Drug R","Depressed mice treated with prednisone","Depressed mice treated with IL-6"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Control changes only the drug","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Test mice must start normal","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same mice, same handling, no drug: placebo."},"exp":"**Answer:** Normal mice treated with a placebo\n\n**Correct: Normal mice treated with a placebo** These match the test mice in every way except prednisone. Any new depression can then be blamed on prednisone.\n\n**Incorrect: Normal mice treated with Drug R** Drug R is an antidepressant and adds a second change.\n\n**Incorrect: Depressed mice treated with prednisone** The test is on normal mice, so the control must be normal too.\n\n**Incorrect: Depressed mice treated with IL-6** It changes both the mice and the treatment.","how":{"ask":"What group differs only by the drug?","know":"A control matches the test group except for the one variable.","steps":[{"text":"The test group is normal mice given prednisone.","hl":"the anti-inflammatory drug prednisone"},{"text":"Depressed mice change the starting state.","strike":["Depressed mice treated with prednisone","Depressed mice treated with IL-6"]},{"text":"Drug R adds a new treatment.","strike":["Normal mice treated with Drug R"]},{"text":"Answer: Normal mice treated with a placebo."}],"trap":"Drug R tempts as a 'comparison drug', but it adds a second variable."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the one variable first.","marks":[{"t":"prednisone","note":"the one variable"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The right control = same subjects, placebo instead of the test drug."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-136a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:136#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:136"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P4","passage":"Drug R eases depression by blocking reuptake of dopamine from the synaptic cleft, so dopamine stays active longer. Drug R also raises brain levels of the cytokine interleukin-6 (IL-6), which induces protein q7 in nearby neurons. Protein q7 binds DRX, a dopamine receptor that spans the plasma membrane. In cells with DRX alone, dopamine raised cAMP 4-fold; in cells with DRX and q7, dopamine raised cAMP 8-fold. In mice given Drug R for weeks, the anti-inflammatory drug prednisone blocked the rise in IL-6 and q7 and blocked the antidepressant behavior.","q":"Which comparison best tests whether IL-6 is required for the Drug R–induced rise in q7? Expression levels of q7 in:","choices":["wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with q7","wild-type mice treated with IL-6 versus wild-type mice treated with Drug R","wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with Drug R","wild-type mice treated with IL-6 versus wild-type mice treated with prednisone"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Both groups need Drug R","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Required? Remove it (knockout) and give both groups the trigger."},"exp":"**Answer:** wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with Drug R\n\n**Incorrect: wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with q7** Giving q7 masks the q7 rise you want to measure.\n\n**Incorrect: wild-type mice treated with IL-6 versus wild-type mice treated with Drug R** No group lacks IL-6, so need cannot be tested.\n\n**Correct: wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with Drug R** Both get the trigger; only IL-6 differs. If q7 fails to rise in knockouts, IL-6 is required.\n\n**Incorrect: wild-type mice treated with IL-6 versus wild-type mice treated with prednisone** Neither group gets Drug R, and two variables differ.","how":{"ask":"How do you test that X is needed for Y?","know":"Remove X, keep the trigger, measure Y.","steps":[{"text":"The claim: Drug R raises IL-6, and IL-6 induces q7.","hl":"which induces protein q7 in nearby neurons"},{"text":"Both groups must get Drug R, the trigger.","strike":["wild-type mice treated with IL-6 versus wild-type mice treated with Drug R","wild-type mice treated with IL-6 versus wild-type mice treated with prednisone"]},{"text":"Giving q7 hides the effect being measured.","strike":["wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with q7"]},{"text":"Answer: wild-type mice versus IL-6 knockout mice, both treated with Drug R."}],"trap":"The q7-treated knockout pair looks right, but it gives the output you are measuring."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the chain: drug, then cytokine, then protein.","marks":[{"t":"interleukin-6 (IL-6)","note":"remove this"},{"t":"protein q7","note":"measure this"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To test a need, knock out the middle step and give both groups the same trigger."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-137a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:137#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:137"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P5","passage":"The marine bacterium Halomonas K builds a sodium gradient across its membrane with the enzyme complex Ndx. Ndx takes electrons from NADH, passes them to quinone, and uses the energy to pump Na+ out of the cell. The cell uses this sodium motive force, not a proton gradient, to make ATP. Ndx has six subunits: NdxA is 52.0 kDa with pI 6.10, NdxB is 51.7 kDa with pI 8.20, and NdxC is 30.4 kDa with pI 7.40. NdxD is 24.9 kDa with pI 5.60, NdxE is 44.6 kDa with pI 5.65, and NdxF is 18.2 kDa with pI 9.00.","q":"Based on information presented in the passage, which relationship between pH and charged functional groups is accurate?","choices":["At pH 6.10, the net charge of the cationic and anionic functional groups in the NdxA subunit is equal to 1.","At pH 7.40, the ratio of the cationic to anionic functional groups in the NdxC subunit is equal to 1.","At pH 7.00, half of the functional groups in the NdxD subunit are protonated.","At pH 9.50, the majority of the functional groups in the NdxF subunit are protonated."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"At pI, net charge is zero","c":[0]},{"f":"Above pI, groups lose protons","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH = pI, plus charges equal minus charges."},"exp":"**Answer:** At pH 7.40, the ratio of the cationic to anionic functional groups in the NdxC subunit is equal to 1.\n\n**Incorrect: At pH 6.10, the net charge of the cationic and anionic functional groups in the NdxA subunit is equal to 1.** pH 6.10 is NdxA's pI, so net charge is zero, not 1.\n\n**Correct: At pH 7.40, the ratio of the cationic to anionic functional groups in the NdxC subunit is equal to 1.** pH 7.40 is NdxC's pI. Plus and minus groups are equal, so the ratio is 1.\n\n**Incorrect: At pH 7.00, half of the functional groups in the NdxD subunit are protonated.** pH 7.00 is above NdxD's pI of 5.60, so most groups are deprotonated.\n\n**Incorrect: At pH 9.50, the majority of the functional groups in the NdxF subunit are protonated.** pH 9.50 is above NdxF's pI of 9.00, so groups lose protons.","how":{"ask":"What is true at, above or below a pI?","know":"pH = pI: net charge 0, + equals −. pH > pI: deprotonated.","steps":[{"text":"NdxC's pI is 7.40, so at pH 7.40 the + and − groups match.","hl":"NdxC is 30.4 kDa with pI 7.40"},{"text":"NdxA at its pI has net charge zero, not 1.","strike":["At pH 6.10, the net charge of the cationic and anionic functional groups in the NdxA subunit is equal to 1."]},{"text":"NdxD at pH 7.00 and NdxF at pH 9.50 are above their pIs, so they are mostly deprotonated.","hl":"NdxD is 24.9 kDa with pI 5.60","strike":["At pH 7.00, half of the functional groups in the NdxD subunit are protonated.","At pH 9.50, the majority of the functional groups in the NdxF subunit are protonated."]},{"text":"Answer: the NdxC ratio equals 1."}],"trap":"'Net charge equal to 1' mixes up ratio = 1 with net charge = 0."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare each pH to that subunit's pI.","marks":[{"t":"pI 7.40","note":"pH = pI"},{"t":"pI 9.00","note":"pH 9.50 is above"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** At pI, ratio = 1 and net = 0; above pI, protons leave."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-138a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"electron transport and gradient","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:138#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:138"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P5","passage":"The marine bacterium Halomonas K builds a sodium gradient across its membrane with the enzyme complex Ndx. Ndx takes electrons from NADH, passes them to quinone, and uses the energy to pump Na+ out of the cell. The cell uses this sodium motive force, not a proton gradient, to make ATP. Ndx has six subunits: NdxA is 52.0 kDa with pI 6.10, NdxB is 51.7 kDa with pI 8.20, and NdxC is 30.4 kDa with pI 7.40. NdxD is 24.9 kDa with pI 5.60, NdxE is 44.6 kDa with pI 5.65, and NdxF is 18.2 kDa with pI 9.00.","q":"What is the most likely effect of adding a sodium ionophore to a culture of Halomonas K?","choices":["Blocked binding of NADH to Ndx","Increased size of the Na+ gradient","Decreased pH of the periplasm","Decreased production of ATP"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Ionophore leaks ions, not blocks enzyme","c":[0]},{"f":"Leak shrinks the gradient","c":[1]},{"f":"Gradient is Na+, not H+","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ionophore = ion leak = gradient gone = less ATP."},"exp":"**Answer:** Decreased production of ATP\n\n**Incorrect: Blocked binding of NADH to Ndx** An ionophore carries ions across membranes; it does not block the enzyme's NADH site.\n\n**Incorrect: Increased size of the Na+ gradient** Leaking Na+ back in shrinks the gradient.\n\n**Incorrect: Decreased pH of the periplasm** The gradient is Na+, so H+ levels are not directly changed.\n\n**Correct: Decreased production of ATP** The ionophore lets Na+ flow back in, collapsing the sodium motive force that makes ATP.","how":{"ask":"What happens when the Na+ gradient leaks?","know":"Ionophores carry ions across membranes and erase gradients.","steps":[{"text":"The cell makes ATP from the Na+ gradient.","hl":"The cell uses this sodium motive force, not a proton gradient, to make ATP"},{"text":"An ionophore lets Na+ leak back, so the gradient shrinks.","strike":["Increased size of the Na+ gradient"]},{"text":"It does not touch NADH binding or H+.","strike":["Blocked binding of NADH to Ndx","Decreased pH of the periplasm"]},{"text":"Answer: Decreased production of ATP."}],"trap":"pH tempts from mitochondria, but this gradient is sodium."},"teach":[{"text":"Find what the gradient powers.","marks":[{"t":"sodium motive force","note":"powers ATP"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Collapse the gradient and you lose what it powers."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-139a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:139#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:139"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P5","passage":"The marine bacterium Halomonas K builds a sodium gradient across its membrane with the enzyme complex Ndx. Ndx takes electrons from NADH, passes them to quinone, and uses the energy to pump Na+ out of the cell. The cell uses this sodium motive force, not a proton gradient, to make ATP. Ndx has six subunits: NdxA is 52.0 kDa with pI 6.10, NdxB is 51.7 kDa with pI 8.20, and NdxC is 30.4 kDa with pI 7.40. NdxD is 24.9 kDa with pI 5.60, NdxE is 44.6 kDa with pI 5.65, and NdxF is 18.2 kDa with pI 9.00.","q":"Which two subunits of Ndx can be separated by gel filtration but NOT by ion exchange chromatography?","choices":["NdxA and NdxB","NdxC and NdxF","NdxD and NdxE","NdxB and NdxD"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Same size: gel filtration fails","c":[0]},{"f":"Different pI: ion exchange works","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Need different size but the same pI."},"exp":"**Answer:** NdxD and NdxE\n\n**Incorrect: NdxA and NdxB** Their sizes match (52.0 vs 51.7 kDa), so gel filtration cannot split them.\n\n**Incorrect: NdxC and NdxF** Their pIs differ (7.40 vs 9.00), so ion exchange can split them.\n\n**Correct: NdxD and NdxE** Sizes differ (24.9 vs 44.6 kDa), so gel filtration works. pIs nearly match (5.60 vs 5.65), so ion exchange fails.\n\n**Incorrect: NdxB and NdxD** Their pIs differ (8.20 vs 5.60), so ion exchange works.","how":{"ask":"Which pair differs in size but not charge?","know":"Gel filtration = size; ion exchange = charge (pI).","steps":[{"text":"NdxA and NdxB are the same size.","hl":"NdxA is 52.0 kDa with pI 6.10, NdxB is 51.7 kDa with pI 8.20","strike":["NdxA and NdxB"]},{"text":"NdxC/NdxF and NdxB/NdxD have very different pIs.","strike":["NdxC and NdxF","NdxB and NdxD"]},{"text":"NdxD and NdxE differ in size but share a pI. Answer: NdxD and NdxE.","hl":"NdxD is 24.9 kDa with pI 5.60, NdxE is 44.6 kDa with pI 5.65"}],"trap":"NdxA and NdxB is the reverse case: split by charge, not size."},"teach":[{"text":"Check two columns: size and pI.","marks":[{"t":"pI 5.60","note":"nearly same pI"},{"t":"pI 5.65","note":"nearly same pI"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Gel filtration needs a size gap; ion exchange needs a pI gap."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-140a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:140#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:140"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P5","passage":"The marine bacterium Halomonas K builds a sodium gradient across its membrane with the enzyme complex Ndx. Ndx takes electrons from NADH, passes them to quinone, and uses the energy to pump Na+ out of the cell. The cell uses this sodium motive force, not a proton gradient, to make ATP. Ndx has six subunits: NdxA is 52.0 kDa with pI 6.10, NdxB is 51.7 kDa with pI 8.20, and NdxC is 30.4 kDa with pI 7.40. NdxD is 24.9 kDa with pI 5.60, NdxE is 44.6 kDa with pI 5.65, and NdxF is 18.2 kDa with pI 9.00.","q":"Which step of the citric acid cycle is NOT directly involved in generating the dinucleotide required for Ndx activity?","choices":["Isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate","Malate to oxaloacetate","α-Ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA","Succinate to fumarate"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"These steps make NADH","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ndx needs NADH; the one cycle step that makes FADH2 instead is succinate to fumarate."},"exp":"**Answer:** Succinate to fumarate\n\n**Incorrect: Isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate** This step makes NADH.\n\n**Incorrect: Malate to oxaloacetate** This step makes NADH.\n\n**Incorrect: α-Ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA** This step makes NADH.\n\n**Correct: Succinate to fumarate** Succinate dehydrogenase makes FADH2, not NADH.","how":{"ask":"Which step does not make NADH?","know":"Three cycle steps make NADH; succinate → fumarate makes FADH2.","steps":[{"text":"Ndx uses NADH.","hl":"Ndx takes electrons from NADH"},{"text":"These three steps each make NADH.","strike":["Isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate","Malate to oxaloacetate","α-Ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA"]},{"text":"Succinate to fumarate makes FADH2. Answer: Succinate to fumarate."}],"trap":"Any dehydrogenase step can look like NADH; succinate DH uses FAD."},"teach":[{"text":"First name the carrier the enzyme needs.","marks":[{"t":"electrons from NADH","note":"needs NADH"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Citric acid cycle: 3 NADH steps, 1 FADH2 step (succinate → fumarate)."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-141a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:141#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:141"],"q":"Which biochemical technique requires an antibody probe?","choices":["Northern blotting","Gel filtration","Western blotting","Isoelectric focusing"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Northern uses nucleic acid probe","c":[0]},{"f":"Separations need no probe","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Western = protein = antibody."},"exp":"**Answer:** Western blotting\n\n**Incorrect: Northern blotting** It finds RNA with a labeled nucleic acid probe.\n\n**Incorrect: Gel filtration** It separates by size; no probe needed.\n\n**Correct: Western blotting** It finds a protein on a membrane by binding an antibody to it.\n\n**Incorrect: Isoelectric focusing** It separates by pI on a pH gradient; no probe needed.","how":{"ask":"Which method detects with antibodies?","know":"Southern = DNA, Northern = RNA, Western = protein (antibody).","steps":[{"text":"Northern blots use a nucleic acid probe.","strike":["Northern blotting"]},{"text":"Gel filtration and focusing only separate.","strike":["Gel filtration","Isoelectric focusing"]},{"text":"Answer: Western blotting."}],"trap":"Northern tempts because it is also a blot with a probe."},"teach":[{"text":"Name each method's basis: size, charge, or probe."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Only Western blotting (and ELISA) needs an antibody."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-142a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"x-linked cross probability","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:142#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:142"],"q":"The gene for hemophilia A is recessive and X-linked. What will be the expected phenotype of the children of a woman who is a carrier and a man who has hemophilia A?","choices":["All of the children will have normal clotting.","All of the daughters will clot normally; half of the sons will have hemophilia.","All of the sons will have hemophilia; all of the daughters will be carriers.","Half of the daughters and half of the sons will have hemophilia."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Dad gives daughters his Xh","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Sons get X from mom","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sons take mom's X; daughters take dad's X plus one of mom's."},"exp":"**Answer:** Half of the daughters and half of the sons will have hemophilia.\n\n**Incorrect: All of the children will have normal clotting.** Half of sons get mom's mutant X.\n\n**Incorrect: All of the daughters will clot normally; half of the sons will have hemophilia.** Every daughter gets dad's mutant X, so half are affected.\n\n**Incorrect: All of the sons will have hemophilia; all of the daughters will be carriers.** Sons get mom's X, and only half of hers carry the allele.\n\n**Correct: Half of the daughters and half of the sons will have hemophilia.** Mom is XHXh, dad is XhY. Daughters are XHXh or XhXh; sons are XHY or XhY.","how":{"ask":"What does each sex inherit?","know":"Sons: mom's X + dad's Y. Daughters: dad's X + one of mom's.","steps":[{"text":"Mom XHXh, dad XhY."},{"text":"Daughters all get Xh from dad, plus XH or Xh from mom. Half affected.","strike":["All of the children will have normal clotting.","All of the daughters will clot normally; half of the sons will have hemophilia."]},{"text":"Sons get XH or Xh from mom. Half affected.","strike":["All of the sons will have hemophilia; all of the daughters will be carriers."]},{"text":"Answer: Half of the daughters and half of the sons will have hemophilia."}],"trap":"Forgetting that an affected dad passes his X to every daughter."},"teach":[{"text":"Draw a 2×2 square: XH, Xh down; Xh, Y across."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In X-linked crosses, sons reflect mom's two X's; daughters always carry dad's X."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-143a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"activation energy vs free energy","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:143#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:143"],"q":"Which type of reaction has a Keq < 1 and is kinetically slow?","choices":["Exergonic with low activation energy level","Endergonic with high activation energy level","Exergonic with high activation energy level","Endergonic with low activation energy level"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Keq < 1 means endergonic","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Low Ea means fast","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Keq sets the sign of ΔG; Ea sets the speed."},"exp":"**Answer:** Endergonic with high activation energy level\n\n**Incorrect: Exergonic with low activation energy level** Keq < 1 means endergonic, and low Ea means fast.\n\n**Correct: Endergonic with high activation energy level** Keq < 1 gives ΔG > 0 (endergonic). High Ea makes it slow.\n\n**Incorrect: Exergonic with high activation energy level** Keq < 1 rules out exergonic.\n\n**Incorrect: Endergonic with low activation energy level** Low Ea makes a reaction fast, not slow.","how":{"ask":"Which pair of labels fits Keq < 1 and slow?","know":"ΔG° = −RT ln Keq; Keq < 1 → ΔG° > 0. High Ea → slow.","steps":[{"text":"Keq < 1 → endergonic.","strike":["Exergonic with low activation energy level","Exergonic with high activation energy level"]},{"text":"Slow → high activation energy.","strike":["Endergonic with low activation energy level"]},{"text":"Answer: Endergonic with high activation energy level."}],"trap":"Mixing up thermodynamics (Keq) with kinetics (Ea)."},"teach":[{"text":"Split the question into two parts: Keq for direction, Ea for speed."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Keq tells favorability; activation energy tells speed; they are separate."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-144a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:144#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:144"],"q":"Blood glucose falls during a long fast at time point A and rises sharply after a large meal at time point B. In response to these changing glucose levels, which events occur at time points A and B?","choices":["Pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point A, and pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon at point B.","Hepatocytes secrete glucagon at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point B.","Pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point B.","Pancreatic alpha cells secrete insulin at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete glucagon at point B."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Low glucose triggers glucagon","c":[0]},{"f":"Liver does not secrete glucagon","c":[1]},{"f":"Alpha = glucagon, beta = insulin","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Low glucose → alpha → glucagon; high glucose → beta → insulin."},"exp":"**Answer:** Pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point B.\n\n**Incorrect: Pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point A, and pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon at point B.** It is backward: low glucose at A calls for glucagon.\n\n**Incorrect: Hepatocytes secrete glucagon at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point B.** The liver responds to glucagon; it does not make it.\n\n**Correct: Pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point B.** Fasting glucose is low, so alpha cells release glucagon. After a meal, beta cells release insulin.\n\n**Incorrect: Pancreatic alpha cells secrete insulin at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete glucagon at point B.** The cell types are swapped.","how":{"ask":"Which hormone and which cell at low and high glucose?","know":"Alpha cells → glucagon (low glucose); beta cells → insulin (high glucose).","steps":[{"text":"A is low glucose, so glucagon is needed.","strike":["Pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point A, and pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon at point B."]},{"text":"Glucagon comes from pancreatic alpha cells, not the liver.","strike":["Hepatocytes secrete glucagon at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete insulin at point B."]},{"text":"Alpha makes glucagon; beta makes insulin.","strike":["Pancreatic alpha cells secrete insulin at point A, and pancreatic beta cells secrete glucagon at point B."]},{"text":"Answer: alpha cells glucagon at A, beta cells insulin at B."}],"trap":"Hepatocytes tempt because the liver releases glucose in response to glucagon."},"teach":[{"text":"Label each time point high or low before reading the choices."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Alpha = glucagon = low sugar; beta = insulin = high sugar."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-145a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"weigh evidence against model","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:145#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:145"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P6","passage":"Two models of membrane structure have been proposed. In the Protein Coat Model, phospholipids form a bilayer with tails inside and heads outside, and a flat sheet of protein covers each face without entering the bilayer. Supporters cite electron micrographs that show two dark outer lines around a lighter middle band. In the Fluid Mosaic Model, proteins sit within the phospholipid bilayer, with their polar parts facing water and their nonpolar parts facing the lipid tails. Supporters cite an experiment in which a mouse cell and a human cell were fused, and their surface proteins fully mixed within 40 minutes.","q":"How does the Protein Coat Model differ from the Fluid Mosaic Model?","choices":["The two models place proteins in different locations.","The Protein Coat Model has one layer of phospholipids, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has two layers.","The Protein Coat Model has a single protein sheet on each face, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has a double protein sheet on each face.","The Protein Coat Model has proteins inside the bilayer, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has a protein sheet on each face."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Both models use a bilayer","c":[1]},{"f":"Fluid Mosaic has no protein sheet","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Both have a bilayer; only protein placement differs."},"exp":"**Answer:** The two models place proteins in different locations.\n\n**Correct: The two models place proteins in different locations.** The Protein Coat Model puts protein sheets outside the bilayer. The Fluid Mosaic Model puts proteins inside it.\n\n**Incorrect: The Protein Coat Model has one layer of phospholipids, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has two layers.** Both models use a bilayer.\n\n**Incorrect: The Protein Coat Model has a single protein sheet on each face, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has a double protein sheet on each face.** The Fluid Mosaic Model has no protein sheets.\n\n**Incorrect: The Protein Coat Model has proteins inside the bilayer, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has a protein sheet on each face.** This swaps the two models.","how":{"ask":"What is the one real difference?","know":"Both are bilayers; protein placement differs.","steps":[{"text":"Protein Coat: protein sheets on the faces, not inside.","hl":"a flat sheet of protein covers each face without entering the bilayer"},{"text":"Fluid Mosaic: proteins inside the bilayer.","hl":"proteins sit within the phospholipid bilayer"},{"text":"Both use a bilayer, and the Fluid Mosaic has no sheets.","strike":["The Protein Coat Model has one layer of phospholipids, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has two layers.","The Protein Coat Model has a single protein sheet on each face, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has a double protein sheet on each face.","The Protein Coat Model has proteins inside the bilayer, while the Fluid Mosaic Model has a protein sheet on each face."]},{"text":"Answer: The two models place proteins in different locations."}],"trap":"The swapped choice sounds detailed, but it reverses the models."},"teach":[{"text":"Write one line per model: where are the proteins?","marks":[{"t":"without entering the bilayer","note":"outside only"},{"t":"proteins sit within the phospholipid bilayer","note":"inside"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** When comparing models, find the one feature that differs and check each choice against it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-146a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"weigh evidence against model","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:146#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:146"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P6","passage":"Two models of membrane structure have been proposed. In the Protein Coat Model, phospholipids form a bilayer with tails inside and heads outside, and a flat sheet of protein covers each face without entering the bilayer. Supporters cite electron micrographs that show two dark outer lines around a lighter middle band. In the Fluid Mosaic Model, proteins sit within the phospholipid bilayer, with their polar parts facing water and their nonpolar parts facing the lipid tails. Supporters cite an experiment in which a mouse cell and a human cell were fused, and their surface proteins fully mixed within 40 minutes.","q":"Which of the following observations would invalidate the Protein Coat Model?","choices":["Electron micrographs show two dark lines around a lighter band.","Labeling shows a single protein chain exposed on both faces of the membrane.","Measurements show that the phospholipid tails are shielded from water.","Chemical tests show that the membrane contains both lipid and protein."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Consistent findings cannot disprove it","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the fact the model says cannot happen: protein crossing the bilayer."},"exp":"**Answer:** Labeling shows a single protein chain exposed on both faces of the membrane.\n\n**Incorrect: Electron micrographs show two dark lines around a lighter band.** This is the model's own support.\n\n**Correct: Labeling shows a single protein chain exposed on both faces of the membrane.** One chain on both faces must cross the bilayer. The model says protein never enters it.\n\n**Incorrect: Measurements show that the phospholipid tails are shielded from water.** The model puts tails inside, so this fits.\n\n**Incorrect: Chemical tests show that the membrane contains both lipid and protein.** The model has both, so this fits.","how":{"ask":"What does the model forbid?","know":"Only a result the model rules out can invalidate it.","steps":[{"text":"The model says protein never enters the bilayer.","hl":"without entering the bilayer"},{"text":"Tails inside, lipid plus protein, and the micrographs all fit the model.","hl":"Supporters cite electron micrographs","strike":["Electron micrographs show two dark lines around a lighter band.","Measurements show that the phospholipid tails are shielded from water.","Chemical tests show that the membrane contains both lipid and protein."]},{"text":"A chain on both faces must span the bilayer. Answer: the labeling result."}],"trap":"Tails shielded from water sounds like Fluid Mosaic, but both models say it."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the model's 'never' statement.","marks":[{"t":"without entering the bilayer","note":"the forbidden fact"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To invalidate a model, pick the result it says cannot happen."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-147a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"weigh evidence against model","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:147#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:147"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P6","passage":"Two models of membrane structure have been proposed. In the Protein Coat Model, phospholipids form a bilayer with tails inside and heads outside, and a flat sheet of protein covers each face without entering the bilayer. Supporters cite electron micrographs that show two dark outer lines around a lighter middle band. In the Fluid Mosaic Model, proteins sit within the phospholipid bilayer, with their polar parts facing water and their nonpolar parts facing the lipid tails. Supporters cite an experiment in which a mouse cell and a human cell were fused, and their surface proteins fully mixed within 40 minutes.","q":"If the mixing of surface proteins after cell fusion were shown to be caused by new protein synthesis, how would the Fluid Mosaic Model have to be modified?","choices":["The proteins would have to stay fixed in the membrane.","There could be no proteins within the bilayer.","The lipids would have to be embedded in the proteins.","It would not necessarily have to be altered, but there would be less evidence supporting it."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Losing evidence is not disproof","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Nothing suggests lipids inside proteins","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Weakened evidence ≠ wrong model."},"exp":"**Answer:** It would not necessarily have to be altered, but there would be less evidence supporting it.\n\n**Incorrect: The proteins would have to stay fixed in the membrane.** The new finding only removes one support; it does not show proteins are fixed.\n\n**Incorrect: There could be no proteins within the bilayer.** Nothing in the finding says where proteins sit.\n\n**Incorrect: The lipids would have to be embedded in the proteins.** No data point to this.\n\n**Correct: It would not necessarily have to be altered, but there would be less evidence supporting it.** The fusion result only supported the model. Losing it weakens support but does not disprove the model.","how":{"ask":"Does losing one support break the model?","know":"Removing evidence weakens a model; it does not refute it.","steps":[{"text":"The fusion result was cited as support.","hl":"Supporters cite an experiment in which a mouse cell and a human cell were fused"},{"text":"New synthesis explains the mixing another way, so the support is gone, not reversed.","strike":["The proteins would have to stay fixed in the membrane.","There could be no proteins within the bilayer."]},{"text":"Nothing involves lipids inside proteins.","strike":["The lipids would have to be embedded in the proteins."]},{"text":"Answer: It would not necessarily have to be altered, but there would be less evidence supporting it."}],"trap":"'Proteins fixed' tempts, but absence of proof of motion is not proof of no motion."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask whether the new fact contradicts the model or just removes support.","marks":[{"t":"Supporters cite","note":"support only"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Explaining away evidence weakens a model; only a contradiction breaks it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-148a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"weigh evidence against model","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:148#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:148"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P6","passage":"Two models of membrane structure have been proposed. In the Protein Coat Model, phospholipids form a bilayer with tails inside and heads outside, and a flat sheet of protein covers each face without entering the bilayer. Supporters cite electron micrographs that show two dark outer lines around a lighter middle band. In the Fluid Mosaic Model, proteins sit within the phospholipid bilayer, with their polar parts facing water and their nonpolar parts facing the lipid tails. Supporters cite an experiment in which a mouse cell and a human cell were fused, and their surface proteins fully mixed within 40 minutes.","q":"Which of the following is a weakness in the Protein Coat Model?","choices":["The Protein Coat Model does not define the cell's shape.","The Protein Coat Model does not explain how ions and sugars cross the membrane.","The Protein Coat Model does not separate the cell from its surroundings.","The hydrophobic core of the bilayer lets charged ions pass freely."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Membranes need not set shape","c":[0]},{"f":"Its bilayer is a barrier","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No protein crosses the bilayer, so nothing can carry polar solutes through."},"exp":"**Answer:** The Protein Coat Model does not explain how ions and sugars cross the membrane.\n\n**Incorrect: The Protein Coat Model does not define the cell's shape.** Shape is not a membrane model's job.\n\n**Correct: The Protein Coat Model does not explain how ions and sugars cross the membrane.** Polar solutes need channels or carriers that span the bilayer. This model has none.\n\n**Incorrect: The Protein Coat Model does not separate the cell from its surroundings.** Its bilayer is a barrier.\n\n**Incorrect: The hydrophobic core of the bilayer lets charged ions pass freely.** A hydrophobic core blocks ions.","how":{"ask":"What can the model not explain?","know":"Polar solutes cross through proteins that span the bilayer.","steps":[{"text":"Protein never enters the bilayer in this model.","hl":"without entering the bilayer"},{"text":"Shape is not a membrane model's job.","strike":["The Protein Coat Model does not define the cell's shape."]},{"text":"Its bilayer does block ions, so it is a barrier.","strike":["The Protein Coat Model does not separate the cell from its surroundings.","The hydrophobic core of the bilayer lets charged ions pass freely."]},{"text":"Answer: it does not explain how ions and sugars cross."}],"trap":"The 'barrier' choice tempts, but the model is all barrier and no doorway."},"teach":[{"text":"No spanning protein means no channel.","marks":[{"t":"covers each face","note":"no channels"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A model's weakness is a known fact it cannot explain."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-149a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"electron transport and gradient","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:149#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:149"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P7","passage":"Cold exposure burns more calories than shivering alone explains, partly because white fat starts making uncoupling protein 1 (UCP1). UCP1 lets protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane, so gradient energy becomes heat instead of ATP. The muscle membrane protein KLN is cut to release kalinin, a soluble fragment that travels in the blood and induces UCP1 in fat. In mice kept at 4 °C for a week, fat UCP1 mRNA rose 20-fold, muscle KLN mRNA rose 3-fold, and blood kalinin rose 2-fold. Mice that overexpress the muscle transcription factor TFX, which turns on KLN, had 70-fold more KLN mRNA and 3-fold more fat UCP1; obese mice injected with a virus carrying the KLN gene had lower blood glucose than mice given a control virus.","q":"UCP1 most likely reduces the activity of which enzyme?","choices":["Hexokinase","ATP synthase","Ca2+ ATPase","Phosphoglycerate kinase"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Those do not use H+ gradient","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Leaky proton gradient = less ATP synthase work."},"exp":"**Answer:** ATP synthase\n\n**Incorrect: Hexokinase** It phosphorylates glucose in the cytosol; no proton gradient.\n\n**Correct: ATP synthase** It uses protons flowing back through it to make ATP. UCP1 lets protons bypass it.\n\n**Incorrect: Ca2+ ATPase** It spends ATP to pump calcium; it does not use the proton gradient.\n\n**Incorrect: Phosphoglycerate kinase** It makes ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme runs on the proton gradient?","know":"ATP synthase is driven by H+ flow back into the matrix.","steps":[{"text":"UCP1 lets protons leak back, so ATP is not made.","hl":"UCP1 lets protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane"},{"text":"The others do not use the proton gradient.","strike":["Hexokinase","Ca2+ ATPase","Phosphoglycerate kinase"]},{"text":"Answer: ATP synthase."}],"trap":"Phosphoglycerate kinase also makes ATP, but not from the gradient."},"teach":[{"text":"Follow the protons.","marks":[{"t":"heat instead of ATP","note":"bypasses ATP synthase"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Uncouplers drain the H+ gradient, so ATP synthase output falls."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-150a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:150#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:150"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P7","passage":"Cold exposure burns more calories than shivering alone explains, partly because white fat starts making uncoupling protein 1 (UCP1). UCP1 lets protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane, so gradient energy becomes heat instead of ATP. The muscle membrane protein KLN is cut to release kalinin, a soluble fragment that travels in the blood and induces UCP1 in fat. In mice kept at 4 °C for a week, fat UCP1 mRNA rose 20-fold, muscle KLN mRNA rose 3-fold, and blood kalinin rose 2-fold. Mice that overexpress the muscle transcription factor TFX, which turns on KLN, had 70-fold more KLN mRNA and 3-fold more fat UCP1; obese mice injected with a virus carrying the KLN gene had lower blood glucose than mice given a control virus.","q":"The information in the passage best supports which hypothesis?","choices":["Cold exposure blocks glucose uptake.","Endocrine secretions of fat act on skeletal muscle.","Cold exposure promotes less efficient cellular respiration.","Exocrine secretions of skeletal muscle act on fat."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"KLN lowered blood glucose","c":[0]},{"f":"Muscle signals fat via blood","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cold → more UCP1 → more heat, less ATP per fuel."},"exp":"**Answer:** Cold exposure promotes less efficient cellular respiration.\n\n**Incorrect: Cold exposure blocks glucose uptake.** The KLN pathway lowered blood glucose, so uptake likely rose.\n\n**Incorrect: Endocrine secretions of fat act on skeletal muscle.** The signal goes from muscle to fat, not fat to muscle.\n\n**Correct: Cold exposure promotes less efficient cellular respiration.** Cold raises UCP1, which turns gradient energy into heat instead of ATP.\n\n**Incorrect: Exocrine secretions of skeletal muscle act on fat.** Kalinin travels in blood, so it is endocrine.","how":{"ask":"Which claim do the data back?","know":"Uncoupling = less ATP per fuel burned.","steps":[{"text":"Cold raised UCP1 20-fold.","hl":"fat UCP1 mRNA rose 20-fold"},{"text":"KLN lowered glucose, so uptake did not stop.","hl":"had lower blood glucose than mice given a control virus","strike":["Cold exposure blocks glucose uptake."]},{"text":"Kalinin goes from muscle to fat in blood: endocrine, muscle to fat.","hl":"travels in the blood and induces UCP1 in fat","strike":["Endocrine secretions of fat act on skeletal muscle.","Exocrine secretions of skeletal muscle act on fat."]},{"text":"Answer: Cold exposure promotes less efficient cellular respiration."}],"trap":"Exocrine tempts because the direction is right, but blood transport means endocrine."},"teach":[{"text":"Check direction and route for every signal.","marks":[{"t":"travels in the blood","note":"endocrine"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Blood-borne = endocrine; ducts = exocrine."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-151a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:151#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:151"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P7","passage":"Cold exposure burns more calories than shivering alone explains, partly because white fat starts making uncoupling protein 1 (UCP1). UCP1 lets protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane, so gradient energy becomes heat instead of ATP. The muscle membrane protein KLN is cut to release kalinin, a soluble fragment that travels in the blood and induces UCP1 in fat. In mice kept at 4 °C for a week, fat UCP1 mRNA rose 20-fold, muscle KLN mRNA rose 3-fold, and blood kalinin rose 2-fold. Mice that overexpress the muscle transcription factor TFX, which turns on KLN, had 70-fold more KLN mRNA and 3-fold more fat UCP1; obese mice injected with a virus carrying the KLN gene had lower blood glucose than mice given a control virus.","q":"Mice that overexpress TFX specifically in their skeletal muscles are most likely to exhibit which phenotype relative to wild-type mice?","choices":["Higher blood glucose levels","Lower body temperature","Lower body weight","Higher physical activity"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"KLN lowers blood glucose","c":[0]},{"f":"UCP1 makes more heat","c":[1]},{"f":"No activity data given","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"TFX → KLN → UCP1 → fuel burned as heat → less stored fat."},"exp":"**Answer:** Lower body weight\n\n**Incorrect: Higher blood glucose levels** More KLN lowered blood glucose.\n\n**Incorrect: Lower body temperature** More UCP1 makes more heat, not less.\n\n**Correct: Lower body weight** TFX raises KLN and fat UCP1. More fuel burns as heat, so less is stored.\n\n**Incorrect: Higher physical activity** The passage gives no data on activity.","how":{"ask":"Follow TFX to its end effect.","know":"More uncoupling = more calories burned.","steps":[{"text":"TFX mice have more KLN and more fat UCP1.","hl":"had 70-fold more KLN mRNA and 3-fold more fat UCP1"},{"text":"KLN lowers glucose; UCP1 makes heat.","strike":["Higher blood glucose levels","Lower body temperature"]},{"text":"Nothing links TFX to activity.","strike":["Higher physical activity"]},{"text":"Answer: Lower body weight."}],"trap":"Higher activity tempts because muscle is involved, but no data support it."},"teach":[{"text":"Chain the steps in order.","marks":[{"t":"which turns on KLN","note":"TFX → KLN"},{"t":"induces UCP1 in fat","note":"KLN → UCP1"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Trace each link of a pathway, then pick the outcome of the last link."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-152a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"virus and bacteria traits","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:152#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:152"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P7","passage":"Cold exposure burns more calories than shivering alone explains, partly because white fat starts making uncoupling protein 1 (UCP1). UCP1 lets protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane, so gradient energy becomes heat instead of ATP. The muscle membrane protein KLN is cut to release kalinin, a soluble fragment that travels in the blood and induces UCP1 in fat. In mice kept at 4 °C for a week, fat UCP1 mRNA rose 20-fold, muscle KLN mRNA rose 3-fold, and blood kalinin rose 2-fold. Mice that overexpress the muscle transcription factor TFX, which turns on KLN, had 70-fold more KLN mRNA and 3-fold more fat UCP1; obese mice injected with a virus carrying the KLN gene had lower blood glucose than mice given a control virus.","q":"The viruses that carry either the KLN gene or a control gene transfer genetic material via:","choices":["conjugation.","transformation.","transduction.","transposition."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Conjugation: bacteria via pilus","c":[0]},{"f":"Transformation: free DNA uptake","c":[1]},{"f":"Transposition: genes jump within genome","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Virus delivers the DNA = transduction."},"exp":"**Answer:** transduction.\n\n**Incorrect: conjugation.** It moves DNA between bacteria through a pilus.\n\n**Incorrect: transformation.** It is uptake of free DNA from the surroundings.\n\n**Correct: transduction.** A virus carries genes into a host cell.\n\n**Incorrect: transposition.** It moves a gene within one genome.","how":{"ask":"What is gene delivery by a virus called?","know":"Transduction = virus-mediated gene transfer.","steps":[{"text":"The gene was delivered by a virus.","hl":"a virus carrying the KLN gene"},{"text":"Conjugation and transformation do not use viruses.","strike":["conjugation.","transformation."]},{"text":"Transposition moves genes within a genome.","strike":["transposition."]},{"text":"Answer: transduction."}],"trap":"Transformation tempts because it also adds new DNA, but no virus is used."},"teach":[{"text":"Spot the carrier.","marks":[{"t":"a virus carrying the KLN gene","note":"virus = transduction"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Virus = transduction; free DNA = transformation; pilus = conjugation."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-153a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"dna replication and repair","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:153#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:153"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P8","passage":"Inflammatory signals such as IL-1β make cells produce reactive oxygen species (ROS), which oxidize cytosine in DNA to 5-hydroxycytosine (5-OH-C). Because 5-OH-C can pair with adenine, it causes C to T mutations after replication. The DNA glycosylase GLX removes 5-OH-C during base excision repair, and GLX bound to 5-OH-C in a promoter also recruits the transcription factor AP-1 to turn on inflammatory genes such as Il8. The compound KM-22 mimics 5-OH-C and fills the active site of GLX, so GLX can no longer bind DNA. After IL-1β exposure, Il8 mRNA rose 6-fold in wild-type mice but only 1.5-fold in Glx–/– mice and 1.5-fold in wild-type mice given KM-22.","q":"Which outcome most likely results from long-term exposure of Glx–/– mice to IL-1β?","choices":["Decrease in cellular ROS levels","Increase in Il8 expression","Increase in cancer incidence","Decrease in KD of KM-22 for its target"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"IL-1β raises ROS","c":[0]},{"f":"Knockouts make less Il8","c":[1]},{"f":"Knockouts lack KM-22's target","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No repair enzyme + more DNA damage = more mutations = cancer."},"exp":"**Answer:** Increase in cancer incidence\n\n**Incorrect: Decrease in cellular ROS levels** IL-1β raises ROS; losing GLX does not change that.\n\n**Incorrect: Increase in Il8 expression** Glx–/– mice made less Il8, not more.\n\n**Correct: Increase in cancer incidence** Without GLX, 5-OH-C stays in DNA and causes C to T mutations. Mutations build up and raise cancer risk.\n\n**Incorrect: Decrease in KD of KM-22 for its target** Knockouts have no GLX for KM-22 to bind.","how":{"ask":"What happens with damage but no repair?","know":"Unrepaired base damage → mutations → cancer risk.","steps":[{"text":"IL-1β raises ROS, which damages C.","hl":"make cells produce reactive oxygen species (ROS)","strike":["Decrease in cellular ROS levels"]},{"text":"Knockouts make less Il8.","hl":"only 1.5-fold in Glx–/– mice","strike":["Increase in Il8 expression"]},{"text":"No GLX means no target for KM-22.","strike":["Decrease in KD of KM-22 for its target"]},{"text":"Unrepaired 5-OH-C causes mutations. Answer: Increase in cancer incidence.","hl":"it causes C to T mutations after replication"}],"trap":"More Il8 tempts because inflammation rises, but GLX is needed to turn Il8 on."},"teach":[{"text":"GLX has two jobs: repair and gene activation.","marks":[{"t":"base excision repair","note":"job 1"},{"t":"recruits the transcription factor AP-1","note":"job 2"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lose a repair enzyme and mutations pile up."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-154a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:154#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:154"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P8","passage":"Inflammatory signals such as IL-1β make cells produce reactive oxygen species (ROS), which oxidize cytosine in DNA to 5-hydroxycytosine (5-OH-C). Because 5-OH-C can pair with adenine, it causes C to T mutations after replication. The DNA glycosylase GLX removes 5-OH-C during base excision repair, and GLX bound to 5-OH-C in a promoter also recruits the transcription factor AP-1 to turn on inflammatory genes such as Il8. The compound KM-22 mimics 5-OH-C and fills the active site of GLX, so GLX can no longer bind DNA. After IL-1β exposure, Il8 mRNA rose 6-fold in wild-type mice but only 1.5-fold in Glx–/– mice and 1.5-fold in wild-type mice given KM-22.","q":"KM-22 acts as which type of inhibitor?","choices":["Mixed","Competitive","Uncompetitive","Noncompetitive"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Those bind outside active site","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Looks like substrate + binds active site = competitive."},"exp":"**Answer:** Competitive\n\n**Incorrect: Mixed** Mixed inhibitors bind a site other than the active site.\n\n**Correct: Competitive** KM-22 mimics the substrate and fills the active site, so it competes with 5-OH-C.\n\n**Incorrect: Uncompetitive** These bind only the enzyme–substrate complex, away from the active site.\n\n**Incorrect: Noncompetitive** These bind an allosteric site.","how":{"ask":"Where does KM-22 bind?","know":"Active-site binder that mimics substrate = competitive.","steps":[{"text":"KM-22 looks like the substrate and sits in the active site.","hl":"mimics 5-OH-C and fills the active site of GLX"},{"text":"The other types bind elsewhere.","strike":["Mixed","Uncompetitive","Noncompetitive"]},{"text":"Answer: Competitive."}],"trap":"Noncompetitive tempts because GLX 'can no longer bind DNA', but the site is the active site."},"teach":[{"text":"Look for two clues: 'mimics' and 'active site'.","marks":[{"t":"mimics 5-OH-C","note":"substrate look-alike"},{"t":"active site","note":"competitive"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Substrate mimic in the active site = competitive inhibitor."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-155a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:155#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:155"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P8","passage":"Inflammatory signals such as IL-1β make cells produce reactive oxygen species (ROS), which oxidize cytosine in DNA to 5-hydroxycytosine (5-OH-C). Because 5-OH-C can pair with adenine, it causes C to T mutations after replication. The DNA glycosylase GLX removes 5-OH-C during base excision repair, and GLX bound to 5-OH-C in a promoter also recruits the transcription factor AP-1 to turn on inflammatory genes such as Il8. The compound KM-22 mimics 5-OH-C and fills the active site of GLX, so GLX can no longer bind DNA. After IL-1β exposure, Il8 mRNA rose 6-fold in wild-type mice but only 1.5-fold in Glx–/– mice and 1.5-fold in wild-type mice given KM-22.","q":"Which conclusions do the data support? I. GLX reduces IL-1β-induced Il8 expression. II. GLX is needed for full IL-1β-induced Il8 expression. III. KM-22 inhibits GLX-mediated Il8 expression. IV. KM-22 stimulates GLX-mediated Il8 expression.","choices":["I and III only","I and IV only","II and III only","II and IV only"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Knockout lowered Il8: GLX needed","c":[0,1]},{"f":"KM-22 lowered Il8: inhibits","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Removing GLX or blocking it both lowered Il8."},"exp":"**Answer:** II and III only\n\n**Incorrect: I and III only** Knockouts made less Il8, so GLX raises, not reduces, it.\n\n**Incorrect: I and IV only** Both statements are reversed.\n\n**Correct: II and III only** Il8 rose 6-fold with GLX but only 1.5-fold without it (II). KM-22 also cut it to 1.5-fold (III).\n\n**Incorrect: II and IV only** KM-22 lowered Il8, so it does not stimulate.","how":{"ask":"Which statements match the numbers?","know":"Loss lowers output → the factor is needed.","steps":[{"text":"Wild type 6-fold vs knockout 1.5-fold: GLX is needed. I is false, II is true.","hl":"only 1.5-fold in Glx–/– mice","strike":["I and III only","I and IV only"]},{"text":"KM-22 also gave 1.5-fold: it inhibits. IV is false.","hl":"1.5-fold in wild-type mice given KM-22","strike":["II and IV only"]},{"text":"Answer: II and III only."}],"trap":"Statement I flips the direction; read 'reduces' carefully."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare each group to wild type.","marks":[{"t":"rose 6-fold in wild-type mice","note":"baseline"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Judge each Roman numeral alone, then pick the matching combo."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-156a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:156#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:156"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P8","passage":"Inflammatory signals such as IL-1β make cells produce reactive oxygen species (ROS), which oxidize cytosine in DNA to 5-hydroxycytosine (5-OH-C). Because 5-OH-C can pair with adenine, it causes C to T mutations after replication. The DNA glycosylase GLX removes 5-OH-C during base excision repair, and GLX bound to 5-OH-C in a promoter also recruits the transcription factor AP-1 to turn on inflammatory genes such as Il8. The compound KM-22 mimics 5-OH-C and fills the active site of GLX, so GLX can no longer bind DNA. After IL-1β exposure, Il8 mRNA rose 6-fold in wild-type mice but only 1.5-fold in Glx–/– mice and 1.5-fold in wild-type mice given KM-22.","q":"Researchers measured AP-1 occupancy of the Il8 promoter following exposure to IL-1β both in the presence and absence of KM-22. Based on the data presented in the passage, which result is expected?","choices":["Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 blocks the rise.","Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 raises it further.","Occupancy falls with IL-1β, and KM-22 has no effect.","Occupancy does not change with IL-1β or KM-22."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"IL-1β adds damage, recruits AP-1","c":[2,3]},{"f":"KM-22 stops GLX binding DNA","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"IL-1β → damage → GLX on DNA → AP-1 up; KM-22 removes GLX → AP-1 down."},"exp":"**Answer:** Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 blocks the rise.\n\n**Correct: Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 blocks the rise.** IL-1β makes 5-OH-C, so GLX binds and recruits AP-1. KM-22 keeps GLX off DNA, so AP-1 is not recruited.\n\n**Incorrect: Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 raises it further.** KM-22 blocks GLX, so it lowers recruitment.\n\n**Incorrect: Occupancy falls with IL-1β, and KM-22 has no effect.** IL-1β raises recruitment, and KM-22 does act.\n\n**Incorrect: Occupancy does not change with IL-1β or KM-22.** Both change the GLX step.","how":{"ask":"How do IL-1β and KM-22 change AP-1 recruitment?","know":"AP-1 recruitment depends on GLX bound at damaged DNA.","steps":[{"text":"IL-1β makes damage, and GLX at damage recruits AP-1. So occupancy rises.","hl":"recruits the transcription factor AP-1","strike":["Occupancy falls with IL-1β, and KM-22 has no effect.","Occupancy does not change with IL-1β or KM-22."]},{"text":"KM-22 keeps GLX off DNA, so AP-1 falls.","hl":"so GLX can no longer bind DNA","strike":["Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 raises it further."]},{"text":"Answer: Occupancy rises with IL-1β, and KM-22 blocks the rise."}],"trap":"KM-22 mimics the damage, so it might seem to boost GLX activity; it blocks it."},"teach":[{"text":"Draw the chain: IL-1β → ROS → 5-OH-C → GLX → AP-1.","marks":[{"t":"GLX bound to 5-OH-C in a promoter","note":"needed for AP-1"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Block a middle step and everything after it drops."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-157a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nervous system pathways","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:157#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:157"],"q":"In a diagram of a pain withdrawal reflex arc, I labels the biceps muscle, II labels a motor neuron leaving by the ventral root, III labels a neuron lying wholly within the spinal cord gray matter, and IV labels a sensory neuron cell body in the dorsal root ganglion. Which Roman numeral represents the CNS integration for this reflex arc?","choices":["I","II","III","IV"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Effector and motor are output","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Sensory neuron is input","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Integration = the interneuron inside the spinal cord."},"exp":"**Answer:** III\n\n**Incorrect: I** The muscle is the effector.\n\n**Incorrect: II** The motor neuron carries the output.\n\n**Correct: III** The interneuron sits wholly inside the spinal cord. It links input to output.\n\n**Incorrect: IV** The sensory neuron carries the input.","how":{"ask":"Which part sits inside the CNS and links in to out?","know":"Reflex arc: receptor → sensory → interneuron (CNS) → motor → effector.","steps":[{"text":"I is the effector, II the motor neuron: output side.","strike":["I","II"]},{"text":"IV is the sensory neuron: input side.","strike":["IV"]},{"text":"III is in the gray matter. Answer: III."}],"trap":"The dorsal root ganglion is near the cord but outside the CNS."},"teach":[{"text":"Place each label on the path from input to output."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** CNS integration in a reflex = the interneuron in spinal gray matter."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-158a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:158#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:158"],"q":"The rising phase of a neuron's action potential is primarily a result of the:","choices":["release of neurotransmitters from the axon terminal.","outflow of K+ ions through leak channels.","active transport of Ca2+ ions across the plasma membrane.","inflow of Na+ ions through voltage-gated channels."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Release happens after the spike","c":[0]},{"f":"K+ outflow repolarizes","c":[1]},{"f":"Pumps are too slow","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Upstroke = Na+ rushing in."},"exp":"**Answer:** inflow of Na+ ions through voltage-gated channels.\n\n**Incorrect: release of neurotransmitters from the axon terminal.** That follows the action potential at the terminal.\n\n**Incorrect: outflow of K+ ions through leak channels.** K+ leaving makes the inside more negative.\n\n**Incorrect: active transport of Ca2+ ions across the plasma membrane.** Pumps are far too slow to cause a spike.\n\n**Correct: inflow of Na+ ions through voltage-gated channels.** Opening Na+ channels lets Na+ rush in and depolarize the cell.","how":{"ask":"What makes the membrane go positive fast?","know":"Depolarization = Na+ in; repolarization = K+ out.","steps":[{"text":"Transmitter release comes after the spike.","strike":["release of neurotransmitters from the axon terminal."]},{"text":"K+ out drives the potential down, not up.","strike":["outflow of K+ ions through leak channels."]},{"text":"Pumps are slow.","strike":["active transport of Ca2+ ions across the plasma membrane."]},{"text":"Answer: inflow of Na+ ions through voltage-gated channels."}],"trap":"K+ tempts because it sets the resting potential, not the upstroke."},"teach":[{"text":"Match each phase to its ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Up = Na+ in; down = K+ out; resting = ion distribution."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-159a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"apply the physics formula","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:159#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:159"],"q":"Relaxation of the diaphragm results in:","choices":["decreased intrathoracic pressure and inhalation.","decreased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation.","increased intrathoracic pressure and inhalation.","increased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Smaller volume raises pressure","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Higher pressure pushes air out","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Relax → diaphragm rises → volume down → pressure up → air out."},"exp":"**Answer:** increased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation.\n\n**Incorrect: decreased intrathoracic pressure and inhalation.** That is what contraction does.\n\n**Incorrect: decreased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation.** Lower pressure would pull air in, not push it out.\n\n**Incorrect: increased intrathoracic pressure and inhalation.** Higher pressure pushes air out.\n\n**Correct: increased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation.** The relaxed diaphragm domes up, shrinking the chest. Pressure rises above air pressure, so air leaves.","how":{"ask":"Which way do volume and pressure go when the diaphragm relaxes?","know":"Boyle's law: smaller volume → higher pressure.","steps":[{"text":"Relaxed diaphragm moves up, so chest volume falls and pressure rises.","strike":["decreased intrathoracic pressure and inhalation.","decreased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation."]},{"text":"Higher pressure inside pushes air out.","strike":["increased intrathoracic pressure and inhalation."]},{"text":"Answer: increased intrathoracic pressure and exhalation."}],"trap":"Mixing up contraction (inhale) and relaxation (exhale)."},"teach":[{"text":"Chain: muscle → volume → pressure → airflow."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Air flows from high to low pressure; volume and pressure move oppositely."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-160a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:160#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:160"],"q":"Which kinetic parameter changes when the enzyme concentration in a reaction is doubled? (Note: Other than enzyme concentration, assume no other changes to reaction conditions.)","choices":["KM","kcat","Vmax","Ki"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"These are per-enzyme constants","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Vmax = kcat × [E], so it scales with enzyme."},"exp":"**Answer:** Vmax\n\n**Incorrect: KM** It reflects substrate affinity and does not depend on enzyme amount.\n\n**Incorrect: kcat** It is the turnover per enzyme and stays the same.\n\n**Correct: Vmax** Vmax = kcat × [E]. Double the enzyme, double Vmax.\n\n**Incorrect: Ki** It is an inhibitor's binding constant and does not depend on enzyme amount.","how":{"ask":"Which value depends on how much enzyme there is?","know":"Vmax = kcat[E]total.","steps":[{"text":"Ask which values are set per enzyme molecule."},{"text":"KM, kcat and Ki are traits of each enzyme molecule.","strike":["KM","kcat","Ki"]},{"text":"Vmax counts all enzyme molecules. Answer: Vmax."}],"trap":"kcat tempts as 'speed', but it is per enzyme."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask: is it per molecule, or for the whole pot?"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More enzyme raises Vmax; KM and kcat stay fixed."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-161a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:161#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:161"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P9","passage":"Protein GK halts human cell division in G1 by binding TFK, a transcription factor that cells need to enter S phase. Kinases called CDKs add phosphates to GK, which stops it from binding TFK and lets the cell move on. The viral protein V12 binds a highly conserved pocket on GK through a short motif called LXSXE. Researchers expressed wild-type GK or one of three pocket mutants (M1, M2, M3) in GK-deficient cells. Wild-type GK and each mutant raised G1 cells to about 70%, and adding V12 cut this to 30% for wild-type GK but left it near 70% for every mutant. Adding the kinase CDK6 cut G1 cells to about 35% for wild-type GK and for every mutant.","q":"These experiments suggest that the mutant GK proteins do not:","choices":["bind to TFK.","arrest cells in G1.","bind to V12.","permit exit from G1."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Mutants still arrest G1","c":[0,1]},{"f":"CDK6 still releases mutants","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"V12 cannot undo mutant arrest, so V12 does not bind them."},"exp":"**Answer:** bind to V12.\n\n**Incorrect: bind to TFK.** Mutants still arrest cells, so they still bind TFK.\n\n**Incorrect: arrest cells in G1.** Mutants raised G1 cells to 70%, like wild type.\n\n**Correct: bind to V12.** V12 freed wild-type cells but not mutant cells. The pocket mutations block V12 binding.\n\n**Incorrect: permit exit from G1.** CDK6 still released mutant cells to 35%.","how":{"ask":"Which interaction did the mutations lose?","know":"If a blocker fails on a mutant, the mutant lost its binding site.","steps":[{"text":"Mutants arrest cells just like wild type, so TFK binding is intact.","hl":"Wild-type GK and each mutant raised G1 cells to about 70%","strike":["bind to TFK.","arrest cells in G1."]},{"text":"CDK6 still lets mutant cells exit G1.","hl":"for wild-type GK and for every mutant","strike":["permit exit from G1."]},{"text":"V12 had no effect on mutants. Answer: bind to V12.","hl":"left it near 70% for every mutant"}],"trap":"'Bind to TFK' tempts because both bind GK, but arrest proves TFK binding is intact."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare each treatment's effect on wild type vs mutants.","marks":[{"t":"cut this to 30% for wild-type GK","note":"V12 works on WT"},{"t":"left it near 70% for every mutant","note":"V12 fails"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The function that differs between WT and mutant is the one the mutation broke."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-162a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:162#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:162"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P9","passage":"Protein GK halts human cell division in G1 by binding TFK, a transcription factor that cells need to enter S phase. Kinases called CDKs add phosphates to GK, which stops it from binding TFK and lets the cell move on. The viral protein V12 binds a highly conserved pocket on GK through a short motif called LXSXE. Researchers expressed wild-type GK or one of three pocket mutants (M1, M2, M3) in GK-deficient cells. Wild-type GK and each mutant raised G1 cells to about 70%, and adding V12 cut this to 30% for wild-type GK but left it near 70% for every mutant. Adding the kinase CDK6 cut G1 cells to about 35% for wild-type GK and for every mutant.","q":"If the GK mutants could not bind to TFK, then:","choices":["V12 would not bind to these mutants.","CDK6 could not phosphorylate these mutants.","G1 arrest would not occur.","TFK would bind to V12."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Passage links TFK only to arrest","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"GK arrests cells only by holding TFK."},"exp":"**Answer:** G1 arrest would not occur.\n\n**Incorrect: V12 would not bind to these mutants.** V12 binding is a separate site and question.\n\n**Incorrect: CDK6 could not phosphorylate these mutants.** Nothing ties phosphorylation to TFK binding.\n\n**Correct: G1 arrest would not occur.** GK arrests cells by binding TFK. Free TFK lets the cell enter S phase.\n\n**Incorrect: TFK would bind to V12.** No data link TFK and V12.","how":{"ask":"What does TFK binding do?","know":"GK–TFK binding = G1 arrest.","steps":[{"text":"GK holds cells in G1 by binding TFK.","hl":"halts human cell division in G1 by binding TFK"},{"text":"The other choices are not tied to TFK binding.","strike":["V12 would not bind to these mutants.","CDK6 could not phosphorylate these mutants.","TFK would bind to V12."]},{"text":"Answer: G1 arrest would not occur."}],"trap":"V12 tempts because these are pocket mutants, but the 'if' is about TFK."},"teach":[{"text":"Answer the 'if' using the passage's cause and effect.","marks":[{"t":"by binding TFK","note":"causes arrest"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In 'if X, then' stems, follow X's stated job only."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-163a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:163#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:163"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P9","passage":"Protein GK halts human cell division in G1 by binding TFK, a transcription factor that cells need to enter S phase. Kinases called CDKs add phosphates to GK, which stops it from binding TFK and lets the cell move on. The viral protein V12 binds a highly conserved pocket on GK through a short motif called LXSXE. Researchers expressed wild-type GK or one of three pocket mutants (M1, M2, M3) in GK-deficient cells. Wild-type GK and each mutant raised G1 cells to about 70%, and adding V12 cut this to 30% for wild-type GK but left it near 70% for every mutant. Adding the kinase CDK6 cut G1 cells to about 35% for wild-type GK and for every mutant.","q":"Comparing the results for GK transfection with those for GK and CDK6 transfection shows that:","choices":["CDK6 transfection results in fewer cells being G1 arrested.","mutant GK is better at arresting cells in G1 than wild-type GK.","CDK6 transfection causes more mutant GK to be phosphorylated.","more cells are G1 arrested in CDK6-transfected cells."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Mutants and WT arrest equally","c":[1]},{"f":"Phosphorylation was not measured","c":[2]},{"f":"G1 fell from 70% to 35%","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read the numbers: 70% to 35% = fewer arrested."},"exp":"**Answer:** CDK6 transfection results in fewer cells being G1 arrested.\n\n**Correct: CDK6 transfection results in fewer cells being G1 arrested.** G1 cells fell from about 70% to 35% for every GK type.\n\n**Incorrect: mutant GK is better at arresting cells in G1 than wild-type GK.** All arrested about 70%.\n\n**Incorrect: CDK6 transfection causes more mutant GK to be phosphorylated.** Phosphorylation was not measured.\n\n**Incorrect: more cells are G1 arrested in CDK6-transfected cells.** It is the reverse.","how":{"ask":"What changed when CDK6 was added?","know":"State only what the numbers show.","steps":[{"text":"Without CDK6, all GK types gave about 70% G1.","hl":"Wild-type GK and each mutant raised G1 cells to about 70%","strike":["mutant GK is better at arresting cells in G1 than wild-type GK."]},{"text":"With CDK6, G1 fell to 35%.","hl":"Adding the kinase CDK6 cut G1 cells to about 35%","strike":["more cells are G1 arrested in CDK6-transfected cells."]},{"text":"Phosphate levels were never measured.","strike":["CDK6 transfection causes more mutant GK to be phosphorylated."]},{"text":"Answer: CDK6 transfection results in fewer cells being G1 arrested."}],"trap":"Phosphorylation is likely true biology, but the data did not measure it."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare the two numbers.","marks":[{"t":"about 70%","note":"before"},{"t":"about 35%","note":"after CDK6"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 'Shows that' = only what was measured."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-164a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"evolution and conservation","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:164#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:164"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P9","passage":"Protein GK halts human cell division in G1 by binding TFK, a transcription factor that cells need to enter S phase. Kinases called CDKs add phosphates to GK, which stops it from binding TFK and lets the cell move on. The viral protein V12 binds a highly conserved pocket on GK through a short motif called LXSXE. Researchers expressed wild-type GK or one of three pocket mutants (M1, M2, M3) in GK-deficient cells. Wild-type GK and each mutant raised G1 cells to about 70%, and adding V12 cut this to 30% for wild-type GK but left it near 70% for every mutant. Adding the kinase CDK6 cut G1 cells to about 35% for wild-type GK and for every mutant.","q":"The GK pocket is described as \"highly conserved.\" Certain parts of the genome, such as that encoding this pocket, are highly conserved because they are:","choices":["chemically incapable of mutation.","vital to an organism's survival.","copied only once in a lifetime.","kept in the cytoplasm away from mutagens."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Any DNA can mutate","c":[0]},{"f":"DNA replicates and stays nuclear","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Conserved = mutations there kill, so selection removes them."},"exp":"**Answer:** vital to an organism's survival.\n\n**Incorrect: chemically incapable of mutation.** Every DNA region can mutate.\n\n**Correct: vital to an organism's survival.** Mutations in vital regions harm fitness and are removed by selection, so the sequence stays the same across species.\n\n**Incorrect: copied only once in a lifetime.** DNA is copied at every cell division.\n\n**Incorrect: kept in the cytoplasm away from mutagens.** Nuclear genes are in the nucleus.","how":{"ask":"Why would a sequence stay the same across species?","know":"Purifying selection removes harmful changes.","steps":[{"text":"The pocket is conserved.","hl":"highly conserved pocket"},{"text":"All DNA can mutate.","strike":["chemically incapable of mutation."]},{"text":"DNA is copied often and stays in the nucleus.","strike":["copied only once in a lifetime.","kept in the cytoplasm away from mutagens."]},{"text":"Answer: vital to an organism's survival."}],"trap":"'Incapable of mutation' tempts, but conservation is about selection, not immunity."},"teach":[{"text":"Conserved means unchanged over evolution.","marks":[{"t":"highly conserved","note":"under selection"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Highly conserved = functionally vital."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-165a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:165#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:165"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P10","passage":"Trehalose is a sugar made of two glucose units, found in mushrooms and insects. The enzyme trehalase splits it into two glucose molecules, and adults with low trehalase get cramps and diarrhea after eating it. In one population, all infants make trehalase, but some adults stop making it while others keep making it into adulthood (trehalase persistence, TP). TP adults make the very same trehalase protein as infants; they simply keep expressing the gene. Only one SNP in the TP haplotype causes the trait, and at this position non-TP adults carry G while TP adults carry A.","q":"Based on the information in the passage, the mutation that causes the TP phenotype is most likely located in:","choices":["an intron within the trehalase gene.","the coding sequence of the trehalase gene.","an enhancer sequence of the trehalase gene.","the stop codon at the end of the trehalase gene."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Same protein: coding region unchanged","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Enhancers most directly set expression","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same protein, different amount = regulatory region."},"exp":"**Answer:** an enhancer sequence of the trehalase gene.\n\n**Incorrect: an intron within the trehalase gene.** Introns can matter, but enhancers most directly control how much a gene is expressed.\n\n**Incorrect: the coding sequence of the trehalase gene.** A coding change would alter the protein, but the protein is the same.\n\n**Correct: an enhancer sequence of the trehalase gene.** TP changes only how long the gene stays on. Enhancers bind activators that keep expression going.\n\n**Incorrect: the stop codon at the end of the trehalase gene.** A stop codon change would alter the protein's length.","how":{"ask":"Did the protein change, or just its amount?","know":"Protein unchanged + expression changed → regulatory DNA.","steps":[{"text":"TP adults make the same protein, just for longer.","hl":"TP adults make the very same trehalase protein as infants; they simply keep expressing the gene"},{"text":"Coding and stop codon changes would alter the protein.","strike":["the coding sequence of the trehalase gene.","the stop codon at the end of the trehalase gene."]},{"text":"Enhancers control expression more directly than introns.","strike":["an intron within the trehalase gene."]},{"text":"Answer: an enhancer sequence of the trehalase gene."}],"trap":"Intron tempts as 'noncoding', but enhancers are the classic expression switch."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask what changed: the protein or the amount.","marks":[{"t":"they simply keep expressing the gene","note":"amount changed"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Expression-only changes point to promoters or enhancers."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-166a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:166#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:166"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P10","passage":"Trehalose is a sugar made of two glucose units, found in mushrooms and insects. The enzyme trehalase splits it into two glucose molecules, and adults with low trehalase get cramps and diarrhea after eating it. In one population, all infants make trehalase, but some adults stop making it while others keep making it into adulthood (trehalase persistence, TP). TP adults make the very same trehalase protein as infants; they simply keep expressing the gene. Only one SNP in the TP haplotype causes the trait, and at this position non-TP adults carry G while TP adults carry A.","q":"Trehalase can be classified as which type of enzyme?","choices":["Isomerase","Ligase","Hydrolase","Oxidoreductase"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Splitting a disaccharide uses water","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Breaks a glycosidic bond with water = hydrolase."},"exp":"**Answer:** Hydrolase\n\n**Incorrect: Isomerase** It rearranges a molecule; it does not split it.\n\n**Incorrect: Ligase** It joins molecules using ATP.\n\n**Correct: Hydrolase** Trehalase uses water to break the glycosidic bond, giving two glucose.\n\n**Incorrect: Oxidoreductase** It moves electrons; no redox happens here.","how":{"ask":"What reaction does trehalase do?","know":"Disaccharide → two monosaccharides = hydrolysis.","steps":[{"text":"Trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose.","hl":"splits it into two glucose molecules"},{"text":"Splitting with water rules out isomerase, ligase and oxidoreductase.","strike":["Isomerase","Ligase","Oxidoreductase"]},{"text":"Answer: Hydrolase."}],"trap":"Isomerase tempts because the product is all glucose, but a bond is broken."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the reaction first, then the class.","marks":[{"t":"splits it into two glucose molecules","note":"hydrolysis"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Digestive enzymes that split bonds are hydrolases."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-167a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme location by pathway","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:167#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:167"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P10","passage":"Trehalose is a sugar made of two glucose units, found in mushrooms and insects. The enzyme trehalase splits it into two glucose molecules, and adults with low trehalase get cramps and diarrhea after eating it. In one population, all infants make trehalase, but some adults stop making it while others keep making it into adulthood (trehalase persistence, TP). TP adults make the very same trehalase protein as infants; they simply keep expressing the gene. Only one SNP in the TP haplotype causes the trait, and at this position non-TP adults carry G while TP adults carry A.","q":"Which cells express trehalase?","choices":["Epithelial cells of the colon lining","Enterocytes of the jejunal villi","Chief cells of the stomach lining","Acinar cells of the pancreas"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Disaccharidases sit on brush border","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Disaccharide enzymes live on small intestine brush border."},"exp":"**Answer:** Enterocytes of the jejunal villi\n\n**Incorrect: Epithelial cells of the colon lining** The colon absorbs water; it does not make disaccharidases.\n\n**Correct: Enterocytes of the jejunal villi** Disaccharidases like trehalase sit on the brush border of small intestine enterocytes.\n\n**Incorrect: Chief cells of the stomach lining** They make pepsinogen for protein.\n\n**Incorrect: Acinar cells of the pancreas** They secrete amylase and other enzymes, not brush border disaccharidases.","how":{"ask":"Where are disaccharides split?","know":"Brush border of small intestine enterocytes.","steps":[{"text":"Trehalase splits a disaccharide.","hl":"Trehalose is a sugar made of two glucose units"},{"text":"Colon, stomach and pancreas do not make brush border disaccharidases.","strike":["Epithelial cells of the colon lining","Chief cells of the stomach lining","Acinar cells of the pancreas"]},{"text":"Answer: Enterocytes of the jejunal villi."}],"trap":"Pancreatic acinar cells tempt because they make carbohydrate enzymes, but those are for starch."},"teach":[{"text":"Note the sugar type: two units = disaccharide.","marks":[{"t":"two glucose units","note":"disaccharide"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lactase, sucrase and trehalase are small intestine brush border enzymes."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-168a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:168#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:168"],"pid":"pe5-bb:P10","passage":"Trehalose is a sugar made of two glucose units, found in mushrooms and insects. The enzyme trehalase splits it into two glucose molecules, and adults with low trehalase get cramps and diarrhea after eating it. In one population, all infants make trehalase, but some adults stop making it while others keep making it into adulthood (trehalase persistence, TP). TP adults make the very same trehalase protein as infants; they simply keep expressing the gene. Only one SNP in the TP haplotype causes the trait, and at this position non-TP adults carry G while TP adults carry A.","q":"The SNP resulted from what type of mutation?","choices":["Purine to pyrimidine","Pyrimidine to purine","Pyrimidine to pyrimidine","Purine to purine"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"G and A are purines","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pure As Gold: A and G are purines."},"exp":"**Answer:** Purine to purine\n\n**Incorrect: Purine to pyrimidine** A is not a pyrimidine.\n\n**Incorrect: Pyrimidine to purine** G is not a pyrimidine.\n\n**Incorrect: Pyrimidine to pyrimidine** Neither base is a pyrimidine.\n\n**Correct: Purine to purine** G changed to A. Both are purines, so this is a transition.","how":{"ask":"What class are the two bases?","know":"Purines: A, G. Pyrimidines: C, T, U.","steps":[{"text":"The SNP changes G to A.","hl":"non-TP adults carry G while TP adults carry A"},{"text":"Both are purines, so any pyrimidine choice is out.","strike":["Purine to pyrimidine","Pyrimidine to purine","Pyrimidine to pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Answer: Purine to purine."}],"trap":"Mixing up which letters are purines."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the two letters at the SNP.","marks":[{"t":"carry G while TP adults carry A","note":"both purines"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Pure As Gold = purines A, G; CUT the Py = pyrimidines C, U, T."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-169a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"membrane transport type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:169#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:169"],"q":"A Na+-selective channel sits in a membrane between two salt solutions, with no voltage applied. Which conditions would result in no net current through the channel? I. Equal concentrations of Na+ in the two solutions II. Na+ in only one of the two solutions III. Only K+ and Cl− in both solutions","choices":["I only","I and II only","I and III only","II and III only"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"One-sided Na+ flows: current","c":[1,3]},{"f":"No Na+ also gives zero","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Current needs a Na+ gradient; no gradient or no Na+ = no current."},"exp":"**Answer:** I and III only\n\n**Incorrect: I only** III also gives no current, since the channel passes no K+ or Cl−.\n\n**Incorrect: I and II only** In II, Na+ flows down its gradient and makes current.\n\n**Correct: I and III only** Equal Na+ means no net flow (I). No Na+ means nothing can pass (III).\n\n**Incorrect: II and III only** II makes current.","how":{"ask":"When does no net Na+ move?","know":"Net flow needs the passing ion and a gradient.","steps":[{"text":"I: equal Na+, so no gradient. No current."},{"text":"II: Na+ on one side flows across. Current.","strike":["I and II only","II and III only"]},{"text":"III: no Na+ at all, and the channel passes nothing else. No current.","strike":["I only"]},{"text":"Answer: I and III only."}],"trap":"Missing III, because K+ and Cl− are ions, but this channel ignores them."},"teach":[{"text":"Check two things: is the right ion there, and is there a gradient?"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** No gradient or no permeant ion = no net current."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-170a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:170#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:170"],"q":"Which molecule is NOT formed during the steps of the citric acid cycle?","choices":["Citrate","Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate","Fumarate","Succinyl-CoA"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"These are cycle intermediates","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphorylated 3-carbon sugars belong to glycolysis."},"exp":"**Answer:** Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate\n\n**Incorrect: Citrate** It is the first product of the cycle.\n\n**Correct: Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate** It is a glycolysis intermediate, not a cycle one.\n\n**Incorrect: Fumarate** Succinate dehydrogenase makes it in the cycle.\n\n**Incorrect: Succinyl-CoA** α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase makes it in the cycle.","how":{"ask":"Which molecule belongs to another pathway?","know":"Cycle: citrate, isocitrate, α-KG, succinyl-CoA, succinate, fumarate, malate, OAA.","steps":[{"text":"List the cycle intermediates from citrate to oxaloacetate."},{"text":"Citrate, fumarate and succinyl-CoA are all in the cycle.","strike":["Citrate","Fumarate","Succinyl-CoA"]},{"text":"G3P is made in glycolysis. Answer: Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate."}],"trap":"Any long name can feel unfamiliar; check it against the cycle list."},"teach":[{"text":"Recite the 8 cycle intermediates in order."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Phosphorylated sugars = glycolysis; CoA thioesters and dicarboxylic acids = citric acid cycle."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe5-171a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mendelian cross probability","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe5-bb:171#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-bb:171"],"q":"A particular diploid organism is heterozygous in each of 4 unlinked genes. Considering only these 4 genes, how many different types of gametes can this organism produce?","choices":["4","8","16","32"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Gametes = 2 to the n","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"2 choices per heterozygous gene: 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16."},"exp":"**Answer:** 16\n\n**Incorrect: 4** This is the number of genes, not gametes.\n\n**Incorrect: 8** This is 2^3, for 3 genes.\n\n**Correct: 16** Each gene gives 2 options, and unlinked genes sort independently: 2^4 = 16.\n\n**Incorrect: 32** This is 2^5, for 5 genes.","how":{"ask":"How many allele combos fit in one gamete?","know":"Gamete types = 2^n, n = heterozygous unlinked genes.","steps":[{"text":"n = 4 heterozygous genes."},{"text":"2^4 = 16, so 4, 8 and 32 are out.","strike":["4","8","32"]},{"text":"Answer: 16."}],"trap":"Picking 8 by multiplying 4 × 2 instead of 2^4."},"teach":[{"text":"Count exponent steps: 2, 4, 8, 16."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Heterozygous unlinked genes → 2^n gamete types."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-113a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"electron transport and gradient","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:113#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:113"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P1","passage":"Colon cancer cells treated with the drug lorvanib lose mitochondrial membrane potential, run low on ATP, and then die. Lorvanib blocks Complex I of the electron transport chain, and 300 nM lorvanib cut oxidative phosphorylation output by 70%. Colon cancer cells and Mito-0 cells, which are colon cancer cells that lack mitochondrial DNA, got either an SOD2 expression vector or an empty vector. SOD2 is a superoxide dismutase. With lorvanib, empty-vector colon cells had high DNA damage, SOD2-vector colon cells had low damage, and Mito-0 cells had low damage with either vector.","q":"Overexpression of SOD2 prevents DNA damage by removing free radicals that form from:","choices":["pyruvate decarboxylation.","GTP hydrolysis.","O2 reduction.","FADH2 oxidation."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Superoxide = O2 plus one electron","c":[0,1]},{"f":"FADH2 oxidation only yields FAD","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"SOD clears superoxide, and superoxide is O2 that gained one extra electron."},"exp":"**Answer:** O2 reduction.\n\n**Incorrect: pyruvate decarboxylation.** This step releases CO2, not superoxide.\n\n**Incorrect: GTP hydrolysis.** This makes GDP and phosphate, not a radical.\n\n**Correct: O2 reduction.** SOD2 removes superoxide. Superoxide forms when O2 takes one electron, which is a reduction.\n\n**Incorrect: FADH2 oxidation.** This makes FAD; the radical forms on O2, not on FADH2.","how":{"ask":"Where do the radicals that SOD2 removes come from?","know":"Superoxide forms when O2 gains one electron.","steps":[{"text":"SOD2 is the enzyme that clears superoxide.","hl":"SOD2 is a superoxide dismutase"},{"text":"Superoxide comes from O2 gaining an electron, so steps without O2 are out.","strike":["pyruvate decarboxylation.","GTP hydrolysis."]},{"text":"FADH2 oxidation just gives FAD, not a radical.","strike":["FADH2 oxidation."]},{"text":"Answer: O2 reduction."}],"trap":"FADH2 oxidation: it feeds the chain, but the radical forms on O2, not on FADH2."},"teach":[{"text":"Link the enzyme to the molecule it clears.","marks":[{"t":"SOD2 is a superoxide dismutase","note":"clears O2 radical"},{"t":"SOD2-vector colon cells had low damage","note":"radicals caused damage"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Superoxide comes from one-electron reduction of O2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-114a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"electron transport and gradient","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:114#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:114"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P1","passage":"Colon cancer cells treated with the drug lorvanib lose mitochondrial membrane potential, run low on ATP, and then die. Lorvanib blocks Complex I of the electron transport chain, and 300 nM lorvanib cut oxidative phosphorylation output by 70%. Colon cancer cells and Mito-0 cells, which are colon cancer cells that lack mitochondrial DNA, got either an SOD2 expression vector or an empty vector. SOD2 is a superoxide dismutase. With lorvanib, empty-vector colon cells had high DNA damage, SOD2-vector colon cells had low damage, and Mito-0 cells had low damage with either vector.","q":"Lorvanib most likely blocks electron transfer from:","choices":["NADH to ubiquinone","cytochrome c to oxygen","ubiquinol to cytochrome c","succinate to ubiquinone"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Complex IV: cyt c to O2","c":[1]},{"f":"Complex III: ubiquinol to cyt c","c":[2]},{"f":"Complex II: succinate to ubiquinone","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Complex I takes electrons from NADH and hands them to ubiquinone."},"exp":"**Answer:** NADH to ubiquinone\n\n**Correct: NADH to ubiquinone** Lorvanib blocks Complex I. Complex I moves electrons from NADH to ubiquinone.\n\n**Incorrect: cytochrome c to oxygen** This is the job of Complex IV.\n\n**Incorrect: ubiquinol to cytochrome c** This is the job of Complex III.\n\n**Incorrect: succinate to ubiquinone** This is the job of Complex II.","how":{"ask":"Which electron hand-off does Complex I do?","know":"I: NADH to Q. II: succinate to Q. III: QH2 to cyt c. IV: cyt c to O2.","steps":[{"text":"The passage says lorvanib blocks Complex I.","hl":"Lorvanib blocks Complex I of the electron transport chain"},{"text":"Cyt c to O2 is Complex IV; ubiquinol to cyt c is Complex III.","strike":["cytochrome c to oxygen","ubiquinol to cytochrome c"]},{"text":"Succinate to ubiquinone is Complex II.","strike":["succinate to ubiquinone"]},{"text":"Answer: NADH to ubiquinone."}],"trap":"Succinate to ubiquinone: it also ends at ubiquinone, but that is Complex II."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the complex, then recall its donor and acceptor.","marks":[{"t":"Complex I","note":"NADH to ubiquinone"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Name each complex by what it takes from and gives to: I NADH to Q, II succinate to Q, III QH2 to cyt c, IV cyt c to O2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-115a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"atp yield and anaerobic pathways","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:115#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:115"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P1","passage":"Colon cancer cells treated with the drug lorvanib lose mitochondrial membrane potential, run low on ATP, and then die. Lorvanib blocks Complex I of the electron transport chain, and 300 nM lorvanib cut oxidative phosphorylation output by 70%. Colon cancer cells and Mito-0 cells, which are colon cancer cells that lack mitochondrial DNA, got either an SOD2 expression vector or an empty vector. SOD2 is a superoxide dismutase. With lorvanib, empty-vector colon cells had high DNA damage, SOD2-vector colon cells had low damage, and Mito-0 cells had low damage with either vector.","q":"What is the principal method of ATP generation in Mito-0 cells?","choices":["Oxidative phosphorylation","Pyruvate oxidation","Glycolysis","β-Oxidation"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"No mtDNA: no working ETC","c":[0]},{"f":"These just feed NADH to ETC","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No mitochondrial DNA means no working chain, so ATP must come from glycolysis in the cytosol."},"exp":"**Answer:** Glycolysis\n\n**Incorrect: Oxidative phosphorylation** Mito-0 cells lack mitochondrial DNA, which codes key chain parts.\n\n**Incorrect: Pyruvate oxidation** It makes NADH and acetyl-CoA, not ATP, and needs the chain to pay off.\n\n**Correct: Glycolysis** It runs in the cytosol and makes ATP without mitochondria. With no working chain, it is the main ATP source.\n\n**Incorrect: β-Oxidation** It makes NADH and FADH2 that need the chain, not ATP directly.","how":{"ask":"How do cells with no working mitochondria make ATP?","know":"Glycolysis makes ATP in the cytosol without O2 or mitochondria.","steps":[{"text":"Mito-0 cells lack mitochondrial DNA, so the chain cannot run.","hl":"cells that lack mitochondrial DNA"},{"text":"No chain means no oxidative phosphorylation.","strike":["Oxidative phosphorylation"]},{"text":"Pyruvate oxidation and β-oxidation mostly make NADH for the chain.","strike":["Pyruvate oxidation","β-Oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: Glycolysis."}],"trap":"β-Oxidation: it breaks down fuel, but its payoff comes through the chain."},"teach":[{"text":"Mito-0 means no mitochondrial genes.","marks":[{"t":"lack mitochondrial DNA","note":"no ETC parts"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** If the chain is gone, glycolysis is the main ATP source."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-116a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:116#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:116"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P1","passage":"Colon cancer cells treated with the drug lorvanib lose mitochondrial membrane potential, run low on ATP, and then die. Lorvanib blocks Complex I of the electron transport chain, and 300 nM lorvanib cut oxidative phosphorylation output by 70%. Colon cancer cells and Mito-0 cells, which are colon cancer cells that lack mitochondrial DNA, got either an SOD2 expression vector or an empty vector. SOD2 is a superoxide dismutase. With lorvanib, empty-vector colon cells had high DNA damage, SOD2-vector colon cells had low damage, and Mito-0 cells had low damage with either vector.","q":"Which statement accurately describes the levels of SOD2 in colon and Mito-0 cells given the empty vector compared with colon and Mito-0 cells given no vector?","choices":["Empty-vector colon cells have more SOD2 than untreated colon cells, and empty-vector Mito-0 cells have less.","Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have about the same SOD2 levels as untreated cells.","Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have lower SOD2 levels than untreated cells.","Empty-vector colon cells have less SOD2 than untreated colon cells, and empty-vector Mito-0 cells have more."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Empty vector adds no SOD2","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Vector alone does not lower SOD2","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"An empty vector carries no gene, so protein levels stay the same as in untreated cells."},"exp":"**Answer:** Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have about the same SOD2 levels as untreated cells.\n\n**Incorrect: Empty-vector colon cells have more SOD2 than untreated colon cells, and empty-vector Mito-0 cells have less.** An empty vector has no SOD2 gene, so it cannot raise or lower SOD2.\n\n**Correct: Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have about the same SOD2 levels as untreated cells.** The empty vector is the control. It carries no gene, so each cell line keeps its normal SOD2 level.\n\n**Incorrect: Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have lower SOD2 levels than untreated cells.** Nothing in an empty vector blocks SOD2.\n\n**Incorrect: Empty-vector colon cells have less SOD2 than untreated colon cells, and empty-vector Mito-0 cells have more.** The vector has no gene, so it changes neither line.","how":{"ask":"Does an empty vector change SOD2 levels?","know":"An empty vector is a control: same delivery, no gene.","steps":[{"text":"Cells got either the SOD2 vector or an empty vector.","hl":"got either an SOD2 expression vector or an empty vector"},{"text":"An empty vector adds no SOD2 gene, so SOD2 cannot rise.","strike":["Empty-vector colon cells have more SOD2 than untreated colon cells, and empty-vector Mito-0 cells have less.","Empty-vector colon cells have less SOD2 than untreated colon cells, and empty-vector Mito-0 cells have more."]},{"text":"It also holds nothing that would lower SOD2.","strike":["Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have lower SOD2 levels than untreated cells."]},{"text":"Answer: Both empty-vector colon and Mito-0 cells have about the same SOD2 levels as untreated cells."}],"trap":"Lower SOD2: it sounds like a real effect, but an empty vector does nothing to the gene."},"teach":[{"text":"Spot the control group.","marks":[{"t":"or an empty vector","note":"control: no gene"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An empty vector controls for the delivery step and leaves protein levels unchanged."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-117a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:117#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:117"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P2","passage":"Cyclic diadenylate (c-di-AMP) is a bacterial signal molecule built from two adenosine monophosphate units. Inside the host, the membrane receptor RX9 binds c-di-AMP and sets off a type I interferon response that turns on innate immune cells. The host protein HLX2 binds RX9 at the same site that c-di-AMP uses. In a crosslinking test, RX9 was mixed with labeled c-di-[32P]AMP plus falling amounts of unlabeled c-di-GMP, dsDNA, or dsRNA. With the most c-di-GMP, the labeled RX9 band was gone, and it came back as c-di-GMP fell. With dsDNA or dsRNA, the band stayed equally strong at every amount.","q":"Which description fits the nucleotide unit of the signal molecule that binds RX9 and causes an IFN response?","choices":["Ribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with NH2","Deoxyribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with CH3","Ribose, phosphate, and a two-ring base with NH2","Ribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with two C=O"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Adenine is a two-ring purine","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The unit is AMP: ribose, one phosphate, and adenine, a two-ring base with an NH2."},"exp":"**Answer:** Ribose, phosphate, and a two-ring base with NH2\n\n**Incorrect: Ribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with NH2** This is CMP; cytosine has one ring.\n\n**Incorrect: Deoxyribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with CH3** This is TMP, a DNA unit with one ring.\n\n**Correct: Ribose, phosphate, and a two-ring base with NH2** The molecule is made of AMP units. Adenine is a two-ring purine with an NH2 group.\n\n**Incorrect: Ribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with two C=O** This is UMP; uracil has one ring.","how":{"ask":"What does one AMP unit look like?","know":"Adenine: two rings plus NH2. C, T, U: one ring.","steps":[{"text":"The signal is built from adenosine monophosphate units.","hl":"built from two adenosine monophosphate units"},{"text":"Adenine is a purine, so it has two rings. One-ring bases are out.","strike":["Ribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with NH2","Deoxyribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with CH3","Ribose, phosphate, and a one-ring base with two C=O"]},{"text":"Answer: Ribose, phosphate, and a two-ring base with NH2."}],"trap":"One-ring base with NH2: that is cytosine, a pyrimidine, even though it has NH2 like adenine."},"teach":[{"text":"Read the unit name in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"adenosine monophosphate","note":"purine, two rings"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Purines (A, G) have two rings; pyrimidines (C, T, U) have one."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-118a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"immune branch and cell type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:118#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:118"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P2","passage":"Cyclic diadenylate (c-di-AMP) is a bacterial signal molecule built from two adenosine monophosphate units. Inside the host, the membrane receptor RX9 binds c-di-AMP and sets off a type I interferon response that turns on innate immune cells. The host protein HLX2 binds RX9 at the same site that c-di-AMP uses. In a crosslinking test, RX9 was mixed with labeled c-di-[32P]AMP plus falling amounts of unlabeled c-di-GMP, dsDNA, or dsRNA. With the most c-di-GMP, the labeled RX9 band was gone, and it came back as c-di-GMP fell. With dsDNA or dsRNA, the band stayed equally strong at every amount.","q":"According to the passage, the immune response set off when c-di-AMP binds RX9 activates which type of cell?","choices":["Plasma cell","Memory T cell","Natural killer cell","Helper T cell"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Passage says innate cells only","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage says innate cells, and NK cells are the only innate cell listed."},"exp":"**Answer:** Natural killer cell\n\n**Incorrect: Plasma cell** Plasma cells come from B cells, part of adaptive immunity.\n\n**Incorrect: Memory T cell** Memory T cells are adaptive cells.\n\n**Correct: Natural killer cell** The response turns on innate immune cells. NK cells are innate.\n\n**Incorrect: Helper T cell** Helper T cells are adaptive cells.","how":{"ask":"Which choice is an innate immune cell?","know":"Innate: NK cells, macrophages, neutrophils. Adaptive: B, plasma and T cells.","steps":[{"text":"The response turns on innate immune cells.","hl":"turns on innate immune cells"},{"text":"Plasma cells and T cells are adaptive.","strike":["Plasma cell","Memory T cell","Helper T cell"]},{"text":"Answer: Natural killer cell."}],"trap":"Helper T cell: interferons help T cells too, but the passage says innate cells."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the branch word in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"innate immune cells","note":"not B or T"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** B cells, plasma cells and all T cells are adaptive; NK cells and phagocytes are innate."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-119a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:119#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:119"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P2","passage":"Cyclic diadenylate (c-di-AMP) is a bacterial signal molecule built from two adenosine monophosphate units. Inside the host, the membrane receptor RX9 binds c-di-AMP and sets off a type I interferon response that turns on innate immune cells. The host protein HLX2 binds RX9 at the same site that c-di-AMP uses. In a crosslinking test, RX9 was mixed with labeled c-di-[32P]AMP plus falling amounts of unlabeled c-di-GMP, dsDNA, or dsRNA. With the most c-di-GMP, the labeled RX9 band was gone, and it came back as c-di-GMP fell. With dsDNA or dsRNA, the band stayed equally strong at every amount.","q":"Which statement about the binding specificity of RX9 is supported by the crosslinking results?","choices":["c-di-[32P]AMP is required for RX9 to bind other molecules.","c-di-GMP competes with c-di-[32P]AMP for binding to RX9.","dsDNA binds RX9 with a higher affinity than c-di-GMP.","RX9 can bind both dsRNA and c-di-[32P]AMP at the same time."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Never tested without labeled AMP","c":[0]},{"f":"Steady band means no binding","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A label band that fades as a rival rises means the rival competes for the site."},"exp":"**Answer:** c-di-GMP competes with c-di-[32P]AMP for binding to RX9.\n\n**Incorrect: c-di-[32P]AMP is required for RX9 to bind other molecules.** No sample lacked the labeled AMP, so this was never tested.\n\n**Correct: c-di-GMP competes with c-di-[32P]AMP for binding to RX9.** High c-di-GMP erased the label band. It came back as c-di-GMP fell, so the two fight for one site.\n\n**Incorrect: dsDNA binds RX9 with a higher affinity than c-di-GMP.** dsDNA never lowered the band, so it showed no binding at all.\n\n**Incorrect: RX9 can bind both dsRNA and c-di-[32P]AMP at the same time.** A steady band shows only that dsRNA did not compete, not that it bound.","how":{"ask":"What does a fading label band tell you?","know":"Unlabeled rival takes the site: label band fades.","steps":[{"text":"High c-di-GMP made the label band vanish.","hl":"With the most c-di-GMP, the labeled RX9 band was gone"},{"text":"Every sample had labeled AMP, so its need was never tested.","strike":["c-di-[32P]AMP is required for RX9 to bind other molecules."]},{"text":"dsDNA and dsRNA left the band unchanged, so they did not bind.","hl":"With dsDNA or dsRNA, the band stayed equally strong at every amount","strike":["dsDNA binds RX9 with a higher affinity than c-di-GMP.","RX9 can bind both dsRNA and c-di-[32P]AMP at the same time."]},{"text":"Answer: c-di-GMP competes with c-di-[32P]AMP for binding to RX9."}],"trap":"Binds dsRNA at the same time: a steady band looks like co-binding, but it just means dsRNA did nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"Read band loss as competition.","marks":[{"t":"the labeled RX9 band was gone","note":"rival took site"},{"t":"stayed equally strong at every amount","note":"no binding"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In a competition assay, only a rival that dims the label band binds the same site."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-120a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"enzyme inhibitor type","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:120#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:120"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P2","passage":"Cyclic diadenylate (c-di-AMP) is a bacterial signal molecule built from two adenosine monophosphate units. Inside the host, the membrane receptor RX9 binds c-di-AMP and sets off a type I interferon response that turns on innate immune cells. The host protein HLX2 binds RX9 at the same site that c-di-AMP uses. In a crosslinking test, RX9 was mixed with labeled c-di-[32P]AMP plus falling amounts of unlabeled c-di-GMP, dsDNA, or dsRNA. With the most c-di-GMP, the labeled RX9 band was gone, and it came back as c-di-GMP fell. With dsDNA or dsRNA, the band stayed equally strong at every amount.","q":"Based on the passage, which graph best represents the binding of c-di-AMP to RX9 in the presence and absence of HLX2?","choices":["With HLX2, the curve shifts left; same maximum","With HLX2, the curve shifts right; lower maximum","With HLX2, the curve is unchanged","With HLX2, the curve shifts right; same maximum"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Competitor needs more ligand: right","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Same site: maximum still reachable","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same-site competitor acts like a competitive inhibitor: shift right, same maximum."},"exp":"**Answer:** With HLX2, the curve shifts right; same maximum\n\n**Incorrect: With HLX2, the curve shifts left; same maximum** A left shift means tighter binding, but a rival makes binding harder.\n\n**Incorrect: With HLX2, the curve shifts right; lower maximum** A lower maximum fits a different-site blocker. Enough c-di-AMP can still win a shared site.\n\n**Incorrect: With HLX2, the curve is unchanged** HLX2 takes the same site, so it must change the curve.\n\n**Correct: With HLX2, the curve shifts right; same maximum** HLX2 competes for the same site. More c-di-AMP is needed for each level of binding, but high c-di-AMP still fills every site.","how":{"ask":"What does a same-site rival do to a binding curve?","know":"Competitive: curve right, same maximum.","steps":[{"text":"HLX2 binds the same site as c-di-AMP.","hl":"binds RX9 at the same site that c-di-AMP uses"},{"text":"A rival makes binding weaker, so the curve must move right.","strike":["With HLX2, the curve shifts left; same maximum","With HLX2, the curve is unchanged"]},{"text":"Enough c-di-AMP outcompetes HLX2, so the maximum stays.","strike":["With HLX2, the curve shifts right; lower maximum"]},{"text":"Answer: With HLX2, the curve shifts right; same maximum."}],"trap":"Right shift with lower maximum: that is a noncompetitive pattern, not a same-site rival."},"teach":[{"text":"Same site means competitive.","marks":[{"t":"at the same site","note":"competitive"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A same-site competitor shifts the binding curve right and keeps the maximum."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-121a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"vessel size along the circuit","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:121#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:121"],"q":"Which statement best describes how blood vessel diameter changes across the human circulatory system?","choices":["The diameter of arteries grows larger as they branch away from the heart.","The diameter of veins grows larger as venules join on the way to the heart.","The diameter of a vein is always smaller than the artery beside it.","The diameter of arterioles responds to hormones but not to local signals."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Arteries narrow away from heart","c":[0]},{"f":"Veins are wider than paired arteries","c":[2]},{"f":"Local signals also change arterioles","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Small vessels merge into big ones on the way back, so veins widen toward the heart."},"exp":"**Answer:** The diameter of veins grows larger as venules join on the way to the heart.\n\n**Incorrect: The diameter of arteries grows larger as they branch away from the heart.** Arteries get narrower as they branch away from the heart.\n\n**Correct: The diameter of veins grows larger as venules join on the way to the heart.** Venules merge into veins, and veins merge into the venae cavae. Each merge makes a wider vessel.\n\n**Incorrect: The diameter of a vein is always smaller than the artery beside it.** Veins are usually wider than the arteries they run beside.\n\n**Incorrect: The diameter of arterioles responds to hormones but not to local signals.** Local signals like CO2, NO and low O2 also widen arterioles.","how":{"ask":"How does vessel width change along the loop?","know":"Arteries branch and narrow; veins merge and widen.","steps":[{"text":"Arteries split into smaller and smaller vessels away from the heart.","strike":["The diameter of arteries grows larger as they branch away from the heart."]},{"text":"Veins have thin walls and wide lumens, wider than paired arteries.","strike":["The diameter of a vein is always smaller than the artery beside it."]},{"text":"Arterioles respond to local signals like NO and CO2.","strike":["The diameter of arterioles responds to hormones but not to local signals."]},{"text":"Answer: The diameter of veins grows larger as venules join on the way to the heart."}],"trap":"Veins always smaller: arteries look sturdier, but veins have the wider lumen."},"teach":[{"text":"Picture a river system: streams merge into a big river on the way back to the heart."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Arteries narrow as they branch out; veins widen as they merge back."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-122a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"synaptic transmission","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:122#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:122"],"q":"Which treatment(s) would most likely cause sustained, uncontrolled skeletal muscle contraction? I. A toxin that blocks ACh release II. A blocker of calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum III. An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor","choices":["I only","II only","III only","I and III only"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"No ACh release: no contraction","c":[0,3]},{"f":"No Ca2+ release: no contraction","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only a treatment that keeps ACh in the cleft keeps the muscle firing."},"exp":"**Answer:** III only\n\n**Incorrect: I only** Blocking ACh release stops the signal, so the muscle goes limp.\n\n**Incorrect: II only** Without SR calcium, actin and myosin cannot bind, so the muscle relaxes.\n\n**Correct: III only** Acetylcholinesterase breaks down ACh. Blocking it leaves ACh in the cleft, so the muscle keeps contracting.\n\n**Incorrect: I and III only** Item I causes paralysis, not contraction.","how":{"ask":"Which treatment keeps the muscle signal on?","know":"ACh starts contraction; acetylcholinesterase ends it.","steps":[{"text":"Item I stops ACh release, so there is no signal.","strike":["I only","I and III only"]},{"text":"Item II stops Ca2+ release, so the fibers cannot contract.","strike":["II only"]},{"text":"Item III leaves ACh in the cleft, so the signal never stops."},{"text":"Answer: III only."}],"trap":"I and III: both act on ACh, but blocking release does the opposite of blocking breakdown."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask of each item: does it add signal or remove it?"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Blocking acetylcholinesterase prolongs ACh action and causes sustained contraction."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-123a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"identify study variables","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:123#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:123"],"q":"A researcher wants to know whether a course of antibiotics changes the bacteria living on a person's skin. What is a potential dependent variable for this study?","choices":["Length of the antibiotic course","Type of antibiotic given","Number of skin bacterial species","Season of the year"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Dependent = the outcome measured","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Season is neither cause nor outcome","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The dependent variable is the thing you measure to see the effect: here, the skin bacteria."},"exp":"**Answer:** Number of skin bacterial species\n\n**Incorrect: Length of the antibiotic course** This is part of the treatment, a factor the researcher sets.\n\n**Incorrect: Type of antibiotic given** This is the independent variable the researcher picks.\n\n**Correct: Number of skin bacterial species** The study asks if skin bacteria change. The count of species is the outcome the researcher measures.\n\n**Incorrect: Season of the year** This is a background factor, not the measured outcome.","how":{"ask":"Which choice is the measured outcome?","know":"Independent = what you change. Dependent = what you measure.","steps":[{"text":"The question is whether antibiotics change skin bacteria.","strike":["Season of the year"]},{"text":"The antibiotic and its length are what the researcher sets.","strike":["Length of the antibiotic course","Type of antibiotic given"]},{"text":"The outcome is the skin bacteria themselves."},{"text":"Answer: Number of skin bacterial species."}],"trap":"Length of the antibiotic course: it can affect results, but it is a cause, not the outcome."},"teach":[{"text":"Split the question into cause and effect: antibiotics (cause) change skin bacteria (effect)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The dependent variable is the effect you measure, never the treatment you choose."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-124a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:124#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:124"],"q":"Of the following, which is NOT accepted as a possible explanation of how mammalian embryonic cells become different tissues?","choices":["Different cells lose different genes from their DNA during cleavage.","Some transcripts are destroyed in the nucleus before export.","Certain genes turn on at set times in development.","Some mRNAs are stored in an inactive form until a signal releases them."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"RNA control shapes gene output","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Timed gene activation is accepted","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Every body cell keeps the same genome, so any choice about losing DNA is wrong."},"exp":"**Answer:** Different cells lose different genes from their DNA during cleavage.\n\n**Correct: Different cells lose different genes from their DNA during cleavage.** Body cells keep the same full genome. They differ in which genes they use, not which genes they have.\n\n**Incorrect: Some transcripts are destroyed in the nucleus before export.** RNA decay is an accepted way to control gene output.\n\n**Incorrect: Certain genes turn on at set times in development.** Timed gene activation is a core part of development.\n\n**Incorrect: Some mRNAs are stored in an inactive form until a signal releases them.** Stored, masked mRNAs are well known in embryos.","how":{"ask":"Which idea about differentiation is false?","know":"Differentiation = same DNA, different gene use.","steps":[{"text":"Destroying or storing RNA changes gene use, which is accepted.","strike":["Some transcripts are destroyed in the nucleus before export.","Some mRNAs are stored in an inactive form until a signal releases them."]},{"text":"Turning genes on at set times is accepted.","strike":["Certain genes turn on at set times in development."]},{"text":"Losing genes from DNA is not how body cells differ."},{"text":"Answer: Different cells lose different genes from their DNA during cleavage."}],"trap":"Transcripts destroyed before export: it sounds wasteful, but RNA decay is a real control step."},"teach":[{"text":"In a NOT question, the three true statements are the wrong choices."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Cells differentiate by using genes differently, never by losing DNA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-125a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:125#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:125"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P3","passage":"Adipokine F is a 14 kDa protein that fat cells release into the blood in proportion to body fat. It acts on the hypothalamus to curb appetite and raise energy use. In mice fed a high-fat diet, lipid overload causes mild, lasting inflammation in the hypothalamus. One obese mouse strain carries a single base change that turned the UCA serine codon at position 92 of Adipokine F mRNA into a stop codon. Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite, while orexigenic signals stimulate it.","q":"The serine at position 92 in human Adipokine F is encoded by an AGC codon. Given this information, as compared to mice, introduction of a stop codon in the human Adipokine F gene is:","choices":["more likely, because a change in only one nucleotide would be required.","less likely, because a change in two nucleotides would be required.","less likely, because purine to purine changes are more common than pyrimidine to purine changes.","more likely, because wobble pairing weakens the third base of a codon."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"AGC to UGA: two changes","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Mutation-type bias isn't the reason","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Line the codon up with UAA, UAG and UGA and count the fewest letters that must change."},"exp":"**Answer:** less likely, because a change in two nucleotides would be required.\n\n**Incorrect: more likely, because a change in only one nucleotide would be required.** No stop codon is one letter away from AGC.\n\n**Correct: less likely, because a change in two nucleotides would be required.** Mouse UCA becomes UAA or UGA with one change. Human AGC needs two changes to become UGA, so a stop is less likely.\n\n**Incorrect: less likely, because purine to purine changes are more common than pyrimidine to purine changes.** The reason is the number of changes needed, not the type of change.\n\n**Incorrect: more likely, because wobble pairing weakens the third base of a codon.** Wobble affects tRNA pairing, not mutation odds.","how":{"ask":"How many base changes turn AGC into a stop codon?","know":"Stop codons: UAA, UAG, UGA.","steps":[{"text":"In mice, UCA became a stop codon with one change.","hl":"single base change that turned the UCA serine codon"},{"text":"Human AGC vs UGA: first and third letters differ, so two changes.","strike":["more likely, because a change in only one nucleotide would be required.","more likely, because wobble pairing weakens the third base of a codon."]},{"text":"The count of changes, not the type, explains it.","strike":["less likely, because purine to purine changes are more common than pyrimidine to purine changes."]},{"text":"Answer: less likely, because a change in two nucleotides would be required."}],"trap":"Purine vs pyrimidine reason: it sounds technical, but the answer is just counting letters."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare codons letter by letter.","marks":[{"t":"UCA serine codon","note":"one change to UAA"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Count the fewest base changes to reach UAA, UAG or UGA; more changes means less likely."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-126a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:126#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:126"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P3","passage":"Adipokine F is a 14 kDa protein that fat cells release into the blood in proportion to body fat. It acts on the hypothalamus to curb appetite and raise energy use. In mice fed a high-fat diet, lipid overload causes mild, lasting inflammation in the hypothalamus. One obese mouse strain carries a single base change that turned the UCA serine codon at position 92 of Adipokine F mRNA into a stop codon. Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite, while orexigenic signals stimulate it.","q":"Adipokine F transport from the bloodstream into the CNS:","choices":["rises during fasting and falls after a meal is eaten and digested.","stays constant because the blood–brain barrier never changes its permeability.","falls as the plasma level of Adipokine F rises.","falls during fasting and rises after a meal is eaten and digested."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Satiety signal rises after eating","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Barrier permeability can change","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"An appetite brake should reach the brain when you are fed, not when you are starving."},"exp":"**Answer:** falls during fasting and rises after a meal is eaten and digested.\n\n**Incorrect: rises during fasting and falls after a meal is eaten and digested.** This is backward; a hunger brake is not needed while fasting.\n\n**Incorrect: stays constant because the blood–brain barrier never changes its permeability.** Barrier transport can change, for example at the hypothalamus.\n\n**Incorrect: falls as the plasma level of Adipokine F rises.** More hormone in blood should mean more reaches the brain, a positive link.\n\n**Correct: falls during fasting and rises after a meal is eaten and digested.** Adipokine F curbs appetite. It should reach the brain less during fasting and more after feeding.","how":{"ask":"When should an appetite brake reach the brain?","know":"Satiety signals rise with feeding and fall with fasting.","steps":[{"text":"Adipokine F curbs appetite.","hl":"It acts on the hypothalamus to curb appetite"},{"text":"So it should reach the brain after meals, not during fasting.","strike":["rises during fasting and falls after a meal is eaten and digested.","falls as the plasma level of Adipokine F rises."]},{"text":"Barrier transport is not fixed.","strike":["stays constant because the blood–brain barrier never changes its permeability."]},{"text":"Answer: falls during fasting and rises after a meal is eaten and digested."}],"trap":"Rises during fasting: fasting is a big change, but this hormone acts when you are fed."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the hormone's job first.","marks":[{"t":"curb appetite","note":"satiety signal"},{"t":"in proportion to body fat","note":"more fat, more signal"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A satiety hormone acts most after feeding and least during fasting."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-127a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"glial cell roles","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:127#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:127"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P3","passage":"Adipokine F is a 14 kDa protein that fat cells release into the blood in proportion to body fat. It acts on the hypothalamus to curb appetite and raise energy use. In mice fed a high-fat diet, lipid overload causes mild, lasting inflammation in the hypothalamus. One obese mouse strain carries a single base change that turned the UCA serine codon at position 92 of Adipokine F mRNA into a stop codon. Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite, while orexigenic signals stimulate it.","q":"According to the passage, feeding mice a high-fat diet will most likely lead to which effect within the brain?","choices":["Faster ependymal cilia beating","Activation of microglia","More myelin from oligodendrocytes","Decreased Schwann cell activity"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Inflammation calls brain immune cells","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Schwann cells live outside CNS","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Brain inflammation means the brain's immune cells, the microglia, switch on."},"exp":"**Answer:** Activation of microglia\n\n**Incorrect: Faster ependymal cilia beating** Ependymal cells move cerebrospinal fluid; nothing links them to inflammation.\n\n**Correct: Activation of microglia** The diet causes brain inflammation. Microglia are the brain's immune cells, so they activate.\n\n**Incorrect: More myelin from oligodendrocytes** Oligodendrocytes make myelin, not an immune response.\n\n**Incorrect: Decreased Schwann cell activity** Schwann cells are in the peripheral nervous system, not the brain.","how":{"ask":"Which glial cell responds to brain inflammation?","know":"Microglia = brain immune cells.","steps":[{"text":"The high-fat diet causes inflammation in the brain.","hl":"lipid overload causes mild, lasting inflammation in the hypothalamus"},{"text":"Ependymal cells and oligodendrocytes do not run immune responses.","strike":["Faster ependymal cilia beating","More myelin from oligodendrocytes"]},{"text":"Schwann cells are outside the brain.","strike":["Decreased Schwann cell activity"]},{"text":"Answer: Activation of microglia."}],"trap":"Oligodendrocytes: they are CNS glia, but they make myelin, not an immune response."},"teach":[{"text":"Turn the passage word into a cell.","marks":[{"t":"inflammation in the hypothalamus","note":"microglia respond"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Inflammation in the CNS activates microglia, the brain's resident immune cells."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-128a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:128#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:128"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P3","passage":"Adipokine F is a 14 kDa protein that fat cells release into the blood in proportion to body fat. It acts on the hypothalamus to curb appetite and raise energy use. In mice fed a high-fat diet, lipid overload causes mild, lasting inflammation in the hypothalamus. One obese mouse strain carries a single base change that turned the UCA serine codon at position 92 of Adipokine F mRNA into a stop codon. Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite, while orexigenic signals stimulate it.","q":"The primary function of which hormone has an anorexigenic effect on feeding behavior?","choices":["Ghrelin","Gastrin","Cholecystokinin","Secretin"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Ghrelin stimulates appetite","c":[0]},{"f":"Gastrin, secretin: digestion, not appetite","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Anorexigenic means appetite-suppressing; CCK is the gut's fullness signal."},"exp":"**Answer:** Cholecystokinin\n\n**Incorrect: Ghrelin** Ghrelin from the stomach stimulates hunger, so it is orexigenic.\n\n**Incorrect: Gastrin** Gastrin drives stomach acid release, not appetite control.\n\n**Correct: Cholecystokinin** CCK is released when food reaches the small intestine. It signals fullness and suppresses appetite.\n\n**Incorrect: Secretin** Secretin makes the pancreas release bicarbonate, not appetite control.","how":{"ask":"Which hormone mainly suppresses appetite?","know":"Ghrelin = hunger. CCK = fullness.","steps":[{"text":"Anorexigenic means it suppresses appetite.","hl":"Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite"},{"text":"Ghrelin is the hunger hormone.","strike":["Ghrelin"]},{"text":"Gastrin and secretin mainly control digestion.","strike":["Gastrin","Secretin"]},{"text":"Answer: Cholecystokinin."}],"trap":"Gastrin: it is released after meals, but its main job is acid, not fullness."},"teach":[{"text":"Decode the word first.","marks":[{"t":"Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite","note":"fullness signal"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ghrelin drives hunger; CCK, PYY and leptin-like signals suppress it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-129a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"tissue type classification","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:129#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:129"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P3","passage":"Adipokine F is a 14 kDa protein that fat cells release into the blood in proportion to body fat. It acts on the hypothalamus to curb appetite and raise energy use. In mice fed a high-fat diet, lipid overload causes mild, lasting inflammation in the hypothalamus. One obese mouse strain carries a single base change that turned the UCA serine codon at position 92 of Adipokine F mRNA into a stop codon. Anorexigenic signals suppress appetite, while orexigenic signals stimulate it.","q":"According to the passage, the cells that secrete Adipokine F belong to which type of tissue?","choices":["Connective","Epithelial","Muscle","Nervous"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Adipose is a connective tissue","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fat cells make adipose tissue, and adipose is a connective tissue."},"exp":"**Answer:** Connective\n\n**Correct: Connective** Fat cells make Adipokine F. Fat cells form adipose tissue, which is a connective tissue.\n\n**Incorrect: Epithelial** Epithelium lines body surfaces; fat cells do not.\n\n**Incorrect: Muscle** Muscle cells contract; fat cells store lipid.\n\n**Incorrect: Nervous** The hormone acts on the brain, but it is made by fat cells.","how":{"ask":"What tissue type are fat cells?","know":"Adipose tissue is connective tissue.","steps":[{"text":"Fat cells release Adipokine F.","hl":"fat cells release into the blood"},{"text":"Fat cells do not line surfaces or contract.","strike":["Epithelial","Muscle"]},{"text":"The brain is the target, not the source.","strike":["Nervous"]},{"text":"Answer: Connective."}],"trap":"Nervous: the hormone acts on the brain, but the question asks who makes it."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the source cell, not the target.","marks":[{"t":"fat cells release into the blood","note":"source = adipose"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Adipose, bone, cartilage and blood are all connective tissues."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-130a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:130#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:130"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P4","passage":"Disorder Q is a blood disease in which the marrow makes too many neutrophils. When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1, two GR-1 units pair up and the attached kinase KIN3 phosphorylates itself and turns on. Changing tyrosine 205 of KIN3 to phenylalanine (KIN3Y205F) blocks this self-phosphorylation. Active KIN3 phosphorylates TF5, a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer, moves to the nucleus, and binds the DNA phosphate backbone at growth genes. The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Two mutations help cause Disorder Q: GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor, and KIN3 V580F silences miR-41, a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels.","q":"Which type of compound can be used as a therapeutic agent for Disorder Q? A compound that:","choices":["counters the effect of PHX2.","counters the effect of miR-41.","mimics the effect of the GR-1 L410R mutation.","mimics the effect of the KIN3Y205F substitution."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"PHX2 and miR-41 brake disease","c":[0,1]},{"f":"L410R itself causes Disorder Q","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The disease is too much KIN3 signaling, so a drug must turn KIN3 off."},"exp":"**Answer:** mimics the effect of the KIN3Y205F substitution.\n\n**Incorrect: counters the effect of PHX2.** PHX2 turns KIN3 off; blocking it would raise signaling and worsen the disease.\n\n**Incorrect: counters the effect of miR-41.** miR-41 lowers GR-1; blocking it would raise GR-1 and worsen the disease.\n\n**Incorrect: mimics the effect of the GR-1 L410R mutation.** L410R turns on signaling and helps cause the disease.\n\n**Correct: mimics the effect of the KIN3Y205F substitution.** Y205F stops KIN3 from turning itself on. Copying it would cut the extra signaling.","how":{"ask":"Which drug lowers GR-1 to KIN3 signaling?","know":"Treat overactive signaling by blocking it, never by removing its brakes.","steps":[{"text":"Y205F blocks KIN3 activation.","hl":"blocks this self-phosphorylation"},{"text":"PHX2 and miR-41 are brakes; countering them worsens the disease.","hl":"The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off","strike":["counters the effect of PHX2.","counters the effect of miR-41."]},{"text":"L410R is a cause of the disease.","strike":["mimics the effect of the GR-1 L410R mutation."]},{"text":"Answer: mimics the effect of the KIN3Y205F substitution."}],"trap":"Counters miR-41: blocking a microRNA sounds like a treatment, but this one is a brake."},"teach":[{"text":"Label each player as gas or brake.","marks":[{"t":"turns KIN3 off","note":"brake"},{"t":"a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels","note":"brake"},{"t":"turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor","note":"gas"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For an overactive pathway, mimic the brakes or the blockers, and never counter them."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-131a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:131#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:131"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P4","passage":"Disorder Q is a blood disease in which the marrow makes too many neutrophils. When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1, two GR-1 units pair up and the attached kinase KIN3 phosphorylates itself and turns on. Changing tyrosine 205 of KIN3 to phenylalanine (KIN3Y205F) blocks this self-phosphorylation. Active KIN3 phosphorylates TF5, a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer, moves to the nucleus, and binds the DNA phosphate backbone at growth genes. The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Two mutations help cause Disorder Q: GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor, and KIN3 V580F silences miR-41, a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels.","q":"Researchers used a western blot to measure phosphorylated KIN3 (P-KIN3) in wild-type (WT) and GR-1 L410R precursor cells, with or without G-factor. Which pattern accurately depicts the expected results?","choices":["WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 only with G-factor","WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor","WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: no P-KIN3 either way","WT: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 only with G-factor"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"L410R signals without G-factor","c":[0,2]},{"f":"WT needs G-factor to signal","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"WT needs the hormone; the L410R mutant is always on, so it shows P-KIN3 in both lanes."},"exp":"**Answer:** WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor\n\n**Incorrect: WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 only with G-factor** This shows L410R needing G-factor, but it signals without it.\n\n**Correct: WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor** WT KIN3 turns on only when G-factor binds. L410R turns on signaling without G-factor, so P-KIN3 shows in both lanes.\n\n**Incorrect: WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: no P-KIN3 either way** L410R activates signaling; it does not kill it.\n\n**Incorrect: WT: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 only with G-factor** This swaps the two cell types.","how":{"ask":"When is KIN3 phosphorylated in each cell type?","know":"Ligand-independent = on with or without the hormone.","steps":[{"text":"In WT, KIN3 turns on when G-factor binds.","hl":"When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1"},{"text":"WT should not signal without G-factor.","strike":["WT: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 only with G-factor"]},{"text":"L410R signals with no G-factor, so it has P-KIN3 in both lanes.","hl":"GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor","strike":["WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 only with G-factor","WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: no P-KIN3 either way"]},{"text":"Answer: WT: P-KIN3 only with G-factor; L410R: P-KIN3 with and without G-factor."}],"trap":"No P-KIN3 in L410R: a mutation sounds like a loss, but this one is activating."},"teach":[{"text":"Predict each lane before reading choices.","marks":[{"t":"without G-factor","note":"mutant always on"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An activating, ligand-independent mutant shows the signal with and without ligand."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-132a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:132#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:132"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P4","passage":"Disorder Q is a blood disease in which the marrow makes too many neutrophils. When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1, two GR-1 units pair up and the attached kinase KIN3 phosphorylates itself and turns on. Changing tyrosine 205 of KIN3 to phenylalanine (KIN3Y205F) blocks this self-phosphorylation. Active KIN3 phosphorylates TF5, a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer, moves to the nucleus, and binds the DNA phosphate backbone at growth genes. The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Two mutations help cause Disorder Q: GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor, and KIN3 V580F silences miR-41, a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels.","q":"Which amino acid is most likely involved in the binding of TF5 to growth gene promoters?","choices":["Glutamate","Valine","Glycine","Lysine"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Glutamate is negative: repelled","c":[0]},{"f":"Nonpolar side chains: no attraction","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The DNA backbone is negative, so pick the positive (basic) side chain."},"exp":"**Answer:** Lysine\n\n**Incorrect: Glutamate** Glutamate is negative, so it is pushed away from the negative backbone.\n\n**Incorrect: Valine** Valine is nonpolar and uncharged.\n\n**Incorrect: Glycine** Glycine has just an H side chain and no charge.\n\n**Correct: Lysine** TF5 binds the phosphate backbone, which is negative. Lysine is positive, so it is drawn to the backbone.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain sticks to negative phosphates?","know":"Basic residues (Lys, Arg, His) are positive.","steps":[{"text":"TF5 binds the DNA phosphate backbone, which is negative.","hl":"binds the DNA phosphate backbone"},{"text":"Glutamate is negative and would repel it.","strike":["Glutamate"]},{"text":"Valine and glycine carry no charge.","strike":["Valine","Glycine"]},{"text":"Answer: Lysine."}],"trap":"Glutamate: it is charged, but with the same sign as DNA."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the binding partner's charge.","marks":[{"t":"phosphate backbone","note":"negative charge"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Proteins grip the DNA backbone with positive residues like lysine and arginine."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-133a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:133#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:133"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P4","passage":"Disorder Q is a blood disease in which the marrow makes too many neutrophils. When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1, two GR-1 units pair up and the attached kinase KIN3 phosphorylates itself and turns on. Changing tyrosine 205 of KIN3 to phenylalanine (KIN3Y205F) blocks this self-phosphorylation. Active KIN3 phosphorylates TF5, a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer, moves to the nucleus, and binds the DNA phosphate backbone at growth genes. The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Two mutations help cause Disorder Q: GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor, and KIN3 V580F silences miR-41, a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels.","q":"Which method(s) could separate the cytoplasmic form of TF5 from its nuclear form? I. Native PAGE II. SDS-PAGE III. Size-exclusion chromatography","choices":["I only","II only","I and III only","II and III only"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"SDS breaks the noncovalent dimer","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Size-exclusion keeps dimers intact too","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Monomer vs dimer only shows if the method keeps the dimer whole, so drop SDS."},"exp":"**Answer:** I and III only\n\n**Incorrect: I only** Native PAGE works, but size-exclusion also keeps the dimer and sorts by size.\n\n**Incorrect: II only** SDS pulls the noncovalent dimer apart, so both forms run as one band.\n\n**Correct: I and III only** The cytoplasmic form is a monomer and the nuclear form is a noncovalent dimer. Native PAGE and size-exclusion keep the dimer intact and separate by size.\n\n**Incorrect: II and III only** SDS-PAGE splits the dimer, so it cannot tell the forms apart.","how":{"ask":"Which methods keep a noncovalent dimer intact?","know":"SDS denatures and splits subunits; native methods keep them.","steps":[{"text":"Cytoplasmic TF5 is a monomer; nuclear TF5 is a noncovalent dimer.","hl":"a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer"},{"text":"SDS breaks noncovalent bonds, so both forms look the same.","strike":["II only","II and III only"]},{"text":"Size-exclusion is native and sorts by size, so it also works.","strike":["I only"]},{"text":"Answer: I and III only."}],"trap":"I only: native PAGE is the obvious pick, but size-exclusion separates by size too."},"teach":[{"text":"Note how the dimer is held together.","marks":[{"t":"noncovalent dimer","note":"SDS breaks it"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To tell a monomer from a noncovalent dimer, use native PAGE or size-exclusion, not SDS-PAGE."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-134a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:134#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:134"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P4","passage":"Disorder Q is a blood disease in which the marrow makes too many neutrophils. When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1, two GR-1 units pair up and the attached kinase KIN3 phosphorylates itself and turns on. Changing tyrosine 205 of KIN3 to phenylalanine (KIN3Y205F) blocks this self-phosphorylation. Active KIN3 phosphorylates TF5, a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer, moves to the nucleus, and binds the DNA phosphate backbone at growth genes. The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Two mutations help cause Disorder Q: GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor, and KIN3 V580F silences miR-41, a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels.","q":"The activating mutation in the sequence of GR-1 described in the passage results in the substitution of a(n):","choices":["acidic amino acid to a basic amino acid.","basic amino acid to a hydrophobic amino acid.","hydrophobic amino acid to a charged amino acid.","aromatic amino acid to an uncharged, polar amino acid."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Leucine (L) is hydrophobic","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Arginine (R) is charged, basic","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Decode the letters: L = leucine (hydrophobic), R = arginine (charged)."},"exp":"**Answer:** hydrophobic amino acid to a charged amino acid.\n\n**Incorrect: acidic amino acid to a basic amino acid.** Leucine is hydrophobic, not acidic.\n\n**Incorrect: basic amino acid to a hydrophobic amino acid.** This is backward: leucine is hydrophobic and arginine is basic.\n\n**Correct: hydrophobic amino acid to a charged amino acid.** L410R swaps leucine, a hydrophobic residue, for arginine, a positively charged residue.\n\n**Incorrect: aromatic amino acid to an uncharged, polar amino acid.** Leucine is not aromatic, and arginine is charged.","how":{"ask":"What kinds of residues are L and R?","know":"L = leucine, nonpolar. R = arginine, basic.","steps":[{"text":"The GR-1 mutation is L410R: leucine to arginine.","hl":"GR-1 L410R"},{"text":"Leucine is hydrophobic, so it is not the acid or base at the start.","strike":["acidic amino acid to a basic amino acid.","basic amino acid to a hydrophobic amino acid."]},{"text":"Leucine is not aromatic, and arginine is charged.","strike":["aromatic amino acid to an uncharged, polar amino acid."]},{"text":"Answer: hydrophobic amino acid to a charged amino acid."}],"trap":"Basic to hydrophobic: right two words, wrong order."},"teach":[{"text":"Mutation names read old residue, position, new residue.","marks":[{"t":"L410R","note":"Leu to Arg"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In XnY, X is the original residue and Y is the new one; classify each by side chain."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-135a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:135#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:135"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P4","passage":"Disorder Q is a blood disease in which the marrow makes too many neutrophils. When the hormone G-factor binds its receptor GR-1, two GR-1 units pair up and the attached kinase KIN3 phosphorylates itself and turns on. Changing tyrosine 205 of KIN3 to phenylalanine (KIN3Y205F) blocks this self-phosphorylation. Active KIN3 phosphorylates TF5, a monomer in the cytoplasm, which then forms a noncovalent dimer, moves to the nucleus, and binds the DNA phosphate backbone at growth genes. The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Two mutations help cause Disorder Q: GR-1 L410R turns on GR-1 signaling without G-factor, and KIN3 V580F silences miR-41, a microRNA that lowers GR-1 levels.","q":"Based on the passage, precursor cells with which genotype have the highest rate of proliferation?","choices":["Wild type","GR-1–/–","PHX2–/–","GR-1–/–, PHX2–/–"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"No GR-1: no signal at all","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Losing the PHX2 brake beats WT","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Remove the brake (PHX2) but keep the gas (GR-1) for the most growth."},"exp":"**Answer:** PHX2–/–\n\n**Incorrect: Wild type** WT still has PHX2 turning KIN3 off, so growth is normal.\n\n**Incorrect: GR-1–/–** With no receptor, KIN3 is never turned on, so growth drops.\n\n**Correct: PHX2–/–** PHX2 turns KIN3 off. Without it, KIN3 stays active and drives the most growth.\n\n**Incorrect: GR-1–/–, PHX2–/–** Losing the brake does not help if the receptor is gone.","how":{"ask":"Which genotype gives the most KIN3 signaling?","know":"Knock out a brake = more signal; knock out the receptor = none.","steps":[{"text":"PHX2 is a brake on KIN3.","hl":"The phosphatase PHX2 turns KIN3 off"},{"text":"Without GR-1 there is no signal, brake or not.","strike":["GR-1–/–","GR-1–/–, PHX2–/–"]},{"text":"WT keeps its brake, so it grows less than PHX2–/–.","strike":["Wild type"]},{"text":"Answer: PHX2–/–."}],"trap":"Double knockout: losing the brake sounds good, but with no receptor there is nothing to release."},"teach":[{"text":"Tag each gene as gas or brake.","marks":[{"t":"turns KIN3 off","note":"brake"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The upstream receptor knockout wins: if the receptor is gone, removing a brake does nothing."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-136a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"x-linked cross probability","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:136#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:136"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P5","passage":"Disease M is an X-linked recessive disorder in which the CNS fails to make normal myelin. It is caused by mutations in the gene for myelin protein X (MPX). MPX-S is an isoform made from the same gene that lacks 30 residues of the cytosolic loop. MPX is the main structural protein of CNS myelin and spans the myelin membrane. MPX is a single 250-residue chain held together by three disulfide bonds. Region 1 is an extracellular loop, Region 2 is a transmembrane α helix, Region 3 is the cytosolic loop, and Region 4 is a second extracellular loop. In one affected family, a single point mutation replaced a proline with an alanine.","q":"A man with Disease M has a child with a woman who does not carry the allele. What is the probability that their child will be female and a carrier?","choices":["0%","25%","50%","100%"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Every daughter gets dad's X","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Only half of children are daughters","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Dad's X goes to every daughter, and half of all children are daughters: 1 × 1/2."},"exp":"**Answer:** 50%\n\n**Incorrect: 0%** Every daughter gets her father's mutant X, so carriers are certain among daughters.\n\n**Incorrect: 25%** All daughters, not half of them, are carriers.\n\n**Correct: 50%** The chance of a daughter is 1/2. Each daughter gets dad's mutant X and mom's normal X, so she is a carrier. 1/2 × 1 = 50%.\n\n**Incorrect: 100%** Sons get dad's Y, not his X, so not every child is a carrier daughter.","how":{"ask":"What share of all children are carrier daughters?","know":"A father gives his X to all daughters and his Y to all sons.","steps":[{"text":"The disease is X-linked recessive.","hl":"X-linked recessive"},{"text":"Every daughter gets dad's mutant X, so each daughter is a carrier.","strike":["0%","25%"]},{"text":"Only half of children are daughters.","strike":["100%"]},{"text":"Answer: 50%."}],"trap":"100%: all daughters are carriers, but the question asks about all children."},"teach":[{"text":"Multiply the sex chance by the carrier chance.","marks":[{"t":"X-linked recessive","note":"dad's X to daughters"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For an affected father, every daughter is a carrier and no son is affected."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-137a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"mrna splicing","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:137#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:137"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P5","passage":"Disease M is an X-linked recessive disorder in which the CNS fails to make normal myelin. It is caused by mutations in the gene for myelin protein X (MPX). MPX-S is an isoform made from the same gene that lacks 30 residues of the cytosolic loop. MPX is the main structural protein of CNS myelin and spans the myelin membrane. MPX is a single 250-residue chain held together by three disulfide bonds. Region 1 is an extracellular loop, Region 2 is a transmembrane α helix, Region 3 is the cytosolic loop, and Region 4 is a second extracellular loop. In one affected family, a single point mutation replaced a proline with an alanine.","q":"MPX-S can be translated from the MPX gene because:","choices":["RNA polymerase skips the parts of the MPX gene not used in MPX-S.","proteases trim the finished MPX protein into the shorter MPX-S.","a spliceosome joins a different set of exons from the same pre-mRNA.","ribosomes skip MPX mRNA codons that MPX-S does not use."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Isoforms come from alternative splicing","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two proteins from one gene = alternative splicing of one pre-mRNA."},"exp":"**Answer:** a spliceosome joins a different set of exons from the same pre-mRNA.\n\n**Incorrect: RNA polymerase skips the parts of the MPX gene not used in MPX-S.** RNA polymerase copies the whole gene; it does not pick exons.\n\n**Incorrect: proteases trim the finished MPX protein into the shorter MPX-S.** MPX-S is an isoform, not a cleavage product.\n\n**Correct: a spliceosome joins a different set of exons from the same pre-mRNA.** MPX-S is an isoform from the same gene. Alternative splicing leaves out the exon for the 30 missing residues.\n\n**Incorrect: ribosomes skip MPX mRNA codons that MPX-S does not use.** Ribosomes read every codon in order; they do not skip.","how":{"ask":"How does one gene make two protein forms?","know":"Isoform from one gene = alternative splicing.","steps":[{"text":"MPX-S is an isoform from the same gene.","hl":"an isoform made from the same gene"},{"text":"Polymerases and ribosomes do not skip parts of a template.","strike":["RNA polymerase skips the parts of the MPX gene not used in MPX-S.","ribosomes skip MPX mRNA codons that MPX-S does not use."]},{"text":"An isoform is not a trimmed protein.","strike":["proteases trim the finished MPX protein into the shorter MPX-S."]},{"text":"Answer: a spliceosome joins a different set of exons from the same pre-mRNA."}],"trap":"Proteases trim the protein: it gives a shorter protein, but that is cleavage, not an isoform."},"teach":[{"text":"Spot the word isoform.","marks":[{"t":"isoform made from the same gene","note":"alternative splicing"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Isoforms from one gene come from alternative splicing of the pre-mRNA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-138a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:138#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:138"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P5","passage":"Disease M is an X-linked recessive disorder in which the CNS fails to make normal myelin. It is caused by mutations in the gene for myelin protein X (MPX). MPX-S is an isoform made from the same gene that lacks 30 residues of the cytosolic loop. MPX is the main structural protein of CNS myelin and spans the myelin membrane. MPX is a single 250-residue chain held together by three disulfide bonds. Region 1 is an extracellular loop, Region 2 is a transmembrane α helix, Region 3 is the cytosolic loop, and Region 4 is a second extracellular loop. In one affected family, a single point mutation replaced a proline with an alanine.","q":"A purified sample of MPX is run on SDS-PAGE under reducing conditions. How many bands of different molecular weights are expected to appear on the gel?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"One chain gives one band","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count chains, not disulfides: one chain gives one band even after reduction."},"exp":"**Answer:** 1\n\n**Correct: 1** MPX is a single chain. Reducing its disulfides unfolds it but does not split it, so one band appears.\n\n**Incorrect: 2** Two bands would need two different chains.\n\n**Incorrect: 3** Three disulfides do not mean three pieces; they are inside one chain.\n\n**Incorrect: 4** MPX has no subunits to make four bands.","how":{"ask":"How many separate chains does MPX have?","know":"Reducing SDS-PAGE: one band per distinct chain.","steps":[{"text":"MPX is a single chain with internal disulfides.","hl":"a single 250-residue chain held together by three disulfide bonds"},{"text":"Breaking bonds inside one chain leaves one chain.","strike":["2","3","4"]},{"text":"Answer: 1."}],"trap":"3: matching the disulfide count, but those bonds sit inside one chain."},"teach":[{"text":"Separate chain count from bond count.","marks":[{"t":"a single 250-residue chain","note":"one band"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Reducing SDS-PAGE shows one band per distinct polypeptide; internal disulfides do not add bands."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-139a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:139#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:139"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P5","passage":"Disease M is an X-linked recessive disorder in which the CNS fails to make normal myelin. It is caused by mutations in the gene for myelin protein X (MPX). MPX-S is an isoform made from the same gene that lacks 30 residues of the cytosolic loop. MPX is the main structural protein of CNS myelin and spans the myelin membrane. MPX is a single 250-residue chain held together by three disulfide bonds. Region 1 is an extracellular loop, Region 2 is a transmembrane α helix, Region 3 is the cytosolic loop, and Region 4 is a second extracellular loop. In one affected family, a single point mutation replaced a proline with an alanine.","q":"MPX is a major structural component of a membrane that functions to:","choices":["raise the axon's resting membrane potential.","reduce neurotransmitter release at the axon terminal.","increase the speed of action potentials along an axon.","lengthen the time an axon takes to depolarize."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Myelin only speeds conduction","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Myelin insulates the axon so signals jump node to node and travel faster."},"exp":"**Answer:** increase the speed of action potentials along an axon.\n\n**Incorrect: raise the axon's resting membrane potential.** Myelin does not set the resting potential; ion pumps and leak channels do.\n\n**Incorrect: reduce neurotransmitter release at the axon terminal.** Myelin works along the axon, not at the terminal.\n\n**Correct: increase the speed of action potentials along an axon.** MPX builds myelin. Myelin lets action potentials jump between nodes, which is much faster.\n\n**Incorrect: lengthen the time an axon takes to depolarize.** Myelin makes conduction faster, not slower.","how":{"ask":"What does myelin do?","know":"Myelin = saltatory conduction = faster signals.","steps":[{"text":"MPX is the main protein of myelin.","hl":"the main structural protein of CNS myelin"},{"text":"Myelin does not set resting potential or slow depolarizing.","strike":["raise the axon's resting membrane potential.","lengthen the time an axon takes to depolarize."]},{"text":"Myelin acts along the axon, not at the terminal.","strike":["reduce neurotransmitter release at the axon terminal."]},{"text":"Answer: increase the speed of action potentials along an axon."}],"trap":"Lengthen depolarizing time: insulation sounds like it slows things, but it speeds them."},"teach":[{"text":"Link the protein to its membrane.","marks":[{"t":"CNS myelin","note":"speeds conduction"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Myelin raises conduction speed by saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-140a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:140#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:140"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P5","passage":"Disease M is an X-linked recessive disorder in which the CNS fails to make normal myelin. It is caused by mutations in the gene for myelin protein X (MPX). MPX-S is an isoform made from the same gene that lacks 30 residues of the cytosolic loop. MPX is the main structural protein of CNS myelin and spans the myelin membrane. MPX is a single 250-residue chain held together by three disulfide bonds. Region 1 is an extracellular loop, Region 2 is a transmembrane α helix, Region 3 is the cytosolic loop, and Region 4 is a second extracellular loop. In one affected family, a single point mutation replaced a proline with an alanine.","q":"The Disease M-causing mutation described in the passage is LEAST likely to be located in which region of MPX?","choices":["Region 1","Region 2","Region 3","Region 4"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Loops tolerate proline well","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The normal residue is proline, and proline breaks α helices, so it is least likely in the helix."},"exp":"**Answer:** Region 2\n\n**Incorrect: Region 1** This is a loop, where proline fits well.\n\n**Correct: Region 2** The normal residue was proline. Proline's ring kinks and breaks α helices, so it rarely sits in the middle of a helix.\n\n**Incorrect: Region 3** The cytosolic loop can hold proline.\n\n**Incorrect: Region 4** This is a loop, where proline fits well.","how":{"ask":"Where would a normal proline be least likely?","know":"Proline is a helix breaker.","steps":[{"text":"The wild-type residue was proline.","hl":"replaced a proline with an alanine"},{"text":"Loops can hold proline.","strike":["Region 1","Region 3","Region 4"]},{"text":"Region 2 is an α helix, where proline rarely sits.","hl":"Region 2 is a transmembrane α helix"},{"text":"Answer: Region 2."}],"trap":"Region 3: the cytosolic loop sounds special, but loops welcome proline."},"teach":[{"text":"Find which residue was there first.","marks":[{"t":"replaced a proline","note":"original = proline"},{"t":"transmembrane α helix","note":"proline breaks helices"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Proline (and glycine) disrupt α helices, so they sit mostly in loops and turns."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-141a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:141#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:141"],"q":"What is the typical function of peroxisomes in eukaryotic cells?","choices":["To modify and package proteins for secretion","To break down very long fatty acids and H2O2","To make ATP by oxidative phosphorylation","To digest engulfed bacteria with acid hydrolases"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Golgi packages; mitochondria make ATP","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Acid hydrolases mean lysosome","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Peroxisome = peroxide: it oxidizes very long fatty acids and breaks down H2O2."},"exp":"**Answer:** To break down very long fatty acids and H2O2\n\n**Incorrect: To modify and package proteins for secretion** This is the Golgi apparatus.\n\n**Correct: To break down very long fatty acids and H2O2** Peroxisomes start the breakdown of very long fatty acids. Their catalase destroys the H2O2 this makes.\n\n**Incorrect: To make ATP by oxidative phosphorylation** This is the mitochondrion.\n\n**Incorrect: To digest engulfed bacteria with acid hydrolases** This is the lysosome.","how":{"ask":"What does a peroxisome do?","know":"Peroxisome: very long fatty acids, H2O2, catalase.","steps":[{"text":"Packaging for secretion is the Golgi.","strike":["To modify and package proteins for secretion"]},{"text":"Oxidative phosphorylation is in mitochondria.","strike":["To make ATP by oxidative phosphorylation"]},{"text":"Acid hydrolases belong to lysosomes.","strike":["To digest engulfed bacteria with acid hydrolases"]},{"text":"Answer: To break down very long fatty acids and H2O2."}],"trap":"Digest bacteria: it sounds like a cleanup job, but that is the lysosome."},"teach":[{"text":"Use the name: peroxisome handles peroxide."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Golgi packages, lysosomes digest with acid hydrolases, peroxisomes oxidize fats and clear H2O2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-142a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"lab technique basis","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:142#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:142"],"q":"Making RNA probes with T7 RNA polymerase in a test tube is a lab application of which nucleic acid-associated enzymatic process?","choices":["Translation","Transcription","RNA splicing","DNA replication"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"RNA polymerase copies DNA into RNA","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name the enzyme and its product: RNA polymerase making RNA from DNA is transcription."},"exp":"**Answer:** Transcription\n\n**Incorrect: Translation** Translation makes protein from mRNA on ribosomes.\n\n**Correct: Transcription** RNA polymerase reads a DNA template and makes RNA. That is transcription.\n\n**Incorrect: RNA splicing** Splicing removes introns from RNA; it does not make new RNA.\n\n**Incorrect: DNA replication** Replication uses DNA polymerase to make DNA, not RNA.","how":{"ask":"Which natural process uses RNA polymerase on DNA?","know":"DNA to RNA = transcription.","steps":[{"text":"The tool is RNA polymerase, so the product is RNA.","strike":["DNA replication"]},{"text":"Translation makes protein, and splicing only edits RNA.","strike":["Translation","RNA splicing"]},{"text":"Answer: Transcription."}],"trap":"DNA replication: PCR-type lab methods copy DNA, but this enzyme makes RNA."},"teach":[{"text":"Map each lab tool to the cell process it copies."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** PCR and Sanger copy DNA replication; RNA polymerase methods copy transcription."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-143a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"count chiral centers","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:143#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:143"],"q":"How many chiral carbons does open-chain D-fructose (a ketohexose with its C=O at C2) contain?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"C1, C2, C6 are not chiral","c":[3]},{"f":"C3, C4, C5 each chiral","c":[0,1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check each carbon for four different groups: ends are CH2OH, the C=O is flat, the rest count."},"exp":"**Answer:** 3\n\n**Incorrect: 1** C3, C4 and C5 each carry H, OH and two different chain parts.\n\n**Incorrect: 2** All three middle carbons from C3 to C5 are chiral, not two.\n\n**Correct: 3** C1 and C6 are CH2OH groups and C2 is a C=O, so none is chiral. C3, C4 and C5 each have four different groups.\n\n**Incorrect: 4** Four fits an aldohexose like glucose; moving the C=O to C2 removes one chiral carbon.","how":{"ask":"Which carbons in fructose have four different groups?","know":"Chiral C = four different groups; C=O and CH2OH carbons are not chiral.","steps":[{"text":"C1 and C6 are CH2OH, with two H atoms, so not chiral."},{"text":"C2 is the ketone C=O, so it has only three groups.","strike":["4"]},{"text":"C3, C4 and C5 each have H, OH and two different chain ends.","strike":["1","2"]},{"text":"Answer: 3."}],"trap":"4: true for glucose, but fructose's C=O sits on C2, which loses a chiral center."},"teach":[{"text":"Walk the chain one carbon at a time and skip CH2OH and C=O carbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Open-chain aldohexoses have 4 chiral carbons; ketohexoses have 3."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-144a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:144#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:144"],"q":"Complete hydrolysis of a lipid molecule yields two fatty acids, one phosphocholine, and one molecule of molecule 1. What is the identity of molecule 1?","choices":["Glycerol","Sphingosine","Glucose","Cholesterol"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Two fatty acids: glycerol backbone","c":[1]},{"f":"Sugars and sterols aren't backbones","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two fatty acids plus a phosphate head is a glycerophospholipid, so the backbone is glycerol."},"exp":"**Answer:** Glycerol\n\n**Correct: Glycerol** Two fatty acids and a phosphocholine head make a glycerophospholipid. Its backbone is glycerol.\n\n**Incorrect: Sphingosine** Sphingosine lipids hold only one added fatty acid.\n\n**Incorrect: Glucose** Glucose is a sugar, not a phospholipid backbone.\n\n**Incorrect: Cholesterol** Cholesterol is a sterol ring lipid; it does not hold fatty acids and phosphocholine.","how":{"ask":"What backbone holds two fatty acids and a phosphate head?","know":"Glycerophospholipid = glycerol + 2 fatty acids + phosphate head.","steps":[{"text":"Two fatty acids plus phosphocholine fits a glycerophospholipid.","strike":["Sphingosine"]},{"text":"Sugars and sterols are not phospholipid backbones.","strike":["Glucose","Cholesterol"]},{"text":"Answer: Glycerol."}],"trap":"Sphingosine: sphingomyelin also has phosphocholine, but it has one added fatty acid, not two."},"teach":[{"text":"Count the fatty acids to pick the backbone."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Glycerol backbones carry two or three fatty acids; sphingosine carries one."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-145a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"receptor type and second messenger","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:145#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:145"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P6","passage":"Basophils release histamine when IgE binds the receptor FcR-B on their surface. Active FcR-B recruits cytoplasmic enzymes that add phosphates to tyrosines in its cytoplasmic tail. Researchers asked whether p22δ, a protein phosphorylated on serine after IgE binding, helps histamine release. Extracellular ATP also rises after IgE binding, and the enzyme apyrase destroys ATP. In Experiment 1, wild-type cells released 40% of their histamine, p22δ–/– cells released 38%, and wild-type cells with apyrase released 10%. In Experiment 2, Compound Y, a competitive inhibitor of the SERCA pump that moves Ca2+ from the cytosol back into the ER, raised histamine release to 70%.","q":"FcR-B receptors are best classified as:","choices":["ligand-gated calcium channels.","protein kinase-associated receptors.","G protein-coupled receptors.","nuclear hormone receptors."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Recruits tyrosine kinases: kinase-linked","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A receptor that recruits enzymes to phosphorylate its own tyrosines is kinase-associated."},"exp":"**Answer:** protein kinase-associated receptors.\n\n**Incorrect: ligand-gated calcium channels.** Channels open a pore; they do not recruit kinases.\n\n**Correct: protein kinase-associated receptors.** Active FcR-B recruits enzymes that phosphorylate tyrosines on its tail. That is a kinase-associated receptor.\n\n**Incorrect: G protein-coupled receptors.** GPCRs signal through G proteins, not tyrosine phosphorylation of their tails.\n\n**Incorrect: nuclear hormone receptors.** FcR-B is on the cell surface, not in the nucleus.","how":{"ask":"What receptor class recruits tyrosine kinases?","know":"Tyrosine phosphorylation of the tail = kinase-associated receptor.","steps":[{"text":"FcR-B recruits enzymes that phosphorylate its tyrosines.","hl":"recruits cytoplasmic enzymes that add phosphates to tyrosines"},{"text":"Channels and GPCRs do not work this way.","strike":["ligand-gated calcium channels.","G protein-coupled receptors."]},{"text":"FcR-B sits on the cell surface.","hl":"FcR-B on their surface","strike":["nuclear hormone receptors."]},{"text":"Answer: protein kinase-associated receptors."}],"trap":"G protein-coupled receptors: common on immune cells, but they act through G proteins, not tail tyrosines."},"teach":[{"text":"Look for the signaling clue.","marks":[{"t":"add phosphates to tyrosines","note":"kinase-linked"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Receptors whose tails get tyrosine-phosphorylated by recruited enzymes are kinase-associated."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-146a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:146#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:146"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P6","passage":"Basophils release histamine when IgE binds the receptor FcR-B on their surface. Active FcR-B recruits cytoplasmic enzymes that add phosphates to tyrosines in its cytoplasmic tail. Researchers asked whether p22δ, a protein phosphorylated on serine after IgE binding, helps histamine release. Extracellular ATP also rises after IgE binding, and the enzyme apyrase destroys ATP. In Experiment 1, wild-type cells released 40% of their histamine, p22δ–/– cells released 38%, and wild-type cells with apyrase released 10%. In Experiment 2, Compound Y, a competitive inhibitor of the SERCA pump that moves Ca2+ from the cytosol back into the ER, raised histamine release to 70%.","q":"According to the results of Experiment 1, the release of histamine from basophils is:","choices":["independent of both p22δ and ATP.","dependent on p22δ but independent of ATP.","dependent on ATP but independent of p22δ.","dependent on both ATP and p22δ."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Apyrase cut release: needs ATP","c":[0,1]},{"f":"p22δ loss barely changed release","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Remove each factor and compare to wild type: a big drop means dependent."},"exp":"**Answer:** dependent on ATP but independent of p22δ.\n\n**Incorrect: independent of both p22δ and ATP.** Removing ATP dropped release from 40% to 10%.\n\n**Incorrect: dependent on p22δ but independent of ATP.** This is backward: ATP mattered and p22δ did not.\n\n**Correct: dependent on ATP but independent of p22δ.** Apyrase dropped release from 40% to 10%, so ATP is needed. p22δ–/– cells still released 38%, so p22δ is not needed.\n\n**Incorrect: dependent on both ATP and p22δ.** Losing p22δ barely changed release.","how":{"ask":"Which removal lowered histamine release?","know":"Big drop when removed = dependent; no change = independent.","steps":[{"text":"Apyrase removes ATP and cut release to 10%.","hl":"wild-type cells with apyrase released 10%"},{"text":"So release needs ATP.","strike":["independent of both p22δ and ATP.","dependent on p22δ but independent of ATP."]},{"text":"p22δ–/– cells still released 38%, close to 40%.","hl":"p22δ–/– cells released 38%","strike":["dependent on both ATP and p22δ."]},{"text":"Answer: dependent on ATP but independent of p22δ."}],"trap":"Both: the passage suggests p22δ may help, but the data show almost no drop without it."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare each group to wild type.","marks":[{"t":"wild-type cells released 40%","note":"baseline"},{"t":"p22δ–/– cells released 38%","note":"no real change"},{"t":"apyrase released 10%","note":"big drop"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A factor is needed only if removing it clearly lowers the output versus wild type."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-147a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"phosphorylation site residue","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:147#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:147"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P6","passage":"Basophils release histamine when IgE binds the receptor FcR-B on their surface. Active FcR-B recruits cytoplasmic enzymes that add phosphates to tyrosines in its cytoplasmic tail. Researchers asked whether p22δ, a protein phosphorylated on serine after IgE binding, helps histamine release. Extracellular ATP also rises after IgE binding, and the enzyme apyrase destroys ATP. In Experiment 1, wild-type cells released 40% of their histamine, p22δ–/– cells released 38%, and wild-type cells with apyrase released 10%. In Experiment 2, Compound Y, a competitive inhibitor of the SERCA pump that moves Ca2+ from the cytosol back into the ER, raised histamine release to 70%.","q":"To study the effects of p22δ phosphorylation, researchers made a variant of this protein that mimicked the phosphorylated state. Researchers most likely replaced the original target residue with which amino acid?","choices":["Lys","Asn","His","Asp"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Phosphate is negative: need acidic","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Asn is neutral, not charged","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A phosphate adds negative charge, so swap in an acidic residue: Asp or Glu."},"exp":"**Answer:** Asp\n\n**Incorrect: Lys** Lysine is positive, the opposite of a phosphate.\n\n**Incorrect: Asn** Asparagine looks like aspartate but carries no charge.\n\n**Incorrect: His** Histidine is basic and can be positive, not negative.\n\n**Correct: Asp** A phosphorylated serine carries negative charge. Aspartate is negative, so it mimics the phosphate.","how":{"ask":"Which residue copies a phosphate's charge?","know":"Phosphomimic = Asp or Glu.","steps":[{"text":"p22δ is phosphorylated on serine, adding negative charge.","hl":"a protein phosphorylated on serine after IgE binding"},{"text":"Lys and His are basic, so they carry the wrong charge.","strike":["Lys","His"]},{"text":"Asn has an amide, so it has no charge.","strike":["Asn"]},{"text":"Answer: Asp."}],"trap":"Asn: its name and shape are close to Asp, but it has no negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"Think charge, not shape.","marks":[{"t":"phosphorylated on serine","note":"adds negative charge"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To mimic phosphorylation, replace Ser, Thr or Tyr with Asp or Glu."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-148a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:148#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:148"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P6","passage":"Basophils release histamine when IgE binds the receptor FcR-B on their surface. Active FcR-B recruits cytoplasmic enzymes that add phosphates to tyrosines in its cytoplasmic tail. Researchers asked whether p22δ, a protein phosphorylated on serine after IgE binding, helps histamine release. Extracellular ATP also rises after IgE binding, and the enzyme apyrase destroys ATP. In Experiment 1, wild-type cells released 40% of their histamine, p22δ–/– cells released 38%, and wild-type cells with apyrase released 10%. In Experiment 2, Compound Y, a competitive inhibitor of the SERCA pump that moves Ca2+ from the cytosol back into the ER, raised histamine release to 70%.","q":"A compound NOT suitable to treat allergic symptoms is one that:","choices":["mimics the effects of apyrase.","blocks FcR-B kinase activity.","mimics the effects of Compound Y.","enhances the activity of SERCA."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Less ATP means less histamine","c":[0]},{"f":"No FcR-B signal, no release","c":[1]},{"f":"More SERCA lowers cytosolic Ca2+","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the choice that raises histamine release; Compound Y did, to 70%."},"exp":"**Answer:** mimics the effects of Compound Y.\n\n**Incorrect: mimics the effects of apyrase.** Apyrase cut release to 10%, so it would help allergy.\n\n**Incorrect: blocks FcR-B kinase activity.** Blocking the receptor's signal stops the release trigger, so it would help.\n\n**Correct: mimics the effects of Compound Y.** Compound Y blocks SERCA, keeps Ca2+ in the cytosol, and raised release to 70%. That would worsen allergy.\n\n**Incorrect: enhances the activity of SERCA.** More SERCA clears cytosolic Ca2+, which should lower release.","how":{"ask":"Which choice would raise histamine release?","know":"Allergy drugs must lower histamine release.","steps":[{"text":"Apyrase lowered release, so mimicking it helps.","hl":"wild-type cells with apyrase released 10%","strike":["mimics the effects of apyrase."]},{"text":"Blocking FcR-B signaling stops the trigger.","strike":["blocks FcR-B kinase activity."]},{"text":"Compound Y raised release by blocking SERCA; boosting SERCA does the reverse.","hl":"raised histamine release to 70%","strike":["enhances the activity of SERCA."]},{"text":"Answer: mimics the effects of Compound Y."}],"trap":"Enhances SERCA: it changes Ca2+, but in the helpful direction."},"teach":[{"text":"Sort each choice: lowers or raises release?","marks":[{"t":"released 10%","note":"apyrase helps"},{"t":"raised histamine release to 70%","note":"Compound Y harms"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In a NOT-suitable drug question, pick the one that pushes the bad output up."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-149a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"translation and codon logic","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:149#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:149"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P7","passage":"UV light can bond two neighboring pyrimidine bases on the same DNA strand, forming a dimer. The dimer kinks the helix and weakens base pairing. Cells fix dimers by excision repair. In Step 1, a nuclease and a helicase find the kink and cut out the damaged stretch. In Steps 2 and 3, DNA polymerase fills the gap by copying the other strand, and DNA ligase seals the backbone. Disorder UVS-2 is autosomal recessive; patients lack a working Step 1 enzyme, so their UV damage goes unrepaired and they get many skin cancers.","q":"Assume that the defect in UVS-2 is the result of a single amino acid substitution. Which of the following mutations would most likely produce the defective enzyme inherited by UVS-2 patients?","choices":["Substitution of a single base in the DNA","Insertion of a single base in the DNA","Substitution of a single base in the mRNA","Insertion of a single base in the mRNA"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Inherited changes must be in DNA","c":[2,3]},{"f":"Insertion shifts frame: many changes","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Inherited means DNA; one amino acid changed means one base swapped, not added."},"exp":"**Answer:** Substitution of a single base in the DNA\n\n**Correct: Substitution of a single base in the DNA** Only DNA changes are passed to offspring. A single base swap can change one codon and so one amino acid.\n\n**Incorrect: Insertion of a single base in the DNA** One added base shifts the reading frame and changes many amino acids.\n\n**Incorrect: Substitution of a single base in the mRNA** mRNA changes are not inherited.\n\n**Incorrect: Insertion of a single base in the mRNA** mRNA changes are not inherited, and an insertion shifts the frame.","how":{"ask":"Which change is inherited and alters only one amino acid?","know":"Inherited = DNA. One residue changed = substitution, not frameshift.","steps":[{"text":"UVS-2 is inherited, so the change must be in DNA.","hl":"Disorder UVS-2 is autosomal recessive","strike":["Substitution of a single base in the mRNA","Insertion of a single base in the mRNA"]},{"text":"An insertion shifts the frame and changes many residues.","strike":["Insertion of a single base in the DNA"]},{"text":"Answer: Substitution of a single base in the DNA."}],"trap":"Insertion in the DNA: it is inherited, but it causes a frameshift, not one swap."},"teach":[{"text":"Check two things: where the change is, and how many residues change.","marks":[{"t":"autosomal recessive","note":"inherited = DNA"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A single amino acid substitution comes from a single base substitution; insertions or deletions shift the frame."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-150a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:150#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:150"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P7","passage":"UV light can bond two neighboring pyrimidine bases on the same DNA strand, forming a dimer. The dimer kinks the helix and weakens base pairing. Cells fix dimers by excision repair. In Step 1, a nuclease and a helicase find the kink and cut out the damaged stretch. In Steps 2 and 3, DNA polymerase fills the gap by copying the other strand, and DNA ligase seals the backbone. Disorder UVS-2 is autosomal recessive; patients lack a working Step 1 enzyme, so their UV damage goes unrepaired and they get many skin cancers.","q":"Based on the passage, a person with UVS-2 would be unable to repair UV-induced dimers between which of the following pairs of nucleotides?","choices":["Guanine and cytosine","Adenine and thymine","Thymine and thymine","Guanine and adenine"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Both bases must be pyrimidines","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"UV dimers form between two pyrimidines (C, T), so drop any pair with A or G."},"exp":"**Answer:** Thymine and thymine\n\n**Incorrect: Guanine and cytosine** Guanine is a purine, so this is not a pyrimidine dimer.\n\n**Incorrect: Adenine and thymine** Adenine is a purine.\n\n**Correct: Thymine and thymine** UV joins two neighboring pyrimidines. Thymine is a pyrimidine, so T–T dimers are the classic case.\n\n**Incorrect: Guanine and adenine** Both are purines.","how":{"ask":"Which pair is two pyrimidines?","know":"Pyrimidines: C, T, U. Purines: A, G.","steps":[{"text":"UV links two neighboring pyrimidines.","hl":"bond two neighboring pyrimidine bases"},{"text":"Any pair with A or G includes a purine.","strike":["Guanine and cytosine","Adenine and thymine","Guanine and adenine"]},{"text":"Answer: Thymine and thymine."}],"trap":"Adenine and thymine: they pair in DNA, but dimers are between neighbors on one strand, both pyrimidines."},"teach":[{"text":"Read the base class in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"pyrimidine bases","note":"C or T only"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** UV dimers join two adjacent pyrimidines on the same strand, most often T–T."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-151a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:151#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:151"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P7","passage":"UV light can bond two neighboring pyrimidine bases on the same DNA strand, forming a dimer. The dimer kinks the helix and weakens base pairing. Cells fix dimers by excision repair. In Step 1, a nuclease and a helicase find the kink and cut out the damaged stretch. In Steps 2 and 3, DNA polymerase fills the gap by copying the other strand, and DNA ligase seals the backbone. Disorder UVS-2 is autosomal recessive; patients lack a working Step 1 enzyme, so their UV damage goes unrepaired and they get many skin cancers.","q":"Changing a single amino acid in an excision repair enzyme can make the enzyme defective. The most likely mechanism by which an amino acid substitution causes the defect in the enzyme would be by:","choices":["blocking transcription of the repair enzyme gene.","altering the folded shape of the repair enzyme.","removing the DNA damage the enzyme acts on.","halting replication of the repair enzyme gene."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Protein change can't block gene use","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Enzyme change doesn't remove substrate","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A new residue changes how the protein folds, and shape sets function."},"exp":"**Answer:** altering the folded shape of the repair enzyme.\n\n**Incorrect: blocking transcription of the repair enzyme gene.** A change inside the protein does not stop its gene from being transcribed.\n\n**Correct: altering the folded shape of the repair enzyme.** Sequence sets folding, and folding sets function. One new residue can bend the active site out of shape.\n\n**Incorrect: removing the DNA damage the enzyme acts on.** The substrate is DNA damage; a change in the enzyme does not remove it.\n\n**Incorrect: halting replication of the repair enzyme gene.** Protein sequence has no effect on copying its gene.","how":{"ask":"How does one residue swap break an enzyme?","know":"Primary sequence sets 3D shape; shape sets activity.","steps":[{"text":"A residue swap is a change in the protein, not the gene's use.","strike":["blocking transcription of the repair enzyme gene.","halting replication of the repair enzyme gene."]},{"text":"The damaged DNA is still there; the enzyme just cannot fix it.","strike":["removing the DNA damage the enzyme acts on."]},{"text":"Answer: altering the folded shape of the repair enzyme."}],"trap":"Blocking transcription: the gene is still read; only the protein made from it is off."},"teach":[{"text":"Keep gene steps and protein steps separate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A missense change acts by altering protein folding or the active site, not by stopping the gene."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-152a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"dna replication and repair","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:152#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:152"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P7","passage":"UV light can bond two neighboring pyrimidine bases on the same DNA strand, forming a dimer. The dimer kinks the helix and weakens base pairing. Cells fix dimers by excision repair. In Step 1, a nuclease and a helicase find the kink and cut out the damaged stretch. In Steps 2 and 3, DNA polymerase fills the gap by copying the other strand, and DNA ligase seals the backbone. Disorder UVS-2 is autosomal recessive; patients lack a working Step 1 enzyme, so their UV damage goes unrepaired and they get many skin cancers.","q":"People with UVS-2 and fair skin will probably have a higher rate of skin cancer than people with UVS-2 and dark skin because skin pigment:","choices":["slows the steps of excision repair.","blocks pyrimidine bases from entering DNA.","absorbs UV light before it reaches DNA.","is built mainly from purine bases."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Melanin shields; it doesn't repair","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Melanin is made from tyrosine","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Melanin is a sunscreen: it absorbs UV so fewer dimers form."},"exp":"**Answer:** absorbs UV light before it reaches DNA.\n\n**Incorrect: slows the steps of excision repair.** Melanin does not act on repair enzymes.\n\n**Incorrect: blocks pyrimidine bases from entering DNA.** Melanin does not change which bases go into DNA.\n\n**Correct: absorbs UV light before it reaches DNA.** UV causes the dimers. Melanin absorbs UV, so dark skin forms fewer dimers that need repair.\n\n**Incorrect: is built mainly from purine bases.** Melanin is made from tyrosine, not nucleic acid bases.","how":{"ask":"How does pigment cut UV damage?","know":"Melanin absorbs UV light.","steps":[{"text":"UV light makes the dimers that UVS-2 cannot fix.","hl":"UV light can bond two neighboring pyrimidine bases"},{"text":"Melanin works before damage; it does not touch repair or DNA building.","strike":["slows the steps of excision repair.","blocks pyrimidine bases from entering DNA."]},{"text":"Melanin comes from tyrosine.","strike":["is built mainly from purine bases."]},{"text":"Answer: absorbs UV light before it reaches DNA."}],"trap":"Slows excision repair: slower repair would cause more cancer, not less."},"teach":[{"text":"Less UV in means fewer dimers to fix.","marks":[{"t":"UV light","note":"melanin absorbs it"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Melanin, made from tyrosine, protects DNA by absorbing UV light."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-153a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"virus and bacteria traits","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:153#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:153"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P8","passage":"The retrovirus RV-L infects human liver cells. A few people never become infected, even after many exposures. RV-L binds a receptor made of two membrane proteins, LR1 and LCR2. Liver cells from resistant people had normal LR1, but their LCR2 was cut short, and part of it never reached the cell surface. Their LCR2 mRNA was made at normal levels but was 26 bases shorter, due to a deletion in the gene. Both parents of each resistant person carried one copy of the deleted allele, and the allele's frequency in the population was 8%.","q":"RV-L is a retrovirus. Which of the following is NOT true of retroviruses?","choices":["The viral genome is made of RNA.","Reverse transcriptase copies viral RNA into DNA.","They copy their genome without any host cell proteins.","Host RNA polymerase transcribes the integrated DNA into viral RNA."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"RNA genome, reverse transcribed: true","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Provirus is transcribed by host","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Every virus needs host machinery; retroviruses use host RNA polymerase and ribosomes."},"exp":"**Answer:** They copy their genome without any host cell proteins.\n\n**Incorrect: The viral genome is made of RNA.** True: retroviruses carry an RNA genome.\n\n**Incorrect: Reverse transcriptase copies viral RNA into DNA.** True: this is what makes them retroviruses.\n\n**Correct: They copy their genome without any host cell proteins.** False, so it is the answer. Retroviruses need host RNA polymerase, ribosomes and other host proteins to make new virus.\n\n**Incorrect: Host RNA polymerase transcribes the integrated DNA into viral RNA.** True: the integrated provirus is read by host enzymes.","how":{"ask":"Which retrovirus statement is false?","know":"Retrovirus: RNA genome, reverse transcriptase, integrates, host makes new copies.","steps":[{"text":"RNA genome and reverse transcription are true.","strike":["The viral genome is made of RNA.","Reverse transcriptase copies viral RNA into DNA."]},{"text":"The integrated DNA is read by host RNA polymerase: true.","strike":["Host RNA polymerase transcribes the integrated DNA into viral RNA."]},{"text":"No virus copies itself without host proteins.","hl":"The retrovirus RV-L infects human liver cells"},{"text":"Answer: They copy their genome without any host cell proteins."}],"trap":"Reverse transcriptase: it is a viral enzyme, but it does not free the virus from needing host proteins."},"teach":[{"text":"In a NOT question, cross off the true facts.","marks":[{"t":"The retrovirus RV-L","note":"RNA to DNA"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Viruses always need host machinery; retroviruses bring reverse transcriptase but use host polymerase and ribosomes."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-154a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:154#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:154"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P8","passage":"The retrovirus RV-L infects human liver cells. A few people never become infected, even after many exposures. RV-L binds a receptor made of two membrane proteins, LR1 and LCR2. Liver cells from resistant people had normal LR1, but their LCR2 was cut short, and part of it never reached the cell surface. Their LCR2 mRNA was made at normal levels but was 26 bases shorter, due to a deletion in the gene. Both parents of each resistant person carried one copy of the deleted allele, and the allele's frequency in the population was 8%.","q":"Which of the following measures would be a practical way to prevent people from acquiring RV-L infection?","choices":["Liver cells from resistant people could be transplanted into others.","Resistant people could be urged to have many children.","People could be given mutagens to change the LCR2 gene.","Drugs could bind LCR2 and block virus attachment to liver cells."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Own liver cells stay infectable","c":[0]},{"f":"Breeding doesn't protect living people","c":[1]},{"f":"Mutagens hit many genes","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Copy what resistant people have: block LCR2 so the virus cannot attach."},"exp":"**Answer:** Drugs could bind LCR2 and block virus attachment to liver cells.\n\n**Incorrect: Liver cells from resistant people could be transplanted into others.** The person's own liver cells would still carry normal LCR2 and get infected.\n\n**Incorrect: Resistant people could be urged to have many children.** This would not protect anyone alive now.\n\n**Incorrect: People could be given mutagens to change the LCR2 gene.** Mutagens damage many genes and can cause cancer.\n\n**Correct: Drugs could bind LCR2 and block virus attachment to liver cells.** RV-L needs LCR2 to bind. A drug that covers LCR2 mimics the resistant cells safely.","how":{"ask":"What safe step mimics the resistant cells?","know":"Block the entry receptor = block infection.","steps":[{"text":"RV-L needs LCR2 to bind the cell.","hl":"RV-L binds a receptor made of two membrane proteins, LR1 and LCR2"},{"text":"Adding some resistant cells leaves the person's own cells open.","strike":["Liver cells from resistant people could be transplanted into others."]},{"text":"Having children helps no one now; mutagens are unsafe.","strike":["Resistant people could be urged to have many children.","People could be given mutagens to change the LCR2 gene."]},{"text":"Answer: Drugs could bind LCR2 and block virus attachment to liver cells."}],"trap":"Mutagens: they could change LCR2, but they would harm many other genes."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the entry step and block it.","marks":[{"t":"LR1 and LCR2","note":"entry receptor"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The most practical antiviral blocks the receptor the virus needs for entry."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-155a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:155#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:155"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P8","passage":"The retrovirus RV-L infects human liver cells. A few people never become infected, even after many exposures. RV-L binds a receptor made of two membrane proteins, LR1 and LCR2. Liver cells from resistant people had normal LR1, but their LCR2 was cut short, and part of it never reached the cell surface. Their LCR2 mRNA was made at normal levels but was 26 bases shorter, due to a deletion in the gene. Both parents of each resistant person carried one copy of the deleted allele, and the allele's frequency in the population was 8%.","q":"What conclusion about normal LCR2 function can be drawn from the fact that, except for resisting RV-L, resistant people seem healthy?","choices":["LCR2 is not essential for liver cell survival.","LCR2 is not made in liver cells.","LCR2 plays no role in RV-L infection.","LCR2 is required for liver cell survival."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Passage: LCR2 made, used by virus","c":[1,2]},{"f":"People lack it yet live","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"People without working LCR2 are healthy, so the cells can live without it."},"exp":"**Answer:** LCR2 is not essential for liver cell survival.\n\n**Correct: LCR2 is not essential for liver cell survival.** Resistant people lack working LCR2 but are healthy. So liver cells survive without it.\n\n**Incorrect: LCR2 is not made in liver cells.** The passage says LCR2 mRNA is made in liver cells.\n\n**Incorrect: LCR2 plays no role in RV-L infection.** RV-L binds LCR2, and losing it blocks infection.\n\n**Incorrect: LCR2 is required for liver cell survival.** If so, resistant people would lack liver cells, but they are healthy.","how":{"ask":"What does a healthy loss-of-function person tell you?","know":"Healthy without a protein = protein not essential.","steps":[{"text":"LCR2 mRNA is made in liver cells, and the virus uses LCR2.","hl":"Their LCR2 mRNA was made at normal levels","strike":["LCR2 is not made in liver cells.","LCR2 plays no role in RV-L infection."]},{"text":"Resistant people lack working LCR2 yet are healthy.","strike":["LCR2 is required for liver cell survival."]},{"text":"Answer: LCR2 is not essential for liver cell survival."}],"trap":"Required for survival: LCR2 is a real receptor, but people live fine without it."},"teach":[{"text":"Treat resistant people as a natural knockout.","marks":[{"t":"part of it never reached the cell surface","note":"natural knockout"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** If a knockout is healthy, the missing protein is not essential."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-156a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"evolution and conservation","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:156#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:156"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P8","passage":"The retrovirus RV-L infects human liver cells. A few people never become infected, even after many exposures. RV-L binds a receptor made of two membrane proteins, LR1 and LCR2. Liver cells from resistant people had normal LR1, but their LCR2 was cut short, and part of it never reached the cell surface. Their LCR2 mRNA was made at normal levels but was 26 bases shorter, due to a deletion in the gene. Both parents of each resistant person carried one copy of the deleted allele, and the allele's frequency in the population was 8%.","q":"People resistant to RV-L may have a selective advantage over others. If this is true, what will happen to the frequency of the mutated LCR2 allele in the general population?","choices":["It will reach Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium in one generation.","It will increase over successive generations.","It will decrease because it makes a non-functional product.","It will stay the same because 8% is too rare to spread."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Selection breaks Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium","c":[0]},{"f":"Advantage raises allele frequency","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"An allele that helps carriers survive becomes more common each generation."},"exp":"**Answer:** It will increase over successive generations.\n\n**Incorrect: It will reach Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium in one generation.** Selection is acting, so the population is not at equilibrium.\n\n**Correct: It will increase over successive generations.** Resistant people survive RV-L better. They pass on the allele more, so it rises over time.\n\n**Incorrect: It will decrease because it makes a non-functional product.** The loss of LCR2 is harmless and helpful, so it is not selected against.\n\n**Incorrect: It will stay the same because 8% is too rare to spread.** Selection can raise even rare alleles.","how":{"ask":"What does positive selection do to an allele?","know":"Advantage = allele frequency rises.","steps":[{"text":"The allele starts at 8% in the population.","hl":"the allele's frequency in the population was 8%"},{"text":"Selection means no Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium.","strike":["It will reach Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium in one generation."]},{"text":"A helpful allele does not fall or stall.","strike":["It will decrease because it makes a non-functional product.","It will stay the same because 8% is too rare to spread."]},{"text":"Answer: It will increase over successive generations."}],"trap":"Non-functional product: a broken protein sounds bad, but here it helps survival."},"teach":[{"text":"Judge the allele by its effect on survival, not by whether the protein works.","marks":[{"t":"8%","note":"starting point"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Any allele that boosts survival or reproduction rises in frequency over generations."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-157a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"paternity band matching","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:157#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:157"],"q":"A DNA fingerprint gives these band sizes. Child: 2, 5 and 7 kb. Mother: 2 and 9 kb. M1: 3 and 9 kb. M2: 4 and 6 kb. M3: 2 and 11 kb. M4: 5 and 7 kb. Which male is most likely the biological father of the child?","choices":["M1","M2","M3","M4"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Father supplies the non-mother bands","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Sharing mom's band proves nothing","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cross off the child's bands that match the mother; the father must have the rest."},"exp":"**Answer:** M4\n\n**Incorrect: M1** M1 shares no band with the child.\n\n**Incorrect: M2** M2 shares no band with the child.\n\n**Incorrect: M3** M3 matches only the 2 kb band, which the child got from the mother.\n\n**Correct: M4** The child's 5 and 7 kb bands are not in the mother. M4 has both, so he is the likely father.","how":{"ask":"Who has the child's non-maternal bands?","know":"Each child band comes from mom or dad.","steps":[{"text":"The 2 kb band came from the mother. Left: 5 and 7 kb.","strike":["M3"]},{"text":"M1 and M2 have neither 5 nor 7 kb.","strike":["M1","M2"]},{"text":"M4 has 5 and 7 kb."},{"text":"Answer: M4."}],"trap":"M3: he shares a band with the child, but it is the mother's band."},"teach":[{"text":"Subtract the mother first, then match the rest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The father must carry every child band that the mother lacks."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-158a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:158#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:158"],"q":"In a neuron stimulated by a threshold stimulus, the change in membrane permeability that comes right after the rise in sodium permeability is increased permeability to:","choices":["sodium ions.","chloride ions.","potassium ions.","calcium ions."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Na+ rise already came first","c":[0]},{"f":"K+ out next: repolarization","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Na+ in first to depolarize, then K+ out to repolarize."},"exp":"**Answer:** potassium ions.\n\n**Incorrect: sodium ions.** The sodium rise is the first step, and Na+ channels then close.\n\n**Incorrect: chloride ions.** Chloride is not a main player in the action potential.\n\n**Correct: potassium ions.** After Na+ rushes in, voltage-gated K+ channels open. K+ flows out and repolarizes the cell.\n\n**Incorrect: calcium ions.** Ca2+ channels matter at the axon terminal, not in the main spike.","how":{"ask":"What opens after Na+ channels in a spike?","know":"Spike order: Na+ in, then K+ out.","steps":[{"text":"The sodium rise is already given as the first change.","strike":["sodium ions."]},{"text":"Cl– and Ca2+ do not drive the main spike.","strike":["chloride ions.","calcium ions."]},{"text":"Voltage-gated K+ channels open next."},{"text":"Answer: potassium ions."}],"trap":"Calcium ions: Ca2+ enters at the terminal, but that is after the spike travels."},"teach":[{"text":"Picture the spike: up with Na+, down with K+."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In a neuron spike, Na+ permeability rises first, then K+ permeability rises to repolarize."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-159a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"ΔG°′ to Keq expression","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:159#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:159"],"q":"An isomerase catalyzes A ⇌ B with ΔG′° = –2.5 kJ/mol. A and B react in a 1-to-1 stoichiometric ratio. If [A] is 4 μM at equilibrium, what is the expression for the concentration of B (in μM)?","choices":["4e2500/RT","4e–2500/RT","2500e4/RT","2500e–4/RT"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Negative ΔG gives positive exponent","c":[1]},{"f":"Coefficient is [A], 4","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Keq = e^(–ΔG°′/RT), and [B] = [A] × Keq."},"exp":"**Answer:** 4e2500/RT\n\n**Correct: 4e2500/RT** ΔG°′ = –RT ln Keq, so Keq = e^(2500/RT). Since Keq = [B]/[A], [B] = 4e2500/RT.\n\n**Incorrect: 4e–2500/RT** The minus sign in the formula cancels the negative ΔG, so the exponent is positive.\n\n**Incorrect: 2500e4/RT** This swaps the concentration and the energy.\n\n**Incorrect: 2500e–4/RT** This swaps the numbers and has the wrong sign.","how":{"ask":"Turn ΔG°′ into Keq, then into [B].","know":"ΔG°′ = –RT ln Keq.","steps":[{"text":"Keq = e^(–ΔG°′/RT) = e^(–(–2500)/RT) = e^(2500/RT).","strike":["4e–2500/RT"]},{"text":"[B] = [A] × Keq, so the coefficient is 4.","strike":["2500e4/RT","2500e–4/RT"]},{"text":"Answer: 4e2500/RT."}],"trap":"4e–2500/RT: copying the sign of ΔG°′ forgets the minus in the formula."},"teach":[{"text":"Convert kJ to J: –2.5 kJ = –2500 J."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Keq = e^(–ΔG°′/RT); negative ΔG°′ gives Keq > 1 and a positive exponent."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-160a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"trace air and blood flow order","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:160#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:160"],"q":"Which of the following correctly lists the order in which blood returning from the lungs passes through the human heart's chambers and valves?","choices":["Left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve","Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve","Left ventricle, mitral valve, left atrium, aortic valve","Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Lung blood enters left atrium","c":[3]},{"f":"Atrium always before ventricle","c":[2]},{"f":"Left side: mitral, then aortic","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Lungs to left atrium, through the mitral valve, to left ventricle, out the aortic valve."},"exp":"**Answer:** Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve\n\n**Incorrect: Left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve** The tricuspid and pulmonary valves are on the right side.\n\n**Correct: Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve** Pulmonary veins bring blood to the left atrium. It passes the mitral valve to the left ventricle and leaves through the aortic valve.\n\n**Incorrect: Left ventricle, mitral valve, left atrium, aortic valve** Blood enters an atrium first, then a ventricle.\n\n**Incorrect: Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve** This is the path of blood coming back from the body.","how":{"ask":"What path does lung blood take through the heart?","know":"Atrium, AV valve, ventricle, semilunar valve.","steps":[{"text":"Blood from the lungs enters the left side.","strike":["Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve"]},{"text":"Blood fills an atrium before a ventricle.","strike":["Left ventricle, mitral valve, left atrium, aortic valve"]},{"text":"Left side valves are mitral and aortic.","strike":["Left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve"]},{"text":"Answer: Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve."}],"trap":"Right atrium path: correct order, but for blood from the body, not the lungs."},"teach":[{"text":"Pair each side with its valves: right has tricuspid and pulmonary; left has mitral and aortic."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Every chamber pass goes atrium, AV valve, ventricle, semilunar valve."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-161a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"le châtelier shift","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:161#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:161"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P9","passage":"Plasma pH is held steady by this equilibrium: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. The kidneys tune plasma pH by controlling how much H+ and HCO3– leave in the urine. In renal acid disorder (RAD), this control fails and the blood becomes too acidic. Type A RAD is a distal tubule defect in H+ excretion, and Type B RAD is a proximal tubule defect in HCO3– reabsorption; both have low plasma K+. Type C RAD is a distal tubule defect in making or responding to aldosterone, with high plasma K+. Aldosterone makes the distal and collecting tubules reabsorb more Na+.","q":"Which of the following conditions, if uncompensated, would most likely tend to decrease the acidity of the plasma?","choices":["Increasing plasma CO2","Decreasing plasma HCO3–","Increasing plasma H+","Decreasing plasma CO2"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"More CO2 or H+: more acid","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Less HCO3–: fewer H+ soaked up","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Remove CO2 and the equilibrium shifts left, using up H+."},"exp":"**Answer:** Decreasing plasma CO2\n\n**Incorrect: Increasing plasma CO2** Adding CO2 pushes the reaction right and makes more H+.\n\n**Incorrect: Decreasing plasma HCO3–** Less base means less H+ is soaked up, so acidity rises.\n\n**Incorrect: Increasing plasma H+** More H+ is more acid by definition.\n\n**Correct: Decreasing plasma CO2** Losing CO2 pulls the equilibrium left. H+ joins HCO3– to replace it, so H+ falls.","how":{"ask":"Which change lowers H+?","know":"Le Châtelier: remove a reactant, the reaction shifts toward it.","steps":[{"text":"CO2 is on the left and H+ is on the right.","hl":"CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–"},{"text":"More CO2 or more H+ raises acidity.","strike":["Increasing plasma CO2","Increasing plasma H+"]},{"text":"Less HCO3– leaves more free H+.","strike":["Decreasing plasma HCO3–"]},{"text":"Answer: Decreasing plasma CO2."}],"trap":"Decreasing HCO3–: removing a product sounds like it shifts right to make HCO3–, but losing base raises acidity."},"teach":[{"text":"Find where each species sits in the equation.","marks":[{"t":"CO2 + H2O","note":"left side"},{"t":"H+ + HCO3–","note":"right side"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Less CO2 (like breathing fast) lowers H+ and raises pH."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-162a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"nephron segment function","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:162#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:162"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P9","passage":"Plasma pH is held steady by this equilibrium: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. The kidneys tune plasma pH by controlling how much H+ and HCO3– leave in the urine. In renal acid disorder (RAD), this control fails and the blood becomes too acidic. Type A RAD is a distal tubule defect in H+ excretion, and Type B RAD is a proximal tubule defect in HCO3– reabsorption; both have low plasma K+. Type C RAD is a distal tubule defect in making or responding to aldosterone, with high plasma K+. Aldosterone makes the distal and collecting tubules reabsorb more Na+.","q":"In Type C RAD, where aldosterone action is missing, plasma [K+] would:","choices":["decrease because K+ reabsorption is coupled to Na+ reabsorption.","increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ secretion.","increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ reabsorption.","not change because K+ secretion is not linked to Na+ reabsorption."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Type C has high plasma K+","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Aldosterone reabsorbs Na+, secretes K+","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Aldosterone swaps Na+ in for K+ out; without it, K+ stays in the blood."},"exp":"**Answer:** increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ reabsorption.\n\n**Incorrect: decrease because K+ reabsorption is coupled to Na+ reabsorption.** Aldosterone makes the tubule secrete K+, not reabsorb it, and Type C has high K+.\n\n**Incorrect: increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ secretion.** Aldosterone drives Na+ reabsorption, not Na+ secretion.\n\n**Correct: increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ reabsorption.** Aldosterone makes the tubule take back Na+ and push out K+. With no aldosterone action, less K+ is secreted, so plasma K+ rises.\n\n**Incorrect: not change because K+ secretion is not linked to Na+ reabsorption.** The two are linked by aldosterone, and Type C K+ is high.","how":{"ask":"What happens to K+ when aldosterone fails?","know":"Aldosterone: Na+ in, K+ out.","steps":[{"text":"Type C lacks aldosterone action and has high plasma K+.","hl":"Type C RAD is a distal tubule defect in making or responding to aldosterone, with high plasma K+","strike":["decrease because K+ reabsorption is coupled to Na+ reabsorption.","not change because K+ secretion is not linked to Na+ reabsorption."]},{"text":"Aldosterone drives Na+ reabsorption, not secretion.","hl":"Aldosterone makes the distal and collecting tubules reabsorb more Na+","strike":["increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ secretion."]},{"text":"Answer: increase because K+ secretion is coupled to Na+ reabsorption."}],"trap":"Na+ secretion: the direction is right for K+, but aldosterone pulls Na+ back in."},"teach":[{"text":"Read Type C's K+ level, then explain it.","marks":[{"t":"with high plasma K+","note":"less K+ secreted"},{"t":"reabsorb more Na+","note":"swapped for K+"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Aldosterone couples Na+ reabsorption to K+ secretion; low aldosterone raises plasma K+."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-163a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"hormone source and target","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:163#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:163"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P9","passage":"Plasma pH is held steady by this equilibrium: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. The kidneys tune plasma pH by controlling how much H+ and HCO3– leave in the urine. In renal acid disorder (RAD), this control fails and the blood becomes too acidic. Type A RAD is a distal tubule defect in H+ excretion, and Type B RAD is a proximal tubule defect in HCO3– reabsorption; both have low plasma K+. Type C RAD is a distal tubule defect in making or responding to aldosterone, with high plasma K+. Aldosterone makes the distal and collecting tubules reabsorb more Na+.","q":"Which of the following physiological conditions would most likely trigger the release of aldosterone?","choices":["High blood pressure","Low plasma [K+]","Low blood volume","High plasma [Na+]"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Aldosterone raises volume and pressure","c":[0,3]},{"f":"High K+, not low, triggers it","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Aldosterone saves Na+ and water, so it is released when volume or pressure is low."},"exp":"**Answer:** Low blood volume\n\n**Incorrect: High blood pressure** Aldosterone raises pressure, so high pressure turns it off.\n\n**Incorrect: Low plasma [K+]** Aldosterone makes the kidney lose K+, so high K+, not low, triggers it.\n\n**Correct: Low blood volume** Aldosterone makes the tubules reabsorb Na+, and water follows. Low volume sets off renin, angiotensin II and then aldosterone.\n\n**Incorrect: High plasma [Na+]** Aldosterone saves Na+, so high Na+ would not call for it.","how":{"ask":"What problem does aldosterone fix?","know":"Aldosterone: save Na+ and water, dump K+.","steps":[{"text":"Aldosterone makes the tubules take back Na+, and water follows.","hl":"Aldosterone makes the distal and collecting tubules reabsorb more Na+"},{"text":"High pressure and high Na+ would not call for more Na+ saving.","strike":["High blood pressure","High plasma [Na+]"]},{"text":"Aldosterone dumps K+, so low K+ would not trigger it.","strike":["Low plasma [K+]"]},{"text":"Answer: Low blood volume."}],"trap":"Low plasma [K+]: K+ does control aldosterone, but it is high K+ that triggers it."},"teach":[{"text":"Ask what the hormone fixes; its trigger is the opposite problem.","marks":[{"t":"reabsorb more Na+","note":"raises volume"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Aldosterone is released for low volume, low pressure or high K+."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-164a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"immune branch and cell type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:164#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:164"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P9","passage":"Plasma pH is held steady by this equilibrium: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. The kidneys tune plasma pH by controlling how much H+ and HCO3– leave in the urine. In renal acid disorder (RAD), this control fails and the blood becomes too acidic. Type A RAD is a distal tubule defect in H+ excretion, and Type B RAD is a proximal tubule defect in HCO3– reabsorption; both have low plasma K+. Type C RAD is a distal tubule defect in making or responding to aldosterone, with high plasma K+. Aldosterone makes the distal and collecting tubules reabsorb more Na+.","q":"Some cases of RAD come from autoimmune diseases that wrongly attack healthy kidney tubule cells. In these cases, the healthy tubule cells are most likely acting as which of the following components of the immune response?","choices":["Antibodies","Antigens","Helper T cells","Plasma cells"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"The thing attacked is antigen","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Whatever the immune system attacks is the antigen."},"exp":"**Answer:** Antigens\n\n**Incorrect: Antibodies** Antibodies are made by plasma cells, not kidney cells.\n\n**Correct: Antigens** The immune system targets molecules on the tubule cells. Anything the immune system targets is acting as an antigen.\n\n**Incorrect: Helper T cells** Helper T cells help run the attack; they are not the target.\n\n**Incorrect: Plasma cells** Plasma cells make the antibodies that attack; they are not kidney cells.","how":{"ask":"What role does an attacked tissue play?","know":"Target = antigen; attackers = antibodies and immune cells.","steps":[{"text":"The disorder lives in the kidney tubules.","hl":"In renal acid disorder (RAD), this control fails"},{"text":"Antibodies, T cells and plasma cells are the attackers.","strike":["Antibodies","Helper T cells","Plasma cells"]},{"text":"Answer: Antigens."}],"trap":"Antibodies: they are in the story, but they are the weapon, not the target."},"teach":[{"text":"Split the story into attacker and target.","marks":[{"t":"renal acid disorder (RAD)","note":"kidney is target"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In autoimmunity, the body's own tissue acts as the antigen."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-165a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:165#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:165"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P10","passage":"The soil bacterium Bacillus terrae forms tough, dormant endospores that can survive for decades. Grazing animals catch the disease it causes by eating spores from the soil. Soil samples were taken in a ring around an old release site and tested for live spores. Positive samples were 6 of 7 between 0° and 90°, 2 of 7 between 90° and 180°, 4 of 7 between 180° and 270°, and 0 of 7 between 270° and 360°. Four agents were then tested on soil 0 to 2 cm and 2 to 4 cm deep. Spores before and after treatment were: bleach 120 to 0 and 300 to 25; hydrogen peroxide 410 to 0, with no data for 2 to 4 cm; chlorine dioxide 210 to 0 and 480 to 0; detergent 45 to 290 and 2900 to 7600.","q":"Using the degree readings for the sampling ring, which quadrant had the fewest positive endospore samples?","choices":["Between 0° and 90°","Between 90° and 180°","Between 180° and 270°","Between 270° and 360°"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Others had 2 to 6 positives","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"List the positives per quadrant and pick the smallest: 0 of 7."},"exp":"**Answer:** Between 270° and 360°\n\n**Incorrect: Between 0° and 90°** This quadrant had 6 of 7 positive, the most.\n\n**Incorrect: Between 90° and 180°** This quadrant had 2 of 7 positive, low but not the lowest.\n\n**Incorrect: Between 180° and 270°** This quadrant had 4 of 7 positive.\n\n**Correct: Between 270° and 360°** This quadrant had 0 of 7 positive samples, the fewest.","how":{"ask":"Which quadrant has the smallest positive count?","know":"Compare counts directly.","steps":[{"text":"Read the four counts: 6, 2, 4 and 0.","hl":"Positive samples were 6 of 7 between 0° and 90°"},{"text":"6, 2 and 4 are all above 0.","strike":["Between 0° and 90°","Between 90° and 180°","Between 180° and 270°"]},{"text":"The last quadrant had none.","hl":"0 of 7 between 270° and 360°"},{"text":"Answer: Between 270° and 360°."}],"trap":"Between 90° and 180°: 2 is low, but 0 is lower."},"teach":[{"text":"Line up the four counts before choosing.","marks":[{"t":"2 of 7 between 90° and 180°","note":"low, not lowest"},{"t":"0 of 7 between 270° and 360°","note":"fewest"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For fewest or most, list every group's number and compare them all."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-166a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"weigh evidence against model","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:166#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:166"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P10","passage":"The soil bacterium Bacillus terrae forms tough, dormant endospores that can survive for decades. Grazing animals catch the disease it causes by eating spores from the soil. Soil samples were taken in a ring around an old release site and tested for live spores. Positive samples were 6 of 7 between 0° and 90°, 2 of 7 between 90° and 180°, 4 of 7 between 180° and 270°, and 0 of 7 between 270° and 360°. Four agents were then tested on soil 0 to 2 cm and 2 to 4 cm deep. Spores before and after treatment were: bleach 120 to 0 and 300 to 25; hydrogen peroxide 410 to 0, with no data for 2 to 4 cm; chlorine dioxide 210 to 0 and 480 to 0; detergent 45 to 290 and 2900 to 7600.","q":"Based on the data, is it accurate to conclude that chlorine dioxide is more effective than any of the other agents tested?","choices":["Yes, because no other agent destroys all the endospores","Yes, because chlorine dioxide leaves fewer spores than the detergent","No, because the data for hydrogen peroxide are incomplete","No, because bleach and hydrogen peroxide also kill endospores"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Missing data blocks a best claim","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Killing some doesn't rank agents","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"You cannot call one agent the best when a rival has a missing data point."},"exp":"**Answer:** No, because the data for hydrogen peroxide are incomplete\n\n**Incorrect: Yes, because no other agent destroys all the endospores** Hydrogen peroxide's 2 to 4 cm result is unknown, so it may also kill all.\n\n**Incorrect: Yes, because chlorine dioxide leaves fewer spores than the detergent** Beating one agent does not make it better than all of them.\n\n**Correct: No, because the data for hydrogen peroxide are incomplete** Chlorine dioxide cleared both depths. But hydrogen peroxide has no 2 to 4 cm data, so it cannot be ruled out as equal.\n\n**Incorrect: No, because bleach and hydrogen peroxide also kill endospores** Killing some spores does not tie them with chlorine dioxide; bleach left 25.","how":{"ask":"Do the data let you rank chlorine dioxide first?","know":"A best-of-all claim needs full data for every rival.","steps":[{"text":"Chlorine dioxide cleared both depths.","hl":"chlorine dioxide 210 to 0 and 480 to 0"},{"text":"Hydrogen peroxide has no deep-soil result, so a yes cannot stand.","hl":"with no data for 2 to 4 cm","strike":["Yes, because no other agent destroys all the endospores","Yes, because chlorine dioxide leaves fewer spores than the detergent"]},{"text":"Killing some spores is not the reason; bleach left 25.","strike":["No, because bleach and hydrogen peroxide also kill endospores"]},{"text":"Answer: No, because the data for hydrogen peroxide are incomplete."}],"trap":"Yes, no other agent kills all: true for the data shown, but one cell is missing."},"teach":[{"text":"Scan the table for gaps before ranking.","marks":[{"t":"no data for 2 to 4 cm","note":"missing value"},{"t":"300 to 25","note":"bleach not perfect"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** If any compared group has missing data, a 'best of all' conclusion is not supported."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-167a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:167#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:167"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P10","passage":"The soil bacterium Bacillus terrae forms tough, dormant endospores that can survive for decades. Grazing animals catch the disease it causes by eating spores from the soil. Soil samples were taken in a ring around an old release site and tested for live spores. Positive samples were 6 of 7 between 0° and 90°, 2 of 7 between 90° and 180°, 4 of 7 between 180° and 270°, and 0 of 7 between 270° and 360°. Four agents were then tested on soil 0 to 2 cm and 2 to 4 cm deep. Spores before and after treatment were: bleach 120 to 0 and 300 to 25; hydrogen peroxide 410 to 0, with no data for 2 to 4 cm; chlorine dioxide 210 to 0 and 480 to 0; detergent 45 to 290 and 2900 to 7600.","q":"Bacillus terrae produces a toxin that has been identified as a Mg2+-dependent nuclease. The toxin is an enzyme that:","choices":["joins nucleic acid strands.","cuts nucleic acids.","removes Mg2+ from nucleic acids.","adds Mg2+ to nucleic acids."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Nucleases cut, ligases join","c":[0]},{"f":"Mg2+ is a cofactor, not cargo","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A nuclease cuts nucleic acids; the metal is just a helper cofactor."},"exp":"**Answer:** cuts nucleic acids.\n\n**Incorrect: joins nucleic acid strands.** Joining is the job of a ligase, not a nuclease.\n\n**Correct: cuts nucleic acids.** A nuclease breaks the bonds in DNA or RNA. Mg2+-dependent means it needs Mg2+ as a cofactor to work.\n\n**Incorrect: removes Mg2+ from nucleic acids.** Mg2+ helps the enzyme; it is not taken from the substrate.\n\n**Incorrect: adds Mg2+ to nucleic acids.** A cofactor stays with the enzyme; it is not added to the substrate.","how":{"ask":"What does a Mg2+-dependent nuclease do?","know":"Nuclease = cuts nucleic acid. Metal-dependent = metal cofactor.","steps":[{"text":"Nucleases cut; ligases join.","strike":["joins nucleic acid strands."]},{"text":"The Mg2+ is a cofactor the enzyme needs, not a group it moves.","strike":["removes Mg2+ from nucleic acids.","adds Mg2+ to nucleic acids."]},{"text":"Answer: cuts nucleic acids."}],"trap":"Adds Mg2+: the name has Mg2+ in it, but the metal only helps the enzyme."},"teach":[{"text":"Split the name: Mg2+-dependent (cofactor) + nuclease (job)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 'Metal-dependent X-ase' means the metal is a cofactor and the -ase name gives the job."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-168a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:168#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:168"],"pid":"pe6-bb:P10","passage":"The soil bacterium Bacillus terrae forms tough, dormant endospores that can survive for decades. Grazing animals catch the disease it causes by eating spores from the soil. Soil samples were taken in a ring around an old release site and tested for live spores. Positive samples were 6 of 7 between 0° and 90°, 2 of 7 between 90° and 180°, 4 of 7 between 180° and 270°, and 0 of 7 between 270° and 360°. Four agents were then tested on soil 0 to 2 cm and 2 to 4 cm deep. Spores before and after treatment were: bleach 120 to 0 and 300 to 25; hydrogen peroxide 410 to 0, with no data for 2 to 4 cm; chlorine dioxide 210 to 0 and 480 to 0; detergent 45 to 290 and 2900 to 7600.","q":"One B. terrae toxin component, Factor G, acts as a guanylate cyclase. It raises 3′,5′-cyclic GMP and lowers GTP in target cells. What chemical reaction does Factor G catalyze?","choices":["GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + 5′-GMP","GTP + pyrophosphate → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP","GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + pyrophosphate","3′,5′-cyclic GMP → GTP + pyrophosphate"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"GTP used up, cGMP made","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Cyclase releases PPi, not GMP","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A cyclase turns a triphosphate into the cyclic monophosphate and releases pyrophosphate."},"exp":"**Answer:** GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + pyrophosphate\n\n**Incorrect: GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + 5′-GMP** One GTP has only enough atoms for one GMP unit; the leftover is pyrophosphate.\n\n**Incorrect: GTP + pyrophosphate → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP** Pyrophosphate is released, so it is a product, not a reactant.\n\n**Correct: GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + pyrophosphate** GTP falls and cGMP rises, so GTP is the reactant. Closing the ring releases the two outer phosphates as pyrophosphate.\n\n**Incorrect: 3′,5′-cyclic GMP → GTP + pyrophosphate** This is backward: cGMP is made, not used.","how":{"ask":"What does guanylate cyclase make from GTP?","know":"Cyclase: NTP → cyclic NMP + PPi.","steps":[{"text":"GTP falls and cGMP rises, so GTP goes in and cGMP comes out.","strike":["3′,5′-cyclic GMP → GTP + pyrophosphate"]},{"text":"Pyrophosphate is released, not used.","strike":["GTP + pyrophosphate → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP"]},{"text":"One GTP gives one cGMP plus PPi, not a second GMP.","strike":["GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + 5′-GMP"]},{"text":"Answer: GTP → 3′,5′-cyclic GMP + pyrophosphate."}],"trap":"cGMP + 5′-GMP: it looks balanced, but one GTP cannot make two GMPs."},"teach":[{"text":"Count phosphates: GTP has 3; cGMP keeps 1; the other 2 leave as PPi."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Adenylate and guanylate cyclases convert ATP or GTP into the cyclic nucleotide plus pyrophosphate."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-169a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"reduction potential sign","freq":1,"tier":"rare","from":["pe6-bb:169#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:169"],"q":"Using these standard reduction potentials, which reaction has a positive potential? NAD+/NADH –0.32 V; FAD/FADH2 –0.22 V; pyruvate/lactate –0.19 V; ubiquinone/ubiquinol +0.05 V; cytochrome c (Fe3+/Fe2+) +0.25 V.","choices":["Reduction of NAD+ by lactate","Oxidation of ubiquinol by FAD","Reduction of cytochrome c by ubiquinol","Oxidation of lactate by FAD"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Acceptor must have higher E°′","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"ΔE°′ = E°′(acceptor) – E°′(donor); it is positive only if the acceptor's value is higher."},"exp":"**Answer:** Reduction of cytochrome c by ubiquinol\n\n**Incorrect: Reduction of NAD+ by lactate** ΔE = –0.32 – (–0.19) = –0.13 V, negative.\n\n**Incorrect: Oxidation of ubiquinol by FAD** ΔE = –0.22 – (+0.05) = –0.27 V, negative.\n\n**Correct: Reduction of cytochrome c by ubiquinol** Cytochrome c accepts and ubiquinol donates. ΔE = +0.25 – (+0.05) = +0.20 V, positive.\n\n**Incorrect: Oxidation of lactate by FAD** ΔE = –0.22 – (–0.19) = –0.03 V, negative.","how":{"ask":"In which pair is the acceptor's E°′ higher than the donor's?","know":"ΔE°′ = acceptor – donor. The thing reduced is the acceptor.","steps":[{"text":"NAD+ (–0.32) is lower than lactate (–0.19): negative.","strike":["Reduction of NAD+ by lactate"]},{"text":"FAD (–0.22) is lower than ubiquinol (+0.05) and lactate (–0.19): negative.","strike":["Oxidation of ubiquinol by FAD","Oxidation of lactate by FAD"]},{"text":"Cytochrome c (+0.25) is higher than ubiquinol (+0.05): positive."},{"text":"Answer: Reduction of cytochrome c by ubiquinol."}],"trap":"Oxidation of lactate by FAD: the values are close, but FAD is still slightly lower."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the acceptor: the thing being reduced, or the agent doing the oxidizing."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Electrons flow spontaneously to the partner with the higher reduction potential."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-170a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:170#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:170"],"q":"Which compound is the substrate of succinate dehydrogenase in the citric acid cycle?","choices":["HOOC–CH2–CO–COOH","HOOC–CH=CH–COOH","HOOC–CH(OH)–CH2–COOH","HOOC–CH2–CH2–COOH"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"C=C one is product, fumarate","c":[1]},{"f":"Ketone, alcohol: oxaloacetate, malate","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Succinate is the plain four-carbon diacid with two CH2 groups; SDH turns it into fumarate."},"exp":"**Answer:** HOOC–CH2–CH2–COOH\n\n**Incorrect: HOOC–CH2–CO–COOH** This is oxaloacetate, made by malate dehydrogenase.\n\n**Incorrect: HOOC–CH=CH–COOH** This is fumarate, the product of succinate dehydrogenase.\n\n**Incorrect: HOOC–CH(OH)–CH2–COOH** This is malate, made from fumarate.\n\n**Correct: HOOC–CH2–CH2–COOH** This is succinate. Succinate dehydrogenase removes two H to form the C=C of fumarate.","how":{"ask":"What does succinate look like?","know":"Succinate → fumarate → malate → oxaloacetate.","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme's name gives its substrate: succinate."},{"text":"The C=C compound is fumarate, the product.","strike":["HOOC–CH=CH–COOH"]},{"text":"The ketone and the alcohol are oxaloacetate and malate.","strike":["HOOC–CH2–CO–COOH","HOOC–CH(OH)–CH2–COOH"]},{"text":"Answer: HOOC–CH2–CH2–COOH."}],"trap":"HOOC–CH=CH–COOH: it is in the right step, but it is what SDH makes."},"teach":[{"text":"Track the middle carbons: CH2–CH2, then CH=CH, then CH(OH), then C=O."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Succinate (CH2–CH2) → fumarate (C=C) → malate (OH) → oxaloacetate (C=O)."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-pe6-171a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["pe6-bb:171#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-bb:171"],"q":"In anaphase cells, a laser bleached a mark on kinetochore microtubules. Over one minute, the distance from the mark to the spindle pole fell from 6 μm to 3 μm, while the distance from the mark to the kinetochore stayed at 4 μm. The results of this experiment best support which of the following hypotheses about the shortening of microtubules during anaphase?","choices":["Microtubules shorten mainly at their kinetochore ends.","Microtubules shorten mainly at their spindle pole ends.","Microtubules shorten equally at both ends.","Bleaching stops microtubules from shortening in anaphase."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Kinetochore side stayed 4 μm","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Total length still shrank","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The segment that got shorter tells you which end is losing tubulin."},"exp":"**Answer:** Microtubules shorten mainly at their spindle pole ends.\n\n**Incorrect: Microtubules shorten mainly at their kinetochore ends.** The mark-to-kinetochore stretch stayed at 4 μm.\n\n**Correct: Microtubules shorten mainly at their spindle pole ends.** Only the mark-to-pole stretch shrank, from 6 to 3 μm. So tubulin is lost at the pole end.\n\n**Incorrect: Microtubules shorten equally at both ends.** One side did not change at all.\n\n**Incorrect: Bleaching stops microtubules from shortening in anaphase.** The total length fell from 10 to 7 μm, so shortening went on.","how":{"ask":"Which side of the mark got shorter?","know":"A fixed mark splits the fiber; the shrinking side is the active end.","steps":[{"text":"Mark to kinetochore stayed 4 μm.","strike":["Microtubules shorten mainly at their kinetochore ends.","Microtubules shorten equally at both ends."]},{"text":"Total length fell, so bleaching did not stop shortening.","strike":["Bleaching stops microtubules from shortening in anaphase."]},{"text":"Mark to pole fell from 6 to 3 μm."},{"text":"Answer: Microtubules shorten mainly at their spindle pole ends."}],"trap":"Kinetochore ends: the classic textbook answer, but these numbers point to the pole."},"teach":[{"text":"Compare each side of the mark before and after."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In a mark-tracking test, the segment that shrinks shows which end is losing subunits."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-113a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"receptor type and second messenger","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:113#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:113"],"pid":"st-bb:P1","passage":"Protein Kyl1 is a small GTPase that builds coated vesicles at the Golgi. An exchange factor turns Kyl1 on by swapping its bound GDP for GTP. GTP-bound Kyl1 changes shape, frees a lipid anchor and binds the Golgi membrane. Kyl1 then recruits coat proteins for retrograde transport out of the Golgi. A helper protein, KAP, cuts Kyl1-bound GTP into GDP and phosphate, which turns Kyl1 off. Drug T7, made by a mold, blocks Kyl1 vesicle budding and breaks up the Golgi.","q":"Hydrolysis of the γ phosphate of GTP bound to Kyl1 results in:","choices":["membrane binding.","activation.","protein unfolding.","inactivation."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Membrane binding needs GTP","c":[0]},{"f":"GDP-to-GTP swap is activation","c":[1]},{"f":"Shape shift is not unfolding","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"GTP means on and GDP means off, so cutting the phosphate switches Kyl1 off."},"exp":"**Answer:** inactivation.\n\n**Incorrect: membrane binding.** Kyl1 binds the membrane only while GTP is bound.\n\n**Incorrect: activation.** Swapping GDP for GTP turns Kyl1 on; hydrolysis is the reverse.\n\n**Incorrect: protein unfolding.** Kyl1 only shifts shape; it does not denature.\n\n**Correct: inactivation.** KAP cuts GTP to GDP, and the passage says this turns Kyl1 off.","how":{"ask":"What state is Kyl1 in after its GTP is cut?","know":"Small GTPases are on with GTP and off with GDP.","steps":[{"text":"Find what cutting GTP does.","hl":"which turns Kyl1 off"},{"text":"Membrane binding comes with GTP, so losing GTP cannot cause it.","hl":"GTP-bound Kyl1 changes shape","strike":["membrane binding."]},{"text":"Activation is the GDP-to-GTP swap, the opposite step.","strike":["activation."]},{"text":"A shape change is not denaturing.","strike":["protein unfolding."]},{"text":"Answer: inactivation."}],"trap":"activation. tempts because GTP is linked to Kyl1, but cutting GTP is the off step."},"teach":[{"text":"G proteins are switches. GTP turns them on. GDP turns them off.","marks":[{"t":"swapping its bound GDP for GTP","note":"turns on"},{"t":"which turns Kyl1 off","note":"turns off"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Hydrolysis of bound GTP switches a G protein off."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-114a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:114#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:114"],"pid":"st-bb:P1","passage":"Protein Kyl1 is a small GTPase that builds coated vesicles at the Golgi. An exchange factor turns Kyl1 on by swapping its bound GDP for GTP. GTP-bound Kyl1 changes shape, frees a lipid anchor and binds the Golgi membrane. Kyl1 then recruits coat proteins for retrograde transport out of the Golgi. A helper protein, KAP, cuts Kyl1-bound GTP into GDP and phosphate, which turns Kyl1 off. Drug T7, made by a mold, blocks Kyl1 vesicle budding and breaks up the Golgi.","q":"The Kyl1-activating molecule GTP is most closely related to which family of biomolecules?","choices":["Amino acids","Lipids","Nucleotides","Carbohydrates"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"GTP = base, sugar, phosphates","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Ribose is only one part","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Any NTP (ATP, GTP) is a nucleotide: base plus ribose plus phosphates."},"exp":"**Answer:** Nucleotides\n\n**Incorrect: Amino acids** GTP has no amino and carboxyl backbone.\n\n**Incorrect: Lipids** GTP is water-soluble and has no fatty chains.\n\n**Correct: Nucleotides** GTP is guanosine triphosphate: guanine, ribose and three phosphates.\n\n**Incorrect: Carbohydrates** Ribose is only one piece; the whole molecule is a nucleotide.","how":{"ask":"What kind of molecule is GTP?","know":"Base + sugar + phosphate = nucleotide.","steps":[{"text":"GTP is the switch fuel for Kyl1.","hl":"swapping its bound GDP for GTP"},{"text":"GTP has no amino acid backbone and no fatty tail.","strike":["Amino acids","Lipids"]},{"text":"It holds a ribose, but the full unit is more than a sugar.","strike":["Carbohydrates"]},{"text":"Answer: Nucleotides."}],"trap":"Carbohydrates tempts because GTP contains ribose, but the base and phosphates make it a nucleotide."},"teach":[{"text":"ATP, GTP, CTP and UTP are all nucleotides.","marks":[{"t":"GTP-bound Kyl1","note":"GTP = nucleotide"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Anything named N-osine/idine triphosphate is a nucleotide."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-115a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:115#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:115"],"pid":"st-bb:P1","passage":"Protein Kyl1 is a small GTPase that builds coated vesicles at the Golgi. An exchange factor turns Kyl1 on by swapping its bound GDP for GTP. GTP-bound Kyl1 changes shape, frees a lipid anchor and binds the Golgi membrane. Kyl1 then recruits coat proteins for retrograde transport out of the Golgi. A helper protein, KAP, cuts Kyl1-bound GTP into GDP and phosphate, which turns Kyl1 off. Drug T7, made by a mold, blocks Kyl1 vesicle budding and breaks up the Golgi.","q":"KAP belongs to what class of enzymes?","choices":["Ligase","Kinase","Phosphatase","Isomerase"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Kinases add phosphate","c":[1]},{"f":"Nothing is joined or rearranged","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Reaction releases free phosphate with water, so the enzyme is a phosphatase."},"exp":"**Answer:** Phosphatase\n\n**Incorrect: Ligase** Ligases join two molecules using ATP; KAP splits one.\n\n**Incorrect: Kinase** Kinases add a phosphate; KAP removes one.\n\n**Correct: Phosphatase** KAP splits GTP into GDP and free phosphate, a phosphate-removing hydrolysis.\n\n**Incorrect: Isomerase** Isomerases rearrange a molecule; KAP breaks a bond.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme class matches GTP to GDP plus phosphate?","know":"Removing a phosphate with water = phosphatase.","steps":[{"text":"Read KAP's reaction.","hl":"cuts Kyl1-bound GTP into GDP and phosphate"},{"text":"A kinase would add phosphate, the reverse.","strike":["Kinase"]},{"text":"Nothing is joined or rearranged.","strike":["Ligase","Isomerase"]},{"text":"Answer: Phosphatase."}],"trap":"Kinase tempts because phosphate is involved, but kinases add phosphate rather than remove it."},"teach":[{"text":"Name the enzyme from what leaves: free phosphate leaving means phosphatase.","marks":[{"t":"GDP and phosphate","note":"phosphate released"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Kinase adds phosphate; phosphatase removes it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-116a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"protein targeting and secretion","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:116#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:116"],"pid":"st-bb:P1","passage":"Protein Kyl1 is a small GTPase that builds coated vesicles at the Golgi. An exchange factor turns Kyl1 on by swapping its bound GDP for GTP. GTP-bound Kyl1 changes shape, frees a lipid anchor and binds the Golgi membrane. Kyl1 then recruits coat proteins for retrograde transport out of the Golgi. A helper protein, KAP, cuts Kyl1-bound GTP into GDP and phosphate, which turns Kyl1 off. Drug T7, made by a mold, blocks Kyl1 vesicle budding and breaks up the Golgi.","q":"Proteins that are packed in Kyl1-coated vesicles are bound for the:","choices":["plasma membrane.","endoplasmic reticulum.","lysosome.","nucleus."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Retrograde = back toward ER","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Nucleus is off the route","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Retrograde from the Golgi means one step back, to the ER."},"exp":"**Answer:** endoplasmic reticulum.\n\n**Incorrect: plasma membrane.** That is the forward (anterograde) route.\n\n**Correct: endoplasmic reticulum.** Retrograde vesicles move backward, and the step before the Golgi is the ER.\n\n**Incorrect: lysosome.** Lysosome delivery is also a forward Golgi exit.\n\n**Incorrect: nucleus.** The nucleus is not on the secretory route.","how":{"ask":"Where do retrograde Golgi vesicles go?","know":"Route: ER to Golgi to membrane; retrograde runs backward.","steps":[{"text":"Note the direction word.","hl":"retrograde transport out of the Golgi"},{"text":"Forward exits are not retrograde.","strike":["plasma membrane.","lysosome."]},{"text":"The nucleus is not on this path.","strike":["nucleus."]},{"text":"Answer: endoplasmic reticulum."}],"trap":"plasma membrane. tempts as the usual Golgi exit, but that is the forward route."},"teach":[{"text":"Retrograde means backward along ER to Golgi to membrane.","marks":[{"t":"retrograde transport","note":"backward to ER"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Retrograde Golgi traffic returns cargo to the ER."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-117a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme location by pathway","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:117#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:117"],"pid":"st-bb:P1","passage":"Protein Kyl1 is a small GTPase that builds coated vesicles at the Golgi. An exchange factor turns Kyl1 on by swapping its bound GDP for GTP. GTP-bound Kyl1 changes shape, frees a lipid anchor and binds the Golgi membrane. Kyl1 then recruits coat proteins for retrograde transport out of the Golgi. A helper protein, KAP, cuts Kyl1-bound GTP into GDP and phosphate, which turns Kyl1 off. Drug T7, made by a mold, blocks Kyl1 vesicle budding and breaks up the Golgi.","q":"Based on the mode of action described for Drug T7 in the passage, the drug would be most effective against:","choices":["bacteria.","archaea.","viruses.","eukarya."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Prokaryotes have no Golgi","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Viruses have no organelles","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A drug that wrecks the Golgi only hits cells that have a Golgi: eukaryotes."},"exp":"**Answer:** eukarya.\n\n**Incorrect: bacteria.** Bacteria have no Golgi to disrupt.\n\n**Incorrect: archaea.** Archaea are prokaryotes with no Golgi.\n\n**Incorrect: viruses.** Viruses are not cells and have no organelles.\n\n**Correct: eukarya.** Only eukaryotic cells have a Golgi, the drug's target.","how":{"ask":"Which group has the organelle Drug T7 attacks?","know":"Only eukaryotes have membrane-bound organelles like the Golgi.","steps":[{"text":"Find the drug's target.","hl":"breaks up the Golgi"},{"text":"Prokaryotes lack a Golgi.","strike":["bacteria.","archaea."]},{"text":"Viruses have no organelles at all.","strike":["viruses."]},{"text":"Answer: eukarya."}],"trap":"bacteria. tempts because drugs from molds are often antibiotics, but this one targets the Golgi."},"teach":[{"text":"Match the drug target to who owns it.","marks":[{"t":"breaks up the Golgi","note":"Golgi = eukaryotes"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** An organelle-targeting drug works only on eukaryotes."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-118a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:118#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:118"],"pid":"st-bb:P2","passage":"When blood flow returns to a kidney after a blockage, the tissue is often badly damaged. A researcher saw monocytes stick to the vessel lining and then crawl into the kidney tissue. The stuck monocytes released toxic products such as free radicals and proteases. Monocytes stick by a surface receptor made of an X subunit held noncovalently to a Y subunit. Rabbit antibody X binds the X subunit, and rabbit antibody Y binds the Y subunit. Rats given antibody X had much less kidney damage, but antibody Y had no effect.","q":"Which of the following experiments would provide the best supporting evidence that monocytes are the cause of the kidney injury?","choices":["Repeating the blood-flow experiment in rats whose T cells are depleted and checking whether kidney damage is reduced","Repeating the experiment with another antibody against the X subunit and checking whether kidney damage is reduced","Repeating the blood-flow experiment in monocyte-depleted rats and checking whether kidney damage is reduced","Repeating the experiment with an antibody against the whole X/Y receptor and checking whether kidney damage is reduced"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Test monocytes, not T cells","c":[0]},{"f":"More antibodies repeat old evidence","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To prove a cell causes harm, remove that cell and see if the harm drops."},"exp":"**Answer:** Repeating the blood-flow experiment in monocyte-depleted rats and checking whether kidney damage is reduced\n\n**Incorrect: Repeating the blood-flow experiment in rats whose T cells are depleted and checking whether kidney damage is reduced** T cells are not the suspect.\n\n**Incorrect: Repeating the experiment with another antibody against the X subunit and checking whether kidney damage is reduced** This only repeats the receptor-block result.\n\n**Correct: Repeating the blood-flow experiment in monocyte-depleted rats and checking whether kidney damage is reduced** Removing monocytes directly tests them. Less damage would show they cause it.\n\n**Incorrect: Repeating the experiment with an antibody against the whole X/Y receptor and checking whether kidney damage is reduced** Another receptor block adds no new kind of evidence.","how":{"ask":"Which test isolates monocytes as the cause?","know":"Best evidence removes the suspected cause and keeps all else equal.","steps":[{"text":"The claim is about monocytes.","hl":"monocytes stick to the vessel lining"},{"text":"T cells are not the suspect.","strike":["Repeating the blood-flow experiment in rats whose T cells are depleted and checking whether kidney damage is reduced"]},{"text":"More antibody tests only repeat the receptor result.","strike":["Repeating the experiment with another antibody against the X subunit and checking whether kidney damage is reduced","Repeating the experiment with an antibody against the whole X/Y receptor and checking whether kidney damage is reduced"]},{"text":"Answer: remove monocytes and check the damage."}],"trap":"The new-antibody choices feel rigorous, but they test the receptor, not the cell itself."},"teach":[{"text":"Remove the suspect. If the harm drops, the suspect caused it.","marks":[{"t":"much less kidney damage","note":"indirect evidence"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To prove X causes Y, take away X and watch Y."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-119a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:119#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:119"],"pid":"st-bb:P2","passage":"When blood flow returns to a kidney after a blockage, the tissue is often badly damaged. A researcher saw monocytes stick to the vessel lining and then crawl into the kidney tissue. The stuck monocytes released toxic products such as free radicals and proteases. Monocytes stick by a surface receptor made of an X subunit held noncovalently to a Y subunit. Rabbit antibody X binds the X subunit, and rabbit antibody Y binds the Y subunit. Rats given antibody X had much less kidney damage, but antibody Y had no effect.","q":"Information in the passage suggests most strongly that the function of the X subunit involves:","choices":["moving proteases from lining to monocytes.","sticking monocytes to the vessel lining.","hydrogen bonding with the Y subunit.","making antibody against the subunit."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Receptors bind; they don't ferry","c":[0]},{"f":"No data on X–Y contact","c":[2]},{"f":"Rabbits made the antibodies","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Blocking X stops the damage, so X does the sticking step that starts it."},"exp":"**Answer:** sticking monocytes to the vessel lining.\n\n**Incorrect: moving proteases from lining to monocytes.** Proteases come from monocytes, and receptors do not ferry them.\n\n**Correct: sticking monocytes to the vessel lining.** The receptor makes monocytes stick, and blocking X cut damage, so X likely does the binding.\n\n**Incorrect: hydrogen bonding with the Y subunit.** Nothing shows antibody X breaks the X–Y pair.\n\n**Incorrect: making antibody against the subunit.** The rabbits made antibodies, not the subunit.","how":{"ask":"What does blocking X tell us X does?","know":"If blocking a part stops a process, that part runs the process.","steps":[{"text":"The receptor's job is sticking.","hl":"Monocytes stick by a surface receptor"},{"text":"Blocking X cut damage.","hl":"Rats given antibody X had much less kidney damage"},{"text":"Receptors do not move proteases, and rabbits made the antibodies.","strike":["moving proteases from lining to monocytes.","making antibody against the subunit."]},{"text":"No data show X–Y contact is broken.","strike":["hydrogen bonding with the Y subunit."]},{"text":"Answer: sticking monocytes to the vessel lining."}],"trap":"hydrogen bonding with the Y subunit. is a true-sounding fact, but the passage gives no evidence for it."},"teach":[{"text":"Antibody block plus lost effect points to that subunit's job.","marks":[{"t":"antibody X had much less kidney damage","note":"X block works"},{"t":"antibody Y had no effect","note":"Y block fails"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The subunit whose block stops the effect performs the effect."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-120a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:120#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:120"],"pid":"st-bb:P2","passage":"When blood flow returns to a kidney after a blockage, the tissue is often badly damaged. A researcher saw monocytes stick to the vessel lining and then crawl into the kidney tissue. The stuck monocytes released toxic products such as free radicals and proteases. Monocytes stick by a surface receptor made of an X subunit held noncovalently to a Y subunit. Rabbit antibody X binds the X subunit, and rabbit antibody Y binds the Y subunit. Rats given antibody X had much less kidney damage, but antibody Y had no effect.","q":"The researcher claimed that antibody X offers a better means of preventing kidney injury than drugs such as free radical or protease blockers. Which of the following reasons offers the best support for this claim?","choices":["Antibody X is a very specific antibody; therefore, it will bind nothing but the X subunit.","Antibody X binds tightly; therefore, it will not be rejected by the patient.","Antibody X lasts long in blood and can be given at any dose at any time.","Antibody X can block the first step that leads to the release of harmful molecules."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Specificity doesn't explain 'better'","c":[0]},{"f":"Affinity doesn't prevent rejection","c":[1]},{"f":"No drug works at any dose","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stopping the first step beats mopping up each toxic product later."},"exp":"**Answer:** Antibody X can block the first step that leads to the release of harmful molecules.\n\n**Incorrect: Antibody X is a very specific antibody; therefore, it will bind nothing but the X subunit.** Specificity does not show it beats the other drugs.\n\n**Incorrect: Antibody X binds tightly; therefore, it will not be rejected by the patient.** Binding strength does not decide rejection.\n\n**Incorrect: Antibody X lasts long in blood and can be given at any dose at any time.** No drug is safe at any dose.\n\n**Correct: Antibody X can block the first step that leads to the release of harmful molecules.** Stopping sticking stops the release of all toxins at once.","how":{"ask":"Why is blocking the receptor better than blocking each toxin?","know":"An upstream block stops every downstream product.","steps":[{"text":"Sticking comes before toxin release.","hl":"The stuck monocytes released toxic products"},{"text":"Specificity alone does not make it better.","strike":["Antibody X is a very specific antibody; therefore, it will bind nothing but the X subunit."]},{"text":"Affinity and dosing claims are false.","strike":["Antibody X binds tightly; therefore, it will not be rejected by the patient.","Antibody X lasts long in blood and can be given at any dose at any time."]},{"text":"Answer: it blocks the first step."}],"trap":"The specificity choice is true-sounding but does not compare it to the toxin blockers."},"teach":[{"text":"Upstream beats downstream: one block stops many products.","marks":[{"t":"free radicals and proteases","note":"many downstream toxins"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Blocking the start of a pathway beats blocking its outputs."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-121a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"immune branch and cell type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:121#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:121"],"pid":"st-bb:P2","passage":"When blood flow returns to a kidney after a blockage, the tissue is often badly damaged. A researcher saw monocytes stick to the vessel lining and then crawl into the kidney tissue. The stuck monocytes released toxic products such as free radicals and proteases. Monocytes stick by a surface receptor made of an X subunit held noncovalently to a Y subunit. Rabbit antibody X binds the X subunit, and rabbit antibody Y binds the Y subunit. Rats given antibody X had much less kidney damage, but antibody Y had no effect.","q":"The researcher wanted to use antibody X to treat humans, but this proposal was rejected. Which of the following is the most logical reason for the rejection?","choices":["Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it can only be used in vitro.","Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it will not recognize human antigens.","Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, repeated use in one patient would trigger human anti-rabbit antibodies.","Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it can never be used in humans."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Animal antibodies are used in humans","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Antibodies often cross-react","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A rabbit protein is foreign, so repeat doses make the patient attack it."},"exp":"**Answer:** Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, repeated use in one patient would trigger human anti-rabbit antibodies.\n\n**Incorrect: Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it can only be used in vitro.** Animal antibodies are given to people, as with antivenoms.\n\n**Incorrect: Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it will not recognize human antigens.** Animal antibodies often bind human proteins.\n\n**Correct: Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, repeated use in one patient would trigger human anti-rabbit antibodies.** The human immune system sees rabbit protein as foreign and makes antibodies to it.\n\n**Incorrect: Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it can never be used in humans.** Too absolute; animal antibodies are used in humans.","how":{"ask":"What goes wrong giving a rabbit antibody to a person?","know":"A foreign protein triggers an immune response, worse with repeat doses.","steps":[{"text":"The antibody is a rabbit protein.","hl":"Rabbit antibody X binds the X subunit"},{"text":"Absolute bans are false; animal antibodies are used in people.","strike":["Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it can only be used in vitro.","Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it can never be used in humans."]},{"text":"Animal antibodies can bind human targets.","strike":["Because the antibody was made in the rabbit, it will not recognize human antigens."]},{"text":"Answer: repeat use triggers anti-rabbit antibodies."}],"trap":"it can never be used in humans. feels safe, but absolute words like never are usually wrong."},"teach":[{"text":"Foreign proteins are antigens. Repeat exposure builds a stronger response.","marks":[{"t":"Rabbit antibody X","note":"foreign protein"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Animal-made antibodies get attacked after repeat human doses."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-122a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:122#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:122"],"pid":"st-bb:P2","passage":"When blood flow returns to a kidney after a blockage, the tissue is often badly damaged. A researcher saw monocytes stick to the vessel lining and then crawl into the kidney tissue. The stuck monocytes released toxic products such as free radicals and proteases. Monocytes stick by a surface receptor made of an X subunit held noncovalently to a Y subunit. Rabbit antibody X binds the X subunit, and rabbit antibody Y binds the Y subunit. Rats given antibody X had much less kidney damage, but antibody Y had no effect.","q":"A monocyte has point mutations in the genes coding for the X and Y subunits of the receptor. However, this cell can still migrate through the vessel lining. Which of the following conclusions about the effect of these mutations can be drawn?","choices":["The cell has a defective cell membrane.","The cell can bind to the vessel lining.","The cell has only working Y subunits.","The cell cannot release proteases."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Migration needs a working membrane","c":[0]},{"f":"Both genes are mutated","c":[2]},{"f":"Protease release isn't tested","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Monocytes stick first, then crawl through, so a cell that still crawls can still stick."},"exp":"**Answer:** The cell can bind to the vessel lining.\n\n**Incorrect: The cell has a defective cell membrane.** A broken membrane would block migration.\n\n**Correct: The cell can bind to the vessel lining.** Sticking comes before crawling through, so migration shows binding still works.\n\n**Incorrect: The cell has only working Y subunits.** Both genes carry mutations, and nothing says which still work.\n\n**Incorrect: The cell cannot release proteases.** Migration says nothing about toxin release.","how":{"ask":"What must still work if the cell still migrates?","know":"A later step working means earlier required steps work too.","steps":[{"text":"Order: stick, then crawl.","hl":"stick to the vessel lining and then crawl into the kidney tissue"},{"text":"A bad membrane would stop migration.","strike":["The cell has a defective cell membrane."]},{"text":"Both genes are mutated; protease release is not tested.","strike":["The cell has only working Y subunits.","The cell cannot release proteases."]},{"text":"Answer: The cell can bind to the vessel lining."}],"trap":"The cell has only working Y subunits. tempts, but both genes are mutated and point mutations may be silent."},"teach":[{"text":"If step 2 happens, step 1 must have happened.","marks":[{"t":"and then crawl","note":"sticking comes first"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A working later step proves the earlier required step works."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-123a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pedigree pattern elimination","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["st-bb:123#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:123"],"pid":"st-bb:P3","passage":"Disease K is caused by too little working Enzyme M, a soluble protein made in muscle cells and released into the blood. Patients tire quickly, and weekly infusions of purified Enzyme M ease their symptoms. The Enzyme M gene is 90,000 nucleotides long, yet the protein has only 1100 amino acids, because introns interrupt the gene. Cells copy the whole gene into RNA, then cut out the introns and join the remaining pieces. The Enzyme M gene lies on the X chromosome, and the disease allele is recessive.","q":"A hypothesis states that all mutations leading to Disease K occur within the Enzyme M gene. Which of the following pairs of inheritance patterns would fail to support this hypothesis? I. Affected daughters born to normal fathers II. Normal sons born to affected fathers III. Normal sons born to affected mothers IV. Affected sons born to normal fathers","choices":["I and II only","I and III only","II and IV only","III and IV only"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Dads give sons Y, not X","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Carrier moms have affected sons","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sons get their X from mom; daughters need a bad X from both parents."},"exp":"**Answer:** I and III only\n\n**Incorrect: I and II only** II fits: a father passes Y, not his X, to sons.\n\n**Correct: I and III only** An affected daughter needs a bad X from her father (I fails), and an affected mother gives every son a bad X (III fails).\n\n**Incorrect: II and IV only** Both fit X-linked recessive inheritance.\n\n**Incorrect: III and IV only** IV fits: a carrier mother can have an affected son.","how":{"ask":"Which patterns break X-linked recessive rules?","know":"Sons get X from mom; a normal dad cannot give a daughter the bad X.","steps":[{"text":"The gene is X-linked recessive.","hl":"lies on the X chromosome, and the disease allele is recessive"},{"text":"II fits, since dads pass Y to sons.","strike":["I and II only","II and IV only"]},{"text":"IV fits, since a carrier mom can pass a bad X.","strike":["III and IV only"]},{"text":"I and III both break the rules. Answer: I and III only."}],"trap":"IV looks wrong because the father is normal, but sons get their X from the mother."},"teach":[{"text":"Draw it: dad's X goes only to daughters; mom's X goes to all kids.","marks":[{"t":"on the X chromosome","note":"X-linked"},{"t":"recessive","note":"needs every X bad"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** X-linked recessive: affected mom means all sons affected; affected daughter needs affected dad."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-124a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:124#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:124"],"pid":"st-bb:P3","passage":"Disease K is caused by too little working Enzyme M, a soluble protein made in muscle cells and released into the blood. Patients tire quickly, and weekly infusions of purified Enzyme M ease their symptoms. The Enzyme M gene is 90,000 nucleotides long, yet the protein has only 1100 amino acids, because introns interrupt the gene. Cells copy the whole gene into RNA, then cut out the introns and join the remaining pieces. The Enzyme M gene lies on the X chromosome, and the disease allele is recessive.","q":"A scientist claimed that Disease K is caused by a functional deficiency in Enzyme M. The evidence in the passage that would best support this claim is that Enzyme M is:","choices":["encoded by a gene that contains introns.","a soluble blood protein.","able to relieve Disease K symptoms.","made from a gene on the X chromosome."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Many proteins share these traits","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"If adding the protein back fixes the disease, lacking it caused the disease."},"exp":"**Answer:** able to relieve Disease K symptoms.\n\n**Incorrect: encoded by a gene that contains introns.** Most human genes have introns.\n\n**Incorrect: a soluble blood protein.** Many blood proteins are soluble.\n\n**Correct: able to relieve Disease K symptoms.** Giving back Enzyme M eases the disease, so its lack is the likely cause.\n\n**Incorrect: made from a gene on the X chromosome.** The X carries many genes.","how":{"ask":"Which fact links missing Enzyme M to the disease?","know":"Rescue by replacing a factor shows that factor's lack causes the problem.","steps":[{"text":"Find the rescue result.","hl":"weekly infusions of purified Enzyme M ease their symptoms"},{"text":"Introns and solubility are shared by many proteins.","strike":["encoded by a gene that contains introns.","a soluble blood protein."]},{"text":"X location alone does not show cause.","strike":["made from a gene on the X chromosome."]},{"text":"Answer: able to relieve Disease K symptoms."}],"trap":"made from a gene on the X chromosome. fits the inheritance, but does not tie the disease to this protein."},"teach":[{"text":"Rescue is strong evidence: put it back, the problem goes away.","marks":[{"t":"ease their symptoms","note":"rescue"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rescue by adding a factor supports that its lack causes the disease."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-125a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"mrna splicing","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:125#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:125"],"pid":"st-bb:P3","passage":"Disease K is caused by too little working Enzyme M, a soluble protein made in muscle cells and released into the blood. Patients tire quickly, and weekly infusions of purified Enzyme M ease their symptoms. The Enzyme M gene is 90,000 nucleotides long, yet the protein has only 1100 amino acids, because introns interrupt the gene. Cells copy the whole gene into RNA, then cut out the introns and join the remaining pieces. The Enzyme M gene lies on the X chromosome, and the disease allele is recessive.","q":"The cutting of introns and the splicing linked to expression of the Enzyme M gene occur during which of the following steps in the protein-synthesis process?","choices":["DNA → tRNA","DNA → mRNA","mRNA → tRNA","mRNA → protein"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Splicing finishes mRNA, before translation","c":[3]},{"f":"Protein genes aren't copied into tRNA","c":[0,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Splicing turns the first RNA copy into mature mRNA, so it sits in DNA → mRNA."},"exp":"**Answer:** DNA → mRNA\n\n**Incorrect: DNA → tRNA** A protein gene is not copied into tRNA.\n\n**Correct: DNA → mRNA** The gene is transcribed, then introns are cut out to make mature mRNA.\n\n**Incorrect: mRNA → tRNA** mRNA is never turned into tRNA.\n\n**Incorrect: mRNA → protein** Translation uses already-spliced mRNA.","how":{"ask":"When are introns removed?","know":"Splicing is processing between transcription and translation.","steps":[{"text":"Introns are cut after the gene is copied into RNA.","hl":"Cells copy the whole gene into RNA, then cut out the introns"},{"text":"Protein genes do not become tRNA.","strike":["DNA → tRNA","mRNA → tRNA"]},{"text":"Translation reads finished mRNA.","strike":["mRNA → protein"]},{"text":"Answer: DNA → mRNA."}],"trap":"mRNA → protein tempts because splicing affects the protein, but it happens before translation."},"teach":[{"text":"Order: transcribe, splice, then translate.","marks":[{"t":"cut out the introns and join the remaining pieces","note":"splicing"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Splicing is part of making mRNA from DNA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-126a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"mrna splicing","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:126#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:126"],"pid":"st-bb:P3","passage":"Disease K is caused by too little working Enzyme M, a soluble protein made in muscle cells and released into the blood. Patients tire quickly, and weekly infusions of purified Enzyme M ease their symptoms. The Enzyme M gene is 90,000 nucleotides long, yet the protein has only 1100 amino acids, because introns interrupt the gene. Cells copy the whole gene into RNA, then cut out the introns and join the remaining pieces. The Enzyme M gene lies on the X chromosome, and the disease allele is recessive.","q":"Recombinant DNA methods let scientists mass-produce some human proteins by putting human genes into bacteria. The trait of bacteria that would most likely keep Enzyme M from being made this way is that bacteria:","choices":["lack a way to splice out introns.","are too small to hold the Enzyme M gene.","lack a membrane-bound nucleus.","have no copy of the human X chromosome."],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Bacteria transcribe without nuclei","c":[2]},{"f":"Only the gene is moved","c":[1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gene has introns and bacteria cannot splice, so the protein comes out wrong."},"exp":"**Answer:** lack a way to splice out introns.\n\n**Correct: lack a way to splice out introns.** The gene is full of introns, and bacteria cannot remove them, so the protein would be wrong.\n\n**Incorrect: are too small to hold the Enzyme M gene.** Size of the cell does not stop gene transfer.\n\n**Incorrect: lack a membrane-bound nucleus.** Bacteria transcribe genes fine without a nucleus.\n\n**Incorrect: have no copy of the human X chromosome.** Only the gene is moved, not the chromosome.","how":{"ask":"What do bacteria lack that this gene needs?","know":"Bacteria have no spliceosome.","steps":[{"text":"The gene is broken up by introns.","hl":"because introns interrupt the gene"},{"text":"Bacteria transcribe without a nucleus.","strike":["lack a membrane-bound nucleus."]},{"text":"Only the gene is transferred, not a chromosome.","strike":["are too small to hold the Enzyme M gene.","have no copy of the human X chromosome."]},{"text":"Answer: lack a way to splice out introns."}],"trap":"lack a membrane-bound nucleus. is true of bacteria, but it does not stop gene expression."},"teach":[{"text":"Bacteria cannot splice. That is why scientists insert cDNA, which has no introns.","marks":[{"t":"introns interrupt the gene","note":"needs splicing"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Human genes with introns fail in bacteria unless given as cDNA."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-127a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"le châtelier shift","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:127#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:127"],"q":"A weak acid, HB, only partly dissociates in pure water. When dissolved in blood, however, HB fully dissociates. What factor can be used to explain this discrepancy?","choices":["In blood, the reaction is coupled to GTP hydrolysis to make it more favorable.","In blood, enzymes are used to catalyze the dissociation reaction.","In blood, the concentration of H+(aq) is kept low by other equilibria.","In blood, the higher salt content makes the solvent more polar than water."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Acid dissociation needs no GTP","c":[0]},{"f":"Catalysts don't shift equilibrium","c":[1]},{"f":"Buffers, not salt, remove H+","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Remove a product (H+) and the equilibrium shifts right until the acid fully dissociates."},"exp":"**Answer:** In blood, the concentration of H+(aq) is kept low by other equilibria.\n\n**Incorrect: In blood, the reaction is coupled to GTP hydrolysis to make it more favorable.** Acid dissociation is not coupled to GTP.\n\n**Incorrect: In blood, enzymes are used to catalyze the dissociation reaction.** Catalysts speed a reaction but do not change where equilibrium lies.\n\n**Correct: In blood, the concentration of H+(aq) is kept low by other equilibria.** Buffers hold blood pH near 7.4, above the acid's pKa, so H+ is pulled away and HB keeps dissociating.\n\n**Incorrect: In blood, the higher salt content makes the solvent more polar than water.** Salt has only a small effect; the buffered pH is what matters.","how":{"ask":"Why does the same acid dissociate more in blood?","know":"Pulling away a product shifts equilibrium toward products.","steps":[{"text":"HB ⇌ H+ + B–; low H+ pulls it right."},{"text":"No energy coupling exists for this step.","strike":["In blood, the reaction is coupled to GTP hydrolysis to make it more favorable."]},{"text":"Catalysts never move equilibrium.","strike":["In blood, enzymes are used to catalyze the dissociation reaction."]},{"text":"Salt is not the main driver.","strike":["In blood, the higher salt content makes the solvent more polar than water."]},{"text":"Answer: H+ is kept low by other equilibria."}],"trap":"The enzyme choice tempts, but a catalyst only changes speed, never the final ratio."},"teach":[{"text":"Blood pH 7.4 is above most organic acid pKa values, so they sit ionized."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** When pH > pKa, the acid is mostly dissociated."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-128a","s":"Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"citric acid cycle steps","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:128#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:128"],"q":"In oxidative phosphorylation, ubiquinone (coenzyme Q) acts as:","choices":["a 1-electron carrier.","a 2-electron carrier.","a 3-electron carrier.","a 4-electron carrier."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Q to QH2 takes 2 e–","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Only O2 takes 4 e–","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Q picks up 2 electrons and 2 H+ to become QH2."},"exp":"**Answer:** a 2-electron carrier.\n\n**Incorrect: a 1-electron carrier.** That is cytochrome c, with one iron that flips Fe3+/Fe2+.\n\n**Correct: a 2-electron carrier.** Ubiquinone is reduced to ubiquinol (QH2) by 2 electrons and 2 protons.\n\n**Incorrect: a 3-electron carrier.** No chain carrier moves 3 electrons.\n\n**Incorrect: a 4-electron carrier.** Four electrons go to O2 at Complex IV, not Q.","how":{"ask":"How many electrons does Q carry?","know":"Q + 2 e– + 2 H+ → QH2.","steps":[{"text":"Write Q's reduction: Q → QH2."},{"text":"One electron is cytochrome c; three is never used.","strike":["a 1-electron carrier.","a 3-electron carrier."]},{"text":"Four electrons reduce O2, not Q.","strike":["a 4-electron carrier."]},{"text":"Answer: a 2-electron carrier."}],"trap":"a 1-electron carrier. tempts because Q passes a single electron at a time in the Q cycle, but it carries two in total."},"teach":[{"text":"Cytochrome c carries 1 electron. NADH, FADH2 and Q carry 2. O2 takes 4."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Count electrons by the reduced form: QH2 holds 2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-129a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:129#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:129"],"q":"Which primer is most suitable for PCR?","choices":["5–ATTAGCTTAACATTAAT–3","5–GCTCAGGACTTGCAGCC–3","5–CGTATAATTATAAATTA–3","5–TATTAAGCTTATAATCG–3"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"A/T at both ends fails","c":[0]},{"f":"Low GC, one weak end","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the primer with the most G/C and a G or C at both ends."},"exp":"**Answer:** 5–GCTCAGGACTTGCAGCC–3\n\n**Incorrect: 5–ATTAGCTTAACATTAAT–3** Very low GC and A/T at both ends, so it binds weakly.\n\n**Correct: 5–GCTCAGGACTTGCAGCC–3** About 65% GC with G/C at both ends, so it binds firmly and starts copying well.\n\n**Incorrect: 5–CGTATAATTATAAATTA–3** Only the 5′ end is G/C, and GC content is very low.\n\n**Incorrect: 5–TATTAAGCTTATAATCG–3** Only the 3′ end is G/C, and GC content is low.","how":{"ask":"Which primer binds template best?","know":"G–C pairs have 3 H-bonds; good primers are about 40–60%+ GC with G/C ends.","steps":[{"text":"Count G and C in each primer."},{"text":"All A/T ends and very low GC.","strike":["5–ATTAGCTTAACATTAAT–3"]},{"text":"Low GC with only one G/C end.","strike":["5–CGTATAATTATAAATTA–3","5–TATTAAGCTTATAATCG–3"]},{"text":"Answer: 5–GCTCAGGACTTGCAGCC–3."}],"trap":"5–TATTAAGCTTATAATCG–3 tempts because its 3′ end is CG, but its overall GC is too low."},"teach":[{"text":"More G/C means a higher melting point and steadier binding."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Best primer = highest GC plus a G/C clamp at the ends."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-130a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"mitosis, meiosis and nondisjunction","freq":4,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:130#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:130"],"q":"In which phase of meiosis II does nondisjunction of sister chromatids occur?","choices":["Metaphase I","Prophase II","Anaphase II","Telophase I"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Stem asks about meiosis II","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Separation happens in anaphase","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Nondisjunction is a failure to separate, and separating happens in anaphase."},"exp":"**Answer:** Anaphase II\n\n**Incorrect: Metaphase I** This is meiosis I, and chromosomes only line up then.\n\n**Incorrect: Prophase II** Chromosomes condense here; nothing separates.\n\n**Correct: Anaphase II** Sister chromatids pull apart in anaphase II, so a failure to split happens there.\n\n**Incorrect: Telophase I** This is meiosis I, and separation is already done.","how":{"ask":"When do sister chromatids separate in meiosis II?","know":"Anaphase = separation phase.","steps":[{"text":"Nondisjunction means failing to separate."},{"text":"Meiosis I phases are off-topic.","strike":["Metaphase I","Telophase I"]},{"text":"Prophase only condenses chromosomes.","strike":["Prophase II"]},{"text":"Answer: Anaphase II."}],"trap":"Metaphase tempts because chromosomes line up there, but lining up is not separating."},"teach":[{"text":"Anaphase I splits homologs. Anaphase II splits sister chromatids."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Nondisjunction always happens in an anaphase."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-131a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:131#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:131"],"pid":"st-bb:P4","passage":"Enzyme RmtB adds a sulfur to y9A, a modified base beside the UUU anticodon of lysine tRNA. The amino acid part of y9A has a side chain of one CH2 joined to a benzene ring that carries an OH group. A reporter enzyme works only if its Lys88 codon, written as AAA or AAG, is read correctly. Reporter activity was 100% for AAA and 92% for AAG in wild-type cells, but 60% for AAA and 25% for AAG in rmtB mutants. Misreading the Lys codon at the proinsulin cut site puts in a wrong residue, so proinsulin folds and is cut abnormally. Rmtb-null mice had 40% less C-peptide and 30% less proinsulin in beta cells than wild-type mice.","q":"The modified base y9A is a derivative of which amino acid?","choices":["Threonine","Phenylalanine","Tyrosine","Serine"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Phe ring has no OH","c":[1]},{"f":"Thr and Ser lack rings","c":[0,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"CH2 plus a ring with OH (a phenol) is tyrosine."},"exp":"**Answer:** Tyrosine\n\n**Incorrect: Threonine** Its side chain is CH(OH)CH3, with no ring.\n\n**Incorrect: Phenylalanine** Its ring has no OH group.\n\n**Correct: Tyrosine** Tyrosine's side chain is CH2 bonded to a phenol ring, an aromatic ring with OH.\n\n**Incorrect: Serine** Its side chain is CH2OH, with no ring.","how":{"ask":"Which amino acid has a CH2-phenol side chain?","know":"Tyr = Phe + OH on the ring.","steps":[{"text":"Read the side chain.","hl":"benzene ring that carries an OH group"},{"text":"No ring means not Thr or Ser.","strike":["Threonine","Serine"]},{"text":"A ring without OH is Phe.","strike":["Phenylalanine"]},{"text":"Answer: Tyrosine."}],"trap":"Phenylalanine tempts because it has the same ring, but it lacks the OH."},"teach":[{"text":"Aromatic trio: Phe (ring), Tyr (ring + OH), Trp (two rings, one with N).","marks":[{"t":"carries an OH group","note":"phenol = Tyr"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Identify the amino acid by its side-chain groups."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-132a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:132#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:132"],"pid":"st-bb:P4","passage":"Enzyme RmtB adds a sulfur to y9A, a modified base beside the UUU anticodon of lysine tRNA. The amino acid part of y9A has a side chain of one CH2 joined to a benzene ring that carries an OH group. A reporter enzyme works only if its Lys88 codon, written as AAA or AAG, is read correctly. Reporter activity was 100% for AAA and 92% for AAG in wild-type cells, but 60% for AAA and 25% for AAG in rmtB mutants. Misreading the Lys codon at the proinsulin cut site puts in a wrong residue, so proinsulin folds and is cut abnormally. Rmtb-null mice had 40% less C-peptide and 30% less proinsulin in beta cells than wild-type mice.","q":"In the reporter assay, which combination of Lys88 codon (AAA or AAG) and genetic background (wild-type or rmtB mutant) resulted in the highest level of mistranslation of the codon?","choices":["AAA codon in the wild-type background","AAG codon in the rmtB mutant background","AAG codon in the wild-type background","AAA codon in the rmtB mutant background"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Wild type reads near 100%","c":[0,2]},{"f":"60% beats 25%","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Lowest reporter activity means the most misreading."},"exp":"**Answer:** AAG codon in the rmtB mutant background\n\n**Incorrect: AAA codon in the wild-type background** 100% activity means almost no misreading.\n\n**Correct: AAG codon in the rmtB mutant background** At 25%, this is the lowest activity, so the codon is misread most.\n\n**Incorrect: AAG codon in the wild-type background** 92% is near normal.\n\n**Incorrect: AAA codon in the rmtB mutant background** 60% is low, but higher than 25%.","how":{"ask":"Which condition has the lowest reporter activity?","know":"Activity needs correct Lys, so lower activity means more errors.","steps":[{"text":"Activity drops when Lys88 is misread.","hl":"works only if its Lys88 codon"},{"text":"Wild-type values are near 100%.","hl":"100% for AAA and 92% for AAG in wild-type cells","strike":["AAA codon in the wild-type background","AAG codon in the wild-type background"]},{"text":"Compare mutants: 60% vs 25%.","hl":"60% for AAA and 25% for AAG in rmtB mutants","strike":["AAA codon in the rmtB mutant background"]},{"text":"Answer: AAG codon in the rmtB mutant background."}],"trap":"Picking the highest number is the trap; high activity means accurate reading."},"teach":[{"text":"Flip the readout: low activity = high mistranslation.","marks":[{"t":"25% for AAG","note":"lowest = most errors"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** When a signal needs correct reading, the lowest signal marks the most errors."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-133a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biology","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:133#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:133"],"pid":"st-bb:P4","passage":"Enzyme RmtB adds a sulfur to y9A, a modified base beside the UUU anticodon of lysine tRNA. The amino acid part of y9A has a side chain of one CH2 joined to a benzene ring that carries an OH group. A reporter enzyme works only if its Lys88 codon, written as AAA or AAG, is read correctly. Reporter activity was 100% for AAA and 92% for AAG in wild-type cells, but 60% for AAA and 25% for AAG in rmtB mutants. Misreading the Lys codon at the proinsulin cut site puts in a wrong residue, so proinsulin folds and is cut abnormally. Rmtb-null mice had 40% less C-peptide and 30% less proinsulin in beta cells than wild-type mice.","q":"Based on the passage, RmtB deficiency most likely causes which of the following to increase in mouse beta cells?","choices":["Synthesis of proinsulin","Misfolding of proinsulin","Cutting of proinsulin","Ratio of normal to variant proinsulin"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Proinsulin fell 30%","c":[0]},{"f":"Less C-peptide, less cutting","c":[2]},{"f":"More misreads, more variants","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"No RmtB means Lys is misread, and misread proinsulin misfolds."},"exp":"**Answer:** Misfolding of proinsulin\n\n**Incorrect: Synthesis of proinsulin** Proinsulin dropped by 30%.\n\n**Correct: Misfolding of proinsulin** Without RmtB the Lys codon is misread, and the wrong residue makes proinsulin fold abnormally.\n\n**Incorrect: Cutting of proinsulin** Less C-peptide means less cutting.\n\n**Incorrect: Ratio of normal to variant proinsulin** More misreading makes more variants, so this ratio falls.","how":{"ask":"What rises when RmtB is missing?","know":"Mistranslation gives wrong residues, which misfold.","steps":[{"text":"Find what misreading does.","hl":"folds and is cut abnormally"},{"text":"Both proinsulin and C-peptide fell.","hl":"40% less C-peptide and 30% less proinsulin","strike":["Synthesis of proinsulin","Cutting of proinsulin"]},{"text":"More variants lower the normal-to-variant ratio.","strike":["Ratio of normal to variant proinsulin"]},{"text":"Answer: Misfolding of proinsulin."}],"trap":"Cutting of proinsulin tempts because the cut site is mentioned, but the data show less cutting."},"teach":[{"text":"Chain it: no RmtB → misread Lys → wrong residue → misfolding.","marks":[{"t":"puts in a wrong residue","note":"mistranslation"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Check each choice's direction against the data before trusting the mechanism."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-134a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"fed vs fasting metabolism","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:134#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:134"],"pid":"st-bb:P4","passage":"Enzyme RmtB adds a sulfur to y9A, a modified base beside the UUU anticodon of lysine tRNA. The amino acid part of y9A has a side chain of one CH2 joined to a benzene ring that carries an OH group. A reporter enzyme works only if its Lys88 codon, written as AAA or AAG, is read correctly. Reporter activity was 100% for AAA and 92% for AAG in wild-type cells, but 60% for AAA and 25% for AAG in rmtB mutants. Misreading the Lys codon at the proinsulin cut site puts in a wrong residue, so proinsulin folds and is cut abnormally. Rmtb-null mice had 40% less C-peptide and 30% less proinsulin in beta cells than wild-type mice.","q":"Based on the passage, the level of which of the following is most likely lower in fed wild-type mice than in fed Rmtb-null mice?","choices":["Cellular glucose uptake","Blood glucose levels","Liver glycogen synthesis","Muscle protein synthesis"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Insulin raises glucose uptake","c":[0]},{"f":"Insulin builds glycogen and protein","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Wild type has more insulin, and more insulin means lower blood glucose."},"exp":"**Answer:** Blood glucose levels\n\n**Incorrect: Cellular glucose uptake** More insulin in wild type means more uptake, not less.\n\n**Correct: Blood glucose levels** Wild-type mice make more insulin (more C-peptide), so they clear glucose from blood faster.\n\n**Incorrect: Liver glycogen synthesis** Insulin drives glycogen storage, so wild type makes more.\n\n**Incorrect: Muscle protein synthesis** Insulin promotes protein synthesis, so wild type is higher.","how":{"ask":"Which value goes down when insulin is higher?","know":"Insulin lowers blood glucose and raises uptake, glycogen and protein synthesis.","steps":[{"text":"Mutants make less insulin.","hl":"40% less C-peptide"},{"text":"Insulin raises uptake.","strike":["Cellular glucose uptake"]},{"text":"Insulin raises glycogen and protein building.","strike":["Liver glycogen synthesis","Muscle protein synthesis"]},{"text":"Answer: Blood glucose levels."}],"trap":"Reading the comparison backward is the trap: the stem asks what is lower in wild type."},"teach":[{"text":"C-peptide is cut out 1:1 with insulin, so it tracks insulin made.","marks":[{"t":"C-peptide","note":"insulin marker"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More insulin: blood glucose down, storage and uptake up."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-135a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"km, kd and rate terms","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:135#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:135"],"pid":"st-bb:P5","passage":"Some sea larvae absorb dissolved amino acids, such as L-serine, straight across their body wall. This uptake may help them live when food is scarce, as may slow use of stored energy. Scientists measured L-serine transport in larvae of a warm-water urchin (Species W, 27°C) and a cold-water urchin (Species C, –1°C). Species W had a Kt of 2 µM and a Jmax of 50 pmol per larva per hour; Species C had a Kt of 1 µM and a Jmax of 15. Species W tissue was 60% protein and 10% lipid, while Species C tissue was 40% protein and 35% lipid.","q":"Which feature of the kinetics of L-serine transport would provide evidence that dissolved amino acids are an important nutrient source under low-food conditions?","choices":["Low transport capacity (Jmax) of L-serine","High-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine","High transport capacity (Jmax) of L-serine","Low-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Jmax matters only at high [S]","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Scarce food needs tight binding","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Low food means low substrate, and only a high-affinity (low Kt) carrier grabs it."},"exp":"**Answer:** High-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine\n\n**Incorrect: Low transport capacity (Jmax) of L-serine** Jmax is the rate at saturating levels, not at scarce levels.\n\n**Correct: High-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine** When amino acids are scarce, a carrier with low Kt still binds and moves them.\n\n**Incorrect: High transport capacity (Jmax) of L-serine** High Jmax only helps when substrate is plentiful.\n\n**Incorrect: Low-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine** A weak-binding carrier misses scarce substrate.","how":{"ask":"Which kinetic trait helps at low substrate?","know":"Low Kt = high affinity = works at low concentration.","steps":[{"text":"The setting is scarce food.","hl":"when food is scarce"},{"text":"Jmax describes saturated, not scarce, conditions.","strike":["Low transport capacity (Jmax) of L-serine","High transport capacity (Jmax) of L-serine"]},{"text":"Low affinity fails at low levels.","strike":["Low-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine"]},{"text":"Answer: High-affinity transport (Kt) of L-serine."}],"trap":"High transport capacity sounds good, but capacity matters only when substrate is plentiful."},"teach":[{"text":"Kt (like Km) tells how well a carrier works at low substrate.","marks":[{"t":"Kt of 1 µM","note":"low Kt = high affinity"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Scarce substrate favors high affinity (low Kt), not high Jmax."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-136a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"km, kd and rate terms","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:136#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:136"],"pid":"st-bb:P5","passage":"Some sea larvae absorb dissolved amino acids, such as L-serine, straight across their body wall. This uptake may help them live when food is scarce, as may slow use of stored energy. Scientists measured L-serine transport in larvae of a warm-water urchin (Species W, 27°C) and a cold-water urchin (Species C, –1°C). Species W had a Kt of 2 µM and a Jmax of 50 pmol per larva per hour; Species C had a Kt of 1 µM and a Jmax of 15. Species W tissue was 60% protein and 10% lipid, while Species C tissue was 40% protein and 35% lipid.","q":"If the concentration of amino acid transport protein is increased, the transport affinity Kt of L-serine will:","choices":["increase.","level off quickly.","soon reach its maximum.","not change."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Kt belongs to each carrier","c":[0]},{"f":"More carriers raise Jmax only","c":[1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Adding more of the same carrier raises Jmax but leaves Kt alone."},"exp":"**Answer:** not change.\n\n**Incorrect: increase.** Affinity is a trait of each carrier, not of how many there are.\n\n**Incorrect: level off quickly.** Kt is a fixed constant, not a curve that levels off.\n\n**Incorrect: soon reach its maximum.** That describes rate approaching Jmax, not Kt.\n\n**Correct: not change.** Each carrier binds serine the same way, so Kt stays the same; only Jmax rises.","how":{"ask":"Does carrier amount change Kt?","know":"Carrier amount sets Jmax; carrier structure sets Kt.","steps":[{"text":"Kt is a per-carrier binding trait.","hl":"Kt of 2 µM"},{"text":"Kt does not depend on carrier number.","strike":["increase."]},{"text":"Leveling off and maximums describe rate, not Kt.","strike":["level off quickly.","soon reach its maximum."]},{"text":"Answer: not change."}],"trap":"increase. tempts because more carriers move more serine, but that is Jmax, not Kt."},"teach":[{"text":"Like enzymes: more enzyme raises Vmax; Km stays put.","marks":[{"t":"Jmax of 50 pmol per larva per hour","note":"scales with carriers"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More carrier or enzyme changes Jmax/Vmax, never Kt/Km."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-137a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"km, kd and rate terms","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:137#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:137"],"pid":"st-bb:P5","passage":"Some sea larvae absorb dissolved amino acids, such as L-serine, straight across their body wall. This uptake may help them live when food is scarce, as may slow use of stored energy. Scientists measured L-serine transport in larvae of a warm-water urchin (Species W, 27°C) and a cold-water urchin (Species C, –1°C). Species W had a Kt of 2 µM and a Jmax of 50 pmol per larva per hour; Species C had a Kt of 1 µM and a Jmax of 15. Species W tissue was 60% protein and 10% lipid, while Species C tissue was 40% protein and 35% lipid.","q":"Assuming the L-serine transporter in Species W follows standard Michaelis–Menten kinetics, Kt is equal to:","choices":["half the maximal transport capacity.","the transport capacity at twice the substrate concentration.","the substrate concentration at one-half the maximal transport capacity.","the substrate concentration at one-quarter the overall transport rate."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Kt is a concentration","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Defined at half Jmax","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Kt is like Km: the substrate level that gives half of Jmax."},"exp":"**Answer:** the substrate concentration at one-half the maximal transport capacity.\n\n**Incorrect: half the maximal transport capacity.** That is a rate, not a concentration.\n\n**Incorrect: the transport capacity at twice the substrate concentration.** Kt is a concentration, not a capacity.\n\n**Correct: the substrate concentration at one-half the maximal transport capacity.** Kt plays the role of Km, the substrate level giving half-maximal rate.\n\n**Incorrect: the substrate concentration at one-quarter the overall transport rate.** The definition uses one-half, not one-quarter.","how":{"ask":"What is Kt in Michaelis–Menten terms?","know":"Km = [S] at half Vmax.","steps":[{"text":"Kt is given in µM, a concentration.","hl":"Kt of 2 µM"},{"text":"Choices that are rates or capacities are wrong.","strike":["half the maximal transport capacity.","the transport capacity at twice the substrate concentration."]},{"text":"The fraction must be one-half.","strike":["the substrate concentration at one-quarter the overall transport rate."]},{"text":"Answer: substrate concentration at half Jmax."}],"trap":"half the maximal transport capacity. mixes up the y-value (rate) with the x-value (Kt)."},"teach":[{"text":"Check units: Kt is in µM, so the answer must be a concentration.","marks":[{"t":"Kt of 2 µM","note":"concentration units"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Km or Kt = substrate concentration at half Vmax or Jmax."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-138a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:138#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:138"],"pid":"st-bb:P5","passage":"Some sea larvae absorb dissolved amino acids, such as L-serine, straight across their body wall. This uptake may help them live when food is scarce, as may slow use of stored energy. Scientists measured L-serine transport in larvae of a warm-water urchin (Species W, 27°C) and a cold-water urchin (Species C, –1°C). Species W had a Kt of 2 µM and a Jmax of 50 pmol per larva per hour; Species C had a Kt of 1 µM and a Jmax of 15. Species W tissue was 60% protein and 10% lipid, while Species C tissue was 40% protein and 35% lipid.","q":"A separate group of scientists repeated the transport experiments described in the passage. All methods were the same with one major exception: D-serine was used as the main amino acid substrate. What effect will this change have on the synthesis of new proteins in the larvae studied? Protein synthesis will:","choices":["decrease by one-third.","be inhibited.","increase three-fold.","not change."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Ribosomes use only L-amino acids","c":[2,3]},{"f":"No fixed fraction applies","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ribosomes build proteins only from L-amino acids, so D-serine cannot be used."},"exp":"**Answer:** be inhibited.\n\n**Incorrect: decrease by one-third.** No data support any exact fraction.\n\n**Correct: be inhibited.** tRNAs and ribosomes accept only L-amino acids, so D-serine cannot feed new proteins.\n\n**Incorrect: increase three-fold.** A D-amino acid cannot boost translation.\n\n**Incorrect: not change.** Swapping L for D removes the usable building block.","how":{"ask":"Can D-serine be built into protein?","know":"Proteins use L-amino acids only.","steps":[{"text":"The study used the L form.","hl":"such as L-serine"},{"text":"D forms cannot help or leave translation the same.","strike":["increase three-fold.","not change."]},{"text":"Exact fractions are made up.","strike":["decrease by one-third."]},{"text":"Answer: be inhibited."}],"trap":"not change. tempts if you forget that L and D are different molecules to the ribosome."},"teach":[{"text":"Life's proteins are built from L-amino acids; D forms are rejected by tRNA charging.","marks":[{"t":"L-serine","note":"usable L form"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Swap an L-amino acid for its D form and translation can't use it."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-139a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:139#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:139"],"pid":"st-bb:P5","passage":"Some sea larvae absorb dissolved amino acids, such as L-serine, straight across their body wall. This uptake may help them live when food is scarce, as may slow use of stored energy. Scientists measured L-serine transport in larvae of a warm-water urchin (Species W, 27°C) and a cold-water urchin (Species C, –1°C). Species W had a Kt of 2 µM and a Jmax of 50 pmol per larva per hour; Species C had a Kt of 1 µM and a Jmax of 15. Species W tissue was 60% protein and 10% lipid, while Species C tissue was 40% protein and 35% lipid.","q":"Which information about the larvae of each species, when combined with the tissue data in the passage, would help scientists predict which species of urchin is best suited to withstand long periods of nutrient deprivation?","choices":["Ambient water temperature","Average body length","Average metabolic rate","Hours of daily light"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Stores plus burn rate predict survival","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Survival time = energy stored ÷ how fast it is burned, so you need metabolic rate."},"exp":"**Answer:** Average metabolic rate\n\n**Incorrect: Ambient water temperature** Temperature is outside the larva and does not give its burn rate.\n\n**Incorrect: Average body length** Size alone does not tell how fast stores are used.\n\n**Correct: Average metabolic rate** The tissue data give energy stores; metabolic rate tells how fast they are spent, which predicts how long larvae last.\n\n**Incorrect: Hours of daily light** Light has no direct link to stored energy use.","how":{"ask":"What pairs with energy stores to predict starvation time?","know":"Time lasted = stores ÷ rate of use.","steps":[{"text":"Tissue data give the stores.","hl":"40% protein and 35% lipid"},{"text":"Slow energy use also helps survival.","hl":"slow use of stored energy"},{"text":"Temperature, length and light do not give use rate.","strike":["Ambient water temperature","Average body length","Hours of daily light"]},{"text":"Answer: Average metabolic rate."}],"trap":"Ambient water temperature tempts because it affects metabolism, but metabolic rate itself is the direct measure."},"teach":[{"text":"Like a fuel tank: size of tank plus burn rate gives time.","marks":[{"t":"slow use of stored energy","note":"burn rate matters"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To predict starvation survival, pair energy stores with metabolic rate."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-140a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biology","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"atp yield and anaerobic pathways","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:140#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:140"],"q":"E. coli cells can grow with or without O2. If the same concentration of glucose were used to grow two different E. coli cultures, would the growth rate be faster with or without O2?","choices":["Without O2, because the final product (lactate) would hold more energy than the final product of respiration (H2O)","Without O2, because the cells would not have to make the proteins of the electron transport chain","With O2, because far more ATP would be made to supply energy for cell division","With O2, because fermentation cannot regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Energy left in lactate is wasted","c":[0]},{"f":"Saved proteins can't offset ATP","c":[1]},{"f":"Fermentation exists to regenerate NAD+","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Respiration gives about 30 ATP per glucose versus 2 for fermentation, so cells grow faster with O2."},"exp":"**Answer:** With O2, because far more ATP would be made to supply energy for cell division\n\n**Incorrect: Without O2, because the final product (lactate) would hold more energy than the final product of respiration (H2O)** Energy left in lactate is not available to the cell.\n\n**Incorrect: Without O2, because the cells would not have to make the proteins of the electron transport chain** The small saving cannot make up for far less ATP.\n\n**Correct: With O2, because far more ATP would be made to supply energy for cell division** Aerobic respiration yields about 30 ATP per glucose; fermentation yields 2.\n\n**Incorrect: With O2, because fermentation cannot regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis** Regenerating NAD+ is exactly what fermentation does.","how":{"ask":"Which condition gives more usable energy per glucose?","know":"Aerobic ≈ 30 ATP; fermentation = 2 ATP.","steps":[{"text":"Compare ATP per glucose."},{"text":"Energy stuck in lactate does not help growth.","strike":["Without O2, because the final product (lactate) would hold more energy than the final product of respiration (H2O)"]},{"text":"Saving a few proteins cannot beat a 15-fold ATP gap.","strike":["Without O2, because the cells would not have to make the proteins of the electron transport chain"]},{"text":"Fermentation does regenerate NAD+.","strike":["With O2, because fermentation cannot regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis"]},{"text":"Answer: With O2, because far more ATP is made."}],"trap":"The lactate choice states a true fact (lactate holds energy) but that energy is not usable."},"teach":[{"text":"More ATP per glucose means more growth from the same food."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Same glucose, more ATP with O2, so faster growth with O2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-141a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:141#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:141"],"q":"A yeast culture was moved from glucose to galactose. The cell count stayed flat for 2 hours, then rose. Which of the following most likely explains why the culture did NOT grow immediately after the galactose was added?","choices":["The galactose directly blocked budding.","The yeast cells unable to use galactose died.","The enzymes for using galactose had to be transcribed and translated.","The breakdown of fatty acids is a slow process."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Flat curve: no death, no block","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Galactose is a sugar, not fat","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A flat lag then growth means cells are making new enzymes for the new sugar."},"exp":"**Answer:** The enzymes for using galactose had to be transcribed and translated.\n\n**Incorrect: The galactose directly blocked budding.** Growth resumed, so budding was not blocked.\n\n**Incorrect: The yeast cells unable to use galactose died.** Death would make the count fall, not stay flat.\n\n**Correct: The enzymes for using galactose had to be transcribed and translated.** Cells first switch on and build the galactose enzymes, then grow.\n\n**Incorrect: The breakdown of fatty acids is a slow process.** Galactose is a sugar, not a fatty acid.","how":{"ask":"Why the pause before growth?","know":"New food needs new enzymes; making them takes time.","steps":[{"text":"The count stayed flat, then rose."},{"text":"No drop means no death; later growth means no block.","strike":["The galactose directly blocked budding.","The yeast cells unable to use galactose died."]},{"text":"Fatty acids are not the food here.","strike":["The breakdown of fatty acids is a slow process."]},{"text":"Answer: the enzymes had to be made."}],"trap":"The death choice tempts, but death would make the curve fall, not stay flat."},"teach":[{"text":"Lag = induction time: transcription plus translation of new enzymes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A flat lag after a food switch means new genes are being turned on."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-142a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"gene expression regulation","freq":10,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:142#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:142"],"q":"If the actin gene is continuously expressed, where is it most likely found?","choices":["In heterochromatin","In a centromere","In euchromatin","In a Barr body"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Packed chromatin is silent","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Centromeres hold few active genes","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Always-on genes sit in loose, open euchromatin."},"exp":"**Answer:** In euchromatin\n\n**Incorrect: In heterochromatin** Tightly packed DNA is mostly silent.\n\n**Incorrect: In a centromere** Centromeric DNA is packed and holds few active genes.\n\n**Correct: In euchromatin** Loose euchromatin lets RNA polymerase reach genes, so constantly used genes live there.\n\n**Incorrect: In a Barr body** A Barr body is a silenced, condensed X chromosome.","how":{"ask":"Where are always-on genes?","know":"Open chromatin = transcribed; packed chromatin = silent.","steps":[{"text":"Constant expression needs open DNA."},{"text":"Heterochromatin and Barr bodies are packed and silent.","strike":["In heterochromatin","In a Barr body"]},{"text":"Centromeres are packed structural DNA.","strike":["In a centromere"]},{"text":"Answer: In euchromatin."}],"trap":"In a Barr body tempts if you forget it is an inactivated X."},"teach":[{"text":"Eu = true/open, active. Hetero = packed, quiet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Housekeeping genes sit in euchromatin."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-143a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"amino acid side-chain charge","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:143#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:143"],"q":"Which amino acid is neutral, but zwitterionic at pH 7, despite possessing two nitrogen atoms in its formula?","choices":["Lys","Arg","Asn","Phe"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Lys, Arg are positive at 7","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Phe has one nitrogen","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"An amide side chain (Asn, Gln) adds a nitrogen but no charge."},"exp":"**Answer:** Asn\n\n**Incorrect: Lys** Its side-chain amine is protonated at pH 7, so Lys is positive.\n\n**Incorrect: Arg** Its guanidino group is protonated at pH 7, so Arg is positive.\n\n**Correct: Asn** Its amide side chain has a second nitrogen but is neither acidic nor basic, so the molecule is a neutral zwitterion.\n\n**Incorrect: Phe** Phe has only one nitrogen.","how":{"ask":"Which has 2 N but no side-chain charge?","know":"Amides are not basic; amines and guanidines are.","steps":[{"text":"Find choices with two or more N."},{"text":"Lys and Arg have basic side chains and are charged.","strike":["Lys","Arg"]},{"text":"Phe has only the backbone N.","strike":["Phe"]},{"text":"Answer: Asn."}],"trap":"Lys tempts because it has exactly two N, but its side chain carries a + charge."},"teach":[{"text":"Amide N (Asn, Gln) is neutral. Amine N (Lys) and guanidine N (Arg) are +1 at pH 7."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Extra N in an amide gives no charge."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-144a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"passage purpose","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:144#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:144"],"pid":"st-bb:P6","passage":"Parasite Z hides from the host immune system by changing the surface protein it places on infected red blood cells. These proteins, called SAPs, come from about 40 sap genes, and only one gene is on at a time. SAPs stick infected cells to vessel walls; one SAP binds protein DR2 in skin capillaries and causes skin sores. Silent sap genes sit at the edge of the nucleus in tightly packed chromatin, and the active gene moves to an open zone. Pull-down tests showed that actin binds a sap gene intron, and an actin-locking drug turned silent sap genes on.","q":"Based on the passage, switching of sap gene expression probably most directly benefits Parasite Z by reducing the parasite's:","choices":["overall stickiness to blood vessel walls.","elimination by the human adaptive immune system.","uptake by red blood cells.","sensitivity to the actin-locking drug."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Every SAP still sticks","c":[0]},{"f":"SAPs don't control cell entry","c":[2]},{"f":"Drug isn't part of infection","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Changing the coat keeps the immune system from recognizing it."},"exp":"**Answer:** elimination by the human adaptive immune system.\n\n**Incorrect: overall stickiness to blood vessel walls.** Each SAP still sticks; sticking helps the parasite.\n\n**Correct: elimination by the human adaptive immune system.** A new SAP is a new antigen, so antibodies made against the old one miss it.\n\n**Incorrect: uptake by red blood cells.** SAPs act after the parasite is inside the cell.\n\n**Incorrect: sensitivity to the actin-locking drug.** The drug is a lab tool, not a host threat.","how":{"ask":"Why does the parasite switch SAPs?","know":"Antigen switching dodges antibodies.","steps":[{"text":"The passage states the purpose.","hl":"hides from the host immune system"},{"text":"Sticking still happens with any SAP.","strike":["overall stickiness to blood vessel walls."]},{"text":"Cell entry and the lab drug are off-topic.","strike":["uptake by red blood cells.","sensitivity to the actin-locking drug."]},{"text":"Answer: elimination by the adaptive immune system."}],"trap":"overall stickiness tempts, but sticking helps survival, so the parasite would not reduce it."},"teach":[{"text":"Antigenic variation: change the coat, and old antibodies no longer match.","marks":[{"t":"changing the surface protein","note":"new antigen"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Switching surface antigens dodges adaptive immunity."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-145a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:145#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:145"],"pid":"st-bb:P6","passage":"Parasite Z hides from the host immune system by changing the surface protein it places on infected red blood cells. These proteins, called SAPs, come from about 40 sap genes, and only one gene is on at a time. SAPs stick infected cells to vessel walls; one SAP binds protein DR2 in skin capillaries and causes skin sores. Silent sap genes sit at the edge of the nucleus in tightly packed chromatin, and the active gene moves to an open zone. Pull-down tests showed that actin binds a sap gene intron, and an actin-locking drug turned silent sap genes on.","q":"The information in the passage supports the hypothesis that which type of filament is involved in moving sap genes from packed chromatin to the active expression site?","choices":["Microtubules","Intermediate filaments","Microfilaments","Myosin thick filaments"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Actin builds microfilaments","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage names actin, and actin polymers are microfilaments."},"exp":"**Answer:** Microfilaments\n\n**Incorrect: Microtubules** Microtubules are built from tubulin, not actin.\n\n**Incorrect: Intermediate filaments** These are built from proteins like keratin and lamin.\n\n**Correct: Microfilaments** Actin binds the sap intron, and actin forms microfilaments.\n\n**Incorrect: Myosin thick filaments** Thick filaments are myosin, not actin.","how":{"ask":"Which filament is made of actin?","know":"Actin = microfilament; tubulin = microtubule; myosin = thick filament.","steps":[{"text":"Find the protein named.","hl":"actin binds a sap gene intron"},{"text":"Tubulin, keratin/lamin and myosin are not actin.","strike":["Microtubules","Intermediate filaments","Myosin thick filaments"]},{"text":"Answer: Microfilaments."}],"trap":"Intermediate filaments tempts because lamins line the nucleus, but the passage names actin."},"teach":[{"text":"Match filament to protein: actin, tubulin, lamin/keratin, myosin.","marks":[{"t":"actin","note":"microfilament"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Actin in the passage means microfilaments."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-146a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"passage purpose","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:146#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:146"],"pid":"st-bb:P6","passage":"Parasite Z hides from the host immune system by changing the surface protein it places on infected red blood cells. These proteins, called SAPs, come from about 40 sap genes, and only one gene is on at a time. SAPs stick infected cells to vessel walls; one SAP binds protein DR2 in skin capillaries and causes skin sores. Silent sap genes sit at the edge of the nucleus in tightly packed chromatin, and the active gene moves to an open zone. Pull-down tests showed that actin binds a sap gene intron, and an actin-locking drug turned silent sap genes on.","q":"The information in the passage suggests that which symptom or feature of Parasite Z infection is SAP-mediated?","choices":["Low platelet count","Blood vessel blockage","Cycles of fever and chills","Sickle-trait protection from infection"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"SAPs stick cells to walls","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Sickle trait is host hemoglobin","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"SAPs glue red cells to vessel walls, and stuck cells clog vessels."},"exp":"**Answer:** Blood vessel blockage\n\n**Incorrect: Low platelet count** Nothing ties SAPs to platelets.\n\n**Correct: Blood vessel blockage** SAPs stick infected red cells to vessel walls, where they pile up and block flow.\n\n**Incorrect: Cycles of fever and chills** These come from immune responses, not sticking.\n\n**Incorrect: Sickle-trait protection from infection** That depends on the host's hemoglobin, not the parasite's SAPs.","how":{"ask":"What does SAP sticking cause?","know":"Cells stuck to vessel walls can block the vessel.","steps":[{"text":"SAPs stick infected cells to vessel walls.","hl":"SAPs stick infected cells to vessel walls"},{"text":"Platelets and fever are not tied to sticking.","strike":["Low platelet count","Cycles of fever and chills"]},{"text":"Sickle trait is a host gene.","strike":["Sickle-trait protection from infection"]},{"text":"Answer: Blood vessel blockage."}],"trap":"Cycles of fever and chills is a famous malaria-like sign, but the passage ties SAPs only to sticking."},"teach":[{"text":"Link the protein's job (adhesion) to the symptom it would cause.","marks":[{"t":"stick infected cells to vessel walls","note":"adhesion"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Pick the symptom that follows directly from the protein's stated job."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-147a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"trace air and blood flow order","freq":3,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:147#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:147"],"pid":"st-bb:P6","passage":"Parasite Z hides from the host immune system by changing the surface protein it places on infected red blood cells. These proteins, called SAPs, come from about 40 sap genes, and only one gene is on at a time. SAPs stick infected cells to vessel walls; one SAP binds protein DR2 in skin capillaries and causes skin sores. Silent sap genes sit at the edge of the nucleus in tightly packed chromatin, and the active gene moves to an open zone. Pull-down tests showed that actin binds a sap gene intron, and an actin-locking drug turned silent sap genes on.","q":"If an infected red blood cell causing skin sores, as described in the passage, lost its adhesion to the vessel wall, it would most likely first flow into which type of blood vessels?","choices":["Arterioles","Venules","Lymph vessels","Arteries"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Capillaries drain into venules","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Red cells stay in blood","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Flow order: arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins; next after capillaries is venules."},"exp":"**Answer:** Venules\n\n**Incorrect: Arterioles** Arterioles come before capillaries.\n\n**Correct: Venules** The cell was stuck in capillaries, and blood leaving capillaries enters venules.\n\n**Incorrect: Lymph vessels** Red cells stay inside blood vessels.\n\n**Incorrect: Arteries** Arteries are upstream of capillaries.","how":{"ask":"What vessel comes right after a capillary?","know":"Artery → arteriole → capillary → venule → vein.","steps":[{"text":"Find where the cell was stuck.","hl":"in skin capillaries"},{"text":"Arteries and arterioles are upstream.","strike":["Arterioles","Arteries"]},{"text":"Red cells do not enter lymph.","strike":["Lymph vessels"]},{"text":"Answer: Venules."}],"trap":"Arterioles tempts because it sounds small like a capillary, but it is upstream."},"teach":[{"text":"Find the stuck site, then step one vessel downstream.","marks":[{"t":"skin capillaries","note":"start point"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Downstream of capillaries is always venules, then veins."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-148a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"action potential ion flow","freq":7,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:148#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:148"],"pid":"st-bb:P7","passage":"Normal human color vision uses three cone pigments, tuned to blue, green and red light. A person with color weakness makes less of one pigment, while a person lacking a pigment sees with only two. The red and green pigment genes lie side by side on the X chromosome and are usually inherited together. For each pigment, the normal allele (+) is dominant over weak (′), which is dominant over very weak (″), which is dominant over absent (–). The blue pigment absorbs 380–520 nm, the green 450–640 nm and the red 470–680 nm.","q":"When fewer pigment molecules are available to absorb light (for example, when a person has a color weakness), which of the following nervous system responses occurs?","choices":["Signals for the weak color are sent to the brain through other, more plentiful pigments.","Fewer signals of the weakly perceived color are sent to the brain.","Light causes the cells to build extra pigment.","A normal number of signals for the weak color reaches the brain, but each signal is weaker."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Each cone reports one color","c":[0]},{"f":"Light doesn't make pigment","c":[2]},{"f":"Action potentials are all-or-none","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Neurons fire all-or-none, so less pigment means fewer signals, not weaker ones."},"exp":"**Answer:** Fewer signals of the weakly perceived color are sent to the brain.\n\n**Incorrect: Signals for the weak color are sent to the brain through other, more plentiful pigments.** Each cone type signals only its own color.\n\n**Correct: Fewer signals of the weakly perceived color are sent to the brain.** Less pigment catches less light, so fewer cells fire, and the brain gets fewer signals.\n\n**Incorrect: Light causes the cells to build extra pigment.** Light does not trigger pigment production.\n\n**Incorrect: A normal number of signals for the weak color reaches the brain, but each signal is weaker.** Action potentials are all-or-none; strength is coded by number, not size.","how":{"ask":"How does less pigment change the signal?","know":"Intensity is coded by firing frequency, not spike size.","steps":[{"text":"Color weakness means less pigment.","hl":"makes less of one pigment"},{"text":"Spikes are all-or-none, never weaker.","strike":["A normal number of signals for the weak color reaches the brain, but each signal is weaker."]},{"text":"Other pigments cannot carry this color, and light makes no pigment.","strike":["Signals for the weak color are sent to the brain through other, more plentiful pigments.","Light causes the cells to build extra pigment."]},{"text":"Answer: fewer signals reach the brain."}],"trap":"The weaker-signal choice feels intuitive, but neurons cannot send a half-size spike."},"teach":[{"text":"Less input means fewer action potentials, never smaller ones.","marks":[{"t":"color weakness","note":"less pigment"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Neurons code strength by spike number, not spike size."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-149a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"read table trend","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:149#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:149"],"pid":"st-bb:P7","passage":"Normal human color vision uses three cone pigments, tuned to blue, green and red light. A person with color weakness makes less of one pigment, while a person lacking a pigment sees with only two. The red and green pigment genes lie side by side on the X chromosome and are usually inherited together. For each pigment, the normal allele (+) is dominant over weak (′), which is dominant over very weak (″), which is dominant over absent (–). The blue pigment absorbs 380–520 nm, the green 450–640 nm and the red 470–680 nm.","q":"Based on the absorption ranges in the passage, which of the following statements best describes the color perception of a person with the phenotype 'green pigment absent'?","choices":["Red colors appear more green than normal.","Green colors appear more red than normal.","Both red and green colors appear more blue than normal.","Neither red nor green colors are perceived."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"No green cones to shift","c":[0]},{"f":"Blue barely overlaps green","c":[2]},{"f":"Red range covers green light","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Green light falls inside the red pigment's range, so without green pigment it excites red cones."},"exp":"**Answer:** Green colors appear more red than normal.\n\n**Incorrect: Red colors appear more green than normal.** That would need green cones, which are missing.\n\n**Correct: Green colors appear more red than normal.** The red range (470–680 nm) covers green light, so green light now drives mostly red cones.\n\n**Incorrect: Both red and green colors appear more blue than normal.** Blue (380–520 nm) barely overlaps red or green light.\n\n**Incorrect: Neither red nor green colors are perceived.** Red cones still work and respond to both.","how":{"ask":"Which cones respond to green light if green pigment is gone?","know":"Overlapping ranges mean light activates the remaining pigment.","steps":[{"text":"Read the ranges.","hl":"the green 450–640 nm and the red 470–680 nm"},{"text":"Red cones still respond, so colors are seen.","strike":["Neither red nor green colors are perceived."]},{"text":"Blue overlap is small; no green cones exist to tint red.","strike":["Both red and green colors appear more blue than normal.","Red colors appear more green than normal."]},{"text":"Answer: Green colors appear more red than normal."}],"trap":"Red colors appear more green is the mirror case for a red-absent person."},"teach":[{"text":"Missing pigment: its light is picked up by the overlapping pigment.","marks":[{"t":"red 470–680 nm","note":"covers green light"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The missing color looks like the neighbor pigment's color."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-150a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1C","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"x-linked cross probability","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:150#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:150"],"pid":"st-bb:P7","passage":"Normal human color vision uses three cone pigments, tuned to blue, green and red light. A person with color weakness makes less of one pigment, while a person lacking a pigment sees with only two. The red and green pigment genes lie side by side on the X chromosome and are usually inherited together. For each pigment, the normal allele (+) is dominant over weak (′), which is dominant over very weak (″), which is dominant over absent (–). The blue pigment absorbs 380–520 nm, the green 450–640 nm and the red 470–680 nm.","q":"A mother and father with normal color vision have a son who is color-blind with red pigment absent. What genotype did the mother most likely have?","choices":["G+R+ / G–R–","G+R+ / G+R–","G′R′ / G′R′","G″R″ / G+R+"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Linked genes pass together","c":[0]},{"f":"Son needs an R– X","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The son's only X came from mom, so she carries one X with G+R–."},"exp":"**Answer:** G+R+ / G+R–\n\n**Incorrect: G+R+ / G–R–** The linked G–R– X would make the son lack both pigments.\n\n**Correct: G+R+ / G+R–** She has normal vision (R+ is dominant) and can pass the G+R– X, giving a son with only red absent.\n\n**Incorrect: G′R′ / G′R′** She would be color-weak, and has no R– allele to pass.\n\n**Incorrect: G″R″ / G+R+** Neither X carries R–.","how":{"ask":"Which X did the son get from mom?","know":"Sons get their only X from the mother; linked genes travel together.","steps":[{"text":"The genes are linked on the X.","hl":"usually inherited together"},{"text":"A G–R– X would remove both pigments.","strike":["G+R+ / G–R–"]},{"text":"No R– allele here to pass.","strike":["G′R′ / G′R′","G″R″ / G+R+"]},{"text":"Answer: G+R+ / G+R–."}],"trap":"G+R+ / G–R– looks like a carrier, but the linked G– would also show in the son."},"teach":[{"text":"Write the son's X first (G+R–), then give it to mom with a normal X.","marks":[{"t":"side by side on the X chromosome","note":"linked, from mom"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For X-linked sons, the son's X genotype is one of mom's X's."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-151a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:151#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:151"],"pid":"st-bb:P7","passage":"Normal human color vision uses three cone pigments, tuned to blue, green and red light. A person with color weakness makes less of one pigment, while a person lacking a pigment sees with only two. The red and green pigment genes lie side by side on the X chromosome and are usually inherited together. For each pigment, the normal allele (+) is dominant over weak (′), which is dominant over very weak (″), which is dominant over absent (–). The blue pigment absorbs 380–520 nm, the green 450–640 nm and the red 470–680 nm.","q":"The genes for red-green color blindness will affect production of proteins in which of the following types of cells?","choices":["Pigment cells of the iris","Cone cells in the retina","Ganglion cells forming the optic nerve","Neurons of the visual cortex"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Iris pigment is melanin","c":[0]},{"f":"Pigments act in photoreceptors only","c":[2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The genes code cone pigments, so they act in cone cells."},"exp":"**Answer:** Cone cells in the retina\n\n**Incorrect: Pigment cells of the iris** Iris color comes from melanin, not visual pigments.\n\n**Correct: Cone cells in the retina** The red and green pigments are made in cones, the cells that absorb colored light.\n\n**Incorrect: Ganglion cells forming the optic nerve** These relay signals but make no visual pigment.\n\n**Incorrect: Neurons of the visual cortex** These process signals; they do not absorb light.","how":{"ask":"Which cells make cone pigments?","know":"Visual pigments are made in photoreceptors (cones for color).","steps":[{"text":"The genes code cone pigments.","hl":"three cone pigments"},{"text":"Iris pigment is melanin.","strike":["Pigment cells of the iris"]},{"text":"Downstream neurons do not make pigments.","strike":["Ganglion cells forming the optic nerve","Neurons of the visual cortex"]},{"text":"Answer: Cone cells in the retina."}],"trap":"Pigment cells of the iris tempts with the word pigment, but iris pigment is melanin."},"teach":[{"text":"Light is caught only by photoreceptors; later cells just relay.","marks":[{"t":"cone pigments","note":"made in cones"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A gene acts in the cells that make its protein."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-152a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:152#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:152"],"pid":"st-bb:P8","passage":"Pineal hormones and heat may both shrink the reproductive organs of rodents. Male voles had either the pineal gland removed (Px) or a fake surgery that left it in place (Sham). After a week, half of each group lived at 20°C (Normal) and half at 34°C (Hot) for 30 days. Testes weighed 2.0 g in Normal/Sham, 0.9 g in Hot/Sham, 2.6 g in Normal/Px and 1.5 g in Hot/Px. Pituitaries weighed 1.6 mg in both Sham groups and 2.1 mg in both Px groups. Brown fat (BAT), the main site of heat production, weighed 250 mg in Normal/Sham, 255 mg in Normal/Px, 160 mg in Hot/Sham and 150 mg in Hot/Px.","q":"Which of the following organelles would be relatively more abundant in BAT than in typical white adipose tissue?","choices":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","Mitochondria","Secretory vesicles"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"BAT makes heat, not secretions","c":[0,1,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"BAT burns fuel for heat, and fuel burning happens in mitochondria."},"exp":"**Answer:** Mitochondria\n\n**Incorrect: Rough endoplasmic reticulum** RER makes secreted proteins, not heat.\n\n**Incorrect: Golgi apparatus** The Golgi packages proteins and makes no heat.\n\n**Correct: Mitochondria** BAT makes heat by letting protons leak back across mitochondrial membranes, so it is packed with mitochondria.\n\n**Incorrect: Secretory vesicles** BAT is not a secretory tissue.","how":{"ask":"Which organelle makes heat?","know":"Uncoupled mitochondria turn fuel into heat.","steps":[{"text":"BAT's job is heat.","hl":"the main site of heat production"},{"text":"RER, Golgi and vesicles serve secretion.","strike":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","Secretory vesicles"]},{"text":"Answer: Mitochondria."}],"trap":"Golgi apparatus tempts if you think of fat storage, but storage and secretion do not make heat."},"teach":[{"text":"Brown color comes from iron-rich mitochondria; UCP1 lets them make heat.","marks":[{"t":"heat production","note":"mitochondria"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Match the tissue's job to its organelle: heat means mitochondria."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-153a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:153#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:153"],"pid":"st-bb:P8","passage":"Pineal hormones and heat may both shrink the reproductive organs of rodents. Male voles had either the pineal gland removed (Px) or a fake surgery that left it in place (Sham). After a week, half of each group lived at 20°C (Normal) and half at 34°C (Hot) for 30 days. Testes weighed 2.0 g in Normal/Sham, 0.9 g in Hot/Sham, 2.6 g in Normal/Px and 1.5 g in Hot/Px. Pituitaries weighed 1.6 mg in both Sham groups and 2.1 mg in both Px groups. Brown fat (BAT), the main site of heat production, weighed 250 mg in Normal/Sham, 255 mg in Normal/Px, 160 mg in Hot/Sham and 150 mg in Hot/Px.","q":"Which of the following served as control conditions in the experiment? I. Pinealectomy II. Sham surgery III. Hot (34°C) housing IV. Normal (20°C) housing","choices":["I and II only","I and III only","II and IV only","III and IV only"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Px is the treatment","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Heat is the treatment","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Controls are the untreated baseline: fake surgery and normal temperature."},"exp":"**Answer:** II and IV only\n\n**Incorrect: I and II only** Pinealectomy is the surgical treatment being tested.\n\n**Incorrect: I and III only** These are the two experimental conditions.\n\n**Correct: II and IV only** Sham surgery controls for surgery itself, and 20°C is the normal baseline temperature.\n\n**Incorrect: III and IV only** Hot housing is the heat treatment.","how":{"ask":"Which conditions are baselines?","know":"Control = same handling without the tested change.","steps":[{"text":"Sham keeps the gland.","hl":"a fake surgery that left it in place"},{"text":"Pinealectomy is a treatment.","strike":["I and II only","I and III only"]},{"text":"Heat is a treatment; 20°C is baseline.","strike":["III and IV only"]},{"text":"Answer: II and IV only."}],"trap":"I and II only tempts because both are surgeries, but only the sham is a control."},"teach":[{"text":"Each tested factor has a control: Sham for Px, Normal for Hot.","marks":[{"t":"Sham","note":"surgery control"},{"t":"20°C (Normal)","note":"temperature control"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The control is the condition missing the tested change."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-154a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"choose the control","freq":14,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:154#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:154"],"pid":"st-bb:P8","passage":"Pineal hormones and heat may both shrink the reproductive organs of rodents. Male voles had either the pineal gland removed (Px) or a fake surgery that left it in place (Sham). After a week, half of each group lived at 20°C (Normal) and half at 34°C (Hot) for 30 days. Testes weighed 2.0 g in Normal/Sham, 0.9 g in Hot/Sham, 2.6 g in Normal/Px and 1.5 g in Hot/Px. Pituitaries weighed 1.6 mg in both Sham groups and 2.1 mg in both Px groups. Brown fat (BAT), the main site of heat production, weighed 250 mg in Normal/Sham, 255 mg in Normal/Px, 160 mg in Hot/Sham and 150 mg in Hot/Px.","q":"According to the passage, the pineal gland has what effect on thermogenesis in voles kept at 20°C?","choices":["Increased thermogenesis, because BAT mass was greater in Normal/Px than in Hot/Px voles","Decreased thermogenesis, because BAT mass was greater in Normal/Sham than in Hot/Sham voles","No effect, because BAT mass was about equal in Normal/Sham and Normal/Px voles","No effect, because BAT mass was about equal in Normal/Px and Hot/Px voles"],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Pineal effect: compare Sham vs Px","c":[0,1]},{"f":"255 vs 150 isn't equal","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To see the gland's effect, compare Sham vs Px at the same temperature."},"exp":"**Answer:** No effect, because BAT mass was about equal in Normal/Sham and Normal/Px voles\n\n**Incorrect: Increased thermogenesis, because BAT mass was greater in Normal/Px than in Hot/Px voles** Both groups lack the gland; this compares temperature.\n\n**Incorrect: Decreased thermogenesis, because BAT mass was greater in Normal/Sham than in Hot/Sham voles** Both groups have the gland; this also compares temperature.\n\n**Correct: No effect, because BAT mass was about equal in Normal/Sham and Normal/Px voles** At 20°C, BAT was 250 mg with the gland and 255 mg without it, so the gland made no difference.\n\n**Incorrect: No effect, because BAT mass was about equal in Normal/Px and Hot/Px voles** 255 mg and 150 mg are far apart.","how":{"ask":"Does removing the gland change BAT at 20°C?","know":"Isolate one variable: change only pineal status.","steps":[{"text":"Find the 20°C BAT values.","hl":"250 mg in Normal/Sham, 255 mg in Normal/Px"},{"text":"Px vs Px and Sham vs Sham test heat, not the gland.","strike":["Increased thermogenesis, because BAT mass was greater in Normal/Px than in Hot/Px voles","Decreased thermogenesis, because BAT mass was greater in Normal/Sham than in Hot/Sham voles"]},{"text":"Normal/Px and Hot/Px differ a lot.","strike":["No effect, because BAT mass was about equal in Normal/Px and Hot/Px voles"]},{"text":"Answer: No effect, Normal/Sham ≈ Normal/Px."}],"trap":"The first two choices have true numbers, but they compare temperatures, not pineal status."},"teach":[{"text":"Hold everything else fixed; change only the factor asked about.","marks":[{"t":"255 mg in Normal/Px","note":"no gland, same BAT"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** To test one factor, compare groups that differ only in that factor."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-155a","s":"Passage & Data Skills","disc":"biology","aamc":"3A","skill":"SIRS 3","method":"knockout and rescue logic","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:155#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:155"],"pid":"st-bb:P8","passage":"Pineal hormones and heat may both shrink the reproductive organs of rodents. Male voles had either the pineal gland removed (Px) or a fake surgery that left it in place (Sham). After a week, half of each group lived at 20°C (Normal) and half at 34°C (Hot) for 30 days. Testes weighed 2.0 g in Normal/Sham, 0.9 g in Hot/Sham, 2.6 g in Normal/Px and 1.5 g in Hot/Px. Pituitaries weighed 1.6 mg in both Sham groups and 2.1 mg in both Px groups. Brown fat (BAT), the main site of heat production, weighed 250 mg in Normal/Sham, 255 mg in Normal/Px, 160 mg in Hot/Sham and 150 mg in Hot/Px.","q":"Consider an experiment in which normal voles are injected with either pineal-gland extract (Pgex) or saline (Psal). Which of the following results would NOT validate the results of the first experiment?","choices":["Pituitary weights of Pgex/Normal voles are less than those of Psal/Normal voles.","Testes weights of Pgex/Hot voles are less than those of Psal/Hot voles.","Pituitary weights of Pgex/Normal voles are greater than those of Psal/Normal voles.","Testes weights of Pgex/Hot voles are less than those of Pgex/Normal voles."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Extract should reverse Px","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Heat shrinks testes in all groups","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Px raised pituitary weight, so extra pineal hormone should lower it; a rise would contradict."},"exp":"**Answer:** Pituitary weights of Pgex/Normal voles are greater than those of Psal/Normal voles.\n\n**Incorrect: Pituitary weights of Pgex/Normal voles are less than those of Psal/Normal voles.** Px raised pituitary weight, so extract lowering it fits.\n\n**Incorrect: Testes weights of Pgex/Hot voles are less than those of Psal/Hot voles.** Px raised testes weight in heat, so extract lowering it fits.\n\n**Correct: Pituitary weights of Pgex/Normal voles are greater than those of Psal/Normal voles.** Removing the gland raised pituitary weight, so adding gland extract should lower it, not raise it.\n\n**Incorrect: Testes weights of Pgex/Hot voles are less than those of Pgex/Normal voles.** Heat shrank testes in every group, so this fits.","how":{"ask":"Which result contradicts the first study?","know":"Adding a gland's extract should do the opposite of removing the gland.","steps":[{"text":"Px raised pituitary weight.","hl":"1.6 mg in both Sham groups and 2.1 mg in both Px groups"},{"text":"Extract lowering pituitary or testes weight fits.","strike":["Pituitary weights of Pgex/Normal voles are less than those of Psal/Normal voles.","Testes weights of Pgex/Hot voles are less than those of Psal/Hot voles."]},{"text":"Heat shrank testes in every group.","hl":"0.9 g in Hot/Sham","strike":["Testes weights of Pgex/Hot voles are less than those of Pgex/Normal voles."]},{"text":"Answer: Pgex pituitary greater than Psal."}],"trap":"NOT questions flip the search: three choices fit, and you want the one that clashes."},"teach":[{"text":"Removal and extract are mirror tests: opposite inputs, opposite results.","marks":[{"t":"2.1 mg in both Px groups","note":"Px raises pituitary"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Hormone added should reverse what gland removal did."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-156a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"cell and organelle features","freq":9,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:156#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:156"],"q":"The individual cells making up a tissue differ from single-celled organisms such as Amoeba in that only the latter:","choices":["contain ribosomes.","can make ATP.","divide by mitosis.","can live on their own for long periods."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"All eukaryotic cells share these","c":[0,1,2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cross out anything every eukaryotic cell does; what is left is independent life."},"exp":"**Answer:** can live on their own for long periods.\n\n**Incorrect: contain ribosomes.** Every cell has ribosomes.\n\n**Incorrect: can make ATP.** Tissue cells make ATP too.\n\n**Incorrect: divide by mitosis.** Many tissue cells divide by mitosis.\n\n**Correct: can live on their own for long periods.** Tissue cells depend on the body for food, signals and support; an Amoeba lives alone.","how":{"ask":"What can only the single-celled organism do?","know":"Shared eukaryote traits cannot be the difference.","steps":[{"text":"Look for a trait tissue cells lack."},{"text":"Ribosomes, ATP and mitosis are shared.","strike":["contain ribosomes.","can make ATP.","divide by mitosis."]},{"text":"Answer: can live on their own for long periods."}],"trap":"divide by mitosis. tempts, but most body cells divide this way too."},"teach":[{"text":"Tissue cells are specialized and need neighbors; protists are self-sufficient."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For 'only X' questions, cross out traits both share."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-157a","s":"Metabolism & Enzymes","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"atp yield and anaerobic pathways","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:157#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:157"],"q":"When a red blood cell metabolizes glucose, which of the following substances is NOT produced in a significant amount?","choices":["Pyruvate","Citrate","Lactate","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Glycolysis makes F-1,6-BP, pyruvate","c":[0,3]},{"f":"RBCs turn pyruvate to lactate","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Red cells have no mitochondria, so nothing past glycolysis and lactate is made."},"exp":"**Answer:** Citrate\n\n**Incorrect: Pyruvate** Pyruvate is the end of glycolysis.\n\n**Correct: Citrate** Citrate is made in the citric acid cycle, inside mitochondria, which red cells lack.\n\n**Incorrect: Lactate** Red cells turn pyruvate into lactate to regenerate NAD+.\n\n**Incorrect: Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate** This is a glycolysis intermediate.","how":{"ask":"Which product needs mitochondria?","know":"Mature red cells rely only on glycolysis plus lactate fermentation.","steps":[{"text":"RBCs lack mitochondria."},{"text":"Glycolysis makes these.","strike":["Pyruvate","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate"]},{"text":"Fermentation makes lactate.","strike":["Lactate"]},{"text":"Answer: Citrate."}],"trap":"Lactate tempts as an odd product, but it is the normal RBC end product."},"teach":[{"text":"No mitochondria means no citric acid cycle and no oxidative phosphorylation."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Without mitochondria, glucose stops at pyruvate and lactate."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-158a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"nucleotide structure and pairing","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:158#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:158"],"q":"A particular genome is composed of 31% thymine. What percentage of the genome is cytosine?","choices":["16%","19%","31%","62%"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"31% is A, not C","c":[2]},{"f":"62% is A plus T","c":[3]},{"f":"C is half of 38%","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A = T = 31%, so G + C = 38% and C = 19%."},"exp":"**Answer:** 19%\n\n**Incorrect: 16%** This matches no correct base-pair calculation.\n\n**Correct: 19%** A = T = 31%, so A + T = 62%. The other 38% splits evenly into G and C: 19% each.\n\n**Incorrect: 31%** That is the share of A (and T), not C.\n\n**Incorrect: 62%** That is A + T together.","how":{"ask":"What is C if T is 31%?","know":"In dsDNA, A = T and G = C.","steps":[{"text":"A = T = 31%, so A + T = 62%."},{"text":"31% and 62% are A/T values.","strike":["31%","62%"]},{"text":"38% ÷ 2 = 19%, not 16%.","strike":["16%"]},{"text":"Answer: 19%."}],"trap":"31% tempts if you pair T with C by mistake."},"teach":[{"text":"Double, subtract from 100, then halve."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** C = (100 − 2 × T) ÷ 2."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-159a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"biomolecule class by structure","freq":8,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:159#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:159"],"q":"A sterol such as cholesterol has four fused rings and a small polar head consisting of:","choices":["a carboxyl group.","a hydroxyl group.","a phosphate group.","an amino group."],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Sterol = steroid alcohol, –OH","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The -ol in cholesterol marks an alcohol: its head is one –OH."},"exp":"**Answer:** a hydroxyl group.\n\n**Incorrect: a carboxyl group.** A carboxyl head belongs to fatty acids.\n\n**Correct: a hydroxyl group.** Cholesterol's only polar part is one –OH on its first ring.\n\n**Incorrect: a phosphate group.** Phosphate heads belong to phospholipids.\n\n**Incorrect: an amino group.** Cholesterol has no nitrogen.","how":{"ask":"What is cholesterol's polar head?","know":"Sterols are steroid alcohols.","steps":[{"text":"The -ol ending means alcohol."},{"text":"Carboxyl is fatty acid; phosphate is phospholipid.","strike":["a carboxyl group.","a phosphate group."]},{"text":"Cholesterol has no N.","strike":["an amino group."]},{"text":"Answer: a hydroxyl group."}],"trap":"a carboxyl group. tempts from fatty acids, but cholesterol is a sterol."},"teach":[{"text":"Heads: fatty acid = –COOH; phospholipid = phosphate; cholesterol = –OH."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Names ending in -ol are alcohols with an –OH head."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-160a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"trace pathway block to outcome","freq":22,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:160#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:160"],"pid":"st-bb:P9","passage":"In working muscle, CO2 leaves the cells and enters the blood in the capillaries. Inside red blood cells (RBCs), carbonic anhydrase speeds Equation 1: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. About 70% of blood CO2 travels as HCO3–, which leaves the RBCs in exchange for Cl– entering from the plasma. Another 23% binds hemoglobin, and H+ also binds hemoglobin, which helps it release O2. The rest dissolves in plasma, and all forms are carried to the lungs and breathed out.","q":"Administration of a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor to the RBCs would most likely cause an increase in the concentration of:","choices":["HCO3– in the RBCs.","Cl– in the RBCs.","CO2 in the tissues.","H+ in the RBCs."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Blocked enzyme makes fewer products","c":[0,3]},{"f":"Less HCO3– out, less Cl– in","c":[1]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Block the enzyme and the starting reactant, CO2, piles up."},"exp":"**Answer:** CO2 in the tissues.\n\n**Incorrect: HCO3– in the RBCs.** HCO3– is a product of the blocked reaction, so it falls.\n\n**Incorrect: Cl– in the RBCs.** Less HCO3– leaves, so less Cl– swaps in.\n\n**Correct: CO2 in the tissues.** RBCs convert CO2 more slowly, so CO2 builds up in the tissues.\n\n**Incorrect: H+ in the RBCs.** H+ is also a product and falls.","how":{"ask":"What builds up when carbonic anhydrase is blocked?","know":"Blocking a step raises what comes before it and lowers what comes after.","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme turns CO2 into H2CO3.","hl":"carbonic anhydrase speeds Equation 1"},{"text":"Products fall.","strike":["HCO3– in the RBCs.","H+ in the RBCs."]},{"text":"Less HCO3– exits, so less Cl– enters.","hl":"in exchange for Cl– entering from the plasma","strike":["Cl– in the RBCs."]},{"text":"Answer: CO2 in the tissues."}],"trap":"HCO3– tempts if you forget it is downstream of the blocked step."},"teach":[{"text":"Blocked step: upstream rises, downstream falls.","marks":[{"t":"CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3","note":"blocked step"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Inhibit an enzyme and its substrate accumulates."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-161a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"le châtelier shift","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:161#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:161"],"pid":"st-bb:P9","passage":"In working muscle, CO2 leaves the cells and enters the blood in the capillaries. Inside red blood cells (RBCs), carbonic anhydrase speeds Equation 1: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. About 70% of blood CO2 travels as HCO3–, which leaves the RBCs in exchange for Cl– entering from the plasma. Another 23% binds hemoglobin, and H+ also binds hemoglobin, which helps it release O2. The rest dissolves in plasma, and all forms are carried to the lungs and breathed out.","q":"Would the Cl– concentration of the plasma be expected to be greater in the systemic veins or the systemic arteries?","choices":["Veins, because the HCO3– concentration is higher in veins than in arteries.","Veins, because there are fewer RBCs in veins than in arteries.","Arteries, because less Cl– has moved into RBCs in arteries than in veins.","Arteries, because there are more RBCs in arteries than in veins."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"RBC counts match in both","c":[1,3]},{"f":"Venous Cl– shifts into RBCs","c":[0]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"In tissue capillaries Cl– moves from plasma into RBCs, so venous plasma has less Cl–."},"exp":"**Answer:** Arteries, because less Cl– has moved into RBCs in arteries than in veins.\n\n**Incorrect: Veins, because the HCO3– concentration is higher in veins than in arteries.** High venous HCO3– means Cl– has left the plasma, lowering plasma Cl–.\n\n**Incorrect: Veins, because there are fewer RBCs in veins than in arteries.** Blood has the same RBC count in veins and arteries.\n\n**Correct: Arteries, because less Cl– has moved into RBCs in arteries than in veins.** After tissues, Cl– has shifted into RBCs, so venous plasma Cl– is lower and arterial plasma Cl– is higher.\n\n**Incorrect: Arteries, because there are more RBCs in arteries than in veins.** RBC counts do not differ.","how":{"ask":"Where is plasma Cl– higher?","know":"Chloride shift: in tissues, HCO3– leaves RBCs and Cl– enters them.","steps":[{"text":"Cl– enters RBCs in the tissues.","hl":"in exchange for Cl– entering from the plasma"},{"text":"RBC numbers are equal.","strike":["Veins, because there are fewer RBCs in veins than in arteries.","Arteries, because there are more RBCs in arteries than in veins."]},{"text":"Venous plasma lost Cl– to RBCs.","strike":["Veins, because the HCO3– concentration is higher in veins than in arteries."]},{"text":"Answer: Arteries, less Cl– has moved in."}],"trap":"The first choice has a true fact (more HCO3– in veins), but that means less plasma Cl– there."},"teach":[{"text":"Note which compartment: RBC Cl– is high in veins, plasma Cl– is high in arteries.","marks":[{"t":"Cl– entering from the plasma","note":"plasma loses Cl–"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Chloride shift moves Cl– into RBCs in tissues and out in lungs."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-162a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"1D","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"le châtelier shift","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:162#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:162"],"pid":"st-bb:P9","passage":"In working muscle, CO2 leaves the cells and enters the blood in the capillaries. Inside red blood cells (RBCs), carbonic anhydrase speeds Equation 1: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. About 70% of blood CO2 travels as HCO3–, which leaves the RBCs in exchange for Cl– entering from the plasma. Another 23% binds hemoglobin, and H+ also binds hemoglobin, which helps it release O2. The rest dissolves in plasma, and all forms are carried to the lungs and breathed out.","q":"Hb binds O2 more tightly in a less acidic environment. This binding will therefore occur most readily when the PCO2 is:","choices":["high, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right.","high, because Equation 1 will proceed to the left.","low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right.","low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the left."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Less acid needs low CO2","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Low CO2 shifts left","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Low CO2 pulls Equation 1 left, using up H+, so blood is less acidic and Hb holds O2."},"exp":"**Answer:** low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the left.\n\n**Incorrect: high, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right.** High CO2 makes more H+, which lowers O2 binding.\n\n**Incorrect: high, because Equation 1 will proceed to the left.** Adding CO2 pushes the reaction right, not left.\n\n**Incorrect: low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right.** Removing CO2 pulls the reaction left.\n\n**Correct: low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the left.** With less CO2, H+ and HCO3– recombine, H+ drops, and Hb binds O2 more tightly, as in the lungs.","how":{"ask":"Which PCO2 gives less acid?","know":"Le Chatelier: remove a reactant and the reaction shifts toward it.","steps":[{"text":"CO2 is a reactant that makes H+.","hl":"CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–"},{"text":"High CO2 means more acid.","strike":["high, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right.","high, because Equation 1 will proceed to the left."]},{"text":"Low CO2 shifts left, not right.","strike":["low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right."]},{"text":"Answer: low, left."}],"trap":"low, because Equation 1 will proceed to the right. gets the PCO2 right but the shift backward."},"teach":[{"text":"Lungs: low CO2, less H+, Hb grabs O2. Tissues: the reverse.","marks":[{"t":"H+ also binds hemoglobin, which helps it release O2","note":"Bohr effect"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Low CO2 → shift left → less H+ → tighter O2 binding."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-163a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"3B","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"membrane transport type","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:163#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:163"],"pid":"st-bb:P9","passage":"In working muscle, CO2 leaves the cells and enters the blood in the capillaries. Inside red blood cells (RBCs), carbonic anhydrase speeds Equation 1: CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3–. About 70% of blood CO2 travels as HCO3–, which leaves the RBCs in exchange for Cl– entering from the plasma. Another 23% binds hemoglobin, and H+ also binds hemoglobin, which helps it release O2. The rest dissolves in plasma, and all forms are carried to the lungs and breathed out.","q":"Alveolar walls and capillary walls are each only one cell thick. This feature aids respiration by:","choices":["increasing the production of CO2 in the RBCs.","letting RBCs pass into the alveoli.","shortening the distance gases must diffuse.","letting Hb bind CO2 but not O2."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"Tissues, not RBCs, make CO2","c":[0]},{"f":"RBCs stay inside capillaries","c":[1]},{"f":"Thin walls don't pick gases","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Thin barrier means short diffusion distance and faster gas exchange."},"exp":"**Answer:** shortening the distance gases must diffuse.\n\n**Incorrect: increasing the production of CO2 in the RBCs.** CO2 comes from respiring tissues, not RBCs.\n\n**Incorrect: letting RBCs pass into the alveoli.** RBCs stay inside capillaries.\n\n**Correct: shortening the distance gases must diffuse.** A one-cell barrier makes the diffusion path very short, so O2 and CO2 cross fast.\n\n**Incorrect: letting Hb bind CO2 but not O2.** Wall thickness does not decide which gas binds Hb.","how":{"ask":"Why are thin walls good for gas exchange?","know":"Diffusion rate rises as distance falls (Fick's law).","steps":[{"text":"Gases cross by diffusion.","hl":"carried to the lungs and breathed out"},{"text":"RBCs neither make much CO2 nor leave vessels.","strike":["increasing the production of CO2 in the RBCs.","letting RBCs pass into the alveoli."]},{"text":"Wall thickness does not choose gases.","strike":["letting Hb bind CO2 but not O2."]},{"text":"Answer: shortening the diffusion distance."}],"trap":"letting RBCs pass into the alveoli. sounds like closer contact, but cells never leave the capillary."},"teach":[{"text":"Gas exchange speeds up with more area and less thickness.","marks":[{"t":"breathed out","note":"needs fast diffusion"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Thin, wide barriers maximize diffusion."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-164a","s":"Cells, Membranes & Transport","disc":"biology","aamc":"2A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"protein targeting and secretion","freq":6,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:164#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:164"],"pid":"st-bb:P10","passage":"Toxin VX, made by a gut bacterium, kills cells by stopping their protein synthesis. VX has an α-helical A subunit and a ring of five B subunits. The B subunits carry the toxin backward from the cell surface through early endosomes and the Golgi to the endoplasmic reticulum. There, subunit A is cut, freeing an A1 piece that damages ribosomes and halts translation. Host protein GLP90 binds VX with a Kd of 40 µM and may help it avoid being destroyed.","q":"By allowing VX to move from the early endosome to the Golgi, the B subunits permit the toxin to bypass which organelle?","choices":["Lysosome","Centriole","Nucleolus","Chloroplast"],"ans":0,"cut":[{"f":"Not on the endosome route","c":[1,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Endosome cargo normally goes on to the lysosome to be destroyed; escaping to the Golgi skips it."},"exp":"**Answer:** Lysosome\n\n**Correct: Lysosome** Early endosomes normally mature and fuse with lysosomes, which digest cargo; going to the Golgi avoids that.\n\n**Incorrect: Centriole** Centrioles organize microtubules, not trafficking.\n\n**Incorrect: Nucleolus** The nucleolus makes ribosomal RNA inside the nucleus.\n\n**Incorrect: Chloroplast** Animal cells have no chloroplasts.","how":{"ask":"Where would endosome cargo go if not rerouted?","know":"Endosome → lysosome is the default degradation path.","steps":[{"text":"The toxin leaves early endosomes for the Golgi.","hl":"through early endosomes and the Golgi"},{"text":"It avoids being destroyed.","hl":"avoid being destroyed"},{"text":"These are not on the endocytic route.","strike":["Centriole","Nucleolus","Chloroplast"]},{"text":"Answer: Lysosome."}],"trap":"Nucleolus tempts because the toxin hits ribosomes, but ribosome assembly is not the route."},"teach":[{"text":"Endocytosis default: early endosome → late endosome → lysosome.","marks":[{"t":"early endosomes","note":"normally to lysosome"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Escaping the endosome means escaping lysosomal digestion."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-165a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:165#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:165"],"pid":"st-bb:P10","passage":"Toxin VX, made by a gut bacterium, kills cells by stopping their protein synthesis. VX has an α-helical A subunit and a ring of five B subunits. The B subunits carry the toxin backward from the cell surface through early endosomes and the Golgi to the endoplasmic reticulum. There, subunit A is cut, freeing an A1 piece that damages ribosomes and halts translation. Host protein GLP90 binds VX with a Kd of 40 µM and may help it avoid being destroyed.","q":"The most likely result of various leucine to proline mutations in subunit A is:","choices":["lack of backward trafficking.","more hydrogen bonding.","loss of secondary structure.","higher catalytic activity."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"B subunits do the trafficking","c":[0]},{"f":"Pro lacks an N–H donor","c":[1]},{"f":"Broken helix lowers activity","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Proline kinks and breaks α-helices, and subunit A is α-helical."},"exp":"**Answer:** loss of secondary structure.\n\n**Incorrect: lack of backward trafficking.** The B subunits, not A, carry the toxin.\n\n**Incorrect: more hydrogen bonding.** Proline's ring N has no H, so helix H-bonds are lost.\n\n**Correct: loss of secondary structure.** Subunit A is α-helical, and proline's rigid ring breaks helices.\n\n**Incorrect: higher catalytic activity.** Disrupting the fold would lower, not raise, activity.","how":{"ask":"What does proline do to an α-helix?","know":"Proline is a helix breaker: rigid ring, no backbone N–H.","steps":[{"text":"Subunit A is a helix.","hl":"α-helical A subunit"},{"text":"Trafficking belongs to B.","hl":"The B subunits carry the toxin backward","strike":["lack of backward trafficking."]},{"text":"Proline removes an H-bond donor.","strike":["more hydrogen bonding."]},{"text":"A broken fold does not boost activity.","strike":["higher catalytic activity."]},{"text":"Answer: loss of secondary structure."}],"trap":"lack of backward trafficking. tempts, but that is subunit B's job."},"teach":[{"text":"Proline and glycine are helix breakers.","marks":[{"t":"α-helical","note":"proline breaks it"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Mutating residues to Pro in a helix destroys the helix."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-166a","s":"Evolution & Microbiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"virus and bacteria traits","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:166#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:166"],"pid":"st-bb:P10","passage":"Toxin VX, made by a gut bacterium, kills cells by stopping their protein synthesis. VX has an α-helical A subunit and a ring of five B subunits. The B subunits carry the toxin backward from the cell surface through early endosomes and the Golgi to the endoplasmic reticulum. There, subunit A is cut, freeing an A1 piece that damages ribosomes and halts translation. Host protein GLP90 binds VX with a Kd of 40 µM and may help it avoid being destroyed.","q":"Several strains of VX-making bacteria cause disease. Despite the availability of antibiotics that burst these bacteria, the reason this is NOT a recommended course of treatment is because:","choices":["VX gives antibiotic resistance once inside the host cell.","antibiotics are not effective against viruses.","GLP90 is also a target of several common antibiotics.","the burst bacteria will release toxin that disrupts host translation."],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"No data on resistance","c":[0]},{"f":"The source is a bacterium","c":[1]},{"f":"GLP90 is a host protein","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Bursting bacteria dumps their stored toxin, which then blocks host translation."},"exp":"**Answer:** the burst bacteria will release toxin that disrupts host translation.\n\n**Incorrect: VX gives antibiotic resistance once inside the host cell.** Nothing links VX to resistance.\n\n**Incorrect: antibiotics are not effective against viruses.** The toxin comes from a bacterium, not a virus.\n\n**Incorrect: GLP90 is also a target of several common antibiotics.** GLP90 is a host protein, not an antibiotic target.\n\n**Correct: the burst bacteria will release toxin that disrupts host translation.** Lysis spills VX all at once, and VX halts host protein synthesis.","how":{"ask":"Why would killing by lysis backfire?","know":"Lysing toxin-filled bacteria releases a burst of toxin.","steps":[{"text":"VX stops host translation.","hl":"halts translation"},{"text":"No resistance data; not a virus.","strike":["VX gives antibiotic resistance once inside the host cell.","antibiotics are not effective against viruses."]},{"text":"GLP90 belongs to the host.","strike":["GLP90 is also a target of several common antibiotics."]},{"text":"Answer: lysate releases toxin that disrupts translation."}],"trap":"antibiotics are not effective against viruses. is a true fact that does not apply to a bacterium."},"teach":[{"text":"Bursting a toxin maker releases its toxin load at once.","marks":[{"t":"stopping their protein synthesis","note":"toxin effect"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lytic antibiotics can worsen toxin-mediated disease."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-167a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"km, kd and rate terms","freq":5,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:167#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:167"],"pid":"st-bb:P10","passage":"Toxin VX, made by a gut bacterium, kills cells by stopping their protein synthesis. VX has an α-helical A subunit and a ring of five B subunits. The B subunits carry the toxin backward from the cell surface through early endosomes and the Golgi to the endoplasmic reticulum. There, subunit A is cut, freeing an A1 piece that damages ribosomes and halts translation. Host protein GLP90 binds VX with a Kd of 40 µM and may help it avoid being destroyed.","q":"A new compound was made that occupies the site on VX where GLP90 binds the toxin. A drug with which value of Kd for binding to VX is most effective?","choices":["400 µM","40 µM","4 µM","0.4 µM"],"ans":3,"cut":[{"f":"Must beat GLP90's 40 µM","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Lowest Kd binds tightest","c":[2]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Lower Kd means tighter binding, so pick the smallest Kd."},"exp":"**Answer:** 0.4 µM\n\n**Incorrect: 400 µM** This binds 10 times weaker than GLP90 and would lose the competition.\n\n**Incorrect: 40 µM** This only ties GLP90.\n\n**Incorrect: 4 µM** This beats GLP90, but 0.4 µM binds even tighter.\n\n**Correct: 0.4 µM** The lowest Kd binds VX most tightly and best blocks GLP90.","how":{"ask":"Which Kd gives the tightest binding?","know":"Kd is the concentration for half binding; lower Kd = higher affinity.","steps":[{"text":"GLP90 sets the bar at 40 µM.","hl":"Kd of 40 µM"},{"text":"Equal or higher Kd cannot outcompete GLP90.","strike":["400 µM","40 µM"]},{"text":"4 µM is good, but not the lowest.","strike":["4 µM"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.4 µM."}],"trap":"400 µM tempts if you think a bigger number means stronger binding."},"teach":[{"text":"Kd works like Km: small number, strong grip.","marks":[{"t":"Kd of 40 µM","note":"bar to beat"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** The best competitor has the lowest Kd."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-168a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"enzyme class from reaction","freq":12,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:168#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:168"],"pid":"st-bb:P10","passage":"Toxin VX, made by a gut bacterium, kills cells by stopping their protein synthesis. VX has an α-helical A subunit and a ring of five B subunits. The B subunits carry the toxin backward from the cell surface through early endosomes and the Golgi to the endoplasmic reticulum. There, subunit A is cut, freeing an A1 piece that damages ribosomes and halts translation. Host protein GLP90 binds VX with a Kd of 40 µM and may help it avoid being destroyed.","q":"Which type of enzyme is responsible for activating subunit A?","choices":["Ribonuclease","Protease","Endonuclease","Ligase"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Subunit A is protein, not nucleic","c":[0,2]},{"f":"Activation cuts, not joins","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Subunit A is a protein that gets cut, so a protease does it."},"exp":"**Answer:** Protease\n\n**Incorrect: Ribonuclease** Ribonucleases cut RNA, not protein.\n\n**Correct: Protease** Subunit A is a polypeptide cut to free A1, and proteases cut peptide bonds.\n\n**Incorrect: Endonuclease** Endonucleases cut inside nucleic acid chains.\n\n**Incorrect: Ligase** Ligases join molecules; activation here is a cut.","how":{"ask":"What enzyme cuts a protein?","know":"Protease = cuts peptide bonds.","steps":[{"text":"Subunit A is cut to activate it.","hl":"subunit A is cut, freeing an A1 piece"},{"text":"Nucleases cut nucleic acids.","strike":["Ribonuclease","Endonuclease"]},{"text":"Ligases join, not cut.","strike":["Ligase"]},{"text":"Answer: Protease."}],"trap":"Ribonuclease tempts because A1 damages ribosomes, but the question asks what activates A."},"teach":[{"text":"Match enzyme to the bond: peptide bond → protease.","marks":[{"t":"subunit A is cut","note":"protein cut"}]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Cutting a protein chain is a protease's job."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-169a","s":"Genetics, DNA & Gene Expression","disc":"biology","aamc":"1B","skill":"SIRS 4","method":"pick hypothesis fitting data","freq":11,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:169#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:169"],"q":"In a study of the gal genes, transcription occurred when protein GalR was absent and stopped when GalR was present. Transcription occurred only when protein Crp was bound. Given these results, are Crp and GalR transcription activators or transcription repressors?","choices":["Crp is a transcription repressor, and GalR is a transcription activator.","Crp is a transcription activator, and GalR is a transcription activator.","Crp is a transcription activator, and GalR is a transcription repressor.","Crp is a transcription repressor, and GalR is a transcription repressor."],"ans":2,"cut":[{"f":"GalR present stops transcription","c":[0,1]},{"f":"Crp needed for transcription","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Present and on = activator; present and off = repressor."},"exp":"**Answer:** Crp is a transcription activator, and GalR is a transcription repressor.\n\n**Incorrect: Crp is a transcription repressor, and GalR is a transcription activator.** Both roles are reversed.\n\n**Incorrect: Crp is a transcription activator, and GalR is a transcription activator.** GalR stops transcription, so it is not an activator.\n\n**Correct: Crp is a transcription activator, and GalR is a transcription repressor.** Transcription needs Crp bound and stops when GalR is present.\n\n**Incorrect: Crp is a transcription repressor, and GalR is a transcription repressor.** Crp is required for transcription, so it activates.","how":{"ask":"Which protein turns transcription on, and which turns it off?","know":"Activator needed for on; repressor causes off.","steps":[{"text":"GalR present: no transcription."},{"text":"So GalR is not an activator.","strike":["Crp is a transcription repressor, and GalR is a transcription activator.","Crp is a transcription activator, and GalR is a transcription activator."]},{"text":"Crp bound: transcription happens.","strike":["Crp is a transcription repressor, and GalR is a transcription repressor."]},{"text":"Answer: Crp activator, GalR repressor."}],"trap":"Mixing up 'absent' and 'present' flips both roles; reread which condition gives transcription."},"teach":[{"text":"Judge each protein by what happens when it is there."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Present + on = activator; present + off = repressor."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-170a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"1A","skill":"SIRS 2","method":"amino acid side-chain structure","freq":13,"tier":"core","from":["st-bb:170#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:170"],"q":"Introduction of which amino acid substitution would result in the largest decrease in the entropic penalty associated with a protein folding into its native conformation?","choices":["Val to Glu substitution at a buried site","Phe to Ser substitution at a surface-exposed site","Pro to Gly substitution in a flexible loop","Lys to Trp substitution at a surface-exposed site"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Buried charge costs more order","c":[0]},{"f":"Gly adds unfolded freedom","c":[2]},{"f":"Surface Trp orders water","c":[3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A greasy side chain on the surface forces water into ordered cages; swapping it for a polar one frees that water."},"exp":"**Answer:** Phe to Ser substitution at a surface-exposed site\n\n**Incorrect: Val to Glu substitution at a buried site** A buried charge disrupts the core and adds ordering, raising the penalty.\n\n**Correct: Phe to Ser substitution at a surface-exposed site** Exposed Phe makes water order around it; polar Ser lets that water move freely, lowering the entropy cost.\n\n**Incorrect: Pro to Gly substitution in a flexible loop** Gly makes the unfolded chain floppier, so folding loses more entropy.\n\n**Incorrect: Lys to Trp substitution at a surface-exposed site** This puts a large greasy group on the surface, ordering more water.","how":{"ask":"Which swap frees the most ordered water or chain freedom?","know":"Exposed hydrophobic groups order water; polar groups on the surface do not.","steps":[{"text":"Look for hydrophobic-to-polar at the surface."},{"text":"A buried charge raises the penalty.","strike":["Val to Glu substitution at a buried site"]},{"text":"Gly adds chain freedom lost on folding.","strike":["Pro to Gly substitution in a flexible loop"]},{"text":"Trp on the surface orders more water.","strike":["Lys to Trp substitution at a surface-exposed site"]},{"text":"Answer: Phe to Ser at a surface site."}],"trap":"Pro to Gly tempts as a 'flexibility' answer, but it makes the penalty larger, not smaller."},"teach":[{"text":"Hydrophobics belong inside; polars belong outside. Fixing a misplaced hydrophobic lowers entropy cost."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Surface hydrophobic → polar lowers the folding entropy penalty."}]},
  {"id":"bbt-st-171a","s":"Organ Systems & Physiology","disc":"biology","aamc":"2C","skill":"SIRS 1","method":"germ layer and cell fate","freq":2,"tier":"common","from":["st-bb:171#1"],"twinOf":["st-bb:171"],"q":"In humans, the lining of which structure is NOT primarily derived from the endoderm?","choices":["Trachea","Nasal cavity","Small intestine","Gallbladder"],"ans":1,"cut":[{"f":"Gut tube and buds: endoderm","c":[0,2,3]}],"fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Endoderm lines the gut tube and its outgrowths; the nose and mouth entrances are ectoderm."},"exp":"**Answer:** Nasal cavity\n\n**Incorrect: Trachea** The airway buds from the foregut, so its lining is endoderm.\n\n**Correct: Nasal cavity** The nasal lining comes from surface ectoderm folding inward.\n\n**Incorrect: Small intestine** The gut lining is the classic endoderm tissue.\n\n**Incorrect: Gallbladder** It buds from the foregut, so its lining is endoderm.","how":{"ask":"Which lining is not from the gut tube?","know":"Endoderm = gut tube linings and buds (lungs, liver, gallbladder, pancreas).","steps":[{"text":"Sort by origin: gut tube or surface."},{"text":"Trachea and gallbladder bud from the foregut.","strike":["Trachea","Gallbladder"]},{"text":"The intestine is gut tube.","strike":["Small intestine"]},{"text":"Answer: Nasal cavity."}],"trap":"Trachea tempts because it carries air like the nose, but it grows out of the foregut."},"teach":[{"text":"Body openings (nose, mouth, anus edge) are lined by ectoderm."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Gut tube and its buds are endoderm; entrances are ectoderm."}]}
];
